’t quietly accept it, they’ll make sure the whole country sees it. I want to know what kind of nominee they could stand behind without blowing up the rest of the coalition. If we’re going to avoid another regional rupture, this conversation has to happen now.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26097, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16025, \"description\": \"If there’s going to be a compromise, it has to start with the people who are most suspicious of the concept. The activist types, McGovern’s delegates, they don’t want a deal that just papers things over, and if they feel boxed out, they’ll take this convention down with them. I want to hear what kind of candidate they could actually sell to their people and where they’re willing to bend, before anyone else starts cutting their own deals.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26098, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16025, \"description\": \"Nothing moves in this party without organized labor deciding it’s time to move. The unions know how many delegates they can deliver, how many they can block, and how far the floor can be pushed before it snaps. If we’re going to land on a compromise nominee, it needs to be someone Meany and his people can live with and defend. I want their buy-in early, while there’s still room to work things out.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26099, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16025, \"description\": \"Right now, it’s the governors, the committee chairs, the House Leadership who are the ones holding this thing together. They have a sense of what’s viable, what will collapse under pressure, and what kind of nominee could actually survive a general election. Before we start floating names, I need to know where their red lines are and how much stability they can still guarantee. What good would it be throwing out some sacrificial lamb?\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26100, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16025, \"description\": \"This convention doesn’t have legitimacy without the Black and Hispanic delegations believing they were heard and respected. They’re not just another interest bloc, they’re the moral center of where the party says it’s been going the past few years. If there’s going to be a compromise nominee, that candidate has to reflect real progress on Civil Rights and representation. I want to start there and build outward, rather than tacking it on at the end like a footnote.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26101, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16025, \"description\": \"Look, I hate to admit it, but after 1968, we can’t go about ignoring the south. They’re organized, disciplined, and fully aware of their leverage. If they feel shut out, they won’t quietly accept it, they’ll make sure the whole country sees it. I want to know what kind of nominee they could stand behind without blowing up the rest of the coalition. If we’re going to avoid another regional rupture, this conversation has to happen now.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 2301, \"fields\": {\"question\": 1226, \"description\": \"Jesus, the guy really did it. Well Godspeed, George. I hope you know what you’re doing.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 2501, \"fields\": {\"question\": 1426, \"description\": \"If we’re going to land this plane without losing half the passengers, then the nominee has to be someone the left can recognize as one of their own, even if they don’t love every detail. That means a serious record on the war, a willingness to challenge the security state, and enough independence from the party machinery that this doesn’t look like a fix. At the same time, he has to be someone the rest of the convention can talk themselves into voting for without feeling like they’re signing a suicide note.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 2502, \"fields\": {\"question\": 1426, \"description\": \"I worry that we’re overthinking the importance of ideology when what this moment really demands is moral authority. The McGovernites didn’t come here for a clever deal, they came because they believe the party has lost its soul. There is someone out there whose personal history carries more weight than any platform vote, who speaks about justice and responsibility in a way that doesn’t sound rehearsed. He wouldn’t be anyone’s first guess, but he might be the only man who can talk McGovern into stepping aside without feeling humiliated.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 2503, \"fields\": {\"question\": 1426, \"description\": \"Whatever deal we cut has to make sense in the places that matter, where people work with their hands and don’t trust anyone who sounds like they flew in for the week. There’s one senator who knows how to talk about reform in plain language, who comes out of the prairie rather than the faculty lounge, and who understands how to connect the anger of the activists with the frustrations of rural and small-town voters. He gives the McGovern people someone they can respect, but he also speaks to the kind of voter we’ve been bleeding for years. If we’re serious about building something that lasts past November, that kind of populism might be the glue we need.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 2504, \"fields\": {\"question\": 1426, \"description\": \"The cleanest break is to embrace what this convention already represents. The party is younger, more urban, more diverse than it was even four years ago, and pretending otherwise won’t make it go away. There’s one candidate whose career reflects that shift almost uncomfortably well, with real standing among city delegates and minority leaders and very little patience for the old wheelin’ and dealin’. The question isn’t whether he excites the insurgents, but whether the rest of the party is ready to accept what that choice would say.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 2701, \"fields\": {\"question\": 1626, \"description\": \"This has been a bruising year for the party, and nobody feels that more than Hubert Humphrey. He took the hard road, held the coalition together when it would have been easier to let it splinter, and he earned this nomination the old-fashioned way. I won’t pretend every Democrat leaves this convention hall happy, but that was never a realistic standard. My job was to deliver a nominee who can win a general election, and that job is done. Now it’s on all of us to remember what’s at stake this November.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 2901, \"fields\": {\"question\": 1826, \"description\": \"This has been a bruising year for the party, and nobody feels that more than Hubert Humphrey. He took the hard road, held the coalition together when it would have been easier to let it splinter, and he earned this nomination the old-fashioned way. I won’t pretend every Democrat leaves this convention hall happy, but that was never a realistic standard. My job was to deliver a nominee who can win a general election, and that job is done. Now it’s on all of us to remember what’s at stake this November.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21101, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11026, \"description\": \"After years of turmoil and chaos, our party made a choice to steady the ship. Edmund Muskie brings experience, discipline, and a seriousness that voters have been asking for since 1968. I won’t deny that some people wanted a different outcome, and some of them will make noise on the way out. That’s politics. My responsibility was to make sure this party didn’t tear itself apart in the process, and that we emerged with a nominee who can credibly take on Richard Nixon. On that front, I have no doubts.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21301, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11226, \"description\": \"After years of turmoil and chaos, our party made a choice to steady the ship. Edmund Muskie brings experience, discipline, and a seriousness that voters have been asking for since 1968. I won’t deny that some people wanted a different outcome, and some of them will make noise on the way out. That’s politics. My responsibility was to make sure this party didn’t tear itself apart in the process, and that we emerged with a nominee who can credibly take on Richard Nixon. On that front, I have no doubts.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21501, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11426, \"description\": \"This convention came closer than it ever should have to nominating George Wallace for president. That’s a fact we’re all going to have to sit with. But he’s gone now. He took his delegates and his grievances and walked out the door, and in doing so he changed the map overnight. Hubert Humphrey is still standing, still respected by organized labor, still capable of holding together what remains of our coalition. Wallace will bleed Nixon dry across the Deep South, and Humphrey gives us a chance to compete everywhere else without apologizing for who we are.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21502, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11426, \"description\": \"I won’t pretend this feels like a triumph. We stared right at the edge of something ugly, and we all know how close we came to falling over it. But Wallace is gone now. And he’s taking his campaign straight into Nixon’s backyard and daring the Republicans to deal with him. That opens a door we didn’t expect to have. Edmund Muskie gives our party steadiness after chaos, credibility after embarrassment, and a chance to present ourselves as the only adult left in the room. My job was to help nominate the strongest possible candidate against Nixon. I believe we’ve done just that.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21701, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11626, \"description\": \"God help us.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21901, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11826, \"description\": \"God help us.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22101, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12026, \"description\": \"God help us.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22301, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12226, \"description\": \"This convention came closer than anyone imagined to making history, and that counts for something. The energy Ms. Chisholm brought was electric, but the reality is half the party wasn’t ready for it. We’ve stalled the floor just enough, and Hubert Humphrey is still standing, respected by labor, trusted by our leaders, and able to hold the coalition together. He may not excite every activist in the room, but he gives the party a chance to pick itself up without alienating the groups who feel this moment matters. Chisholm’s supporters will watch, but at least they’ll see that their voices shaped the process and that their energy is no longer being ignored.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22302, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12226, \"description\": \"I won’t lie, this is tense. Ms. Chisholm has made her case, with far more support than I could’ve imagined, and that counts for something. But the party can’t survive if we let the momentum alone decide the nominee. Ed Muskie gives us credibility, steadiness, and a bridge to the moderates who would otherwise bolt to Dick Nixon. The activists may not get exactly what they wanted, but their influence matters, and this choice signals that the party has listened and negotiated in good faith. Our coalition will stay intact, and we at least preserve the chance to compete nationally without betraying the principles these delegates fought for.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22501, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12426, \"description\": \"I’ll be damned, she really did it. We’ll all pay the price in November, but by God, she earned it.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22701, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12626, \"description\": \"So help me God.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22901, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12826, \"description\": \"So help me God.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23101, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13026, \"description\": \"Here’s what’s been sitting with me. The people in that room care about trust more than anything else right now. They want someone they believe will tell the truth even when it costs him something. Someone who goes after the forces of power when they need to be challenged. If we’re going to calm these people down, it will be with someone who carries real intellectual weight and a reputation for independence, someone who sounds like he’s thinking about the country instead of the next applause line.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23102, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13026, \"description\": \"I keep thinking about how much of this is about moral credibility. These people are tired, and a lot of them feel like politics has stopped talking about people altogether. If there’s a way through this, it may be with someone whose voice feels grounded in personal experience, someone who speaks plainly about suffering and responsibility and hardship because he has lived it. That kind of presence changes the temperature in a room. I don’t know if it solves everything, but it gives the party something human to hold onto.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23103, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13026, \"description\": \"We have to be honest about where our party is losing ground. If the activists walk out with someone who has never had to fight for votes outside a friendly audience, we’re just setting ourselves up for another 1968. We need someone who has already won in tough places, who knows how to talk to white farmers and urban blacks and working families without sounding like he’s reading from some pamphlet. It doesn’t have to be someone who’s polished, practiced, let ‘em be rough around the edges. That combination is rare, but it matters.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23104, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13026, \"description\": \"There’s another argument on the table, and it’s about visibility and energy. A lot of these organizers are urban, young, going to college, they’re used to seeing leadership up close. They respond to someone they recognize as a governing figure, someone who takes public stands and lives with the consequences. There is risk there, everyone knows it, but there’s also momentum. If we’re trying to signal that the party is moving forward instead of circling the wagons, that kind of profile is going to come up whether we like it or not.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23301, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13226, \"description\": \"Alright, if they’re walking in here looking for reassurance, we have to start with someone they already trust. Someone who’s spent real time with the unions, who knows their leadership, who understands how these fights actually play out when the doors are closed. If we float a name like that, it tells them we’re not freelancing and we’re not trying to cut them out. It gives us a chance to talk about holding things together before anyone starts digging in.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23302, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13226, \"description\": \"There’s a big question of trust here, on both sides of the hall. The activists want to know they’re not being sold out, and the guys on the shop floor want to know they’re not being talked down to. Meany will understand. If there’s a way to hold that together, it’s with someone who has put in the time legislating, who has stood with workers publicly and privately, and who people believe when he says he can deliver. That kind of credibility buys us room to breathe.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23303, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13226, \"description\": \"I don’t think we should ignore the appeal of steadiness right now. Labor leaders are looking for reassurance, stability, consistency. Someone who has earned their backing over time, who has shown up and done the work, and who also plays better in parts of the country where this party has been slipping. If they feel confident their interests won’t get sidelined once the gavel drops, they’re a lot more likely to hold the line for us through this convention.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23501, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13426, \"description\": \"Alright, if they’re walking in here looking for reassurance, we have to start with someone they already trust. Someone who’s spent real time in Washington, who knows the House and Senate leadership, who understands how these fights actually play out when the doors are closed. If we float a name like that, it tells them we’re not freelancing and we’re not trying to cut them out. It gives us a chance to talk about holding things together before anyone starts digging in.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23502, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13426, \"description\": \"There’s a big question of trust here, on both sides of the hall. The activists want to know they’re not being sold out, and the governors and senators want to get stuff done. Mansfield and Byrd will surely understand. If there’s a way to hold that together, it’s with someone who has put in the time legislating, who has stood with our party publicly and privately, and who people believe when he says he can deliver. That kind of credibility buys us room to breathe.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23503, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13426, \"description\": \"If the House and Senate leadership are scared this thing is getting away from us, they’re going to want to hear a name that calms them down. Someone they already associate with order, with knowing how the place actually works. The kind of person who doesn’t frighten anyone. Someone the committee chairs trust, the donors recognize, and the leadership believes can keep the lights on. I’m not saying it solves the bigger problem, but it buys us time, and right now time might be the only thing keeping this convention from blowing up.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23701, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13626, \"description\": \"We have to face the fact that the South isn’t just sitting this out. If we walk out of here with someone who sounds like he’s never had to ask for votes in a courthouse town or a farming county, we’re going to lose them outright. We need someone who has actually competed in tough territory, who knows how to talk to white farmers, small town voters, and working class communities without it feeling forced or rehearsed. It doesn’t have to be elegant. In fact, it probably shouldn’t be. A little roughness goes a long way down there, and people can tell when it’s real.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23702, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13626, \"description\": \"If we’re talking about keeping the South from blowing up on us, we should be thinking about someone who’s already done the job in the first place. Someone who has worked in a tense environment, kept things from spiraling, and didn’t let either side drag open another culture war. Ideally someone who knows how to talk to white Southerners as equals, without preaching to them and without indulging their worst instincts either. We’ve got to show that you can move the region forward quietly, keep the activists from overrunning the place, and still hold the center. By God, we’ve got to hold the center.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23703, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13626, \"description\": \"There’s also an argument here about steadiness, especially for the border states and the Upper South. The south wants to know they’re not being left behind, not being forgotten by the party mainstream up north. Someone who doesn’t make them feel like they’re being dragged someplace against their will. There’s plenty of solid, loyal party men that can head down below the Mason-Dixon and not be pelted with tomatoes. That kind of reliability reassures people who are nervous about where this party is headed that they’re not gonna be ignored going forward.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23704, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13626, \"description\": \"There’s a cold reality we gotta live with - preventing another 1968. If the southern delegations and their senior leaders in Congress think this convention is slipping out of control, they’re going to gravitate toward a name that feels familiar. Someone who represents order, who understands the machinery, who committee chairs and donors already trust. It doesn’t answer every question about the future of the party, but it tells the South that the adults are still in the room. Right now, that alone might keep even a few delegations from bolting.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23901, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13826, \"description\": \"We have to be honest about where our party is losing ground. If the black and women’s groups walk out with someone who has never had to fight for votes outside a friendly audience, we’re just setting ourselves up for another 1968. We need someone who has already won in tough places, who knows how to talk to white farmers and urban blacks and working families without sounding like he’s reading from some pamphlet. It doesn’t have to be someone who’s polished, practiced, let ‘em be rough around the edges. That combination is rare, but it matters.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23902, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13826, \"description\": \"If we’re talking about winning over the Black delegates, we can’t pretend this is just a northern problem. They’re going to ask whether anyone at this table has actually stood up for the southern blacks, back when it really cost something. Not just voted right when it was safe, but taken the heat back home and kept going. Someone who knows how to talk to white Southerners without winking at segregation, and who has already proven he can bring Black voters into the party without setting the whole region on fire. That kind of record carries weight in a room like this, even if it doesn’t make headlines.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23903, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13826, \"description\": \"There’s another argument on the table, and it’s about visibility and energy. A lot of these organizers are urban, young, poor, they’re used to seeing leadership up close. They respond to someone they recognize as a governing figure, someone who takes public stands and lives with the consequences. There is risk there, everyone knows it, but there’s also momentum. If we’re trying to signal that the party is moving forward instead of circling the wagons, that kind of profile is going to come up whether we like it or not.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 24101, \"fields\": {\"question\": 14026, \"description\": \"Hey. It’s me. Thanks for getting back to me. I won’t pretend this is easy, but I’m coming to you empty-handed. The convention is tearing itself apart at the seams, and whoever I talk to, whoever I ask, one name gets brought up each time. They’re looking for someone who can remind them what this party is supposed to stand for. I’m not calling to twist your arm, but we’re days away from being without a nominee. Please, I’m running out of options here.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21305, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11227, \"description\": \"I have never believed that a strong country is built by leaving people to fend for themselves. I grew up around working families who did everything right and still worried about whether the next layoff or illness would knock them flat. That is why I’ve spent my career fighting for full employment, fair wages, and the right of workers to organize as they need. This party exists because we understand that work is about dignity as much as it is about income. When I am president, the measure of our economy will be whether people who put in an honest day’s work can build a decent life without fear of falling behind.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21306, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11227, \"description\": \"I know there are Americans listening tonight who want to know whether I understand the cost of this war, not in theory, but in human terms. I do. I’ve wrestled with the consequences of the decisions I made in a difficult time, and I’ve learned from them. What I believe now is simple. This war has gone on too long, and it has asked too much of too many. Ending it responsibly is not a sign of weakness. It is a recognition that leadership requires judgment, humility, and the courage to change course when the facts demand it. I am committed to bringing this war to an end and beginning the work of healing at home.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21307, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11227, \"description\": \"There are voices in this country telling us that government is the problem, that caring too much is a mistake, and that we should lower our expectations of what we can do together. I reject that view. The Great Society has expanded healthcare access, opened the doors of education, and lifted millions out of poverty. If these programs faltered, it wasn’t because they were wrong. They faltered when we stopped tending to them. I am running because I believe we can govern with both compassion and competence, and because walking away from responsibility has never made this country stronger.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21308, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11227, \"description\": \"I learned a long time ago that justice doesn’t arrive on its own. It has to be argued for, voted for, and defended when it’s under attack. Civil Rights are not a chapter we can close or a promise we can postpone. They are a continuing obligation. I believe our party must always be willing to say plainly that equality under the law is not optional and not negotiable. If we lose our moral compass, no amount of political skill will ever make up for it.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21505, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11427, \"description\": \"I have never believed that a strong country is built by leaving people to fend for themselves. I grew up around working families who did everything right and still worried about whether the next layoff or illness would knock them flat. That is why I’ve spent my career fighting for full employment, fair wages, and the right of workers to organize as they need. This party exists because we understand that work is about dignity as much as it is about income. When I am president, the measure of our economy will be whether people who put in an honest day’s work can build a decent life without fear of falling behind.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21506, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11427, \"description\": \"I know there are Americans listening tonight who want to know whether I understand the cost of this war, not in theory, but in human terms. I do. I’ve wrestled with the consequences of the decisions I made in a difficult time, and I’ve learned from them. What I believe now is simple. This war has gone on too long, and it has asked too much of too many. Ending it responsibly is not a sign of weakness. It is a recognition that leadership requires judgment, humility, and the courage to change course when the facts demand it. I am committed to bringing this war to an end and beginning the work of healing at home.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21507, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11427, \"description\": \"There are voices in this country telling us that government is the problem, that caring too much is a mistake, and that we should lower our expectations of what we can do together. I reject that view. The Great Society has expanded healthcare access, opened the doors of education, and lifted millions out of poverty. If these programs faltered, it wasn’t because they were wrong. They faltered when we stopped tending to them. I am running because I believe we can govern with both compassion and competence, and because walking away from responsibility has never made this country stronger.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21508, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11427, \"description\": \"I learned a long time ago that justice doesn’t arrive on its own. It has to be argued for, voted for, and defended when it’s under attack. Civil Rights are not a chapter we can close or a promise we can postpone. They are a continuing obligation. I believe our party must always be willing to say plainly that equality under the law is not optional and not negotiable. If we lose our moral compass, no amount of political skill will ever make up for it.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21705, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11627, \"description\": \"I have never believed that a strong country is built by leaving people to fend for themselves. I grew up around working families who did everything right and still worried about whether the next layoff or illness would knock them flat. That is why I’ve spent my career fighting for full employment, fair wages, and the right of workers to organize as they need. This party exists because we understand that work is about dignity as much as it is about income. When I am president, the measure of our economy will be whether people who put in an honest day’s work can build a decent life without fear of falling behind.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21706, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11627, \"description\": \"I know there are Americans listening tonight who want to know whether I understand the cost of this war, not in theory, but in human terms. I do. I’ve wrestled with the consequences of the decisions I made in a difficult time, and I’ve learned from them. What I believe now is simple. This war has gone on too long, and it has asked too much of too many. Ending it responsibly is not a sign of weakness. It is a recognition that leadership requires judgment, humility, and the courage to change course when the facts demand it. I am committed to bringing this war to an end and beginning the work of healing at home.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21707, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11627, \"description\": \"There are voices in this country telling us that government is the problem, that caring too much is a mistake, and that we should lower our expectations of what we can do together. I reject that view. The Great Society has expanded healthcare access, opened the doors of education, and lifted millions out of poverty. If these programs faltered, it wasn’t because they were wrong. They faltered when we stopped tending to them. I am running because I believe we can govern with both compassion and competence, and because walking away from responsibility has never made this country stronger.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21708, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11627, \"description\": \"I learned a long time ago that justice doesn’t arrive on its own. It has to be argued for, voted for, and defended when it’s under attack. Civil Rights are not a chapter we can close or a promise we can postpone. They are a continuing obligation. I believe our party must always be willing to say plainly that equality under the law is not optional and not negotiable. If we lose our moral compass, no amount of political skill will ever make up for it.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21309, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11228, \"description\": \"The simple truth is this: we spend too much time in Washington talking to each other instead of listening to the people we claim to represent. I want someone who comes out of small town America, someone who talks the way families talk when they’re sitting around a kitchen table worrying about the bills or the future of their farm. A voice like that keeps politics grounded. It reminds the country that the Democratic Party is supposed to stand with people who feel like the system stopped noticing them.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21310, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11228, \"description\": \"This moment calls for seriousness. The country has watched too many politicians treat government like a stage for clever speeches. I want a running mate who approaches public office like a responsibility that has to be studied and respected. Someone who has spent years thinking carefully about the Constitution, how government is structured, and the way laws actually shape people’s lives. That kind of temperament would show voters that this ticket intends to govern thoughtfully and with purpose.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21311, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11228, \"description\": \"The party needs someone who can speak to people in parts of the country where Democrats have been losing ground. Not by lecturing them or pretending the last few years never happened, but by meeting them where they are and talking about progress in a language they trust. A Southern leader ideally, one who has governed responsibly and who understands the pace of change in that region could help us reopen conversations we have not had in a long time.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21909, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11828, \"description\": \"Look, I know some people are raising eyebrows about George’s reputation. I know what people say about him. But I’ve seen him on the trail, and I see the energy he brings to young voters and minorities and people who have felt ignored by politics for too long. That passion is a strength. He can help our campaign speak to a generation that wants to see change, and that’s something we can’t afford to dismiss.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21910, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11828, \"description\": \"I know some young voters think that I’m a relic of the past. And I admit, I was misled on the Vietnam War as much as anyone. But I’ve spent the last four years committed to bringing an end to this endless bloodshed. And I wouldn’t have gotten there if it weren’t for principled voices like George McGovern. Who better to have by my side in that effort than someone who has made that commitment his central cause?\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21911, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11828, \"description\": \"People may see him as radical, but that couldn’t be further from the truth. He’s been elected twice statewide in South Dakota of all places. He’s a man of the prairie who knows how to talk to his neighbors, keep his word, and make hard decisions without the typical theatrics we’ve come to expect. George is honest, grounded, and capable of representing a broad spectrum of Americans. I’m proud to have him by my side.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21313, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11229, \"description\": \"The images coming out of Southeast Asia are deeply troubling, and any loss of innocent life weighs heavily on the conscience of this country. War is never clean, and tragic errors can occur even when our forces are acting in good faith. If military authorities say this incident was unintended, that claim deserves serious consideration. Our responsibility is to reduce civilian harm, improve oversight, and continue the difficult work of bringing this conflict to a responsible conclusion.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21314, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11229, \"description\": \"No American can look at photographs of burned villages and wounded children without feeling shaken. These are human beings caught in the middle of forces far beyond their control. That moral reality can’t be brushed aside with technical explanations alone. If we are to justify this war to ourselves and to the world, we must show that civilian lives matter, and that our conduct reflects the values we claim to defend.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21315, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11229, \"description\": \"Incidents like this raise serious questions about whether the war is being conducted in a way that actually serves American interests. When civilian casualties mount, they undermine our credibility abroad, harden opposition at home, and make a political settlement more difficult to achieve. That isn’t just a moral problem, but a strategic one as well. It is time to reassess our tactics and press harder for a negotiated end to the conflict.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21316, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11229, \"description\": \"What troubles Americans most is the growing distance between what they see with their own eyes and what they are told by their government. Simply put, there is a clear credibility gap today that has undermined the legitimacy of our government. A democracy cannot sustain a long war without honesty from its leaders. If this administration cannot be candid about what is happening in Southeast Asia, then it has no business asking the American people to continue supporting this conflict.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21317, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11230, \"description\": \"I’ve heard that line before, that experience somehow becomes a liability the longer you serve. I reject that notion. The problems this country faces are not theoretical. They involve real issues that affect the daily lives of working families. I was there when President Johnson passed the Civil Rights Act and the Great Society. I’ve also been in the room when tough decisions were made. This election is not about chasing every new slogan or novel idea. It’s about whether the next president understands the machinery of government well enough to make it serve the people again.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21318, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11230, \"description\": \"I don’t believe that America is divided into camps old and young that can’t speak to each other. There’s plenty of people of all ages who want the same things, who want peace, fairness, a future they can trust. Those aren’t radical ideas, they’re the same goals that brought me into public life decades ago. I may not speak in the language of every new movement, but I listen, and I learn. Progress won’t come from discarding one generation for another. It comes from building bridges between them, and that is the kind of leadership I offer.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21319, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11230, \"description\": \"People forget that many of the ideas now described as bold or disruptive were once considered reckless when I first fought for them. Civil rights wasn’t fashionable in 1948. Medicare was not inevitable. Labor protections did not pass themselves. Change doesn’t come from standing on the sidelines declaring purity. It comes from pushing institutions forward, step by step, until they can’t go back. I’m proud of my record to push our nation forward.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21320, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11230, \"description\": \"This election is not a referendum on the latest fad. It’s a choice between two kinds of leadership. Dick Nixon governs through secrecy, division, and distrust. But I believe that democracy works best when the public is treated as a partner, not an obstacle. I’m not running to be the newest face on television. I am running to restore honesty, decency, and integrity to the presidency. Contrast that with the Nixon White House, the choice couldn’t be clearer.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21321, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11231, \"description\": \"I’m not going to pretend the people who pushed this party forward over the last decade don’t matter. I’ve known many of them for years, and I respect what brought them into politics. If the energy has cooled down, then that’s on us to reignite it. That means being present on the campuses, meeting directly with student leaders, women’s groups, civil rights organizations, and antiwar activists, not through press releases or surrogates but face-to-face. More field offices near colleges, more listening sessions, fewer assumptions. I’ve spent my career building coalitions the hard way, and I’m willing to do it again.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21322, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11231, \"description\": \"This campaign has to speak to the whole country, not just the most energized corners of the party. A lot of Americans are tired, worried about prices, jobs, and whether things feel stable again. Working families, union households, farmers, suburban voters, and independents need to know they can rely on steady leadership. I’m not backing away from my record or my values, but I’m not going to let this campaign be driven by constant internal debates either. If we hold the center together, we give ourselves a real chance to win and govern.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21323, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11231, \"description\": \"I’ve been in enough campaigns to know this pattern. There’s always frustration on the left when the general election starts. They’re young, idealistic. Of course they’re gonna be frustrated. But most Democrats understand what’s at stake when November comes. They may be disappointed, they may argue, but they know the difference between disagreement and surrender. I’ve been clear about who I am and what I believe for a long time now. This campaign isn’t going to be making any sudden pivots. It’s about experience, seriousness, and giving Democrats a nominee they can trust when it’s time to cast a ballot.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21324, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11231, \"description\": \"I don’t think the answer is for me to lecture people who already know my record. But there are voices in this party who have real credibility with the activists and the student movement. I want people like George McGovern, Frank Church, and other respected progressives to sit down with the student organizers, the antiwar leaders, the women’s groups, and talk honestly about where this campaign is headed. Not to strong-arm anyone, just to keep the conversation open and make it clear they haven’t been pushed aside. Keeping those relationships alive matters, even when we don’t agree on every tactic.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21325, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11232, \"description\": \"An unfortunate, but entirely expected development. All we can do is redouble our campaign efforts on the ground. I’ll visit every major city in the South, meet with local Democrats, and do as many rallies as I can. People need to see that we hear them and understand the challenges they face. Let’s see if we can’t light up some old loyalties the old-fashioned way.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21326, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11232, \"description\": \"John Connally is trying to use his connections to push Democrats into Nixon’s camp, but we have an ace up our sleeve here. It’s no secret that President Johnson and I have our disagreements, but there’s no way he’d ever endorse Nixon. I plan to go to President Johnson’s ranch, talk with him candidly, and ask him to publicly back our ticket. There’s no way Connally’s voice can compete with a heavyweight like Johnson.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21327, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11232, \"description\": \"Four years ago, Connally stood by my side against Dick Nixon. Today, he completely switches parties. You can’t possibly tell me this has anything to do with his convictions. I’ll make it clear that his endorsement isn’t a mark of wisdom or courage, it’s blatant opportunism, and it isn’t worth a damned nickel. Democrats deserve to know that the man who left his party to chase power in Washington doesn’t represent our values.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21328, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11232, \"description\": \"If John Connally wants to run this ‘Democrats for Nixon’ shtick, then fine. Two can play at that game. I’ll reach out to some former and even current Republicans who share our concerns. John Lindsay can take the lead, and see if we can’t get in touch with McCloskey and Reid. I hear Don Riegle might be switching over soon too. Nixon has spent the past four years pandering to the far right. Let’s show the voters just how many Republicans he’s chased away.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21329, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11233, \"description\": \"I understand why so many people are angry, and many of the concerns being raised in these protests are completely legitimate. The war, civil rights, and economic inequality have left a lot of Americans feeling shut out. But violence and destruction of property only weaken those causes and drag innocent people into the crossfire, and that’s not something I can support.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21330, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11233, \"description\": \"I’m not going to throw anyone under the bus, let alone so many patriotic Americans speaking out for what they feel is right. Peaceful protest is a constitutional right, and dissent is not the same thing as disorder. It is dangerous to treat protest itself as a threat to the country. President Nixon should be ashamed of himself for his brazen attempts to politicize our freedoms.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21331, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11233, \"description\": \"Look, a lot of these folks supported Mr. McGovern this year. They’re young, they’re idealistic. And they’re angry too, they want change. But ultimately, so many of these protests have gone too far, and they have done real damage to the credibility of the very causes these well-meaning young people claim to champion. If people want to change this country, they need to grow up and learn to work within the system instead of disrupting public life and turning voters against them.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21332, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11233, \"description\": \"I’m a proud Democrat, and I am no radical. Anyone who thinks I condone any kind of reckless law-breaking or unprovoked violence is mistaken. If a protest or march turns violent or disrupts the peace, then I support the protesters being punished to the extent the law calls for. To claim I think anything else is just another rumor started by Dick Nixon’s flying monkeys.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21333, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11234, \"description\": \"I’m not running against Spiro Agnew. I’m running against Richard Nixon and his record. The vice president seems to spend his days picking fights and throwing insults, and I don’t intend to join him. I’m going to keep talking about the war, the economy, and the direction of the country, and let voters decide who sounds like a president.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21334, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11234, \"description\": \"Agnew isn’t some independent actor. He says what Nixon wants to say, just louder and cruder. When he attacks protesters, the press, or whole groups of Americans, he’s doing it to distract from the failures of this administration. I’m going to remind people that his tone comes straight from the top.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21335, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11234, \"description\": \"The vice president is deliberately trying to pit Americans against each other. He mocks legitimate dissent, ridicules any disagreement, and treats whole parts of this country like mortal enemies. That might win applause at his campaign rallies, but it’s no way to govern a democracy, and it’s certainly no way to heal a country that’s already deeply divided.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21336, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11234, \"description\": \"I think most Americans are tired of being yelled at by their own government. They want a leadership that lowers the temperature, not raises it. I still believe that character counts in our country. I’ve been an honest public servant for 30 years. I’ve committed my life to serving the people of Minnesota. Spiro Agnew? He’s just a lying attack dog who spends his free time golfing.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21337, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11235, \"description\": \"I was there. I don’t dodge that, and I won’t pretend otherwise. This war changed the country, and it changed me. If someone tells you they learned nothing from the last decade, they’re either lying or not paying attention. I didn’t turn against this war because it became unpopular. I turned against it because it was wrong. Real leadership doesn’t mean freezing yourself in time. It’s about knowing when the facts have changed and having the courage to say so out loud, even when it costs you.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21338, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11235, \"description\": \"People forget what the Vice Presidency is and what it isn’t. I served loyally, because that is what the job entails, but I was not the president and I did not command the war. I pushed President Johnson again and again for negotiation, for restraint, for a way out that would not keep feeding young men into an endless grinder. When I speak now, I speak with my own voice, unbound by that office. Voters deserve honesty about what power I had and what I did with it.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21339, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11235, \"description\": \"No single man made this war, and no single man could stop it once it was in motion. The quagmire in Vietnam was the product of years of assumptions, institutions, and Cold War habits that went unchallenged for too long. I was part of that system, and I saw how it failed. That’s exactly why I know it can’t be trusted to correct itself. Ending this war will take someone who understands how those decisions were made, how our ideological beliefs supplanted honest judgment, and how to dismantle a policy machine that keeps saying that we’ll fix tomorrow what we’ll break today.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21340, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11235, \"description\": \"I refuse to accept lectures on consistency from an administration that promised peace with honor and gave us a creeping expansion. I have been honest about my journey on this war. The president hasn’t been honest about his. I’m offering the country a clean break, a real end to this bloody conflict, and a foreign policy that doesn’t confuse toughness with stubbornness. Voters can decide for themselves whether they want careful judgment shaped by hard-earned experience or empty assurances wrapped in secrecy and deception.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21341, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11236, \"description\": \"What happened in Munich was a tragedy, plain and simple. Innocent athletes went to represent their countries in peace, and they were murdered instead. Tonight, my thoughts are with the families of the Israeli team and with the German people who tried to protect their guests. Acts like this offend the conscience of all nations, and they deserve our shared grief and resolve to see that such crimes are never repeated.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21342, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11236, \"description\": \"The murder of Israeli athletes in Munich was an act of terror against innocent people and against the very idea of peaceful competition. Israel has the right to exist, the right to security, and the right to see its citizens protected from violence wherever they go. The United States must stand firmly with Israel in condemning this attack and in opposing terrorism in all its forms. There is no political cause that justifies the killing of civilians, ever.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21343, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11236, \"description\": \"What happened in Munich is a grim reminder that violence isn’t confined by borders, or uniforms, or battlefields. It threatens civilians, the international order, and the basic sense of safety people expect in the modern world. The United States must work with its allies to strengthen international cooperation against terrorism, improve intelligence sharing, and ensure that people are safe wherever they go.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21345, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11237, \"description\": \"If we reach out to Wallace, we have to keep the focus on the economic concerns that drove much of his support. Talk about wages, inflation, and the sense that working people have been squeezed while Washington argues over everything else. Make the case that this campaign is serious about those issues and isn’t just speaking to one part of the country. If he sees that we’re addressing the same frustrations his voters feel, he may not endorse us outright, but he could soften his tone and speak more favorably about the campaign.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21346, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11237, \"description\": \"If we reach out to Wallace, we need to ensure he feels respected and included. His supporters feel dismissed by both parties, and that resentment runs deep. Make it clear that we’re not writing anyone off and that there’s room in this campaign for voters who don’t feel at home anywhere else right now. This isn’t about agreeing on everything, it’s about acknowledging that their concerns deserve to be heard. If Wallace believes we’re serious about that, he might be willing to say something positive publicly, even if he stops short of endorsing us.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21347, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11237, \"description\": \"If we reach out to Wallace, we ought to talk to him about the broader need to settle things down politically. The country’s been through years of tension, and there’s an argument to be made for lowering the temperature and trying to bring people back into a shared political space. We can frame our campaign as an attempt to move past the constant division and give voters a sense that things don’t have to stay this polarized. It’s a harder sell, and he may push back, but it at least opens the door to a serious conversation about where the country goes from here.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21348, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11237, \"description\": \"No, we’re not going to seek George Wallace’s endorsement. His record and the kind of politics he’s built his career on are fundamentally at odds with what this campaign is trying to do, especially on civil rights and the broader direction of the party. Going after his backing would raise real questions about where we stand and risk undercutting the coalition we’ve spent months trying to build. If Wallace chooses to stay out of the race, that’s his decision, but we’re not going to invite him in.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21545, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11437, \"description\": \"We will not build this campaign around George Wallace. Our message should stand on its own, grounded in Democratic values, economic security, and national unity. The more we go looking for a fight, the more we risk validating his campaign as a defining force in this election and allowing him to set the agenda. Our responsibility is to speak directly to the broader electorate, present a strong Democratic Party, and trust that voters will judge our vision fairly. Staying focused on our own priorities keeps us disciplined and prevents us from being pulled off message.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21546, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11437, \"description\": \"George Wallace’s record and rhetoric demand a direct response. We will not let him hide behind his campaign theatrics without being held accountable for the consequences of his words and actions. Voters deserve to hear clearly what he has said, what he has done, and what his candidacy truly stands for. I will challenge him openly, forcefully, and without ambiguity. If he thinks he can shape this election, he should have to answer for his positions in front of the American people.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21547, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11437, \"description\": \"George Wallace is not someone we agree with, and I’m not going to frame him as some kind of ideological compatriot. But in a three-way race, his presence changes the dynamics in ways we cannot ignore. We can acknowledge that reality while keeping our focus on the Republican ticket. Because the truth is, President Nixon represents the broader establishment forces that frustrate many voters on all sides of the aisle. George Wallace is just another reaction to that. Whether you’re voting for him or for me, that’s one less vote for Dick Nixon.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21548, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11437, \"description\": \"George Wallace is a bigot and a racist - and Dick Nixon and Ted Agnew aren’t far behind. The president has spent four years trying to appeal to the same voters who support Mr. Wallace while presenting himself as a steady, mainstream alternative. If Nixon wants to speak out of both sides of his mouth, if he wants to mirror Mr. Wallace when convenient and claim moderation when necessary, then he should be forced to explain that contradiction clearly. The American people deserve honesty, and if the Republican ticket wants the support of the Wallace voters, they must answer for the strategy they’re using to get it.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21349, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11238, \"description\": \"If Secretary Kissinger’s statement proves true, then that is welcome news for every American family and every Vietnamese family that has paid the price for this war. I hope the administration is finally prepared to bring this conflict to an honorable conclusion, and I will judge their actions by whether the fighting actually stops and our troops truly come home.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21350, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11238, \"description\": \"We have been told for four years now that peace is just around the corner. And in that time, the president has invaded two other countries, authorized civilian bombing campaigns, and has continued to prop up a corrupt dictatorship abroad. I want this war to end as much as anyone, but I’m not going to take these sudden assurances at face value. I will believe peace is at hand when the war is actually over.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21351, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11238, \"description\": \"What the administration is now describing sounds very much like the settlement President Johnson was prepared to accept years ago. The difference is that since then, thousands more Americans and countless Vietnamese have died. If this is where we were always headed, then the real question is why this war was prolonged for so long to begin with.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21352, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11238, \"description\": \"Peace is not something you can announce at a press conference while we still have planes dropping bombs and soldiers knee deep in the Vietnamese mud. Peace means ending the war, bringing our troops home, and stopping the killing now. Anything less is not peace. This war has gone on long enough.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21353, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11239, \"description\": \"These are serious developments, and they deserve to be handled with utmost care. The facts need to be gathered, verified, and examined outside of partisan politics - I fully support Mr. Cronkite’s investigative work on the matter. And I’m calling for a full, independent congressional investigation so the public can know exactly what happened and who was responsible. The rule of law has to speak for itself.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21354, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11239, \"description\": \"When Americans watch a story like this unfold, they don’t just wonder about the kinds of things being done behind closed doors. They ask what kind of country we’re becoming. A democracy can’t survive on this level of secrecy, of paranoia, of political sabotage. We need honesty and integrity back in our public life. We need leaders who believe that winning an election is not worth losing the nation’s conscience.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21355, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11239, \"description\": \"This is a lifeline for our campaign, and it’s all the more reason we have to be careful here. There’s a criminal investigation underway, and that’s where this belongs. If the President says he had no involvement with the break-in this summer, that statement should be taken seriously unless proven otherwise. We shouldn’t rush to judgment or turn every accusation into a political weapon. Let the authorities do their work.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21356, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11239, \"description\": \"This isn’t your everyday dirty campaign trick, Mr. Cronkite is making that increasingly clear. If the White House is involved in breaking into the opposition party’s headquarters, that’s an abuse of power, plain and simple. No president is above the law. If these reports are true, Richard Nixon has no business being a free man, let alone occupying the Oval Office.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21357, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11240, \"description\": \"This election doesn’t have to be about fear or resentment. It can be about steady leadership and the hard work of healing a country strained by war and mistrust. I’m asking Americans to step back from the anger of the last few years and choose a future grounded in decency, responsibility, and the belief that we can do better than the status quo.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21358, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11240, \"description\": \"Every day this war drags on, more young Americans are asked to risk their lives without a clear purpose or end in sight. No last-minute announcement changes that fact. If you believe, as I do, that it’s time to bring this war to a responsible close and bring our sons home, then this election gives you a chance to make that a reality.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21359, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11240, \"description\": \"I’ve spent this campaign talking about good jobs, fair wages, and economic opportunity because that’s what working families think about long after the campaign speeches are over. They want a fair economy, good schools, and a government that works for people who play by the rules, not just those with influence. That’s always been the Democratic tradition, and it’s the choice voters still have in this election.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 21360, \"fields\": {\"question\": 11240, \"description\": \"The president would prefer this election pass quietly, without facing any scrutiny or hard questions. I don’t believe that’s healthy for a democracy. The American people deserve honesty, accountability, and a government that levels with them, even when the truth is uncomfortable. If you want a change in tone and direction, this election is your opportunity to choose decency, responsibility, and integrity in public life.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22105, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12027, \"description\": \"Our land and water are not abstract concepts. They are what you live with and what you pass on to the next generation. When a river is polluted, a town pays the price. When the air is fouled, children pay it first. I’ve spent years writing laws and forcing votes so that clean water and clean air are not promises but guarantees, because I believe that government should take responsibility for the health and dignity of the people it serves. I am running because I know these problems can be solved, and because I know how to do the work it takes to solve them.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22106, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12027, \"description\": \"For too long, this war has asked for patience while offering no honesty in return. It has taken lives, divided families, and drained the confidence Americans once had in their leaders. Ending it won’t be easy, and anyone who tells you otherwise isn’t being straight with you. But it is necessary. I believe in a negotiated peace that brings our troops home and restores a sense of seriousness to American foreign policy. No more secret escalations, no more false deadlines, no more asking the country to carry a burden without being told the truth. Peace requires resolve, clarity, and respect for the costs that have already been paid.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22107, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12027, \"description\": \"Nixon and the Republicans often like to pretend the last decade was one solely of chaos and disorder. I couldn’t disagree more. Medicare, school funding, public housing, these programs did not weaken this country. They strengthened it. The failure of the past few years has not been too much compassion, but neglect and mismanagement. The answer is not to walk away, but to govern better. I believe we politicians should be judged the same way working people are judged, by whether we show up, do the job, and take responsibility when things go wrong. Our fight for the poor and the indigent isn’t over. Let’s finish the job.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22108, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12027, \"description\": \"The strength of this country rests on whether its laws apply to everyone, and whether its promises mean the same thing in every community. Civil Rights are not a regional issue and they’re not a bargaining chip. They are a measure of whether our democracy truly means something. I have supported voting rights, fair housing, and justice under the law, because the rule of law depends on it. When government tolerates discrimination or looks the other way, it only loses legitimacy. I am running to restore confidence that the law protects the powerless as surely as it protects the powerful, and that citizenship carries real meaning for every American.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22305, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12227, \"description\": \"Our land and water are not abstract concepts. They are what you live with and what you pass on to the next generation. When a river is polluted, a town pays the price. When the air is fouled, children pay it first. I’ve spent years writing laws and forcing votes so that clean water and clean air are not promises but guarantees, because I believe that government should take responsibility for the health and dignity of the people it serves. I am running because I know these problems can be solved, and because I know how to do the work it takes to solve them.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22306, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12227, \"description\": \"For too long, this war has asked for patience while offering no honesty in return. It has taken lives, divided families, and drained the confidence Americans once had in their leaders. Ending it won’t be easy, and anyone who tells you otherwise isn’t being straight with you. But it is necessary. I believe in a negotiated peace that brings our troops home and restores a sense of seriousness to American foreign policy. No more secret escalations, no more false deadlines, no more asking the country to carry a burden without being told the truth. Peace requires resolve, clarity, and respect for the costs that have already been paid.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22307, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12227, \"description\": \"Nixon and the Republicans often like to pretend the last decade was one solely of chaos and disorder. I couldn’t disagree more. Medicare, school funding, public housing, these programs did not weaken this country. They strengthened it. The failure of the past few years has not been too much compassion, but neglect and mismanagement. The answer is not to walk away, but to govern better. I believe we politicians should be judged the same way working people are judged, by whether we show up, do the job, and take responsibility when things go wrong. Our fight for the poor and the indigent isn’t over. Let’s finish the job.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22308, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12227, \"description\": \"The strength of this country rests on whether its laws apply to everyone, and whether its promises mean the same thing in every community. Civil Rights are not a regional issue and they’re not a bargaining chip. They are a measure of whether our democracy truly means something. I have supported voting rights, fair housing, and justice under the law, because the rule of law depends on it. When government tolerates discrimination or looks the other way, it only loses legitimacy. I am running to restore confidence that the law protects the powerless as surely as it protects the powerful, and that citizenship carries real meaning for every American.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22505, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12427, \"description\": \"Our land and water are not abstract concepts. They are what you live with and what you pass on to the next generation. When a river is polluted, a town pays the price. When the air is fouled, children pay it first. I’ve spent years writing laws and forcing votes so that clean water and clean air are not promises but guarantees, because I believe that government should take responsibility for the health and dignity of the people it serves. I am running because I know these problems can be solved, and because I know how to do the work it takes to solve them.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22506, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12427, \"description\": \"For too long, this war has asked for patience while offering no honesty in return. It has taken lives, divided families, and drained the confidence Americans once had in their leaders. Ending it won’t be easy, and anyone who tells you otherwise isn’t being straight with you. But it is necessary. I believe in a negotiated peace that brings our troops home and restores a sense of seriousness to American foreign policy. No more secret escalations, no more false deadlines, no more asking the country to carry a burden without being told the truth. Peace requires resolve, clarity, and respect for the costs that have already been paid.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22507, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12427, \"description\": \"Nixon and the Republicans often like to pretend the last decade was one solely of chaos and disorder. I couldn’t disagree more. Medicare, school funding, public housing, these programs did not weaken this country. They strengthened it. The failure of the past few years has not been too much compassion, but neglect and mismanagement. The answer is not to walk away, but to govern better. I believe we politicians should be judged the same way working people are judged, by whether we show up, do the job, and take responsibility when things go wrong. Our fight for the poor and the indigent isn’t over. Let’s finish the job.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22508, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12427, \"description\": \"The strength of this country rests on whether its laws apply to everyone, and whether its promises mean the same thing in every community. Civil Rights are not a regional issue and they’re not a bargaining chip. They are a measure of whether our democracy truly means something. I have supported voting rights, fair housing, and justice under the law, because the rule of law depends on it. When government tolerates discrimination or looks the other way, it only loses legitimacy. I am running to restore confidence that the law protects the powerless as surely as it protects the powerful, and that citizenship carries real meaning for every American.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22109, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12028, \"description\": \"I’ve been around the block for a while now, and the reality is that this campaign can’t just look like a reunion of the same old names that have been circling Washington for decades. I want someone who represents the generation stepping forward now, someone comfortable on a national stage and willing to talk openly about the importance of cleaning up our government and giving more power to ordinary people. That kind of energy tells voters the party is not afraid of its own future.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22110, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12028, \"description\": \"This war has shaken people’s faith in the judgment of their leaders, and we cannot pretend otherwise. I want a partner who has been willing to ask hard questions about foreign policy and who speaks about America’s power with a sense of restraint and responsibility. Someone who has taken the time to study these decisions and has not been afraid to question them when they no longer made sense. That kind of thoughtfulness matters right now. Strength in leadership is not just about resolve, it’s about knowing the limits of force and being willing to say so.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22111, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12028, \"description\": \"I want this campaign to sound like it is thinking about the country ten or twenty years from now, not just about the next election. Americans are starting to understand that the air they breathe and the water they drink are not abstract concerns. They are part of everyday life. And there is someone who has been speaking about conservation and environmental responsibility long before it became fashionable. He would show voters that we are willing to look beyond the latest polls and talk about the future with some honesty for a change.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22709, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12628, \"description\": \"Look, I know some people are raising eyebrows about George’s reputation. I know what people say about him. But I’ve seen him on the trail, and I see the energy he brings to young voters and minorities and people who have felt ignored by politics for too long. That passion is a strength. He can help our campaign speak to a generation that wants to see change, and that’s something we can’t afford to dismiss.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22710, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12628, \"description\": \"I know some voters don’t trust me on Vietnam. But since returning to the Senate, I’ve worked full time to bring the country out of a war that has cost too much. And I wouldn’t have gotten there if it weren’t for principled voices like George McGovern. Who better to have by my side in that effort than someone who has made that commitment his central cause?\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22711, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12628, \"description\": \"People may see him as radical, but that couldn’t be further from the truth. He’s been elected twice statewide in South Dakota of all places. He’s a man of the prairie who knows how to talk to his neighbors, keep his word, and make hard decisions without the typical theatrics we’ve come to expect. George is honest, grounded, and capable of representing a broad spectrum of Americans. I’m proud to have him by my side.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22113, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12029, \"description\": \"The images coming out of Southeast Asia are deeply troubling, and any loss of innocent life weighs heavily on the conscience of this country. War is never clean, and tragic errors can occur even when our forces are acting in good faith. If military authorities say this incident was unintended, that claim deserves serious consideration. Our responsibility is to reduce civilian harm, improve oversight, and continue the difficult work of bringing this conflict to a responsible conclusion.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22114, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12029, \"description\": \"No American can look at photographs of burned villages and wounded children without feeling shaken. These are human beings caught in the middle of forces far beyond their control. That moral reality can’t be brushed aside with technical explanations alone. If we are to justify this war to ourselves and to the world, we must show that civilian lives matter, and that our conduct reflects the values we claim to defend.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22115, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12029, \"description\": \"Incidents like this raise serious questions about whether the war is being conducted in a way that actually serves American interests. When civilian casualties mount, they undermine our credibility abroad, harden opposition at home, and make a political settlement more difficult to achieve. That isn’t just a moral problem, but a strategic one as well. It is time to reassess our tactics and press harder for a negotiated end to the conflict.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22116, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12029, \"description\": \"What troubles Americans most is the growing distance between what they see with their own eyes and what they are told by their government. Simply put, there is a clear credibility gap today that has undermined the legitimacy of our government. A democracy cannot sustain a long war without honesty from its leaders. If this administration cannot be candid about what is happening in Southeast Asia, then it has no business asking the American people to continue supporting this conflict.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22117, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12030, \"description\": \"I will be honest with the American people. I did not handle every moment of this situation perfectly. I was angry because my wife’s name was dragged into the gutter, and I reacted as a husband before I reacted as a candidate. I understand how that looked to the voters. And I also believe that people understand that a politician should be accountable when he falls short. But I took responsibility, I spoke plainly, and I did not try to spin my way out of it. Can the same thing ever be said of Dick Nixon?\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22118, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12030, \"description\": \"One small incident doesn’t define a career. Throughout my time in the Senate, I’ve negotiated through tough legislation, helped handle international crises, and worked through moments when the pressure was real and the stakes were high. I’ve done that work calmly and responsibly, without bluster. The question is not whether a candidate ever shows emotion. The question is whether he can make sound decisions when it matters. My record answers that question far better than any anonymous letter ever could.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22119, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12030, \"description\": \"Forgive me if I don’t like to see my wife slandered by Dick Nixon’s flying monkeys. I don’t treat public life as a game, and I don’t believe that leadership requires a cold emotional detachment. It requires judgment, patience, and respect for the people who put you in power. I listen carefully, I think before I act, and I weigh the human cost of every decision. That’s what builds trust in moments of crisis. I’m proud to bring those qualities to the presidency.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22120, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12030, \"description\": \"What this episode really exposes is the kind of campaign the Nixon operation is running. Forged letters, anonymous smears, and whisper campaigns aimed at candidates’ families? It’s unacceptable. That is not strength. That is fear. It’s cowardice. The American people deserve a president who wins on the merits, and instead we have one who relies on an attack machine operating in the shadows. I am fully prepared to stand up to that kind of politics, and I am prepared to lead this country with honesty first and foremost.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22121, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12031, \"description\": \"I’m not going to pretend the people who pushed this party forward over the last decade don’t matter. I’ve known many of them for years, and I respect what brought them into politics. If the energy has cooled down, then that’s on us to reignite it. That means being present on the campuses, meeting directly with student leaders, women’s groups, civil rights organizations, and antiwar activists, not through press releases or surrogates but face-to-face. More field offices near colleges, more listening sessions, fewer assumptions. I’ve spent my career building coalitions the hard way, and I’m willing to do it again.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22122, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12031, \"description\": \"This campaign has to speak to the whole country, not just the most energized corners of the party. A lot of Americans are tired, worried about prices, jobs, and whether things feel stable again. Working families, union households, farmers, suburban voters, and independents need to know they can rely on steady leadership. I’m not backing away from my record or my values, but I’m not going to let this campaign be driven by constant internal debates either. If we hold the center together, we give ourselves a real chance to win and govern.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22123, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12031, \"description\": \"I’ve been in enough campaigns to know this pattern. There’s always frustration on the left when the general election starts. They’re young, idealistic. Of course they’re gonna be frustrated. But most Democrats understand what’s at stake when November comes. They may be disappointed, they may argue, but they know the difference between disagreement and surrender. I’ve been clear about who I am and what I believe for a long time now. This campaign isn’t going to be making any sudden pivots. It’s about experience, seriousness, and giving Democrats a nominee they can trust when it’s time to cast a ballot.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22124, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12031, \"description\": \"I don’t think the answer is for me to lecture people who already know my record. But there are voices in this party who have real credibility with the activists and the student movement. I want people like George McGovern, Frank Church, and other respected progressives to sit down with the student organizers, the antiwar leaders, the women’s groups, and talk honestly about where this campaign is headed. Not to strong-arm anyone, just to keep the conversation open and make it clear they haven’t been pushed aside. Keeping those relationships alive matters, even when we don’t agree on every tactic.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22125, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12032, \"description\": \"An unfortunate, but entirely expected development. All we can do is redouble our campaign efforts on the ground. I’ll visit every major city in the South, meet with local Democrats, and do as many rallies as I can. People need to see that we hear them and understand the challenges they face. Let’s see if we can’t light up some old loyalties the old-fashioned way.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22126, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12032, \"description\": \"John Connally is trying to use his connections to push Democrats into Nixon’s camp, but we have an ace up our sleeve here. It’s no secret that President Johnson and I have our disagreements, but there’s no way he’d ever endorse Nixon. I plan to go to President Johnson’s ranch, talk with him candidly, and ask him to publicly back our ticket. There’s no way Connally’s voice can compete with a heavyweight like Johnson.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22127, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12032, \"description\": \"Four years ago, Connally supported Hubert and I in our campaign against Mr. Nixon. Today, he completely switches parties. You can’t possibly tell me this has anything to do with his convictions. I’ll make it clear that his endorsement isn’t a mark of wisdom or courage, it’s blatant opportunism, and it isn’t worth a damned nickel. Democrats deserve to know that the man who left his party to chase power in Washington doesn’t represent our values.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22128, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12032, \"description\": \"If John Connally wants to run this ‘Democrats for Nixon’ shtick, then fine. Two can play at that game. I’ll reach out to some former and even current Republicans who share our concerns. John Lindsay can take the lead, and see if we can’t get in touch with McCloskey and Reid. I hear Don Riegle might be switching over soon too. Nixon has spent the past four years pandering to the far right. Let’s show the voters just how many Republicans he’s chased away.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22129, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12033, \"description\": \"I understand why so many people are angry, and many of the concerns being raised in these protests are completely legitimate. The war, civil rights, and economic inequality have left a lot of Americans feeling shut out. But violence and destruction of property only weaken those causes and drag innocent people into the crossfire, and that’s not something I can support.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22130, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12033, \"description\": \"I’m not going to throw anyone under the bus, let alone so many patriotic Americans speaking out for what they feel is right. Peaceful protest is a constitutional right, and dissent is not the same thing as disorder. It is dangerous to treat protest itself as a threat to the country. President Nixon should be ashamed of himself for his brazen attempts to politicize our freedoms.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22131, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12033, \"description\": \"Look, a lot of these folks supported Mr. McGovern this year. They’re young, they’re idealistic. And they’re angry too, they want change. But ultimately, so many of these protests have gone too far, and they have done real damage to the credibility of the very causes these well-meaning young people claim to champion. If people want to change this country, they need to grow up and learn to work within the system instead of disrupting public life and turning voters against them.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22132, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12033, \"description\": \"I’m a proud Democrat, and I am no radical. Anyone who thinks I condone any kind of reckless law-breaking or unprovoked violence is mistaken. If a protest or march turns violent or disrupts the peace, then I support the protesters being punished to the extent the law calls for. To claim I think anything else is just another rumor started by Dick Nixon’s flying monkeys.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22133, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12034, \"description\": \"I’m not running against Spiro Agnew. I’m running against Richard Nixon and his record. The vice president seems to spend his days picking fights and throwing insults, and I don’t intend to join him. I’m going to keep talking about the war, the economy, and the direction of the country, and let voters decide who sounds like a president.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22134, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12034, \"description\": \"Agnew isn’t some independent actor. He says what Nixon wants to say, just louder and cruder. When he attacks protesters, the press, or whole groups of Americans, he’s doing it to distract from the failures of this administration. I’m going to remind people that his tone comes straight from the top.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22135, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12034, \"description\": \"The vice president is deliberately trying to pit Americans against each other. He mocks legitimate dissent, ridicules any disagreement, and treats whole parts of this country like mortal enemies. That might win applause at his campaign rallies, but it’s no way to govern a democracy, and it’s certainly no way to heal a country that’s already deeply divided.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22136, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12034, \"description\": \"I think most Americans are tired of being yelled at by their own government. They want a leadership that lowers the temperature, not raises it. I still believe that character counts in our country. I’ve been an honest public servant for 30 years. I’ve committed my life to serving the people of Maine. Spiro Agnew? He’s just a lying attack dog who spends his free time golfing.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22137, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12035, \"description\": \"I didn’t take up this issue because it was popular. I took it up because it was right. Long before it filled the headlines, I believed the country had an obligation to protect its land, its water, and the people who depend on both. I'm proud to have stood by that view even when it was inconvenient. Voters deserve to know where a candidate has been consistent, and on this issue my record speaks for itself.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22138, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12035, \"description\": \"Republicans like to claim that I’m some radical hippie here; that couldn’t be further from the truth. Clean air and clean water are workers’ issues too, whether Nixon wants to admit it or not. The same smoke that poisons a river poisons the lungs of the people who work nearby. The same waste dumped downstream ends up in the homes of working families. I supported OSHA for the same reason I fought for environmental protections. A job should not come at the cost of a worker’s health or their child’s future.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22139, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12035, \"description\": \"What hurts workers and businesses alike isn’t any regulation, it’s chaotic regulation. When standards change from state to state, county to county, our attempts at effective enforcement become uneven and businesses small and large struggle to comply. Having national, consistent rules gives everyone clarity. Companies know what to expect, workers know they’re protected, and states are not forced into a race to the bottom. Don’t believe me? Nixon has said the same.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22140, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12035, \"description\": \"Funny thing about this. The president can’t seem to decide where he stands on the environment. One minute, he’s the biggest environmentalist there is; the next, he’s standing up against the tree-huggers. He signed into law the EPA and then vetoed clean water protections that Congress forced through anyway. You can’t govern out of both sides of your mouth. Honest leadership means standing up for what you believe in, and to hell with what everyone else thinks. On that test, this administration has talked far more than it has delivered.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22141, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12036, \"description\": \"What happened in Munich was a tragedy, plain and simple. Innocent athletes went to represent their countries in peace, and they were murdered instead. Tonight, my thoughts are with the families of the Israeli team and with the German people who tried to protect their guests. Acts like this offend the conscience of all nations, and they deserve our shared grief and resolve to see that such crimes are never repeated.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22142, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12036, \"description\": \"The murder of Israeli athletes in Munich was an act of terror against innocent people and against the very idea of peaceful competition. Israel has the right to exist, the right to security, and the right to see its citizens protected from violence wherever they go. The United States must stand firmly with Israel in condemning this attack and in opposing terrorism in all its forms. There is no political cause that justifies the killing of civilians, ever.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22143, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12036, \"description\": \"What happened in Munich is a grim reminder that violence isn’t confined by borders, or uniforms, or battlefields. It threatens civilians, the international order, and the basic sense of safety people expect in the modern world. The United States must work with its allies to strengthen international cooperation against terrorism, improve intelligence sharing, and ensure that people are safe wherever they go.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22145, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12037, \"description\": \"If we reach out to Wallace, we have to keep the focus on the economic concerns that drove much of his support. Talk about wages, inflation, and the sense that working people have been squeezed while Washington argues over everything else. Make the case that this campaign is serious about those issues and isn’t just speaking to one part of the country. If he sees that we’re addressing the same frustrations his voters feel, he may not endorse us outright, but he could soften his tone and speak more favorably about the campaign.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22146, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12037, \"description\": \"If we reach out to Wallace, we need to ensure he feels respected and included. His supporters feel dismissed by both parties, and that resentment runs deep. Make it clear that we’re not writing anyone off and that there’s room in this campaign for voters who don’t feel at home anywhere else right now. This isn’t about agreeing on everything, it’s about acknowledging that their concerns deserve to be heard. If Wallace believes we’re serious about that, he might be willing to say something positive publicly, even if he stops short of endorsing us.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22147, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12037, \"description\": \"If we reach out to Wallace, we ought to talk to him about the broader need to settle things down politically. The country’s been through years of tension, and there’s an argument to be made for lowering the temperature and trying to bring people back into a shared political space. We can frame our campaign as an attempt to move past the constant division and give voters a sense that things don’t have to stay this polarized. It’s a harder sell, and he may push back, but it at least opens the door to a serious conversation about where the country goes from here.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22148, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12037, \"description\": \"No, we’re not going to seek George Wallace’s endorsement. His record and the kind of politics he’s built his career on are fundamentally at odds with what this campaign is trying to do, especially on civil rights and the broader direction of the party. Going after his backing would raise real questions about where we stand and risk undercutting the coalition we’ve spent months trying to build. If Wallace chooses to stay out of the race, that’s his decision, but we’re not going to invite him in.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22345, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12237, \"description\": \"We will not build this campaign around George Wallace. Our message should stand on its own, grounded in Democratic values, economic security, and national unity. The more we go looking for a fight, the more we risk validating his campaign as a defining force in this election and allowing him to set the agenda. Our responsibility is to speak directly to the broader electorate, present a strong Democratic Party, and trust that voters will judge our vision fairly. Staying focused on our own priorities keeps us disciplined and prevents us from being pulled off message.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22346, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12237, \"description\": \"George Wallace’s record and rhetoric demand a direct response. We will not let him hide behind his campaign theatrics without being held accountable for the consequences of his words and actions. Voters deserve to hear clearly what he has said, what he has done, and what his candidacy truly stands for. I will challenge him openly, forcefully, and without ambiguity. If he thinks he can shape this election, he should have to answer for his positions in front of the American people.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22347, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12237, \"description\": \"George Wallace is not someone we agree with, and I’m not going to frame him as some kind of ideological compatriot. But in a three-way race, his presence changes the dynamics in ways we cannot ignore. We can acknowledge that reality while keeping our focus on the Republican ticket. Because the truth is, President Nixon represents the broader establishment forces that frustrate many voters on all sides of the aisle. George Wallace is just another reaction to that. Whether you’re voting for him or for me, that’s one less vote for Dick Nixon.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22348, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12237, \"description\": \"George Wallace is a bigot and a racist - and Dick Nixon and Ted Agnew aren’t far behind. The president has spent four years trying to appeal to the same voters who support Mr. Wallace while presenting himself as a steady, mainstream alternative. If Nixon wants to speak out of both sides of his mouth, if he wants to mirror Mr. Wallace when convenient and claim moderation when necessary, then he should be forced to explain that contradiction clearly. The American people deserve honesty, and if the Republican ticket wants the support of the Wallace voters, they must answer for the strategy they’re using to get it.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22149, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12038, \"description\": \"If Secretary Kissinger’s statement proves true, then that is welcome news for every American family and every Vietnamese family that has paid the price for this war. I hope the administration is finally prepared to bring this conflict to an honorable conclusion, and I will judge their actions by whether the fighting actually stops and our troops truly come home.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22150, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12038, \"description\": \"We have been told for four years now that peace is just around the corner. And in that time, the president has invaded two other countries, authorized civilian bombing campaigns, and has continued to prop up a corrupt dictatorship abroad. I want this war to end as much as anyone, but I’m not going to take these sudden assurances at face value. I will believe peace is at hand when the war is actually over.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22151, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12038, \"description\": \"What the administration is now describing sounds very much like the settlement President Johnson was prepared to accept years ago. The difference is that since then, thousands more Americans and countless Vietnamese have died. If this is where we were always headed, then the real question is why this war was prolonged for so long to begin with.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22152, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12038, \"description\": \"Peace is not something you can announce at a press conference while we still have planes dropping bombs and soldiers knee deep in the Vietnamese mud. Peace means ending the war, bringing our troops home, and stopping the killing now. Anything less is not peace. This war has gone on long enough.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22153, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12039, \"description\": \"These are serious developments, and they deserve to be handled with utmost care. The facts need to be gathered, verified, and examined outside of partisan politics - I fully support Mr. Cronkite’s investigative work on the matter. And I’m calling for a full, independent congressional investigation so the public can know exactly what happened and who was responsible. The rule of law has to speak for itself.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22154, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12039, \"description\": \"When Americans watch a story like this unfold, they don’t just wonder about the kinds of things being done behind closed doors. They ask what kind of country we’re becoming. A democracy can’t survive on this level of secrecy, of paranoia, of political sabotage. We need honesty and integrity back in our public life. We need leaders who believe that winning an election is not worth losing the nation’s conscience.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22155, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12039, \"description\": \"This is a lifeline for our campaign, and it’s all the more reason we have to be careful here. There’s a criminal investigation underway, and that’s where this belongs. If the President says he had no involvement with the break-in this summer, that statement should be taken seriously unless proven otherwise. We shouldn’t rush to judgment or turn every accusation into a political weapon. Let the authorities do their work.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22156, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12039, \"description\": \"This isn’t your everyday dirty campaign trick, Mr. Cronkite is making that increasingly clear. If the White House is involved in breaking into the opposition party’s headquarters, that’s an abuse of power, plain and simple. No president is above the law. If these reports are true, Richard Nixon has no business being a free man, let alone occupying the Oval Office.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22157, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12040, \"description\": \"This election doesn’t have to be about fear or resentment. It can be about steady leadership and the hard work of healing a country strained by war and mistrust. I’m asking Americans to step back from the anger of the last few years and choose a future grounded in decency, responsibility, and the belief that we can do better than the status quo.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22158, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12040, \"description\": \"Every day this war drags on, more young Americans are asked to risk their lives without a clear purpose or end in sight. No last-minute announcement changes that fact. If you believe, as I do, that it’s time to bring this war to a responsible close and bring our sons home, then this election gives you a chance to make that a reality.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22159, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12040, \"description\": \"I’ve spent this campaign talking about good jobs, fair wages, and economic opportunity because that’s what working families think about long after the campaign speeches are over. They want a fair economy, good schools, and a government that works for people who play by the rules, not just those with influence. That’s always been the Democratic tradition, and it’s the choice voters still have in this election.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22160, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12040, \"description\": \"The president would prefer this election pass quietly, without facing any scrutiny or hard questions. I don’t believe that’s healthy for a democracy. The American people deserve honesty, accountability, and a government that levels with them, even when the truth is uncomfortable. If you want a change in tone and direction, this election is your opportunity to choose decency, responsibility, and integrity in public life.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22905, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12827, \"description\": \"A lot of Americans watching tonight feel like their country is slipping out from under them. They see disorder in the streets, violence on the evening news, and leaders who seem more interested in explaining it away than stopping it. I don’t accept that chaos is inevitable, and I don’t believe ordinary people should have to live in fear in their own damned neighborhoods. Government has a basic duty to keep the peace and protect those who play by the rules. As president, I will enforce the law to the fullest extent possible. Nobody has the right to impose his will through intimidation or destruction.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22906, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12827, \"description\": \"There is something deeply wrong when a man can work his whole life without ever making a damned decision for himself. Too much power has drifted away from ordinary Americans and settled in places they never voted for. Federal bureaucracies that answer to no one. Corporations that move money and jobs wherever it suits them. Grocery bills add up and paychecks fall behind. I’m running to put government back on the side of the working man. That means fighting inflation, standing up to corporate interests, and giving honest laborers a fair shake in an economy that too often rewards the powerful and forgets the people who keep it running.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22907, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12827, \"description\": \"A lot of Americans are tired of being told that their values are outdated or shameful or wrong. They don’t recognize a politics that seems more concerned with catering to dope-smoking radicals than listening to parents, workers, and churchgoing families. I don’t believe forced busing fixes our schools, any more than I believe celebrating disorder fixes our culture. This country doesn’t need more excuses for chaos or more indulgence for a counterculture that rejects responsibility. It needs leadership that respects hard work, family life, and the idea that freedom comes with obligations.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22908, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12827, \"description\": \"The war in Vietnam has dragged on because it has been managed without clarity or purpose. Too many lives have been spent while politicians like Nixon talk tough at home and cut deals abroad. I believe in peace, but I also believe peace comes from strength and honesty, not from making symbolic gestures to foreign powers. I’ll never prolong a war for appearances, but neither will I trade away this country’s interests through half-hearted negotiations that leave everyone worse off. Leadership means knowing when to fight, when to end a fight, and how to protect the nation’s standing when you do.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22909, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12828, \"description\": \"There’s no getting around the fact that a lot of people in this party don’t trust me, and pretending otherwise isn’t going to help. If we’re serious about calming things down, then we ought to at least make the effort. Bring in someone the liberals respect, someone with real standing with organized labor and the people who feel like they’ve been pushed to the margins of this campaign. He’s not going to see eye to eye with me on everything, but that’s not the point right now. The point is showing the country that we’re still capable of reaching across the divide when it matters.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22910, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12828, \"description\": \"Look, let’s not overthink this. What we need is a straightforward approach. Keep the ticket grounded, stay in familiar territory, bring in another Southern governor who knows how to win votes without making a lot of noise about it. Someone who talks about responsibility in government, keeps a steady hand, and doesn’t pull the campaign too far in any one direction. That kind of balance has its advantages, especially in a year when things already feel chaotic enough as is.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22911, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12828, \"description\": \"At a certain point, we’ve got to deal with the party as it is, not as we might like it to be. There are still people here who understand how to make the machinery work, who know how to keep things moving without turning every decision into a spectacle. And who can appeal to most factions of the party. Bringing in someone with that kind of standing would show that this campaign isn’t just running on frustration. It would tell people that we’re serious about governing, not just making a point. \"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22913, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12829, \"description\": \"What people are seeing in those photographs is painful, and no decent person should turn away from it. War is brutal, and civilians suffer in ways that never make the briefing books in Washington. But this is also a war fought in dangerous conditions, and it does no good to pretend every tragedy is a crime or every mistake is malicious. Leaders have a duty to face reality, protect American lives, and make sure the war is conducted with discipline. The real question is how to end this conflict without tearing the country apart even further.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22914, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12829, \"description\": \"I’ve seen those images, and I don’t understand how anyone can see a child burned by war and speak in cold, technical language. Those are human beings. They bleed, they scream, and they suffer the same way any child in Alabama or Ohio would. If this war has reached the point where that kind of suffering is routine, then something is deeply wrong. The American people have a conscience, and they know when that conscience is being tested. We cannot harden ourselves to this, and we cannot keep asking families to accept it as normal.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22915, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12829, \"description\": \"This was not an accident. It is the result of a war that has been badly mismanaged by the same group of elitist Washington insiders that has been running things for years, all while ordinary Americans pay the price. The American people have not been told the truth about how this war is being fought or what it is costing in lives. Richard Nixon promised to wind this war down, yet the fighting keeps spreading and the facts keep getting hidden. When a President carries on a war this way, he has to answer for it. If these actions amount to war crimes, then Congress should investigate to the fullest extent possible.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22916, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12829, \"description\": \"I want this war ended, but I want it ended the right way. What we have now is the worst of both worlds. Endless fighting abroad and weakness at the negotiating table. The Nixon Administration talks tough, but it drags its feet, sends mixed signals, and chases headlines instead of results. You do not bring peace by stretching a war out year after year or by bargaining from a position of confusion. You bring peace by being clear, firm, and serious about protecting American interests while bringing our men home.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22917, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12830, \"description\": \"It's simple. I’m going to talk about John Lindsay’s record in New York, because voters shouldn't forget about it. The city's debt is exploding just to keep basic services functioning. They've faced repeated transit strikes, a massive police corruption scandal, and rising disorder that even his own allies struggle to defend. Whatever rhetoric he’s using now, people remember what it felt like when the system stopped working. Lindsay's third-rate candidacy is as much of a failure as his own career.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22918, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12830, \"description\": \"What this moment shows is how out of step those yankee elitists really are with the country. Most Americans are not interested in boutique lectures from Manhattan and Georgetown. They’re worried about prices at the grocery store, crime in their neighborhoods, and whether their kids have a future. That is the ground I am going to fight on. If the country’s biggest limousine liberal wants to pal around with some yuppie Republican for the cocktail circuit and the editorial boards, let him. I am going to talk to the broad middle of this country and build a real majority that doesn’t depend on appeasing the loudest voices in the room.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22919, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12830, \"description\": \"I’m not blind to the anger coming out of this year’s convention. I know a lot of good Democrats walked out because they felt ignored and shut out. Guess they know how my constituents have felt these past few years. But I’m not writing them off, I’m asking them to remember why this party exists in the first place. We have always been a rough coalition. We argue, we fight, and then we come back together because the alternative is turning the country over to Republicans who have never cared about working people a damned day in their lives. My door is open to anyone who wants to come back and help win this election and keep our party alive.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22920, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12830, \"description\": \"If these yuppies are allowed to turn this race into a contest over who sounds the most righteous, they could siphon off voters we can’t afford to lose. I’m not going to let that happen. We’re going to speak plainly about economic justice, about wages and jobs, and about ending this war quickly and decisively. I will make sure voters see the Black supporters who have stood with me on these issues. On school busing, I will talk honestly about how Washington’s court-ordered plans hurt working families of every race, in every district. Once the voters see that I’m not the enemy they think I am, they’ll come home.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22921, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12831, \"description\": \"They can call me whatever names they want. It doesn’t change who I am or who I am fighting for. I didn’t come into politics to be liked by Washington or the press corps. I came in to stand up for ordinary people who feel like nobody listens to them anymore. Every time they throw one of these insults at me, it reminds people why I’m running in the first place.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22922, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12831, \"description\": \"I understand that some voters are gonna feel uneasy when they hear that kind of rhetoric about our campaign. That’s exactly why the Nixon campaign is using it. I am going to respond calmly and directly. I will talk about my record in Alabama, my plans, and my respect for the office I am seeking. People can disagree with me, but they deserve the truth. I am not reckless. I am not running solely out of anger. And the problems of the 60s are old news. Hell, half my supporters are black! Dick Nixon is gonna try to scare his way into a second term. But I know the American people are better than that.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22923, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12831, \"description\": \"I am going to speak to the country directly, in prime time, and I’m not going to dodge my past or let it be rewritten by Dick Nixon of all people. I will say plainly that the divisions of the 1960s damaged this country, and that I was part of a fight that belongs to that era. I am running in 1972 to bring this country together around fairness, work, and dignity for everyone. Segregation is not the future of this nation. Pointless shouting and bitterness are not the future either. I ask the American people to judge me by the program I offer now and by whether I am willing to lead the country forward instead of keeping it trapped in old battles.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22924, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12831, \"description\": \"I’m not going to take lectures about character from a White House that runs on leaks, lies, and political sabotage. Everyone in this damned country knows Dick Nixon didn’t stumble into this kind of campaign by accident. It’s how he operates, he’s been pulling this garbage since the 1940s. He can’t defend his record, so he smears the man running against him. I’m going to call this out for what it is, a dirty trick designed to distract from rising prices, a war he promised to end, and a government that has lost the people’s trust. If they want a fight, they picked the wrong man to go after.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22925, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12832, \"description\": \"Unfortunate as it is, I’m not going to let some third-rate academic destroy our campaign. The best way to fight back is to be where the people are. I’ll spend every day on the streets, in factories, in neighborhoods, talking to the men and women who actually live with the problems that Moynihan waxes poetic about in his fancy editorials. Sure we can’t rely on every big wig to endorse our campaign. But we can show the people that we hear them and that we’re fighting for them. That is how we stop the bleeding and remind Democrats why this campaign exists.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22926, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12832, \"description\": \"Moynihan and his friends might have influence in Washington, but the people who built this party know how much loyalty truly matters. And no one has more clout in that field than President Johnson. I’ll personally reach out to him, make the case for the country, and ask him to speak to those who have supported the party for decades. When someone like Johnson shows his support, the votes that Moynihan hopes to sway will see whose side truly represents their interests.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22927, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12832, \"description\": \"The man may be smart on paper, but he’s never walked the streets where people work and struggle. He has never had to face a factory closing, a family scraping to make ends meet, or a community worried about their kids’ safety. His support for Nixon is about his own career and nothing else. Moynihan is an ivory tower Democrat, and he doesn’t speak for working families. I’m confident that the voters will see exactly who he is.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22928, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12832, \"description\": \"This is a man who wears party labels like he’s ordering breakfast at a diner. One day he’s a Democrat faithfully serving the Johnson White House, the next he’s lending his name to Dick Nixon and telling people which way to vote. That isn’t courage. It isn’t leadership either. The voters deserve honesty, consistency, and someone who will stand with them through thick and thin. They don’t need someone trading their convictions for influence in Washington. Say what you will about me, but at least I’m not a traitor.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22929, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12833, \"description\": \"I don’t think most Americans are asking for anything complicated. They just want to feel safe in their own neighborhoods again. People have every right to protest, that’s part of this country’s history. But what we’ve seen in some of these cities goes beyond that. When families feel unsafe, that’s not just a protest anymore. We don’t have to choose one or the other. We can restore order and still insist on fairness and equal treatment under the law.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22930, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12833, \"description\": \"You can’t look at what’s been happening and pretend it came out of nowhere. A lot of people feel like nobody in Washington is listening anymore. They see prices rising, wages stagnating, and their communities changing, and they feel pushed aside. I’m not excusing the violence, believe me, but if all we talk about is cracking heads and never deal with why people are angry in the first place, then we’re not solving jack shit.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22931, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12833, \"description\": \"A lot of Americans feel like their country is slipping away. They turn on the television and see fires in the streets and officials who stand by and watch. That has to stop. We are a nation of laws, and those laws have to mean something. I will stand behind the police and make sure of one thing: when the looting starts, the shooting starts. Basic order in our country is not too much to ask for.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22932, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12833, \"description\": \"I find it a little rich hearing the way Dick Nixon talks about law and order. He’s been running the country for four years now, and yet he still acts surprised that things are falling apart. He and Agnew stir up division, then go on television and pretend they can fix it. If they were going to fix it, they would have done it already. The American people know the difference between real leadership and empty political theater.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22933, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12834, \"description\": \"I’ve dealt with men like Spiro Agnew my whole life. One minute he’s a respectable businessman nodding along with Nelson Rockefeller. The next minute he discovers a microphone and decides he’s a street fighter. Nothing has changed except for who’s giving the applause. He didn’t find religion on law and order. He found a crowd that liked hearing it. Folks know when a man’s saying what he believes and when he’s saying what sells. I’ve stood in the same place for years. You can’t say the same about Spiro Agnew.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22934, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12834, \"description\": \"You know, Spiro Agnew sure talks a lot for a man who doesn’t seem to do much. While prices climb, wages stagnate, and the Vietnam War drags on, the Vice President’s out there giving speeches and picking fights with every journalist on 5th Ave. He isn’t running anything. He isn’t fixing anything. He’s a loud voice sent out to distract the people while Nixon and his men make the real decisions. Americans don’t need another scold. They are sick and tired of that kind of politics. They want someone who will take responsibility and actually get their hands dirty.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22935, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12834, \"description\": \"If the country feels exhausted and divided, it didn’t happen by accident. This administration has spent four years stirring resentment behind the scenes, then falling over themselves to make condemnations when things fall apart. They talk about order while cities decay, prices soar, and the war drags on without an end in sight. They use anger as a tool and then act surprised when people are angry. I’m not running to lecture Americans about their frustrations. I am running because those frustrations are real, and they deserve an answer that goes beyond empty speeches and insults.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22936, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12834, \"description\": \"It’s simple. I’ll stand before the cameras and speak plainly. I am not the same man I was a decade ago, neither is our country. The fights of the sixties are over. People want good jobs, safe cities, and a government that fights for them, no matter the color of their skin. I’ve spoken before about the black supporters who stand with me because they want safer streets and a fair shake just like anyone else. I’m running to govern this country, all of this country, and it’s high time we move our nation forward, and restore stability in a country that has had more than enough turmoil.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22937, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12835, \"description\": \"People can squawk all they want about made-up scandals and rumors about my family, but I’m going to stay right where I’ve always been. The voters I talk to every day are worried about prices they can’t afford, jobs that get shipped out of town, kids who come home to empty streets at night. I’m going to keep talking about those problems every chance I get, because that is what matters in people’s lives. Let the press look like idiots chasing every stupid anecdote they hear.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22938, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12835, \"description\": \"Sure, mistakes have been made. I don’t pretend I’m perfect. I’ve made choices, tough ones, and some people will twist those things into scandal. But I’ve never made excuses for myself or run from a crowd when the going gets tough. And I’ve never pretended to be someone I’m not just to please the powerful. In the end, the voters will look at what I’ve stood for, and they’ll decide whether someone who’s been honest about who he is deserves their trust more than someone who hides behind a polished corporate suit.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22939, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12835, \"description\": \"Let me tell you something about these so-called scandals. The people throwing dirt around want you to forget about the career politicians who’ve been running this country into the ground. They’re only looking for a story they can sell in the papers. I’ve spent my life actually getting things done for ordinary folks, not reading from a teleprompter or lining my pockets with lobbyist money. If they want a safe choice, fine, but I’m not going to let them scare my supporters away with lies and exaggerations.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22940, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12835, \"description\": \"Let’s take a step back, look at who we’re running against. Richard Nixon, he talks about good leadership, but behind him swarm questions no man has ever answered. His old friend from Florida got a hundred thousand dollars from Howard Hughes that has never been explained, his own brother got even more. And this Watergate break-in keeps growing like a sore that won’t heal. When you judge a man by who he surrounds himself with and the shadows in his record, you have to ask who really stands for clean government.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22941, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12836, \"description\": \"What happened at the Munich Olympics was a crime against innocent people who were there just to compete, not to fight a war. Eleven Israeli athletes never came home, and a German officer died trying to do his duty. Any decent country ought to say plainly that this was wrong, that it was evil, and that the families deserve our sympathy and our respect. There is nothing complicated about mourning the dead and condemning murder.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22942, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12836, \"description\": \"This was an act of terrorism, full stop, and it was directed at civilians. Israel has the right to exist, the right to defend its people, and the right to expect that its athletes can travel without being hunted down. The United States should stand squarely with Israel and make clear that there is no excuse, political or otherwise, for slaughtering unarmed men in front of the world. There is no political cause that justifies the killing of civilians.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22943, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12836, \"description\": \"These events show us what happens when violent extremists are allowed to operate across national borders without consequence. This kind of violence doesn’t stay contained, and it doesn’t sort itself out. The United States must take the lead on tracking these terrorists, gathering intelligence, and making it clear that anyone who plans or carries out attacks like this will be pursued and stopped at all costs. In moments like this, the American people expect their leaders to take action.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22945, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12837, \"description\": \"Absolutely, I’ll reach out directly and sit down with Humphrey, Muskie, and the party leadership. And when I do, I’m going to keep the focus where it belongs, on the economic concerns that are weighing on working people across this country. I’ll talk plainly about stagnating wages, rising inflation, and the sense that Washington has lost touch with everyday Americans. If they see that this campaign is serious about those issues and not just caught up in some kinda populist rebellion, then maybe we can find some common ground and move toward a united front.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22946, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12837, \"description\": \"Absolutely, I’ll reach out directly and request a meeting with Humphrey, Muskie, and the rest of the party leadership. I want to have an honest conversation about the need for Democratic unity and the direction of our party. The country is tired of the constant fighting, and there’s an opportunity here to lower the temperature and bring people back together. I’ll make the case that this campaign can be a vehicle for that kind of stability, if they’re willing to meet me halfway.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22947, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12837, \"description\": \"Absolutely, I’ll reach out and meet with Humphrey, Muskie, and the party leadership face-to-face. And I’ll be straight with them. I know there are concerns about my past, and I’m not going to dodge that. I’ll tell them plainly that I’m a different candidate now, a different man, that I understand the stakes of this election, and that I’m prepared to lead in a way that brings the party together instead of pulling it apart. If they’re willing to hear me out, we might be able to turn the page.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22948, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12837, \"description\": \"No, I’m not going to go chasing after the likes of Humphrey, or Muskie, or anyone else in Washington. I’ve spent my career - and my campaign - speaking directly to the people, not to a handful of party leaders who’ve already made up their minds. If they want to come around, they can. But I’m not going to water down what our campaign stands for just to win over a few endorsements.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22949, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12838, \"description\": \"If Secretary Kissinger’s statement proves true, then that is welcome news for every American family and every Vietnamese family that has paid the price for this war. I hope the administration is finally prepared to bring this conflict to an honorable conclusion, and I will judge their actions by whether the fighting actually stops and our troops truly come home.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22950, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12838, \"description\": \"We have been told for four years now that peace is just around the corner. And in that time, the president has invaded two other countries, authorized civilian bombing campaigns, and has continued to prop up a corrupt dictatorship abroad. I want this war to end as much as anyone, but I’m not going to take these sudden assurances at face value. I will believe peace is at hand when the war is actually over.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22951, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12838, \"description\": \"What the administration is now describing sounds very much like the settlement President Johnson was prepared to accept years ago. The difference is that since then, thousands more Americans and countless Vietnamese have died. If this is where we were always headed, then the real question is why this war was prolonged for so long to begin with.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22952, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12838, \"description\": \"Peace is not something you can announce at a press conference while we still have planes dropping bombs and soldiers knee deep in the Vietnamese mud. Peace means ending the war, bringing our troops home, and stopping the killing now. Anything less is not peace. This war has gone on long enough.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22953, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12839, \"description\": \"People all over this country already think Washington protects its own, and stories like this are why. If a journalist like Walter Cronkite is laying out the facts in front of millions of Americans, then Congress has no excuse for ducking the issue. I want a full investigation, carried out in public, with no favors and no quiet deals. The truth ought to belong to the people, not to the politicians.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22954, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12839, \"description\": \"What eats away at this country is the sense that the powerful play by a different set of rules. When voters see conspiracies and dirty tricks, secrecy and cover-ups, they do not just lose faith in one party. They lose faith in the whole system. A government that operates behind closed doors cannot expect respect from people who live by the law every day. If we want stability, we start with honesty, and we start now.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22955, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12839, \"description\": \"I am not going to turn this campaign into a shouting match before the facts are in. There is a criminal investigation underway, and that is where the judgment belongs. These accusations ought to be proven, not traded like political ammunition. Let the evidence come out, let the law do its job, and let the country see who is telling the truth and who is not.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22956, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12839, \"description\": \"This goes far beyond hardball politics. If the White House ordered or tolerated a break-in at the opposition party’s headquarters, that is corruption at the highest level, and no amount of power makes it acceptable. Richard Nixon is not entitled to special treatment just because he sits in the Oval Office. If these reports are true, Richard Nixon has no business being a free man, let alone occupying the Oval Office.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22957, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12840, \"description\": \"This election doesn’t have to be another shouting match between people who never have to live with the consequences. For too long, working Americans have been stuck in the middle while Washington argues and nothing gets fixed. I’m asking people to put aside the chaos of the last few years and decide whether they want more of the same, or a government that finally pays attention to the folks who keep this country running and asks nothing fancy in return.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22958, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12840, \"description\": \"Every day this war drags on, more American boys don’t come home. Not politicians’ sons, not commentators’ sons, but kids from mill towns, farm towns, and working neighborhoods. No last-minute promises change that. If you believe this war has gone on long enough, and that America shouldn’t be bleeding without a clear purpose, then this is your chance to say so plainly, without excuses, without bullshit.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22959, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12840, \"description\": \"Most families aren’t arguing about abstract ideas at the dinner table. They’re worried about prices, paychecks, and whether the next year will be harder than the last. I’ve spent this campaign talking about work because work is what gives people dignity and stability. A fair shake, a decent wage, and a government that doesn’t bend every rule for the powerful. That’s not radical. That’s common sense, and it’s long overdue.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 22960, \"fields\": {\"question\": 12840, \"description\": \"The president wants this election wrapped up quietly, no questions asked, no answers required. That’s not how a country ought to be run. People deserve to know what their government is doing in their name, whether it’s war overseas or power being abused at home. I’m asking voters to take one clear stand and say that honesty still matters, and that nobody in Washington is entitled to trust that they haven’t earned.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23105, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13027, \"description\": \"Tonight, I stand before you as a candidate who was never supposed to be here. I did not come to this stage with the blessing of party bosses or powerful interests. I came here because ordinary people decided they were tired of being ignored. I am unbought and unbossed, and that scares a political system built on backroom deals, racial division, and the hunger for power at any cost. This campaign has always been David against Goliath, and if we have learned anything in this long fight, it is that conscience is stronger than corruption, and courage can still move this country forward.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23106, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13027, \"description\": \"For generations, this party has been the only political force willing to take risks in the cause for justice. From Franklin Roosevelt’s fight against economic despair, to Harry Truman’s stand for civil rights, to the unfinished work carried forward by John F. Kennedy and Lyndon Johnson, Democrats have been pushed forward by people who refused to accept exclusion as tradition. Tonight, I accept this nomination not as a symbol, but as a responsibility. A responsibility to widen the doors of opportunity, to restore faith in government, and to prove that leadership belongs to us all.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23107, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13027, \"description\": \"This election is about whether Americans are willing to accept a politics built on fear, secrecy, and division, or whether we believe our democracy can be honest again. The Nixon administration asks the country to look away from corruption, from inequality, and from a war without moral purpose. I will not do that. I believe Americans are strong enough to face the truth, brave enough to demand peace, and determined enough to reject a system that protects the powerful and destroys the vulnerable. That is the choice before us, and I will not soften it for anyone’s comfort.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23108, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13027, \"description\": \"It is very late, and this convention has tested all of us. Tonight, I will formally accept the nomination, thank the delegates, and honor the long road that brought us here. But tomorrow night, in prime time, I will return to speak directly to the American people. I will lay out why this campaign matters, why inclusion is not radical, and why a government that serves everyone is not only possible, but necessary. Our party has always been David against Goliath. The country deserves to hear that message clearly, and I intend to deliver it.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23109, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13028, \"description\": \"I’m thinking about who can speak to people who feel like this system stopped listening to them a long time ago. Not in a polished way, not in the language we use on television, but plainly, directly, the way people talk when they’re worried about their jobs or their communities. I want someone who has never been afraid to challenge organize power and who understands that economic justice is intertwined with everything else we’re fighting for. If we’re serious about building something broader than what we’ve had before, that kind of voice belongs on this ticket.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23110, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13028, \"description\": \"There is a real opportunity here to bring together people who have been fighting the same battles from different places. The young people who have organized against the war, who have been out there on the campuses and in the streets, and the communities I’ve been speaking to, who have been asking for dignity and a fair chance for a long time now, are not at odds with one another. They’ve just never been asked to stand together on the same terms. If this campaign is serious about building something lasting, then it ought to reflect that full coalition of reformers, not just one piece of it.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23111, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13028, \"description\": \"This campaign cannot afford to become isolated from the rest of the party, no matter how strongly people feel about me or my candidacy. We need someone who can reassure as many nervous Democrats as possible that there is still room in this coalition for experience, stability, and practical politics alongside the energy that brought us here. Someone who understands both worlds, who has credibility with the party regulars, union workers, and reform minded liberals alike, and who carries himself in a way that puts people at ease. If we want this ticket to look capable of governing the country instead of simply making history, then we ought to reflect that balance.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23113, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13029, \"description\": \"What I’m seeing in this photograph is horrifying, and no one should pretend otherwise. But war is chaotic, and mistakes do happen, especially in a conflict that has dragged on this long and sprawled across borders. If the Pentagon says this was an unintended strike, then that claim deserves to be examined seriously. The real obligation now is to make sure incidents like this don’t happen again, and to press harder for an end to a war that keeps producing them.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23114, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13029, \"description\": \"Is there anything braver about burning up children who live north of the seventeenth parallel or who live in Cambodia or Laos? They all feel pain. They all shed the same blood. They're all children of the same God. I don’t know how anyone can look at that photograph and see anything but a terrified child in unbearable pain. These bombings are indefensible, and these are the kinds of conditions we have to recover if we’re going to save the soul of this nation.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23115, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13029, \"description\": \"This is not an isolated tragedy, and it is not an accident in any meaningful sense. It is the product of a war that should never have been fought and a policy that treats civilian lives as collateral damage. Richard Nixon and Henry Kissinger have expanded this war, hidden its costs, and lied about its consequences. Richard Nixon is a war criminal. This war must end immediately, and the men responsible for carrying it on must be held accountable to the fullest extent possible.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23116, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13029, \"description\": \"What troubles Americans most is the growing distance between what they see with their own eyes and what they are told by their government. Simply put, there is a clear credibility gap today that has undermined the legitimacy of our government. A democracy cannot sustain a long war without honesty from its leaders. If this administration cannot be candid about what is happening in Southeast Asia, then it has no business asking the American people to continue supporting this conflict.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23117, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13030, \"description\": \"I’ve never been a creature of this party’s machinery, and that is exactly why I am standing here. I’m not bound to the same donors, consultants, or habits that have narrowed our politics for years. That independence lets me speak to people Democrats rarely even try to reach. Educated professionals, the yuppies, people who care about ethics and competence as much as ideology. They listen to me because they know I’m not reciting any lines written for me by some corrupt union boss. That kind of credibility helps the party in places it has written off for far too long.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23118, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13030, \"description\": \"For years, this party has talked about voters it struggles to reach while ignoring millions who are rarely invited into the process at all. Young people, Black voters, women, working families who feel shut out of politics are not a liability. They are an opportunity. My campaign brings new voters into the Democratic Party, voters who care deeply about their families, about their morals, about creating a better world. I promise that energy doesn’t stop at the top of the ticket.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23119, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13030, \"description\": \"This may be hard for the party bosses to believe, but I’m not a protest candidate. I am running a serious campaign grounded in good legislation, coalition building, and practical reform. I’ve worked with colleagues across our party and across regions to pass laws that make people’s lives better. Democratic candidates running in difficult districts can stand on their own records, just as I am standing on mine.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23120, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13030, \"description\": \"I’m not going to pretend this is an easy road. I know the history we’re up against, I know what the odds look like. But our party has made history this year, and that still counts for something. We nominated a black woman for president before we nominated a white woman or a black man, and that matters whether we win or lose. We’re showing the country that the Democratic Party is still alive, still capable of growth, still willing to face the future head on instead of hiding from it.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23121, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13031, \"description\": \"The Nixon campaign would like voters to believe I’m some caricature they invented in a back room. I am not running to tear down the country, or to impose societal chaos. I believe in equal rights under the law, in peace abroad, and in a government that serves ordinary people. I have been clear about my positions from the start, and they are right there in the Democratic platform. This is nothing more than a Nixon hit-job designed to gaslight the voters about where I stand.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23122, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13031, \"description\": \"If standing up for women, for working people, and for communities that have been shut out of power makes me “radical” in their eyes, then I will not apologize for it. I am proud to fight for equality, for peace, and for the right of every American to be treated with dignity. Those values are not reckless. They are rooted in the Constitution and in the basic promise of this country. What is truly dangerous is a politics that relies on fear instead of fairness.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23123, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13031, \"description\": \"I’m not going to let my campaign be dragged down to the level of name-calling and coded insults. People are worried about the war, about rising prices, about whether their children will have a future. That’s what I’m running to address. Voters are smarter than the people who think a catchy slogan can substitute for real leadership. I’ll keep talking about the issues that actually affect people’s lives, and let the rest speak for itself.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23124, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13031, \"description\": \"Let’s take a step back, here. I have spent my life earning my seat. I taught school, I worked in my community, and I ran for office knowing the obstacles were real and often unfair. The people of Brooklyn elected me because they trusted my judgment and my independence, not because I fit anyone’s idea of what a candidate is supposed to look like. I know who I am, and so do the voters who sent me to Congress. No whisper campaign gets to rewrite my record.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23125, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13032, \"description\": \"An unfortunate, but entirely expected development. All we can do is redouble our campaign efforts on the ground. I’ll visit every major city in the South, meet with local Democrats, and do as many rallies as I can. People need to see that we hear them and understand the challenges they face. Let’s see if we can’t light up some old loyalties the old-fashioned way.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23126, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13032, \"description\": \"John Connally is trying to use his connections to push Democrats into Nixon’s camp, but we have an ace up our sleeve here. It’s no secret that President Johnson and I have our disagreements, but there’s no way he’d ever endorse Nixon. I plan to go to President Johnson’s ranch, talk with him candidly, and ask him to publicly back our ticket. There’s no way Connally’s voice can compete with a heavyweight like Johnson.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23127, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13032, \"description\": \"Four years ago, Connally endorsed Hubert Humphrey in his campaign against Nixon. Today, he completely switches parties. You can’t possibly tell me this has anything to do with his convictions. I’ll make it clear that his endorsement isn’t a mark of wisdom or courage, it’s blatant opportunism, and it isn’t worth a damned nickel. Democrats deserve to know that the man who left his party to chase power in Washington doesn’t represent our values.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23128, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13032, \"description\": \"If John Connally wants to run this ‘Democrats for Nixon’ shtick, then fine. Two can play at that game. I’ll reach out to some former and even current Republicans who share our concerns. John Lindsay can take the lead, and see if we can’t get in touch with McCloskey and Reid. I hear Don Riegle might be switching over soon too. Nixon has spent the past four years pandering to the far right. Let’s show the voters just how many Republicans he’s chased away.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23129, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13033, \"description\": \"I understand why so many people are angry, and many of the concerns being raised in these protests are completely legitimate. The war, civil rights, and economic inequality have left a lot of Americans feeling shut out. But violence and destruction of property only weaken those causes and drag innocent people into the crossfire, and that’s not something I can support.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23130, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13033, \"description\": \"I’m not going to throw anyone under the bus, let alone so many patriotic Americans speaking out for what they feel is right. Peaceful protest is a constitutional right, and dissent is not the same thing as disorder. It is dangerous to treat protest itself as a threat to the country. President Nixon should be ashamed of himself for his brazen attempts to politicize our freedoms.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23131, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13033, \"description\": \"Look, a lot of these kids are my supporters. And I get it, they’re angry, and they want change. But ultimately, so many of these protests have gone too far, and they have done real damage to the credibility of the very causes these well-meaning young people claim to champion. If people want to change this country, they need to grow up and learn to work within the system instead of disrupting public life and turning voters against them.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23132, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13033, \"description\": \"I’m a proud Democrat, and I am no radical. Anyone who thinks I condone any kind of reckless law-breaking or unprovoked violence is mistaken. If a protest or march turns violent or disrupts the peace, then I support the protesters being punished to the extent the law calls for. To claim I think anything else is just another rumor started by Dick Nixon’s flying monkeys.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23133, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13034, \"description\": \"I’m not running against Spiro Agnew. I’m running against Richard Nixon and his record. The vice president seems to spend his days picking fights and throwing insults, and I don’t intend to join him. I’m going to keep talking about the war, the economy, and the direction of the country, and let voters decide who sounds like a president.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23134, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13034, \"description\": \"Agnew isn’t some independent actor. He says what Nixon wants to say, just louder and cruder. When he attacks protesters, the press, or whole groups of Americans, he’s doing it to distract from the failures of this administration. I’m going to remind people that his tone comes straight from the top.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23135, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13034, \"description\": \"The vice president is deliberately trying to pit Americans against each other. He mocks legitimate dissent, ridicules any disagreement, and treats whole parts of this country like mortal enemies. That might win applause at his campaign rallies, but it’s no way to govern a democracy, and it’s certainly no way to heal a country that’s already deeply divided.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23136, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13034, \"description\": \"I think most Americans are tired of being yelled at by their own government. They want a leadership that lowers the temperature, not raises it. I still believe that character counts in our country. I’m a lifelong public servant who’s taught the least fortunate amongst us, not a lying attack dog who spends his free time golfing.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23137, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13035, \"description\": \"I visited George after he was shot because I believe political violence poisons everything it touches. I didn’t go to endorse segregation or excuse what the man stood for. I went as someone who has spent her life insisting that our politics must never descend into hatred or bloodshed. I’ve opposed his ideas at every turn, and I will continue to do so. But I refuse to let this country become a place where we celebrate anyone being shot in the political sphere.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23138, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13035, \"description\": \"I made that visit with my eyes open. As a black woman, I know all too well what Wallace stands for, and I’ve never hesitated to condemn it. My record on Civil Rights is not in question, as is my opposition to all forms of racism and hatred. Visiting a wounded man doesn’t erase years of disagreement or struggle. It simply says that disagreement doesn’t cancel our shared humanity, even in the hardest moments.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23139, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13035, \"description\": \"Our movement has always asked America to rise above its worst instincts, and as a black woman, I feel that responsibility keenly. We fight for justice without vengeance, dignity without cruelty, and change without fear. I went to that hospital because I believe those values must guide us, even when it’s uncomfortable. I understand why some supporters felt hurt, and I respect that. But standing firm in the principles that brought us together is how we make real progress for the Black community and for all Americans who have been left out.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23140, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13035, \"description\": \"That visit did not change a single thing about why I am running. I am still fighting for equal opportunity, fair wages, an end to the racial discrimination that has plagued my community, and a country that lives up to its promises. Nothing can erase my work or weaken my resolve. I’m not running to soothe anyone’s conscience. I am running to challenge organized power, to open doors that have been closed, and to make sure justice is not postponed again.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23141, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13036, \"description\": \"What happened in Munich was a tragedy, plain and simple. Innocent athletes went to represent their countries in peace, and they were murdered instead. Tonight, my thoughts are with the families of the Israeli team and with the German people who tried to protect their guests. Acts like this offend the conscience of all nations, and they deserve our shared grief and resolve to see that such crimes are never repeated.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23142, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13036, \"description\": \"The murder of Israeli athletes in Munich was an act of terror against innocent people and against the very idea of peaceful competition. Israel has the right to exist, the right to security, and the right to see its citizens protected from violence wherever they go. The United States must stand firmly with Israel in condemning this attack and in opposing terrorism in all its forms. There is no political cause that justifies the killing of civilians, ever.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23143, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13036, \"description\": \"What happened in Munich is a grim reminder that violence isn’t confined by borders, or uniforms, or battlefields. It threatens civilians, the international order, and the basic sense of safety people expect in the modern world. The United States must work with its allies to strengthen international cooperation against terrorism, improve intelligence sharing, and ensure that people are safe wherever they go.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23145, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13037, \"description\": \"If we reach out to Wallace, we have to keep the focus on the economic concerns that drove much of his support. Talk about wages, inflation, and the sense that working people have been squeezed while Washington argues over everything else. Make the case that this campaign is serious about those issues and isn’t just speaking to one part of the country. If he sees that we’re addressing the same frustrations his voters feel, he may not endorse us outright, but he could soften his tone and speak more favorably about the campaign.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23146, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13037, \"description\": \"If we reach out to Wallace, we need to ensure he feels respected and included. His supporters feel dismissed by both parties, and that resentment runs deep. Make it clear that we’re not writing anyone off and that there’s room in this campaign for voters who don’t feel at home anywhere else right now. This isn’t about agreeing on everything, it’s about acknowledging that their concerns deserve to be heard. If Wallace believes we’re serious about that, he might be willing to say something positive publicly, even if he stops short of endorsing us.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23147, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13037, \"description\": \"If we reach out to Wallace, we ought to talk to him about the broader need to settle things down politically. The country’s been through years of tension, and there’s an argument to be made for lowering the temperature and trying to bring people back into a shared political space. We can frame our campaign as an attempt to move past the constant division and give voters a sense that things don’t have to stay this polarized. It’s a harder sell, and he may push back, but it at least opens the door to a serious conversation about where the country goes from here.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23148, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13037, \"description\": \"No, we’re not going to seek George Wallace’s endorsement. His record and the kind of politics he’s built his career on are fundamentally at odds with what this campaign is trying to do, especially on civil rights and the broader direction of the party. Going after his backing would raise real questions about where we stand and risk undercutting the coalition we’ve spent months trying to build. If Wallace chooses to stay out of the race, that’s his decision, but we’re not going to invite him in.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23149, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13038, \"description\": \"If Secretary Kissinger’s statement proves true, then that is welcome news for every American family and every Vietnamese family that has paid the price for this war. I hope the administration is finally prepared to bring this conflict to an honorable conclusion, and I will judge their actions by whether the fighting actually stops and our troops truly come home.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23150, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13038, \"description\": \"We have been told for four years now that peace is just around the corner. And in that time, the president has invaded two other countries, authorized civilian bombing campaigns, and has continued to prop up a corrupt dictatorship abroad. I want this war to end as much as anyone, but I’m not going to take these sudden assurances at face value. I will believe peace is at hand when the war is actually over.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23151, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13038, \"description\": \"What the administration is now describing sounds very much like the settlement President Johnson was prepared to accept years ago. The difference is that since then, thousands more Americans and countless Vietnamese have died. If this is where we were always headed, then the real question is why this war was prolonged for so long to begin with.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23152, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13038, \"description\": \"Peace is not something you can announce at a press conference while we still have planes dropping bombs and soldiers knee deep in the Vietnamese mud. Peace means ending the war, bringing our troops home, and stopping the killing now. Anything less is not peace. This war has gone on long enough.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23153, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13039, \"description\": \"These are serious developments, and they deserve to be handled with utmost care. The facts need to be gathered, verified, and examined outside of partisan politics - I fully support Mr. Cronkite’s investigative work on the matter. And I’m calling for a full, independent congressional investigation so the public can know exactly what happened and who was responsible. The rule of law has to speak for itself.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23154, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13039, \"description\": \"When Americans watch a story like this unfold, they don’t just wonder about the kinds of things being done behind closed doors. They ask what kind of country we’re becoming. A democracy can’t survive on this level of secrecy, of paranoia, of political sabotage. We need honesty and integrity back in our public life. We need leaders who believe that winning an election is not worth losing the nation’s conscience.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23155, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13039, \"description\": \"This is a lifeline for our campaign, and it’s all the more reason we have to be careful here. There’s a criminal investigation underway, and that’s where this belongs. If the President says he had no involvement with the break-in this summer, that statement should be taken seriously unless proven otherwise. We shouldn’t rush to judgment or turn every accusation into a political weapon. Let the authorities do their work.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23156, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13039, \"description\": \"This isn’t your everyday dirty campaign trick, Mr. Cronkite is making that increasingly clear. If the White House is involved in breaking into the opposition party’s headquarters, that’s an abuse of power, plain and simple. No president is above the law. If these reports are true, Richard Nixon has no business being a free man, let alone occupying the Oval Office.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23157, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13040, \"description\": \"This election doesn’t have to be about anger or fear or division. It can be about healing a country that’s been pulled apart by war and distrust. I’m asking Americans to look past the noise of the last few years and decide whether we want more chaos, or whether we want to start bringing people back together again, honestly and without illusion.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23158, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13040, \"description\": \"Every day this war continues, more young Americans die for reasons no one can clearly explain anymore. No late promises and no last-minute announcements change that fact. If you believe, as I do, that this war has gone on long enough and that it’s time to bring our sons home, then you don’t have to sacrifice your principles anymore.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23159, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13040, \"description\": \"I’ve spent this campaign talking about the dignity of work, about fair wages, and about economic justice because that’s what most families worry about when the cameras are gone. A fair economy, decent schools, and a government that works for ordinary people instead of the well-connected. That’s what Democrats have fought for since the very beginning, and that’s the choice voters still have in front of them today.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23160, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13040, \"description\": \"The president wants this election to be over quietly, without questions and without accountability. Well I don’t think the country should be run that way. I think the American people deserve straight answers, an end to secret wars and operations, and a government that tells the truth, even when it’s inconvenient. If you want change - real change - then take a leap of faith this Fall and give honesty a chance.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23305, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13227, \"description\": \"I came into public life because I believe the Constitution is a living, breathing, sacred promise. When our institutions fail to watch the powerful or protect the vulnerable, it is our responsibility to fix them. I have fought to expand the right to vote, make our democracy more responsive, and make sure our laws reflect the country we are becoming, not the country we once were. That work is not glamorous, but it matters. Tonight, I accept this nomination committed to restoring faith in our democracy and to ensuring justice under the rule of law.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23306, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13227, \"description\": \"For generations, this party has carried the hopes and dreams of ordinary Americans, from the depression years of Franklin Roosevelt, to the courage and conviction of Harry Truman, through the promise of John Kennedy and the hard work of Lyndon Johnson. In times of trial and uncertainty, Democrats have always chosen action over despair and compassion over indifference. I am proud to stand in that tradition tonight, and I accept the responsibility to help lead our country out of the confusion and turmoil of these past few years, toward a future rooted in fairness, opportunity, and faith in one another.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23307, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13227, \"description\": \"The American people have been asked to accept too many half-truths and too many closed doors. They have been told one thing while being shown another, time and time again. That credibility gap has eroded confidence in our government at home and abroad. This administration believes that secrecy is a virtue and that questioning authority is a vice. I do not. I believe honesty is the first obligation of leadership, and accountability is not an option. This campaign will offer a clear alternative to a politics that demands the American people to stop asking questions.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23308, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13227, \"description\": \"My wife Marvella, the love of my life, is battling the scourge of cancer. And walking through that with her has made everything clearer for me. It has reminded me that dignity and equality aren’t abstract ideas, they’re about whether people feel seen, protected, and valued when it matters most. Marvella has shaped my life and my politics from the very beginning. Her belief in the potential of women is why I fought to pass the Title IX bill, because no child’s future should be limited by their gender. I believe America can be better than it has been, more fair, more decent, more equal in spirit, and that belief is what brings me here tonight and what I’m asking this party and this country to hold onto together.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23309, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13228, \"description\": \"The simple truth is this: we spend too much time in Washington talking to each other instead of listening to the people we claim to represent. I want someone who comes out of small town America, someone who talks the way families talk when they’re sitting around a kitchen table worrying about the bills or the future of their farm. A voice like that keeps politics grounded. It reminds the country that the Democratic Party is supposed to stand with people who feel like the system stopped noticing them.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23310, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13228, \"description\": \"The way I see it, this ticket needs steadiness. The convention showed how many different voices exist in this party, and the only way we win in November is by keeping those voices talking to each other instead of drifting apart again. I want a running mate who understands the interests of labor and the interests of farmers, who speaks decently on civil rights, and who knows the legislative process well enough to keep a coalition together when the pressure builds. Someone serious, disciplined, and trusted inside the party. Not a showman. Someone who knows how the work actually gets done.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23311, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13228, \"description\": \"This war has shaken people’s faith in the judgment of their leaders, and we cannot pretend otherwise. I want a partner who has been willing to ask hard questions about foreign policy and who speaks about America’s power with a sense of restraint and responsibility. Someone who has taken the time to study these decisions and has not been afraid to question them when they no longer made sense. That kind of thoughtfulness matters right now. Strength in leadership is not just about resolve, it’s about knowing the limits of force and being willing to say so.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23313, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13229, \"description\": \"The images coming out of Southeast Asia are deeply troubling, and any loss of innocent life weighs heavily on the conscience of this country. War is never clean, and tragic errors can occur even when our forces are acting in good faith. If military authorities say this incident was unintended, that claim deserves serious consideration. Our responsibility is to reduce civilian harm, improve oversight, and continue the difficult work of bringing this conflict to a responsible conclusion.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23314, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13229, \"description\": \"No American can look at photographs of burned villages and wounded children without feeling shaken. These are human beings caught in the middle of forces far beyond their control. That moral reality can’t be brushed aside with technical explanations alone. If we are to justify this war to ourselves and to the world, we must show that civilian lives matter, and that our conduct reflects the values we claim to defend.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23315, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13229, \"description\": \"This is not an accident. It is the inevitable result of a war that has been recklessly expanded and a policy that treats civilian lives as expendable. The Nixon administration has widened this conflict, hidden its true costs, and misled the American people about what is being done in their name. When a government wages war this way, when it knowingly brings devastation upon civilians and refuses to answer for it, that isn’t just bad judgment. It’s a crime. Richard Nixon must answer for it. This war must end, and those responsible for prolonging it must be held to account to the fullest extent the law allows.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23316, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13229, \"description\": \"What troubles Americans most is the growing distance between what they see with their own eyes and what they are told by their government. Simply put, there is a clear credibility gap today that has undermined the legitimacy of our government. A democracy cannot sustain a long war without honesty from its leaders. If this administration cannot be candid about what is happening in Southeast Asia, then it has no business asking the American people to continue supporting this conflict.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23317, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13230, \"description\": \"I’ve spent much of my career doing the kind of work that doesn’t always grab headlines but ends up shaping the country in lasting ways. I helped write two constitutional amendments because I believe our democracy has to keep expanding who it works for and how it protects vulnerable people. I’ve been at the forefront of civil rights, voting rights, and equal opportunity, not because it was safe, but because it was the right thing to do. My record shows very clearly what I stand for, and I’m proud to let voters judge me on the merits.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23318, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13230, \"description\": \"There’s an assumption floating around that leadership has to sound angry and vulgar to be real. I don’t buy that. I’ve worked across parties, across regions, and across generations because I believe government reform only matters if it survives. What I offer voters is not a performance, but a seriousness of purpose. In a year this volatile, I think Americans are looking for someone who won’t gamble with the country just to prove a point.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23319, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13230, \"description\": \"If being cautious means thinking before acting, then I’ll take that label. We’ve lived through years of secrecy, impulse, and arrogance at the highest levels of government. The result has been a mounting credibility gap that’s created distrust at home and instability abroad. I believe strength comes from restraint, from knowing when power should be used and when it should not. That kind of leadership may not always be loud, but it lasts.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23320, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13230, \"description\": \"I don’t accept the idea that calmness means weakness or that thoughtfulness means indecision. This country is facing real challenges, and I intend to campaign with the urgency those challenges demand. I’ll be traveling across the country, laying out clear choices on the war, our economy, and on restoring trust in government. If some people think I’m too careful, they’re going to see a campaign that’s anything but passive. I’m going to make the case directly to the American people that steady leadership and decisive action are not mutually exclusive.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23321, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13231, \"description\": \"I’m not going to write off the people who pushed this party to change. If the energy has cooled down, then that’s on us to reignite it. I want this campaign back on the campuses, meeting with women’s groups, with the Civil Rights groups, with the grassroots organizations that brought new voices into politics over the last few years. That means more field offices near colleges, more listening sessions, and more time spent face to face instead of talking past each other. Trust is a two-way street. It’s gotta be earned, and I’m willing to put in the work.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23322, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13231, \"description\": \"This campaign is about winning a national election, not settling every argument inside the party. A lot of voters are exhausted by the confrontation of the past few years and want steadiness. Farmers, union households, suburban families, and independents need to know they can trust me to govern responsibly. I’m not running away from my beliefs, but I’m not going to let the campaign be defined by the loudest voices on the left either. If we hold the center, we can win and then govern from a position of strength.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23323, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13231, \"description\": \"Every Democratic nominee hears this argument sooner or later. The reality is that most Democrats understand the stakes in November. They may grumble, but when it’s time to vote, they know how to put aside any internal party squabbles. I’ve been clear about where I stand, and I’m not going to reinvent myself every few weeks. This campaign is steady, serious, and liberal enough to earn their votes when it counts.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23324, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13231, \"description\": \"I don’t think this is something I can solve by lecturing people who already share many of my goals. But there’s plenty of good liberals who these activist types will trust. I want George McGovern, Frank Church, and other progressives who earned that confidence to sit down with the student leaders, antiwar organizers, and women’s groups, and talk honestly about what this campaign is trying to do. Not to pressure them, but to keep the lines open and make clear that they still have a seat at the table.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23325, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13232, \"description\": \"An unfortunate, but entirely expected development. All we can do is redouble our campaign efforts on the ground. I’ll visit every major city in the South, meet with local Democrats, and do as many rallies as I can. People need to see that we hear them and understand the challenges they face. Let’s see if we can’t light up some old loyalties the old-fashioned way.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23326, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13232, \"description\": \"John Connally is trying to use his connections to push Democrats into Nixon’s camp, but we have an ace up our sleeve here. It’s no secret that President Johnson and I have our disagreements, but there’s no way he’d ever endorse Nixon. I plan to go to President Johnson’s ranch, talk with him candidly, and ask him to publicly back our ticket. There’s no way Connally’s voice can compete with a heavyweight like Johnson.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23327, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13232, \"description\": \"Four years ago, Connally endorsed Hubert Humphrey against Dick Nixon. Today, he completely switches parties. You can’t possibly tell me this has anything to do with his convictions. I’ll make it clear that his endorsement isn’t a mark of wisdom or courage, it’s blatant opportunism, and it isn’t worth a damned nickel. Democrats deserve to know that the man who left his party to chase power in Washington doesn’t represent our values.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23328, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13232, \"description\": \"If John Connally wants to run this ‘Democrats for Nixon’ shtick, then fine. Two can play at that game. I’ll reach out to some former and even current Republicans who share our concerns. John Lindsay can take the lead, and see if we can’t get in touch with McCloskey and Reid. I hear Don Riegle might be switching over soon too. Nixon has spent the past four years pandering to the far right. Let’s show the voters just how many Republicans he’s chased away.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23329, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13233, \"description\": \"I understand why so many people are angry, and many of the concerns being raised in these protests are completely legitimate. The war, civil rights, and economic inequality have left a lot of Americans feeling shut out. But violence and destruction of property only weaken those causes and drag innocent people into the crossfire, and that’s not something I can support.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23330, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13233, \"description\": \"I’m not going to throw anyone under the bus, let alone so many patriotic Americans speaking out for what they feel is right. Peaceful protest is a constitutional right, and dissent is not the same thing as disorder. It is dangerous to treat protest itself as a threat to the country. President Nixon should be ashamed of himself for his brazen attempts to politicize our freedoms.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23331, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13233, \"description\": \"Look, a lot of these folks supported Mr. McGovern this year. They’re young, they’re idealistic. And they’re angry too, they want change. But ultimately, so many of these protests have gone too far, and they have done real damage to the credibility of the very causes these well-meaning young people claim to champion. If people want to change this country, they need to grow up and learn to work within the system instead of disrupting public life and turning voters against them.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23332, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13233, \"description\": \"I’m a proud Democrat, and I am no radical. Anyone who thinks I condone any kind of reckless law-breaking or unprovoked violence is mistaken. If a protest or march turns violent or disrupts the peace, then I support the protesters being punished to the extent the law calls for. To claim I think anything else is just another rumor started by Dick Nixon’s flying monkeys.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23333, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13234, \"description\": \"I’m not running against Spiro Agnew. I’m running against Richard Nixon and his record. The vice president seems to spend his days picking fights and throwing insults, and I don’t intend to join him. I’m going to keep talking about the war, the economy, and the direction of the country, and let voters decide who sounds like a president.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23334, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13234, \"description\": \"Agnew isn’t some independent actor. He says what Nixon wants to say, just louder and cruder. When he attacks protesters, the press, or whole groups of Americans, he’s doing it to distract from the failures of this administration. I’m going to remind people that his tone comes straight from the top.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23335, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13234, \"description\": \"The vice president is deliberately trying to pit Americans against each other. He mocks legitimate dissent, ridicules any disagreement, and treats whole parts of this country like mortal enemies. That might win applause at his campaign rallies, but it’s no way to govern a democracy, and it’s certainly no way to heal a country that’s already deeply divided.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23336, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13234, \"description\": \"I think most Americans are tired of being yelled at by their own government. They want a leadership that lowers the temperature, not raises it. I still believe that character counts in our country. I’ve been an honest public servant for 30 years. I’ve committed my life to serving the people of Indiana. Spiro Agnew? He’s just a lying attack dog who spends his free time golfing.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23337, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13235, \"description\": \"There’s been a lot of talk about whether my record feels removed from what people are living through right now. I understand that concern, but I also think the country has learned the hard way that constitutional stability isn’t an academic issue. The Twenty-Fifth Amendment came out of a period when Americans were left in the dark about who was really running the government. After years of secrecy and confusion in Washington, I think voters understand why clear rules and accountability at the top actually matter to our security.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23338, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13235, \"description\": \"I don’t want this campaign to drift past the people who have carried the heaviest burden of the last few years. The Twenty-Sixth Amendment was about recognizing that young Americans were being asked to fight and die without a voice in the decisions being made. That was wrong, and fixing it was urgent. When people say they feel shut out of politics, I can point to a record that opened the door wider, and did it at a moment when it truly counted.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23339, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13235, \"description\": \"There are many parts of my record speak most clearly through everyday life, and Title IX is one of them. I supported it because I saw the effects of discrimination on women and girls in schools and workplaces across the country. Lots of ordinary families understand that instinctively. Any good father wants his daughter to be given the same opportunities as his son. That’s the kind of work I want this campaign to be associated with.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23340, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13235, \"description\": \"I’m proud of what I’ve done in the Senate, but this campaign can’t become a seminar on constitutional law. People are dealing with rising prices, uncertainty about their jobs, and a war that has gone on far too long. I’m going to be out there talking more about where the country is headed next and less about the mechanics of how we got here. My record shows I can do the hard work. Now the focus has to be on what it means for people trying to get by right now.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23341, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13236, \"description\": \"What happened in Munich was a tragedy, plain and simple. Innocent athletes went to represent their countries in peace, and they were murdered instead. Tonight, my thoughts are with the families of the Israeli team and with the German people who tried to protect their guests. Acts like this offend the conscience of all nations, and they deserve our shared grief and resolve to see that such crimes are never repeated.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23342, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13236, \"description\": \"The murder of Israeli athletes in Munich was an act of terror against innocent people and against the very idea of peaceful competition. Israel has the right to exist, the right to security, and the right to see its citizens protected from violence wherever they go. The United States must stand firmly with Israel in condemning this attack and in opposing terrorism in all its forms. There is no political cause that justifies the killing of civilians, ever.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23343, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13236, \"description\": \"What happened in Munich is a grim reminder that violence isn’t confined by borders, or uniforms, or battlefields. It threatens civilians, the international order, and the basic sense of safety people expect in the modern world. The United States must work with its allies to strengthen international cooperation against terrorism, improve intelligence sharing, and ensure that people are safe wherever they go.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23345, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13237, \"description\": \"If we reach out to Wallace, we have to keep the focus on the economic concerns that drove much of his support. Talk about wages, inflation, and the sense that working people have been squeezed while Washington argues over everything else. Make the case that this campaign is serious about those issues and isn’t just speaking to one part of the country. If he sees that we’re addressing the same frustrations his voters feel, he may not endorse us outright, but he could soften his tone and speak more favorably about the campaign.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23346, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13237, \"description\": \"If we reach out to Wallace, we need to ensure he feels respected and included. His supporters feel dismissed by both parties, and that resentment runs deep. Make it clear that we’re not writing anyone off and that there’s room in this campaign for voters who don’t feel at home anywhere else right now. This isn’t about agreeing on everything, it’s about acknowledging that their concerns deserve to be heard. If Wallace believes we’re serious about that, he might be willing to say something positive publicly, even if he stops short of endorsing us.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23347, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13237, \"description\": \"If we reach out to Wallace, we ought to talk to him about the broader need to settle things down politically. The country’s been through years of tension, and there’s an argument to be made for lowering the temperature and trying to bring people back into a shared political space. We can frame our campaign as an attempt to move past the constant division and give voters a sense that things don’t have to stay this polarized. It’s a harder sell, and he may push back, but it at least opens the door to a serious conversation about where the country goes from here.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23348, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13237, \"description\": \"No, we’re not going to seek George Wallace’s endorsement. His record and the kind of politics he’s built his career on are fundamentally at odds with what this campaign is trying to do, especially on civil rights and the broader direction of the party. Going after his backing would raise real questions about where we stand and risk undercutting the coalition we’ve spent months trying to build. If Wallace chooses to stay out of the race, that’s his decision, but we’re not going to invite him in.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23349, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13238, \"description\": \"If Secretary Kissinger’s statement proves true, then that is welcome news for every American family and every Vietnamese family that has paid the price for this war. I hope the administration is finally prepared to bring this conflict to an honorable conclusion, and I will judge their actions by whether the fighting actually stops and our troops truly come home.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23350, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13238, \"description\": \"We have been told for four years now that peace is just around the corner. And in that time, the president has invaded two other countries, authorized civilian bombing campaigns, and has continued to prop up a corrupt dictatorship abroad. I want this war to end as much as anyone, but I’m not going to take these sudden assurances at face value. I will believe peace is at hand when the war is actually over.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23351, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13238, \"description\": \"What the administration is now describing sounds very much like the settlement President Johnson was prepared to accept years ago. The difference is that since then, thousands more Americans and countless Vietnamese have died. If this is where we were always headed, then the real question is why this war was prolonged for so long to begin with.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23352, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13238, \"description\": \"Peace is not something you can announce at a press conference while we still have planes dropping bombs and soldiers knee deep in the Vietnamese mud. Peace means ending the war, bringing our troops home, and stopping the killing now. Anything less is not peace. This war has gone on long enough.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23353, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13239, \"description\": \"These are serious developments, and they deserve to be handled with utmost care. The facts need to be gathered, verified, and examined outside of partisan politics - I fully support Mr. Cronkite’s investigative work on the matter. And I’m calling for a full, independent congressional investigation so the public can know exactly what happened and who was responsible. The rule of law has to speak for itself.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23354, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13239, \"description\": \"When Americans watch a story like this unfold, they don’t just wonder about the kinds of things being done behind closed doors. They ask what kind of country we’re becoming. A democracy can’t survive on this level of secrecy, of paranoia, of political sabotage. We need honesty and integrity back in our public life. We need leaders who believe that winning an election is not worth losing the nation’s conscience.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23355, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13239, \"description\": \"This is a lifeline for our campaign, and it’s all the more reason we have to be careful here. There’s a criminal investigation underway, and that’s where this belongs. If the President says he had no involvement with the break-in this summer, that statement should be taken seriously unless proven otherwise. We shouldn’t rush to judgment or turn every accusation into a political weapon. Let the authorities do their work.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23356, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13239, \"description\": \"This isn’t your everyday dirty campaign trick, Mr. Cronkite is making that increasingly clear. If the White House is involved in breaking into the opposition party’s headquarters, that’s an abuse of power, plain and simple. No president is above the law. If these reports are true, Richard Nixon has no business being a free man, let alone occupying the Oval Office.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23357, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13240, \"description\": \"This election doesn’t have to be about fear or resentment. It can be about steady leadership and the hard work of healing a country strained by war and mistrust. I’m asking Americans to step back from the anger of the last few years and choose a future grounded in decency, responsibility, and the belief that we can do better than the status quo.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23358, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13240, \"description\": \"Every day this war drags on, more young Americans are asked to risk their lives without a clear purpose or end in sight. No last-minute announcement changes that fact. If you believe, as I do, that it’s time to bring this war to a responsible close and bring our sons home, then this election gives you a chance to make that a reality.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23359, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13240, \"description\": \"I’ve spent this campaign talking about good jobs, fair wages, and economic opportunity because that’s what working families think about long after the campaign speeches are over. They want a fair economy, good schools, and a government that works for people who play by the rules, not just those with influence. That’s always been the Democratic tradition, and it’s the choice voters still have in this election.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23360, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13240, \"description\": \"The president would prefer this election pass quietly, without facing any scrutiny or hard questions. I don’t believe that’s healthy for a democracy. The American people deserve honesty, accountability, and a government that levels with them, even when the truth is uncomfortable. If you want a change in tone and direction, this election is your opportunity to choose decency, responsibility, and integrity in public life.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23905, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13827, \"description\": \"I didn’t come up through politics chasing attention or easy applause. I came up doing the work, sitting in rooms where compromises get made and laws actually get written. I believe in liberal ideals, but I also believe they only matter if they survive contact with reality. This party needs a nominee who can turn its values into governing majorities, who understands institutions instead of sneering at them, and who knows that progress is built step by step. That’s why I’m here, and that’s the kind of Democrat I intend to be.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23906, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13827, \"description\": \"I’m proud to stand in the Democratic tradition that taught me this work, the tradition of Hubert Humphrey and the Minnesota Farmer Labor movement, where civil rights, workers’ rights, and human dignity were never abstract ideas. This party built Social Security, expanded opportunity, and stood up for people who were told to wait their turn. I accept this nomination in that spirit, committed to a Democratic Party that governs with compassion, discipline, and a clear sense of responsibility to working families.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23907, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13827, \"description\": \"The American people have been asked to accept too many half-truths and too many explanations. They have been told one thing while being shown another, time and time again. That credibility gap has eroded confidence in our government at home and abroad. This administration believes that secrecy is a virtue and that questioning authority is a vice. I do not. I believe honesty is the first obligation of leadership, and accountability is not an option. This campaign will offer a clear alternative to a politics that demands the American people to stop asking questions.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23908, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13827, \"description\": \"I was raised to believe that public service is a moral obligation, not a spectacle or a performance. My family taught me that government matters because people matter, and that dignity is not something you earn by luck or privilege. I believe in a country where fairness is built into our laws, where hard work matters, and where leadership is measured by whether it leaves people more secure than it found them. That belief has guided my life in public service, and it is the reason I accept this responsibility tonight.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23909, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13828, \"description\": \"The simple truth is this: we spend too much time in Washington talking to each other instead of listening to the people we claim to represent. I want someone who comes out of small town America, someone who talks the way families talk when they’re sitting around a kitchen table worrying about the bills or the future of their farm. A voice like that keeps politics grounded. It reminds the country that the Democratic Party is supposed to stand with people who feel like the system stopped noticing them.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23910, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13828, \"description\": \"This moment calls for seriousness. The country has watched too many politicians treat government like a stage for clever speeches. I want a running mate who approaches public office like a responsibility that has to be studied and respected. Someone who has spent years thinking carefully about the Constitution, how government is structured, and the way laws actually shape people’s lives. That kind of temperament would show voters that this ticket intends to govern thoughtfully and with purpose.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23911, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13828, \"description\": \"The party needs someone who can speak to people in parts of the country where Democrats have been losing ground. Not by lecturing them or pretending the last few years never happened, but by meeting them where they are and talking about progress in a language they trust. A Southern governor who has governed responsibly and who understands the pace of change in that region could help us reopen conversations we have not had in a long time.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23913, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13829, \"description\": \"The images coming out of Southeast Asia are deeply troubling, and any loss of innocent life weighs heavily on the conscience of this country. War is never clean, and tragic errors can occur even when our forces are acting in good faith. If military authorities say this incident was unintended, that claim deserves serious consideration. Our responsibility is to reduce civilian harm, improve oversight, and continue the difficult work of bringing this conflict to a responsible conclusion.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23914, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13829, \"description\": \"No American can look at photographs of burned villages and wounded children without feeling shaken. These are human beings caught in the middle of forces far beyond their control. That moral reality can’t be brushed aside with technical explanations alone. If we are to justify this war to ourselves and to the world, we must show that civilian lives matter, and that our conduct reflects the values we claim to defend.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23915, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13829, \"description\": \"This is not an accident. It is the inevitable result of a war that has been recklessly expanded and a policy that treats civilian lives as expendable. The Nixon administration has widened this conflict, hidden its true costs, and misled the American people about what is being done in their name. When a government wages war this way, when it knowingly brings devastation upon civilians and refuses to answer for it, that isn’t just bad judgment. It’s a crime. Richard Nixon must answer for it. This war must end, and those responsible for prolonging it must be held to account to the fullest extent the law allows.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23916, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13829, \"description\": \"What troubles Americans most is the growing distance between what they see with their own eyes and what they are told by their government. Simply put, there is a clear credibility gap today that has undermined the legitimacy of our government. A democracy cannot sustain a long war without honesty from its leaders. If this administration cannot be candid about what is happening in Southeast Asia, then it has no business asking the American people to continue supporting this conflict.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23917, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13830, \"description\": \"I come out of the Democratic-Farmer-Labor tradition, where politics has always been about improving people’s lives first and foremost. I learned that firsthand working alongside Hubert Humphrey, fighting for civil rights, fair employment, and a government that actually listens to working families. That kind of work isn’t always loud, but it shapes the country in lasting ways. If voters want someone grounded in that tradition of steady progress and practical reform, I’m confident they’ll see exactly where I stand. My record speaks for itself.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23918, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13830, \"description\": \"There’s an assumption floating around that leadership has to sound angry and vulgar to be real. I don’t buy that. I’ve worked across parties, across regions, and across generations because I believe government reform only matters if it survives. What I offer voters is not a performance, but a seriousness of purpose. In a year this volatile, I think Americans are looking for someone who won’t gamble with the country just to prove a point.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23919, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13830, \"description\": \"If being cautious means thinking before acting, then I’ll take that label. We’ve lived through years of secrecy, impulse, and arrogance at the highest levels of government. The result has been a mounting credibility gap that’s created distrust at home and instability abroad. I believe strength comes from restraint, from knowing when power should be used and when it should not. That kind of leadership may not always be loud, but it lasts.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23920, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13830, \"description\": \"I don’t accept the idea that being a team player means that you don’t have your own identity, or understand the issues of the day. This country is facing real challenges, and I intend to campaign with the urgency those challenges demand. I’ll be traveling across the country, laying out clear choices on the war, our economy, and on restoring trust in government. If some people think I’m too careful, they’re going to see a campaign that’s anything but passive. I’m going to make the case directly to the American people that steady leadership and decisive action are not mutually exclusive.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23921, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13831, \"description\": \"I’m not going to pretend the people who pushed this party forward over the last decade don’t matter. I’ve known many of them for years, and I respect what brought them into politics. If the energy has cooled down, then that’s on us to reignite it. That means being present on the campuses, meeting directly with student leaders, women’s groups, civil rights organizations, and antiwar activists, not through press releases or surrogates but face-to-face. More field offices near colleges, more listening sessions, fewer assumptions. I’ve spent my career building coalitions the hard way, and I’m willing to do it again.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23922, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13831, \"description\": \"This campaign has to speak to the whole country, not just the most energized corners of the party. A lot of Americans are tired, worried about prices, jobs, and whether things feel stable again. Working families, union households, farmers, suburban voters, and independents need to know they can rely on steady leadership. I’m not backing away from my record or my values, but I’m not going to let this campaign be driven by constant internal debates either. If we hold the center together, we give ourselves a real chance to win and govern.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23923, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13831, \"description\": \"I’ve been in enough campaigns to know this pattern. There’s always frustration on the left when the general election starts. They’re young, idealistic. Of course they’re gonna be frustrated. But most Democrats understand what’s at stake when November comes. They may be disappointed, they may argue, but they know the difference between disagreement and surrender. I’ve been clear about who I am and what I believe for a long time now. This campaign isn’t going to be making any sudden pivots. It’s about experience, seriousness, and giving Democrats a nominee they can trust when it’s time to cast a ballot.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23924, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13831, \"description\": \"I don’t think the answer is for me to lecture people who already know my record. But there are voices in this party who have real credibility with the activists and the student movement. I want people like George McGovern, Frank Church, and other respected progressives to sit down with the student organizers, the antiwar leaders, the women’s groups, and talk honestly about where this campaign is headed. Not to strong-arm anyone, just to keep the conversation open and make it clear they haven’t been pushed aside. Keeping those relationships alive matters, even when we don’t agree on every tactic.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23925, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13832, \"description\": \"An unfortunate, but entirely expected development. All we can do is redouble our campaign efforts on the ground. I’ll visit every major city in the South, meet with local Democrats, and do as many rallies as I can. People need to see that we hear them and understand the challenges they face. Let’s see if we can’t light up some old loyalties the old-fashioned way.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23926, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13832, \"description\": \"John Connally is trying to use his connections to push Democrats into Nixon’s camp, but we have an ace up our sleeve here. It’s no secret that President Johnson and I have our disagreements, but there’s no way he’d ever endorse Nixon. I plan to go to President Johnson’s ranch, talk with him candidly, and ask him to publicly back our ticket. There’s no way Connally’s voice can compete with a heavyweight like Johnson.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23927, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13832, \"description\": \"Four years ago, Connally endorsed Hubert Humphrey against Dick Nixon. Today, he completely switches parties. You can’t possibly tell me this has anything to do with his convictions. I’ll make it clear that his endorsement isn’t a mark of wisdom or courage, it’s blatant opportunism, and it isn’t worth a damned nickel. Democrats deserve to know that the man who left his party to chase power in Washington doesn’t represent our values.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23928, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13832, \"description\": \"If John Connally wants to run this ‘Democrats for Nixon’ shtick, then fine. Two can play at that game. I’ll reach out to some former and even current Republicans who share our concerns. John Lindsay can take the lead, and see if we can’t get in touch with McCloskey and Reid. I hear Don Riegle might be switching over soon too. Nixon has spent the past four years pandering to the far right. Let’s show the voters just how many Republicans he’s chased away.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23929, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13833, \"description\": \"I understand why so many people are angry, and many of the concerns being raised in these protests are completely legitimate. The war, civil rights, and economic inequality have left a lot of Americans feeling shut out. But violence and destruction of property only weaken those causes and drag innocent people into the crossfire, and that’s not something I can support.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23930, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13833, \"description\": \"I’m not going to throw anyone under the bus, let alone so many patriotic Americans speaking out for what they feel is right. Peaceful protest is a constitutional right, and dissent is not the same thing as disorder. It is dangerous to treat protest itself as a threat to the country. President Nixon should be ashamed of himself for his brazen attempts to politicize our freedoms.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23931, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13833, \"description\": \"Look, a lot of these folks supported Mr. McGovern this year. They’re young, they’re idealistic. And they’re angry too, they want change. But ultimately, so many of these protests have gone too far, and they have done real damage to the credibility of the very causes these well-meaning young people claim to champion. If people want to change this country, they need to grow up and learn to work within the system instead of disrupting public life and turning voters against them.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23932, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13833, \"description\": \"I’m a proud Democrat, and I am no radical. Anyone who thinks I condone any kind of reckless law-breaking or unprovoked violence is mistaken. If a protest or march turns violent or disrupts the peace, then I support the protesters being punished to the extent the law calls for. To claim I think anything else is just another rumor started by Dick Nixon’s flying monkeys.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23933, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13834, \"description\": \"I’m not running against Spiro Agnew. I’m running against Richard Nixon and his record. The vice president seems to spend his days picking fights and throwing insults, and I don’t intend to join him. I’m going to keep talking about the war, the economy, and the direction of the country, and let voters decide who sounds like a president.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23934, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13834, \"description\": \"Agnew isn’t some independent actor. He says what Nixon wants to say, just louder and cruder. When he attacks protesters, the press, or whole groups of Americans, he’s doing it to distract from the failures of this administration. I’m going to remind people that his tone comes straight from the top.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23935, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13834, \"description\": \"The vice president is deliberately trying to pit Americans against each other. He mocks legitimate dissent, ridicules any disagreement, and treats whole parts of this country like mortal enemies. That might win applause at his campaign rallies, but it’s no way to govern a democracy, and it’s certainly no way to heal a country that’s already deeply divided.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23936, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13834, \"description\": \"I think most Americans are tired of being yelled at by their own government. They want a leadership that lowers the temperature, not raises it. I still believe that character counts in our country. I’ve been an honest public servant for 30 years. I’ve committed my life to serving the people of Minnesota. Spiro Agnew? He’s just a lying attack dog who spends his free time golfing.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23937, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13835, \"description\": \"I did not come to public life to be anyone’s stand in. I ran for office in Minnesota on my own name, my own views, and my own work. I have taken positions that cost me support, including inside my own party, because I believed they were right. Voters can look at my record on Vietnam, on welfare, on civil rights, and decide for themselves whether I sound like a follower or someone who has made up his own mind.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23938, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13835, \"description\": \"I’m having a real hard time understanding why being a loyal Democrat is treated like an insult. This party passed civil rights laws, built Medicare, created Social Security, and expanded economic opportunity for the working American. I am proud to have worked with its leaders and to have carried my share of the load. Governing is not about striking poses for the press. It is about showing up, doing the work, and standing with your party when it counts.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23939, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13835, \"description\": \"I have worked within the party, and I have also pushed it to change. Those two things are not in conflict. I support reform because I want a party that earns trust again, especially among younger voters who have every reason to be skeptical. I know how this party works, and I also know where it has failed. That puts me in a position to help move it forward instead of tearing it apart.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23940, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13835, \"description\": \"Anyone who thinks I am here to keep the lights on for the old order has not been paying attention to who stands with me. George McGovern, Harold Hughes, and Frank Church have all lent me their support. They didn’t put their names behind this campaign because they wanted a placeholder. They did it because they know where I stand on the war and on the issues of the day. I am not asking voters to take my word for it. I am asking them to look at who trusts me and why.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23941, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13836, \"description\": \"What happened in Munich was a tragedy, plain and simple. Innocent athletes went to represent their countries in peace, and they were murdered instead. Tonight, my thoughts are with the families of the Israeli team and with the German people who tried to protect their guests. Acts like this offend the conscience of all nations, and they deserve our shared grief and resolve to see that such crimes are never repeated.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23942, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13836, \"description\": \"The murder of Israeli athletes in Munich was an act of terror against innocent people and against the very idea of peaceful competition. Israel has the right to exist, the right to security, and the right to see its citizens protected from violence wherever they go. The United States must stand firmly with Israel in condemning this attack and in opposing terrorism in all its forms. There is no political cause that justifies the killing of civilians, ever.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23943, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13836, \"description\": \"What happened in Munich is a grim reminder that violence isn’t confined by borders, or uniforms, or battlefields. It threatens civilians, the international order, and the basic sense of safety people expect in the modern world. The United States must work with its allies to strengthen international cooperation against terrorism, improve intelligence sharing, and ensure that people are safe wherever they go.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23945, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13837, \"description\": \"If we reach out to Wallace, we have to keep the focus on the economic concerns that drove much of his support. Talk about wages, inflation, and the sense that working people have been squeezed while Washington argues over everything else. Make the case that this campaign is serious about those issues and isn’t just speaking to one part of the country. If he sees that we’re addressing the same frustrations his voters feel, he may not endorse us outright, but he could soften his tone and speak more favorably about the campaign.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23946, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13837, \"description\": \"If we reach out to Wallace, we need to ensure he feels respected and included. His supporters feel dismissed by both parties, and that resentment runs deep. Make it clear that we’re not writing anyone off and that there’s room in this campaign for voters who don’t feel at home anywhere else right now. This isn’t about agreeing on everything, it’s about acknowledging that their concerns deserve to be heard. If Wallace believes we’re serious about that, he might be willing to say something positive publicly, even if he stops short of endorsing us.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23947, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13837, \"description\": \"If we reach out to Wallace, we ought to talk to him about the broader need to settle things down politically. The country’s been through years of tension, and there’s an argument to be made for lowering the temperature and trying to bring people back into a shared political space. We can frame our campaign as an attempt to move past the constant division and give voters a sense that things don’t have to stay this polarized. It’s a harder sell, and he may push back, but it at least opens the door to a serious conversation about where the country goes from here.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23948, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13837, \"description\": \"No, we’re not going to seek George Wallace’s endorsement. His record and the kind of politics he’s built his career on are fundamentally at odds with what this campaign is trying to do, especially on civil rights and the broader direction of the party. Going after his backing would raise real questions about where we stand and risk undercutting the coalition we’ve spent months trying to build. If Wallace chooses to stay out of the race, that’s his decision, but we’re not going to invite him in.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23949, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13838, \"description\": \"If Secretary Kissinger’s statement proves true, then that is welcome news for every American family and every Vietnamese family that has paid the price for this war. I hope the administration is finally prepared to bring this conflict to an honorable conclusion, and I will judge their actions by whether the fighting actually stops and our troops truly come home.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23950, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13838, \"description\": \"We have been told for four years now that peace is just around the corner. And in that time, the president has invaded two other countries, authorized civilian bombing campaigns, and has continued to prop up a corrupt dictatorship abroad. I want this war to end as much as anyone, but I’m not going to take these sudden assurances at face value. I will believe peace is at hand when the war is actually over.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23951, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13838, \"description\": \"What the administration is now describing sounds very much like the settlement President Johnson was prepared to accept years ago. The difference is that since then, thousands more Americans and countless Vietnamese have died. If this is where we were always headed, then the real question is why this war was prolonged for so long to begin with.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23952, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13838, \"description\": \"Peace is not something you can announce at a press conference while we still have planes dropping bombs and soldiers knee deep in the Vietnamese mud. Peace means ending the war, bringing our troops home, and stopping the killing now. Anything less is not peace. This war has gone on long enough.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23953, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13839, \"description\": \"These are serious developments, and they deserve to be handled with utmost care. The facts need to be gathered, verified, and examined outside of partisan politics - I fully support Mr. Cronkite’s investigative work on the matter. And I’m calling for a full, independent congressional investigation so the public can know exactly what happened and who was responsible. The rule of law has to speak for itself.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23954, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13839, \"description\": \"When Americans watch a story like this unfold, they don’t just wonder about the kinds of things being done behind closed doors. They ask what kind of country we’re becoming. A democracy can’t survive on this level of secrecy, of paranoia, of political sabotage. We need honesty and integrity back in our public life. We need leaders who believe that winning an election is not worth losing the nation’s conscience.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23955, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13839, \"description\": \"This is a lifeline for our campaign, and it’s all the more reason we have to be careful here. There’s a criminal investigation underway, and that’s where this belongs. If the President says he had no involvement with the break-in this summer, that statement should be taken seriously unless proven otherwise. We shouldn’t rush to judgment or turn every accusation into a political weapon. Let the authorities do their work.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23956, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13839, \"description\": \"This isn’t your everyday dirty campaign trick, Mr. Cronkite is making that increasingly clear. If the White House is involved in breaking into the opposition party’s headquarters, that’s an abuse of power, plain and simple. No president is above the law. If these reports are true, Richard Nixon has no business being a free man, let alone occupying the Oval Office.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23957, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13840, \"description\": \"This election doesn’t have to be about fear or resentment. It can be about steady leadership and the hard work of healing a country strained by war and mistrust. I’m asking Americans to step back from the anger of the last few years and choose a future grounded in decency, responsibility, and the belief that we can do better than the status quo.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23958, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13840, \"description\": \"Every day this war drags on, more young Americans are asked to risk their lives without a clear purpose or end in sight. No last-minute announcement changes that fact. If you believe, as I do, that it’s time to bring this war to a responsible close and bring our sons home, then this election gives you a chance to make that a reality.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23959, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13840, \"description\": \"I’ve spent this campaign talking about good jobs, fair wages, and economic opportunity because that’s what working families think about long after the campaign speeches are over. They want a fair economy, good schools, and a government that works for people who play by the rules, not just those with influence. That’s always been the Democratic tradition, and it’s the choice voters still have in this election.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 23960, \"fields\": {\"question\": 13840, \"description\": \"The president would prefer this election pass quietly, without facing any scrutiny or hard questions. I don’t believe that’s healthy for a democracy. The American people deserve honesty, accountability, and a government that levels with them, even when the truth is uncomfortable. If you want a change in tone and direction, this election is your opportunity to choose decency, responsibility, and integrity in public life.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 24505, \"fields\": {\"question\": 14427, \"description\": \"I didn’t come to this convention to fit neatly into anyone’s box. I came here because I believe this party has to recover its conscience. For too long, Americans have been told that questioning power is weakness and that loyalty means silence. I reject that. I believe the strength of this country lies in citizens who ask hard questions and leaders who respect them enough to answer honestly. That is the spirit I bring to this campaign.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 24506, \"fields\": {\"question\": 14427, \"description\": \"For generations, this party has carried the hopes and dreams of ordinary Americans, from the depression years of Franklin Roosevelt, to the courage and conviction of Harry Truman, through the promise of John Kennedy and the hard work of Lyndon Johnson. In times of trial and uncertainty, Democrats have always chosen action over despair and compassion over indifference. I am proud to stand in that tradition tonight, and I accept the responsibility to help lead our country out of the confusion and turmoil of these past few years, toward a future rooted in fairness, opportunity, and faith in one another.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 24507, \"fields\": {\"question\": 14427, \"description\": \"The American people have been asked to accept too many half-truths and too many closed doors. They have been told one thing while being shown another, time and time again. That credibility gap has eroded confidence in our government at home and abroad. This administration believes that secrecy is a virtue and that questioning authority is a vice. I do not. I believe honesty is the first obligation of leadership, and accountability is not an option. This campaign will offer a clear alternative to a politics that demands the American people to stop asking questions.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 24508, \"fields\": {\"question\": 14427, \"description\": \"I grew up believing that this country was capable of more than fear, more than suspicion, more than endless conflict. I still believe that. I believe we can build a nation that values peace without illusion, liberty without qualification, and dignity without exception. That belief has guided my public life, and it is what brings me here tonight. I accept this responsibility with humility and with faith that America can still choose its better angels.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 24509, \"fields\": {\"question\": 14428, \"description\": \"I think what voters want right now more than anything is someone with good judgment. Someone who takes the time to understand a problem before speaking on it, who weighs the consequences instead of rushing to a headline, and who carries himself with a certain discipline in public life. I want a running mate known for that kind of steadiness, who has spent years working through difficult issues in detail and has earned respect for it. There’s a difference between sounding thoughtful and actually doing the work, and I think people can tell which is which.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 24510, \"fields\": {\"question\": 14428, \"description\": \"This moment calls for seriousness. The country has watched too many politicians treat government like a stage for clever speeches. I want a running mate who approaches public office like a responsibility that has to be studied and respected. Someone who has spent years thinking carefully about the Constitution, how government is structured, and the way laws actually shape people’s lives. That kind of temperament would show voters that this ticket intends to govern thoughtfully and with purpose.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 24511, \"fields\": {\"question\": 14428, \"description\": \"I want this campaign to sound like it is thinking about the country ten or twenty years from now, not just about the next election. Americans are starting to understand that the air they breathe and the water they drink are not abstract concerns. They are part of everyday life. And there is someone who has been speaking about conservation and environmental responsibility long before it became fashionable. He would show voters that we are willing to look beyond the latest polls and talk about the future with some honesty for a change.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 24513, \"fields\": {\"question\": 14429, \"description\": \"What I’m seeing in this photograph is horrifying, and no one should pretend otherwise. But war is chaotic, and mistakes do happen, especially in a conflict that has dragged on this long and sprawled across borders. If the Pentagon says this was an unintended strike, then that claim deserves to be examined seriously. The real obligation now is to make sure incidents like this don’t happen again, and to press harder for an end to a war that keeps producing them.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 24514, \"fields\": {\"question\": 14429, \"description\": \"Is there anything braver about burning up children who live north of the seventeenth parallel or who live in Cambodia or Laos? They all feel pain. They all shed the same blood. They're all children of the same God. I don’t know how anyone can look at that photograph and see anything but a terrified child in unbearable pain. These bombings are indefensible, and these are the kinds of conditions we have to recover if we’re going to save the soul of this nation.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 24515, \"fields\": {\"question\": 14429, \"description\": \"This is not an isolated tragedy, and it is not an accident in any meaningful sense. It is the product of a war that should never have been fought and a policy that treats civilian lives as collateral damage. Richard Nixon and Henry Kissinger have expanded this war, hidden its costs, and lied about its consequences. Richard Nixon is a war criminal. This war must end immediately, and the men responsible for carrying it on must be held accountable to the fullest extent possible.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 24516, \"fields\": {\"question\": 14429, \"description\": \"What troubles Americans most is the growing distance between what they see with their own eyes and what they are told by their government. Simply put, there is a clear credibility gap today that has undermined the legitimacy of our government. A democracy cannot sustain a long war without honesty from its leaders. If this administration cannot be candid about what is happening in Southeast Asia, then it has no business asking the American people to continue supporting this conflict.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 24517, \"fields\": {\"question\": 14430, \"description\": \"This story falls apart the moment you look at my record. I’ve spent my career working within the party, writing legislation, building coalitions, and taking votes that actually hold up under scrutiny. I’m a liberal, yes, but a liberal who knows how to govern. I didn’t come here through some big protest or primary performance. I came here through the work, and I’m comfortable standing on it.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 24518, \"fields\": {\"question\": 14430, \"description\": \"I don’t shy away from my ideals, and I never have. I believe the country needs to change course on the war, on civil rights, and on who gets a fair shake in this economy. If that makes some people uneasy, so be it. I wasn’t chosen to hide what I believe. I was chosen because those beliefs are shared by a growing part of this party and this country.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 24519, \"fields\": {\"question\": 14430, \"description\": \"The country is in the middle of a generational shift, whether Washington is ready for it or not. Younger voters are demanding honesty, and older voters want a stability they can trust. I don’t see those instincts as enemies. I see them as two halves of the same problem, and my job is to bring them together without pretending either side is wrong to feel the way it does.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 24520, \"fields\": {\"question\": 14430, \"description\": \"This is certainly a strange accusation coming from a president who reinvented himself overnight to chase a new constituency when it suited him. I’ve been saying the same things, casting the same votes, and standing in the same place for years. There’s no Trojan Horse here. What there is, is a campaign trying to distract from its own record by pretending that consistency is something to be afraid of.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 24521, \"fields\": {\"question\": 14431, \"description\": \"I’ve never believed that secrecy, by itself, makes this country safer. My work in the Senate has been about accountability, not sabotage. When our intelligence agencies operate without any meaningful oversight, mistakes are buried, and abuses multiply by the day. A strong America is one that trusts its institutions enough to question them, correct them, and keep them aligned with the very values they claim to defend.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 24522, \"fields\": {\"question\": 14431, \"description\": \"I respect the seriousness of our national security, and I’ve always approached it with discretion and care. Proper government oversight is not the same thing as tearing down our intelligence services. It means making sure they are focused, lawful, and effective. That kind of discipline strengthens our hand abroad and prevents the kinds of failures that actually put American lives at risk.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 24523, \"fields\": {\"question\": 14431, \"description\": \"The current administration talks a big game about stability and security, but it asks the public to accept everything they say on blind faith. I don’t think that’s healthy for a democracy. We can pursue diplomacy abroad and still insist that power be exercised responsibly at home. Those goals are not in conflict, and pretending they are only lowers our standards of leadership.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 24524, \"fields\": {\"question\": 14431, \"description\": \"I grew up believing that this country was truly exceptional because it placed the rule of law above the goals and desires of any one politician. When intelligence work drifts beyond that boundary, it damages our credibility abroad and our confidence at home. Asking the hard questions is not weakness. It’s a way of making sure that American power reflects American principles.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 24525, \"fields\": {\"question\": 14432, \"description\": \"An unfortunate, but entirely expected development. All we can do is redouble our campaign efforts on the ground. I’ll visit every major city in the South, meet with local Democrats, and do as many rallies as I can. People need to see that we hear them and understand the challenges they face. Let’s see if we can’t light up some old loyalties the old-fashioned way.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 24526, \"fields\": {\"question\": 14432, \"description\": \"John Connally is trying to use his connections to push Democrats into Nixon’s camp, but we have an ace up our sleeve here. It’s no secret that President Johnson and I have our disagreements, but there’s no way he’d ever endorse Nixon. I plan to go to President Johnson’s ranch, talk with him candidly, and ask him to publicly back our ticket. There’s no way Connally’s voice can compete with a heavyweight like Johnson.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 24527, \"fields\": {\"question\": 14432, \"description\": \"Four years ago, Connally endorsed Hubert Humphrey in his campaign against Nixon. Today, he completely switches parties. You can’t possibly tell me this has anything to do with his convictions. I’ll make it clear that his endorsement isn’t a mark of wisdom or courage, it’s blatant opportunism, and it isn’t worth a damned nickel. Democrats deserve to know that the man who left his party to chase power in Washington doesn’t represent our values.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 24528, \"fields\": {\"question\": 14432, \"description\": \"If John Connally wants to run this ‘Democrats for Nixon’ shtick, then fine. Two can play at that game. I’ll reach out to some former and even current Republicans who share our concerns. John Lindsay can take the lead, and see if we can’t get in touch with McCloskey and Reid. I hear Don Riegle might be switching over soon too. Nixon has spent the past four years pandering to the far right. Let’s show the voters just how many Republicans he’s chased away.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 24529, \"fields\": {\"question\": 14433, \"description\": \"I understand why so many people are angry, and many of the concerns being raised in these protests are completely legitimate. The war, civil rights, and economic inequality have left a lot of Americans feeling shut out. But violence and destruction of property only weaken those causes and drag innocent people into the crossfire, and that’s not something I can support.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 24530, \"fields\": {\"question\": 14433, \"description\": \"I’m not going to throw anyone under the bus, let alone so many patriotic Americans speaking out for what they feel is right. Peaceful protest is a constitutional right, and dissent is not the same thing as disorder. It is dangerous to treat protest itself as a threat to the country. President Nixon should be ashamed of himself for his brazen attempts to politicize our freedoms.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 24531, \"fields\": {\"question\": 14433, \"description\": \"Look, a lot of these kids are my supporters. And I get it, they’re angry, and they want change. But ultimately, so many of these protests have gone too far, and they have done real damage to the credibility of the very causes these well-meaning young people claim to champion. If people want to change this country, they need to grow up and learn to work within the system instead of disrupting public life and turning voters against them.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 24532, \"fields\": {\"question\": 14433, \"description\": \"I’m a proud Democrat, and I am no radical. Anyone who thinks I condone any kind of reckless law-breaking or unprovoked violence is mistaken. If a protest or march turns violent or disrupts the peace, then I support the protesters being punished to the extent the law calls for. To claim I think anything else is just another rumor started by Dick Nixon’s flying monkeys.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 24533, \"fields\": {\"question\": 14434, \"description\": \"I’m not running against Spiro Agnew. I’m running against Richard Nixon and his record. The vice president seems to spend his days picking fights and throwing insults, and I don’t intend to join him. I’m going to keep talking about the war, the economy, and the direction of the country, and let voters decide who sounds like a president.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 24534, \"fields\": {\"question\": 14434, \"description\": \"Agnew isn’t some independent actor. He says what Nixon wants to say, just louder and cruder. When he attacks protesters, the press, or whole groups of Americans, he’s doing it to distract from the failures of this administration. I’m going to remind people that his tone comes straight from the top.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 24535, \"fields\": {\"question\": 14434, \"description\": \"The vice president is deliberately trying to pit Americans against each other. He mocks legitimate dissent, ridicules any disagreement, and treats whole parts of this country like mortal enemies. That might win applause at his campaign rallies, but it’s no way to govern a democracy, and it’s certainly no way to heal a country that’s already deeply divided.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 24536, \"fields\": {\"question\": 14434, \"description\": \"I think most Americans are tired of being yelled at by their own government. They want a leadership that lowers the temperature, not raises it. I still believe that character counts in our country. I’m an honest, hard-working man of the prairie, not a lying attack dog who spends his free time golfing.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 24537, \"fields\": {\"question\": 14435, \"description\": \"Listen to me. Put this back exactly where you found it. None of this ever happened, you hear me? If I report this or start asking questions, then whoever planted it will know that it was found, and we don’t want to open that can of worms. For now, the safest move is to leave it untouched, say nothing, and give them no signal that anything has changed. And you don’t say a word either, to anyone. Not a soul.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 24538, \"fields\": {\"question\": 14435, \"description\": \"I am not going to live with this hanging over my head. Someone put it there on purpose, and staying quiet only gives them control over what happens next. I would rather take the risk of exposure and force this into the open, where there are witnesses, where the media is talking about it, than sit quietly and wait for the other shoe to drop.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 24539, \"fields\": {\"question\": 14435, \"description\": \"If someone was willing to hide something here once, there is no reason to think they stopped there. Listen to me. I want a full sweep of this office, the phones, the cars, everything. We keep it discrete, with as few people involved as possible. Until we know exactly what we are dealing with, the safest assumption is that we are not alone.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 24540, \"fields\": {\"question\": 14435, \"description\": \"This crosses out of politics and into something much more serious. Someone illegally planted a device in a United States Senator’s office, and that cannot just be handled quietly. I will call the authorities, document everything we know, and make it clear that I am not going to cover for this or absorb the risk myself.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 24541, \"fields\": {\"question\": 14436, \"description\": \"What happened in Munich was a tragedy, plain and simple. Innocent athletes went to represent their countries in peace, and they were murdered instead. Tonight, my thoughts are with the families of the Israeli team and with the German people who tried to protect their guests. Acts like this offend the conscience of all nations, and they deserve our shared grief and resolve to see that such crimes are never repeated.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 24542, \"fields\": {\"question\": 14436, \"description\": \"The murder of Israeli athletes in Munich was an act of terror against innocent people and against the very idea of peaceful competition. Israel has the right to exist, the right to security, and the right to see its citizens protected from violence wherever they go. The United States must stand firmly with Israel in condemning this attack and in opposing terrorism in all its forms. There is no political cause that justifies the killing of civilians, ever.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 24543, \"fields\": {\"question\": 14436, \"description\": \"What happened in Munich is a grim reminder that violence isn’t confined by borders, or uniforms, or battlefields. It threatens civilians, the international order, and the basic sense of safety people expect in the modern world. The United States must work with its allies to strengthen international cooperation against terrorism, improve intelligence sharing, and ensure that people are safe wherever they go.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 24545, \"fields\": {\"question\": 14437, \"description\": \"If we reach out to Wallace, we have to keep the focus on the economic concerns that drove much of his support. Talk about wages, inflation, and the sense that working people have been squeezed while Washington argues over everything else. Make the case that this campaign is serious about those issues and isn’t just speaking to one part of the country. If he sees that we’re addressing the same frustrations his voters feel, he may not endorse us outright, but he could soften his tone and speak more favorably about the campaign.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 24546, \"fields\": {\"question\": 14437, \"description\": \"If we reach out to Wallace, we need to ensure he feels respected and included. His supporters feel dismissed by both parties, and that resentment runs deep. Make it clear that we’re not writing anyone off and that there’s room in this campaign for voters who don’t feel at home anywhere else right now. This isn’t about agreeing on everything, it’s about acknowledging that their concerns deserve to be heard. If Wallace believes we’re serious about that, he might be willing to say something positive publicly, even if he stops short of endorsing us.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 24547, \"fields\": {\"question\": 14437, \"description\": \"If we reach out to Wallace, we ought to talk to him about the broader need to settle things down politically. The country’s been through years of tension, and there’s an argument to be made for lowering the temperature and trying to bring people back into a shared political space. We can frame our campaign as an attempt to move past the constant division and give voters a sense that things don’t have to stay this polarized. It’s a harder sell, and he may push back, but it at least opens the door to a serious conversation about where the country goes from here.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 24548, \"fields\": {\"question\": 14437, \"description\": \"No, we’re not going to seek George Wallace’s endorsement. His record and the kind of politics he’s built his career on are fundamentally at odds with what this campaign is trying to do, especially on civil rights and the broader direction of the party. Going after his backing would raise real questions about where we stand and risk undercutting the coalition we’ve spent months trying to build. If Wallace chooses to stay out of the race, that’s his decision, but we’re not going to invite him in.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 24549, \"fields\": {\"question\": 14438, \"description\": \"If Secretary Kissinger’s statement proves true, then that is welcome news for every American family and every Vietnamese family that has paid the price for this war. I hope the administration is finally prepared to bring this conflict to an honorable conclusion, and I will judge their actions by whether the fighting actually stops and our troops truly come home.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 24550, \"fields\": {\"question\": 14438, \"description\": \"We have been told for four years now that peace is just around the corner. And in that time, the president has invaded two other countries, authorized civilian bombing campaigns, and has continued to prop up a corrupt dictatorship abroad. I want this war to end as much as anyone, but I’m not going to take these sudden assurances at face value. I will believe peace is at hand when the war is actually over.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 24551, \"fields\": {\"question\": 14438, \"description\": \"What the administration is now describing sounds very much like the settlement President Johnson was prepared to accept years ago. The difference is that since then, thousands more Americans and countless Vietnamese have died. If this is where we were always headed, then the real question is why this war was prolonged for so long to begin with.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 24552, \"fields\": {\"question\": 14438, \"description\": \"Peace is not something you can announce at a press conference while we still have planes dropping bombs and soldiers knee deep in the Vietnamese mud. Peace means ending the war, bringing our troops home, and stopping the killing now. Anything less is not peace. This war has gone on long enough.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 24553, \"fields\": {\"question\": 14439, \"description\": \"These are serious developments, and they deserve to be handled with utmost care. The facts need to be gathered, verified, and examined outside of partisan politics - I fully support Mr. Cronkite’s investigative work on the matter. And I’m calling for a full, independent congressional investigation so the public can know exactly what happened and who was responsible. The rule of law has to speak for itself.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 24554, \"fields\": {\"question\": 14439, \"description\": \"When Americans watch a story like this unfold, they don’t just wonder about the kinds of things being done behind closed doors. They ask what kind of country we’re becoming. A democracy can’t survive on this level of secrecy, of paranoia, of political sabotage. We need honesty and integrity back in our public life. We need leaders who believe that winning an election is not worth losing the nation’s conscience.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 24555, \"fields\": {\"question\": 14439, \"description\": \"This is a lifeline for our campaign, and it’s all the more reason we have to be careful here. There’s a criminal investigation underway, and that’s where this belongs. If the President says he had no involvement with the break-in this summer, that statement should be taken seriously unless proven otherwise. We shouldn’t rush to judgment or turn every accusation into a political weapon. Let the authorities do their work.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 24556, \"fields\": {\"question\": 14439, \"description\": \"This isn’t your everyday dirty campaign trick, Mr. Cronkite is making that increasingly clear. If the White House is involved in breaking into the opposition party’s headquarters, that’s an abuse of power, plain and simple. No president is above the law. If these reports are true, Richard Nixon has no business being a free man, let alone occupying the Oval Office.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 24557, \"fields\": {\"question\": 14440, \"description\": \"This election doesn’t have to be about anger or fear or division. It can be about healing a country that’s been pulled apart by war and distrust. I’m asking Americans to look past the noise of the last few years and decide whether we want more chaos, or whether we want to start bringing people back together again, honestly and without illusion.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 24558, \"fields\": {\"question\": 14440, \"description\": \"Every day this war continues, more young Americans die for reasons no one can clearly explain anymore. No late promises and no last-minute announcements change that fact. If you believe, as I do, that this war has gone on long enough and that it’s time to bring our sons home, then you don’t have to sacrifice your principles anymore.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 24559, \"fields\": {\"question\": 14440, \"description\": \"I’ve spent this campaign talking about the dignity of work, about fair wages, and about economic justice because that’s what most families worry about when the cameras are gone. A fair economy, decent schools, and a government that works for ordinary people instead of the well-connected. That’s what Democrats have fought for since the very beginning, and that’s the choice voters still have in front of them today.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 24560, \"fields\": {\"question\": 14440, \"description\": \"The president wants this election to be over quietly, without questions and without accountability. Well I don’t think the country should be run that way. I think the American people deserve straight answers, an end to secret wars and operations, and a government that tells the truth, even when it’s inconvenient. If you want change - real change - then take a leap of faith this Fall and give honesty a chance.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25105, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15027, \"description\": \"I stand here tonight not as a perfect man, but as an honest one. I’ve known failure in my own life, and I’ve had my share of regrets. But I’ve also known what it means to climb back up with hard work, faith, and true humility. That’s why I believe so deeply in redemption, not just for any one individual, but for this country too. America cannot afford any more political talking points or polished excuses. She needs a leadership that understands what it means to struggle and refuses to look away.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25106, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15027, \"description\": \"I’m a Democrat because I’ve seen what government can do when it chooses action over despair and compassion over indifference. I’ve seen it lift up working families, protect the most vulnerable, and give people a second chance when the deck is stacked against them. This party has always stood for dignity in work, fairness in opportunity, and a justice that reaches the heart. That is the great Democratic tradition I’m proud to carry forward.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25107, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15027, \"description\": \"The American people have been asked to accept too many half-truths and too many explanations. They have been told one thing while being shown another, time and time again. That credibility gap has eroded confidence in our government at home and abroad. This administration believes that secrecy is a virtue and that questioning authority is a vice. I do not. I believe honesty is the first obligation of leadership, and accountability is not an option. This campaign will offer a clear alternative to a politics that demands the American people to stop asking questions.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25108, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15027, \"description\": \"Twenty years ago, I was a very different man than the one you see here today. I was at my lowest point, and I was drowning. But I called out to God, and He saved my life that night. If there’s one thing my faith has taught me, it’s that peace is not weakness and conscience is not naïveté. I believe in this country because God believed in me. I believe we can still choose mercy over cruelty, honesty over fear, and hope over despair. I dream of an America where no one is written off as beyond saving and no leader forgets who they serve. That is the America I have spent my life fighting for, and that is the America I ask this party to believe in tonight.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25109, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15028, \"description\": \"I want this campaign to sound like it is thinking about the country ten or twenty years from now, not just about the next election. Americans are starting to understand that the air they breathe and the water they drink are not abstract concerns. They are part of everyday life. And there is someone who has been speaking about conservation and environmental responsibility long before it became fashionable. He would show voters that we are willing to look beyond the latest polls and talk about the future with some honesty for a change.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25110, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15028, \"description\": \"This war has shaken people’s faith in the judgment of their leaders, and we cannot pretend otherwise. I want a running mate who has been willing to ask hard questions about foreign policy and who speaks about America’s power with a sense of restraint and responsibility. Someone who has taken the time to study these decisions and has not been afraid to question them when they no longer made sense. That kind of thoughtfulness matters right now. Strength in leadership is not just about resolve, it’s about knowing the limits of force and being willing to say so.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25111, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15028, \"description\": \"This convention proved something very important. A large part of the party wants the country to rethink the direction it has taken in the world, especially when it comes to the war in ‘Nam. This didn’t come out of nowhere. It came from a campaign that drew real strength from young people and activists who aren’t interested in half measures, who want a clean break with what’s been done. I want someone whose views are clear and whose support reflects that energy. A running mate who reminds the country that leadership includes the courage to say when something has gone wrong.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25113, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15029, \"description\": \"What I’m seeing in this photograph is horrifying, and no one should pretend otherwise. But war is chaotic, and mistakes do happen, especially in a conflict that has dragged on this long and sprawled across borders. If the Pentagon says this was an unintended strike, then that claim deserves to be examined seriously. The real obligation now is to make sure incidents like this don’t happen again, and to press harder for an end to a war that keeps producing them.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25114, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15029, \"description\": \"Is there anything braver about burning up children who live north of the seventeenth parallel or who live in Cambodia or Laos? They all feel pain. They all shed the same blood. They're all children of the same God. I don’t know how anyone can look at that photograph and see anything but a terrified child in unbearable pain. These bombings are indefensible, and these are the kinds of conditions we have to recover if we’re going to save the soul of this nation.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25115, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15029, \"description\": \"This is not an isolated tragedy, and it is not an accident in any meaningful sense. It is the product of a war that should never have been fought and a policy that treats civilian lives as collateral damage. Richard Nixon and Henry Kissinger have expanded this war, hidden its costs, and lied about its consequences. Richard Nixon is a war criminal. This war must end immediately, and the men responsible for carrying it on must be held accountable to the fullest extent possible.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25116, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15029, \"description\": \"What troubles Americans most is the growing distance between what they see with their own eyes and what they are told by their government. Simply put, there is a clear credibility gap today that has undermined the legitimacy of our government. A democracy cannot sustain a long war without honesty from its leaders. If this administration cannot be candid about what is happening in Southeast Asia, then it has no business asking the American people to continue supporting this conflict.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25117, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15030, \"description\": \"This story falls apart the moment you look at my record. I’ve spent my career working within the party, writing legislation, building coalitions, and taking votes that actually hold up under scrutiny. I’m a liberal, yes, but a liberal who knows how to govern. I didn’t come here through some big protest or primary performance. I came here through the work, and I’m comfortable standing on it.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25118, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15030, \"description\": \"I don’t shy away from my ideals, and I never have. I believe the country needs to change course on the war, on civil rights, and on who gets a fair shake in this economy. If that makes some people uneasy, so be it. I wasn’t chosen to hide what I believe. I was chosen because those beliefs are shared by a growing part of this party and this country.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25119, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15030, \"description\": \"The country is in the middle of a generational shift, whether Washington is ready for it or not. Younger voters are demanding honesty, and older voters want a stability they can trust. I don’t see those instincts as enemies. I see them as two halves of the same problem, and my job is to bring them together without pretending either side is wrong to feel the way it does.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25120, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15030, \"description\": \"This is certainly a strange accusation coming from a president who reinvented himself overnight to chase a new constituency when it suited him. I’ve been saying the same things, casting the same votes, and standing in the same place for years. There’s no Trojan Horse here. What there is, is a campaign trying to distract from its own record by pretending that consistency is something to be afraid of.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25121, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15031, \"description\": \"I’m not going to pretend this is some new revelation. I did struggle with alcoholism, I won’t hide that. But by the Grace of the Lord I was saved from my despair. I’ve been sober for years, I’ve done the work, and I live with that discipline every day. That experience didn’t weaken me, it forced me to tell the truth to myself and to others. Voters can decide whether they want a man who hides his mistakes, or one who faced them and came out the other side with humility and resolve.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25122, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15031, \"description\": \"I won’t let this campaign turn into a referendum on my past instead of the country’s future. I’ve been open about my history with alcoholism, and my record as governor and senator speaks for itself. I’ve handled responsibility, pressure, and hard decisions for years now. If people want to judge me, they should judge me on how I’ve governed and what I’m fighting for, not on whispers from the Nixon campaign meant to distract from the real issues.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25123, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15031, \"description\": \"A lot of families in this country know what addiction looks like, even if they don’t talk about it out loud. I do. I lived it. And because of that, I understand their struggle in a way some politicians never will. My recovery taught me things. Accountability, patience, respect for the people who rely on you. I don’t claim to be perfect, but I know what it means to get back up and keep your word, and that’s what I bring to this job.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25124, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15031, \"description\": \"I find it rich that people close to this White House want to whisper about my past, when Richard Nixon has spent years leaning on the bottle and governing through secrecy, suspicion, and fear. I faced my problems in the open and dealt with it honestly. I didn’t hide behind my staff, nor did I create an enemies list, nor attack the media for all of my faults. If this campaign wants to talk about good character, then let’s talk about how a man governs when the doors are closed, not about what he has lived out in the light.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25125, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15032, \"description\": \"An unfortunate, but entirely expected development. All we can do is redouble our campaign efforts on the ground. I’ll visit every major city in the South, meet with local Democrats, and do as many rallies as I can. People need to see that we hear them and understand the challenges they face. Let’s see if we can’t light up some old loyalties the old-fashioned way.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25126, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15032, \"description\": \"John Connally is trying to use his connections to push Democrats into Nixon’s camp, but we have an ace up our sleeve here. It’s no secret that President Johnson and I have our disagreements, but there’s no way he’d ever endorse Nixon. I plan to go to President Johnson’s ranch, talk with him candidly, and ask him to publicly back our ticket. There’s no way Connally’s voice can compete with a heavyweight like Johnson.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25127, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15032, \"description\": \"Four years ago, Connally endorsed Hubert Humphrey in his campaign against Nixon. Today, he completely switches parties. You can’t possibly tell me this has anything to do with his convictions. I’ll make it clear that his endorsement isn’t a mark of wisdom or courage, it’s blatant opportunism, and it isn’t worth a damned nickel. Democrats deserve to know that the man who left his party to chase power in Washington doesn’t represent our values.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25128, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15032, \"description\": \"If John Connally wants to run this ‘Democrats for Nixon’ shtick, then fine. Two can play at that game. I’ll reach out to some former and even current Republicans who share our concerns. John Lindsay can take the lead, and see if we can’t get in touch with McCloskey and Reid. I hear Don Riegle might be switching over soon too. Nixon has spent the past four years pandering to the far right. Let’s show the voters just how many Republicans he’s chased away.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25129, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15033, \"description\": \"I understand why so many people are angry, and many of the concerns being raised in these protests are completely legitimate. The war, civil rights, and economic inequality have left a lot of Americans feeling shut out. But violence and destruction of property only weaken those causes and drag innocent people into the crossfire, and that’s not something I can support.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25130, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15033, \"description\": \"I’m not going to throw anyone under the bus, let alone so many patriotic Americans speaking out for what they feel is right. Peaceful protest is a constitutional right, and dissent is not the same thing as disorder. It is dangerous to treat protest itself as a threat to the country. President Nixon should be ashamed of himself for his brazen attempts to politicize our freedoms.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25131, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15033, \"description\": \"Look, a lot of these kids are my supporters. And I get it, they’re angry, and they want change. But ultimately, so many of these protests have gone too far, and they have done real damage to the credibility of the very causes these well-meaning young people claim to champion. If people want to change this country, they need to grow up and learn to work within the system instead of disrupting public life and turning voters against them.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25132, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15033, \"description\": \"I’m a proud Democrat, and I am no radical. Anyone who thinks I condone any kind of reckless law-breaking or unprovoked violence is mistaken. If a protest or march turns violent or disrupts the peace, then I support the protesters being punished to the extent the law calls for. To claim I think anything else is just another rumor started by Dick Nixon’s flying monkeys.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25133, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15034, \"description\": \"I’m not running against Spiro Agnew. I’m running against Richard Nixon and his record. The vice president seems to spend his days picking fights and throwing insults, and I don’t intend to join him. I’m going to keep talking about the war, the economy, and the direction of the country, and let voters decide who sounds like a president.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25134, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15034, \"description\": \"Agnew isn’t some independent actor. He says what Nixon wants to say, just louder and cruder. When he attacks protesters, the press, or whole groups of Americans, he’s doing it to distract from the failures of this administration. I’m going to remind people that his tone comes straight from the top.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25135, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15034, \"description\": \"The vice president is deliberately trying to pit Americans against each other. He mocks legitimate dissent, ridicules any disagreement, and treats whole parts of this country like mortal enemies. That might win applause at his campaign rallies, but it’s no way to govern a democracy, and it’s certainly no way to heal a country that’s already deeply divided.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25136, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15034, \"description\": \"I think most Americans are tired of being yelled at by their own government. They want a leadership that lowers the temperature, not raises it. I still believe that character counts in our country. I’m an honest, hard-working man of the prairie, not a lying attack dog who spends his free time golfing.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25137, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15035, \"description\": \"I’ve never said the country should ignore the scourge of addiction, and I’m not running to legalize drugs willy-nilly. What I have said is that addiction is an illness, not a moral failure, and pretending otherwise has filled our prisons without fixing the problem. That’s one part of my record, not the whole of it. We need to invest more in mental healthcare, especially for our decorated veterans. And we need to scrap this idea that you can treat a man’s illness by throwing him in prison.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25138, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15035, \"description\": \"I’m not interested in hearing lectures about law and order while the problem keeps getting worse. We cannot arrest our way out of addiction, and we cannot ignore the root causes either. Poverty, hunger, broken communities, and untreated mental illness all play a role, and pretending otherwise only keeps the cycle going. We should also be working with other countries to disrupt the drug trade where it begins, instead of focusing almost entirely on punishment here at home. That’s not permissiveness. That’s trying to actually solve the problem instead of reacting to it.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25139, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15035, \"description\": \"I think the American people should ask who really bears the brunt of this so called war on drugs. Too often, it falls hardest on newly enfranchised Black communities, young antiwar protesters, and Hispanic neighborhoods already struggling to be heard. That’s not justice, and it’s not effective policy either. I believe in safe streets and enforcing the law, but it has to be fair and it has to work. If we’re serious about law and order, then it cannot become a tool that punishes some communities while ignoring the actual problem.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25140, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15035, \"description\": \"It’s a little rich to be spoken of like some kind of druggie. President Nixon himself appointed the Shafer Commission to study drug policy, and that commission reached many of the same conclusions I’ve been talking about. Treatment for drug addiction works better than punishment alone. I didn’t invent that idea, and I didn’t get it from some fringe group. It came from experts that Nixon chose, chaired by a Nixon ally. If supporting their findings is radical, then the White House ought to explain why it asked the question in the first place.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25141, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15036, \"description\": \"What happened in Munich was a tragedy, plain and simple. Innocent athletes went to represent their countries in peace, and they were murdered instead. Tonight, my thoughts are with the families of the Israeli team and with the German people who tried to protect their guests. Acts like this offend the conscience of all nations, and they deserve our shared grief and resolve to see that such crimes are never repeated.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25142, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15036, \"description\": \"The murder of Israeli athletes in Munich was an act of terror against innocent people and against the very idea of peaceful competition. Israel has the right to exist, the right to security, and the right to see its citizens protected from violence wherever they go. The United States must stand firmly with Israel in condemning this attack and in opposing terrorism in all its forms. There is no political cause that justifies the killing of civilians, ever.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25143, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15036, \"description\": \"What happened in Munich is a grim reminder that violence isn’t confined by borders, or uniforms, or battlefields. It threatens civilians, the international order, and the basic sense of safety people expect in the modern world. The United States must work with its allies to strengthen international cooperation against terrorism, improve intelligence sharing, and ensure that people are safe wherever they go.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25145, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15037, \"description\": \"If we reach out to Wallace, we have to keep the focus on the economic concerns that drove much of his support. Talk about wages, inflation, and the sense that working people have been squeezed while Washington argues over everything else. Make the case that this campaign is serious about those issues and isn’t just speaking to one part of the country. If he sees that we’re addressing the same frustrations his voters feel, he may not endorse us outright, but he could soften his tone and speak more favorably about the campaign.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25146, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15037, \"description\": \"If we reach out to Wallace, we need to ensure he feels respected and included. His supporters feel dismissed by both parties, and that resentment runs deep. Make it clear that we’re not writing anyone off and that there’s room in this campaign for voters who don’t feel at home anywhere else right now. This isn’t about agreeing on everything, it’s about acknowledging that their concerns deserve to be heard. If Wallace believes we’re serious about that, he might be willing to say something positive publicly, even if he stops short of endorsing us.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25147, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15037, \"description\": \"If we reach out to Wallace, we ought to talk to him about the broader need to settle things down politically. The country’s been through years of tension, and there’s an argument to be made for lowering the temperature and trying to bring people back into a shared political space. We can frame our campaign as an attempt to move past the constant division and give voters a sense that things don’t have to stay this polarized. It’s a harder sell, and he may push back, but it at least opens the door to a serious conversation about where the country goes from here.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25148, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15037, \"description\": \"No, we’re not going to seek George Wallace’s endorsement. His record and the kind of politics he’s built his career on are fundamentally at odds with what this campaign is trying to do, especially on civil rights and the broader direction of the party. Going after his backing would raise real questions about where we stand and risk undercutting the coalition we’ve spent months trying to build. If Wallace chooses to stay out of the race, that’s his decision, but we’re not going to invite him in.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25149, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15038, \"description\": \"If Secretary Kissinger’s statement proves true, then that is welcome news for every American family and every Vietnamese family that has paid the price for this war. I hope the administration is finally prepared to bring this conflict to an honorable conclusion, and I will judge their actions by whether the fighting actually stops and our troops truly come home.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25150, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15038, \"description\": \"We have been told for four years now that peace is just around the corner. And in that time, the president has invaded two other countries, authorized civilian bombing campaigns, and has continued to prop up a corrupt dictatorship abroad. I want this war to end as much as anyone, but I’m not going to take these sudden assurances at face value. I will believe peace is at hand when the war is actually over.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25151, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15038, \"description\": \"What the administration is now describing sounds very much like the settlement President Johnson was prepared to accept years ago. The difference is that since then, thousands more Americans and countless Vietnamese have died. If this is where we were always headed, then the real question is why this war was prolonged for so long to begin with.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25152, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15038, \"description\": \"Peace is not something you can announce at a press conference while we still have planes dropping bombs and soldiers knee deep in the Vietnamese mud. Peace means ending the war, bringing our troops home, and stopping the killing now. Anything less is not peace. This war has gone on long enough.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25153, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15039, \"description\": \"These are serious developments, and they deserve to be handled with utmost care. The facts need to be gathered, verified, and examined outside of partisan politics - I fully support Mr. Cronkite’s investigative work on the matter. And I’m calling for a full, independent congressional investigation so the public can know exactly what happened and who was responsible. The rule of law has to speak for itself.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25154, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15039, \"description\": \"When Americans watch a story like this unfold, they don’t just wonder about the kinds of things being done behind closed doors. They ask what kind of country we’re becoming. A democracy can’t survive on this level of secrecy, of paranoia, of political sabotage. We need honesty and integrity back in our public life. We need leaders who believe that winning an election is not worth losing the nation’s conscience.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25155, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15039, \"description\": \"This is a lifeline for our campaign, and it’s all the more reason we have to be careful here. There’s a criminal investigation underway, and that’s where this belongs. If the President says he had no involvement with the break-in this summer, that statement should be taken seriously unless proven otherwise. We shouldn’t rush to judgment or turn every accusation into a political weapon. Let the authorities do their work.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25156, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15039, \"description\": \"This isn’t your everyday dirty campaign trick, Mr. Cronkite is making that increasingly clear. If the White House is involved in breaking into the opposition party’s headquarters, that’s an abuse of power, plain and simple. No president is above the law. If these reports are true, Richard Nixon has no business being a free man, let alone occupying the Oval Office.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25157, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15040, \"description\": \"This election doesn’t have to be about anger or fear or division. It can be about healing a country that’s been pulled apart by war and distrust. I’m asking Americans to look past the noise of the last few years and decide whether we want more chaos, or whether we want to start bringing people back together again, honestly and without illusion.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25158, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15040, \"description\": \"Every day this war continues, more young Americans die for reasons no one can clearly explain anymore. No late promises and no last-minute announcements change that fact. If you believe, as I do, that this war has gone on long enough and that it’s time to bring our sons home, then you don’t have to sacrifice your principles anymore.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25159, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15040, \"description\": \"I’ve spent this campaign talking about the dignity of work, about fair wages, and about economic justice because that’s what most families worry about when the cameras are gone. A fair economy, decent schools, and a government that works for ordinary people instead of the well-connected. That’s what Democrats have fought for since the very beginning, and that’s the choice voters still have in front of them today.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25160, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15040, \"description\": \"The president wants this election to be over quietly, without questions and without accountability. Well I don’t think the country should be run that way. I think the American people deserve straight answers, an end to secret wars and operations, and a government that tells the truth, even when it’s inconvenient. If you want change - real change - then take a leap of faith this Fall and give honesty a chance.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25705, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15627, \"description\": \"I didn’t come to this moment by talking around the hard parts of American life. I came here by talking about them directly. I have spent my time in politics listening to people who work hard and still fall behind, who live far from the centers of power, and who are tired of being told that their struggles are a fact of life. Poverty is something we have chosen to tolerate. And if this party is going to stand for anything again, it has to stand with the people who have been pushed to the margins and told to live with it.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25706, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15627, \"description\": \"For generations, our party has spoken for people who were told the system was not built for them. From the farmers and workers Franklin Roosevelt fought for during the Depression, to the safety and security that Harry Truman dreamed of, to the promise of opportunity under John Kennedy and the unfinished work Lyndon Johnson began on poverty and justice. At our best, Democrats have chosen action over resignation and fairness over favoritism. I am proud to stand in that tradition tonight, and I accept the responsibility to help lead this party back to the people it was meant to serve.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25707, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15627, \"description\": \"The American people have been asked to swallow too many half-truths from people who think their power excuses anything. They have been told that the books are balanced, the war is winding down, and the system is working just fine. But wages are falling, farms are disappearing, and young men keep getting shipped overseas. That credibility gap has done real damage to our trust in government, here at home and around the world. This administration treats secrecy like a shield and expects working people to stop asking the hard questions. I do not. I believe honesty is the first duty of leadership, and accountability belongs to the people.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25708, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15627, \"description\": \"I grew up knowing what it means to rely on your neighbors and to measure people by their character. I have seen how dignity can survive even when opportunity does not, and how badly people want to believe that their government still sees them. I believe in an America where no one is written off because of where they live, how much they make, or who they are. If we are willing to listen to one another and to govern with true fairness, then I believe this country can still live up to its responsibilities.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25709, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15628, \"description\": \"The way I see it, this ticket needs steadiness. The convention showed how many different voices exist in this party, and the only way we win in November is by keeping those voices talking to each other instead of drifting apart again. I want a partner who understands labor, civil rights, and the legislative process well enough to keep a coalition together when the pressure builds. Someone serious, disciplined, and trusted inside the party. Not a showman. Someone who knows how the work actually gets done.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25710, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15628, \"description\": \"I want this campaign to sound like it is thinking about the country ten or twenty years from now, not just about the next election. Americans are starting to understand that the air they breathe and the water they drink are not abstract concerns. They are part of everyday life. And there is someone who has been speaking about conservation and environmental responsibility long before it became fashionable. He would show voters that we are willing to look beyond the latest polls and talk about the future with some honesty for a change.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25711, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15628, \"description\": \"This convention proved something very important. A large part of the party wants the country to rethink the direction it has taken in the world, especially when it comes to the war in ‘Nam. This didn’t come out of nowhere. It came from a campaign that drew real strength from young people and activists who aren’t interested in half measures, who want a clean break with what’s been done. I want someone whose views are clear and whose support reflects that energy. A running mate who reminds the country that leadership includes the courage to say when something has gone wrong.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25713, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15629, \"description\": \"What I see in that photograph is awful, and nobody should try to smooth it over. But this war has gone on so long, and been stretched so far, that it keeps putting ordinary people in impossible situations. If the Pentagon says this strike was unintended, then that claim needs to be examined carefully and openly, not brushed aside. The real question is why we are still in a war where these kinds of horrors keep happening, and why working families are asked to live with the consequences of decisions made far away from them.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25714, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15629, \"description\": \"There is nothing brave about burning children, whether they live north of the seventeenth parallel or in Cambodia or Laos. Pain does not stop at any border. Those children feel it just the same as ours would. I do not know how any decent person can look at that photograph and talk about abstractions or strategy. This war has stripped away our common humanity, and if we want to call ourselves a moral nation, we have to stop pretending that this suffering is the cost of doing business.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25715, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15629, \"description\": \"This was not a mistake in any honest sense of the word. It is the foreseeable result of a war that has been expanded in secret and justified with half-truths. The people who suffer are civilians overseas and working families here at home, while the men who made these decisions sit insulated in Washington. Richard Nixon and Henry Kissinger escalated this war, concealed its scope, and misled the public about its costs. If the law means anything, then they should be held to account. At a minimum, that means resignations. And if the facts warrant it, it means impeachment and criminal proceedings. No one is above the law.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25716, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15629, \"description\": \"What troubles people most is the credibility gap between what they are seeing with their own eyes and what they are being told by their government. Americans are not fools. They know when the truth is being shaded or withheld. A democracy cannot fight this kind of war when its leaders are not honest about what is happening or why. If this administration cannot close that credibility gap and speak plainly about the reality in Southeast Asia, then it has no business asking the American people to keep paying the price.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25717, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15630, \"description\": \"I’ve heard this argument my whole life, and it always comes from people who confuse age with good judgment. I’ve served eight years in the Senate, not warming a seat for the party bosses, but fighting over the budgets, farm price supports, wages, and a war that keeps taking innocent sons out of small towns like the ones I grew up in. Leadership is not about how long you’ve been in Washington. It’s about whether you are willing to make the hard calls and live with them. I’ve done that, and I’ll keep doing it.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25718, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15630, \"description\": \"The President has his ticker-tape parades and fancy rallies and a whole machine built around him. What I have is a record of standing up for the forgotten people who don’t get parades thrown for them when the factory closes or when their sons come home in a casket. This country doesn’t need another four years of Nixon’s “experience.” It needs someone who understands what inflation feels like at the grocery store and what a draft notice does to a family. What I lack in experience I make up for in integrity, and what Dick Nixon lacks in integrity, he could never make up for with experience.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25719, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15630, \"description\": \"Every generation eventually has to take responsibility for the country it inherits. That doesn’t mean disrespecting those who came before us, and it sure doesn’t mean pretending that experience doesn’t count. It means recognizing that the problems we’re facing now were created over the span of decades, they won’t be solved by simply doing the same things again and again. I am ready to lead because I am willing to listen, willing to learn, and willing to challenge the age-old habits that no longer serve this country.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25720, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15630, \"description\": \"I find it a little rich to be lectured about my supposed inexperience by Dick Nixon of all people. When he was plucked for the vice presidency, he had barely six years in Congress and was a one-trick pony who covered for the likes of Joe McCarthy. Then he spent eight years out of office as his party’s grand loser before weaseling his way back and begging the country to trust him. We all can see how that turned out. But I am here, I am working for the people, and I have stood my ground for the fights this country has faced these past few years.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25721, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15631, \"description\": \"People in the Southwest don’t need me to explain who I am. They already know. I have never been a rubber stamp for any party, and I am not about to start now. I’ve broken with Democrats when they lost their way, and I’ve taken heat for it more than once. That independence is not something I put on for a campaign. It’s how I’ve always done the job. Voters out here can trust that when I speak, it is my own voice, not something handed down from a national committee.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25722, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15631, \"description\": \"The divide I see out here is not left versus right, or urban versus rural, or black versus white. It is the people who work for a living versus the people who write the rules in their favor. That is what I talk about, because that is what people feel every day. Farmers, oil workers, small business owners, and clerks all know the system is tilted toward the very top. I am running to reset that balance. Not with empty slogans, but by naming who is winning, who is losing, and who has been ignored for far too long.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25723, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15631, \"description\": \"I don’t lecture people about how they ought to live or talk down to them like they’re a problem to be fixed. Folks in rural America have their own values, their own pride, and their own sense of fairness. I respect that. You can stand for civil rights, fair wages, and peace in Vietnam without sneering at people who live differently from the fancy suburbs on the coasts. I’m not asking anyone to change who they are. I’m asking them to take a hard look at who has their back and who doesn’t.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25724, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15631, \"description\": \"There is a new coalition rising across the south, and it deserves to be heard. Black voters, Hispanic communities, Native nations, all sorts of working people who have been pushed to the margins for generations. These patriotic Americans know what it means to be left out of power, and they are done waiting their turn. I am going to speak directly to them, organize with them, and fight alongside them. That is not a gamble. That is the future of our nation, and I am not afraid to say so out loud.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25725, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15632, \"description\": \"An unfortunate, but entirely expected development. All we can do is redouble our campaign efforts on the ground. I’ll visit every major city in the South, meet with local Democrats, and do as many rallies as I can. People need to see that we hear them and understand the challenges they face. Let’s see if we can’t light up some old loyalties the old-fashioned way.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25726, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15632, \"description\": \"John Connally is trying to use his connections to push Democrats into Nixon’s camp, but we have an ace up our sleeve here. It’s no secret that President Johnson and I have our disagreements, but there’s no way he’d ever endorse Nixon. I plan to go to President Johnson’s ranch, talk with him candidly, and ask him to publicly back our ticket. There’s no way Connally’s voice can compete with a heavyweight like Johnson.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25727, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15632, \"description\": \"Four years ago, Connally endorsed Hubert Humphrey in his campaign against Nixon. Today, he completely switches parties. You can’t possibly tell me this has anything to do with his convictions. I’ll make it clear that his endorsement isn’t a mark of wisdom or courage, it’s blatant opportunism, and it isn’t worth a damned nickel. Democrats deserve to know that the man who left his party to chase power in Washington doesn’t represent our values.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25728, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15632, \"description\": \"If John Connally wants to run this ‘Democrats for Nixon’ shtick, then fine. Two can play at that game. I’ll reach out to some former and even current Republicans who share our concerns. John Lindsay can take the lead, and see if we can’t get in touch with McCloskey and Reid. I hear Don Riegle might be switching over soon too. Nixon has spent the past four years pandering to the far right. Let’s show the voters just how many Republicans he’s chased away.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25729, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15633, \"description\": \"I understand why so many people are angry, and many of the concerns being raised in these protests are completely legitimate. The war, civil rights, and economic inequality have left a lot of Americans feeling shut out. But violence and destruction of property only weaken those causes and drag innocent people into the crossfire, and that’s not something I can support.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25730, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15633, \"description\": \"I’m not going to throw anyone under the bus, let alone so many patriotic Americans speaking out for what they feel is right. Peaceful protest is a constitutional right, and dissent is not the same thing as disorder. It is dangerous to treat protest itself as a threat to the country. President Nixon should be ashamed of himself for his brazen attempts to politicize our freedoms.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25731, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15633, \"description\": \"Look, a lot of these kids are my supporters. And I get it, they’re angry, and they want change. But ultimately, so many of these protests have gone too far, and they have done real damage to the credibility of the very causes these well-meaning young people claim to champion. If people want to change this country, they need to grow up and learn to work within the system instead of disrupting public life and turning voters against them.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25732, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15633, \"description\": \"I’m a proud Democrat, and I am no radical. Anyone who thinks I condone any kind of reckless law-breaking or unprovoked violence is mistaken. If a protest or march turns violent or disrupts the peace, then I support the protesters being punished to the extent the law calls for. To claim I think anything else is just another rumor started by Dick Nixon’s flying monkeys.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25733, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15634, \"description\": \"I’m not running against Spiro Agnew. I’m running against Richard Nixon and his record. The vice president seems to spend his days picking fights and throwing insults, and I don’t intend to join him. I’m going to keep talking about the war, the economy, and the direction of the country, and let voters decide who sounds like a president.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25734, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15634, \"description\": \"Agnew isn’t some independent actor. He says what Nixon wants to say, just louder and cruder. When he attacks protesters, the press, or whole groups of Americans, he’s doing it to distract from the failures of this administration. I’m going to remind people that his tone comes straight from the top.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25735, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15634, \"description\": \"The vice president is deliberately trying to pit Americans against each other. He mocks legitimate dissent, ridicules any disagreement, and treats whole parts of this country like mortal enemies. That might win applause at his campaign rallies, but it’s no way to govern a democracy, and it’s certainly no way to heal a country that’s already deeply divided.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25736, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15634, \"description\": \"I think most Americans are tired of being yelled at by their own government. They want a leadership that lowers the temperature, not raises it. I still believe that character counts in our country. I’m an honest, hard-working man of the prairie, not a lying attack dog who spends his free time golfing.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25737, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15635, \"description\": \"I am not going to apologize for telling the truth about racism in this country. The Kerner Commission didn’t invent the problems that plague us, and we didn’t write that report to make anyone feel guilty. We wrote it because pretending everything is fine was already tearing this country apart. The protests, the riots, the generations of hatred and oppression and exhaustion. Ignoring the facts might feel easier in the short term, but it only guarantees that the bill comes due later, and it always lands on working people first.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25738, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15635, \"description\": \"What the Kerner Commission said was not just about race, it was about how inequality piles up and who pays the price for it. When neighborhoods are starved of good jobs, good schools, and financial investment, the fallout doesn’t stop at the city limits and it doesn’t just hit folks with a different skin color. It shows up in higher taxes, higher crime, and a weaker economy for everyone. This is not about blaming anyone. It’s about fixing a system that has failed all of us in different ways.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25739, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15635, \"description\": \"I understand why some people who read the Kerner Report feel like they are being talked about instead of being talked to. That was never the intention. Most Americans are doing the best they can, raising families, paying bills, and trying to get by. The commission wasn’t pointing fingers at ordinary people, I promise everyone that. It was calling out years of bad decisions from the top that left whole communities without a fair shot, and asking the country to finally face that reality together.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25740, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15635, \"description\": \"What bothers Dick Nixon and Spiro Agnew is not that the Kerner Commission was wrong, but that it refused to sugarcoat the consequences of their own style of politics. They would rather shout about the cynicism and division that’s plagued our country these past few years than deal with the conditions that have produced it. It’s obvious why. It's easier to do that than to invest in good jobs, in affordable housing, in good schools, in communities. I’m not interested in pretending the racial divisions of our country don’t exist just to spare the administration some discomfort. Real leadership means facing the hard truths when we need to, not yelling at the messenger.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25741, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15636, \"description\": \"What happened in Munich was a tragedy, plain and simple. Innocent athletes went to represent their countries in peace, and they were murdered instead. Tonight, my thoughts are with the families of the Israeli team and with the German people who tried to protect their guests. Acts like this offend the conscience of all nations, and they deserve our shared grief and resolve to see that such crimes are never repeated.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25742, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15636, \"description\": \"The murder of Israeli athletes in Munich was an act of terror against innocent people and against the very idea of peaceful competition. Israel has the right to exist, the right to security, and the right to see its citizens protected from violence wherever they go. The United States must stand firmly with Israel in condemning this attack and in opposing terrorism in all its forms. There is no political cause that justifies the killing of civilians, ever.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25743, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15636, \"description\": \"What happened in Munich is a grim reminder that violence isn’t confined by borders, or uniforms, or battlefields. It threatens civilians, the international order, and the basic sense of safety people expect in the modern world. The United States must work with its allies to strengthen international cooperation against terrorism, improve intelligence sharing, and ensure that people are safe wherever they go.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25745, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15637, \"description\": \"If we reach out to Wallace, we have to keep the focus on the economic concerns that drove much of his support. Talk about wages, inflation, and the sense that working people have been squeezed while Washington argues over everything else. Make the case that this campaign is serious about those issues and isn’t just speaking to one part of the country. If he sees that we’re addressing the same frustrations his voters feel, he may not endorse us outright, but he could soften his tone and speak more favorably about the campaign.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25746, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15637, \"description\": \"If we reach out to Wallace, we need to ensure he feels respected and included. His supporters feel dismissed by both parties, and that resentment runs deep. Make it clear that we’re not writing anyone off and that there’s room in this campaign for voters who don’t feel at home anywhere else right now. This isn’t about agreeing on everything, it’s about acknowledging that their concerns deserve to be heard. If Wallace believes we’re serious about that, he might be willing to say something positive publicly, even if he stops short of endorsing us.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25747, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15637, \"description\": \"If we reach out to Wallace, we ought to talk to him about the broader need to settle things down politically. The country’s been through years of tension, and there’s an argument to be made for lowering the temperature and trying to bring people back into a shared political space. We can frame our campaign as an attempt to move past the constant division and give voters a sense that things don’t have to stay this polarized. It’s a harder sell, and he may push back, but it at least opens the door to a serious conversation about where the country goes from here.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25748, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15637, \"description\": \"No, we’re not going to seek George Wallace’s endorsement. His record and the kind of politics he’s built his career on are fundamentally at odds with what this campaign is trying to do, especially on civil rights and the broader direction of the party. Going after his backing would raise real questions about where we stand and risk undercutting the coalition we’ve spent months trying to build. If Wallace chooses to stay out of the race, that’s his decision, but we’re not going to invite him in.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25749, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15638, \"description\": \"If Secretary Kissinger’s statement proves true, then that is welcome news for every American family and every Vietnamese family that has paid the price for this war. I hope the administration is finally prepared to bring this conflict to an honorable conclusion, and I will judge their actions by whether the fighting actually stops and our troops truly come home.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25750, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15638, \"description\": \"We have been told for four years now that peace is just around the corner. And in that time, the president has invaded two other countries, authorized civilian bombing campaigns, and has continued to prop up a corrupt dictatorship abroad. I want this war to end as much as anyone, but I’m not going to take these sudden assurances at face value. I will believe peace is at hand when the war is actually over.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25751, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15638, \"description\": \"What the administration is now describing sounds very much like the settlement President Johnson was prepared to accept years ago. The difference is that since then, thousands more Americans and countless Vietnamese have died. If this is where we were always headed, then the real question is why this war was prolonged for so long to begin with.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25752, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15638, \"description\": \"Peace is not something you can announce at a press conference while we still have planes dropping bombs and soldiers knee deep in the Vietnamese mud. Peace means ending the war, bringing our troops home, and stopping the killing now. Anything less is not peace. This war has gone on long enough.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25753, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15639, \"description\": \"These are serious developments, and they deserve to be handled with utmost care. The facts need to be gathered, verified, and examined outside of partisan politics - I fully support Mr. Cronkite’s investigative work on the matter. And I’m calling for a full, independent congressional investigation so the public can know exactly what happened and who was responsible. The rule of law has to speak for itself.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25754, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15639, \"description\": \"When Americans watch a story like this unfold, they don’t just wonder about the kinds of things being done behind closed doors. They ask what kind of country we’re becoming. A democracy can’t survive on this level of secrecy, of paranoia, of political sabotage. We need honesty and integrity back in our public life. We need leaders who believe that winning an election is not worth losing the nation’s conscience.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25755, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15639, \"description\": \"This is a lifeline for our campaign, and it’s all the more reason we have to be careful here. There’s a criminal investigation underway, and that’s where this belongs. If the President says he had no involvement with the break-in this summer, that statement should be taken seriously unless proven otherwise. We shouldn’t rush to judgment or turn every accusation into a political weapon. Let the authorities do their work.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25756, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15639, \"description\": \"This isn’t your everyday dirty campaign trick, Mr. Cronkite is making that increasingly clear. If the White House is involved in breaking into the opposition party’s headquarters, that’s an abuse of power, plain and simple. No president is above the law. If these reports are true, Richard Nixon has no business being a free man, let alone occupying the Oval Office.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25757, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15640, \"description\": \"This election doesn’t have to be about fear, anger, or the usual Washington squabbles. It can be about bringing a country back together after years of war, broken promises, and leaders who look out for the powerful while the rest of us pay the price. I’m asking Americans to see through the noise and decide whether we want more chaos, or whether we want a government that works for ordinary people, honestly and without spin.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25758, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15640, \"description\": \"Every day this war drags on, more young Americans die for reasons no one can clearly explain anymore. No last-minute speeches or photo ops change that fact. If you believe, as I do, that this bloodshed has gone on far too long and it’s time to bring our sons home, then you don’t have to compromise your principles to support real leadership. I won’t ask anyone to choose between their country and their conscience.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25759, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15640, \"description\": \"I’ve spent this campaign talking about the dignity of work, about fair wages, and about economic justice, because that’s what most families worry about when the cameras are gone. The same system that lets the well-connected thrive leaves everyone else struggling. A fair economy, strong schools, and a government that fights for ordinary Americans instead of special interests - those are the causes in front of voters today.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 25760, \"fields\": {\"question\": 15640, \"description\": \"The president wants this election to pass quietly, without questions, without accountability, and without consequences. I don’t think this country should be run that way. The American people deserve straight answers, an end to secret wars and deceptive schemes, and a government that tells the truth even when it’s inconvenient. If you want real change this Fall, you don’t have to settle. Demand honesty from your government, and fight back against those who put their own power above their conscience.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26305, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16227, \"description\": \"I come here from a part of the country that has learned some hard lessons the long way these past few years. I have seen what happens when fear is allowed to stand in for leadership and when investing in people’s lives is treated as charity instead of common sense. I believe the future of this country depends on whether we are willing to support our children, strengthen our communities, and give people a fair shot no matter who they are or where they were born. That is not a radical idea. It is how a nation renews itself, and it is the work I want this party to lead again come this November.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26306, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16227, \"description\": \"For generations, this party has carried the hopes and dreams of ordinary Americans, from the depression years of Franklin Roosevelt, to the courage and conviction of Harry Truman, through the promise of John Kennedy and the hard work of Lyndon Johnson. In times of trial and uncertainty, Democrats have always chosen action over despair and compassion over indifference. I am proud to stand in that tradition tonight, and I accept the responsibility to help lead our country out of the confusion and turmoil of these past few years, toward a future rooted in fairness, opportunity, and faith in one another.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26307, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16227, \"description\": \"The American people have been asked to accept too many half-truths and too many explanations. They have been told one thing while being shown another, time and time again. That credibility gap has eroded confidence in our government at home and abroad. This administration believes that secrecy is a virtue and that questioning authority is a vice. I do not. I believe honesty is the first obligation of leadership, and accountability is not an option. This campaign will offer a clear alternative to a politics that demands the American people to stop asking questions.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26308, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16227, \"description\": \"I believe deeply in the decency of this country. I have seen it in classrooms filled with kids who were never expected to succeed and in communities that chose to move forward instead of tearing themselves apart. I believe our politics can reflect that same decency if we ask more of ourselves and less of where we stand apart. I am running because I want my children and yours to inherit a country that invests in their potential and treats their future as something worth protecting. That is the work ahead of us, and I am ready to take it on with you this Fall.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26309, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16228, \"description\": \"I think what voters want right now more than anything is someone with good judgment. Someone who takes the time to understand a problem before speaking on it, who weighs the consequences instead of rushing to a headline, and who carries himself with a certain discipline in public life. I want a running mate known for that kind of steadiness, who has spent years working through difficult issues in detail and has earned respect for it. There’s a difference between sounding thoughtful and actually doing the work, and I think people can tell which is which.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26310, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16228, \"description\": \"The way I see it, this ticket needs steadiness. The convention showed how many different voices exist in this party, and the only way we win in November is by keeping those voices talking to each other instead of drifting apart again. I want a partner who understands labor, civil rights, and the legislative process well enough to keep a coalition together when the pressure builds. Someone serious, disciplined, and trusted inside the party. Not a showman. Someone who knows how the work actually gets done.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26311, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16228, \"description\": \"This moment calls for seriousness. The country has watched too many politicians treat government like a stage for clever speeches. I want a running mate who approaches public office like a responsibility that has to be studied and respected. Someone who has spent years thinking carefully about the Constitution, how government is structured, and the way laws actually shape people’s lives. That kind of temperament would show voters that this ticket intends to govern thoughtfully and with purpose.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26313, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16229, \"description\": \"The images coming out of Southeast Asia are deeply troubling, and any loss of innocent life weighs heavily on the conscience of this country. War is never clean, and tragic errors can occur even when our forces are acting in good faith. If military authorities say this incident was unintended, that claim deserves serious consideration. Our responsibility is to reduce civilian harm, improve oversight, and continue the difficult work of bringing this conflict to a responsible conclusion.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26314, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16229, \"description\": \"No American can look at photographs of burned villages and wounded children without feeling shaken. These are human beings caught in the middle of forces far beyond their control. That moral reality can’t be brushed aside with technical explanations alone. If we are to justify this war to ourselves and to the world, we must show that civilian lives matter, and that our conduct reflects the values we claim to defend.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26315, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16229, \"description\": \"This was not some unfortunate accident. It is the result of a war that has been stretched and escalated long past the point where it makes sense, with too little regard for the people caught in the middle. The American people have not been given the full truth about how this war is being fought or what it is costing in human lives. President Nixon promised to wind this war down, yet it keeps spreading and the facts keep coming out after the fact. If these decisions mean the President has crossed the line into war crimes, then Congress has a duty to say so plainly and act on it. This war needs to end, and the law needs to apply to everyone, even the President of the United States.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26316, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16229, \"description\": \"What troubles Americans most is the growing distance between what they see with their own eyes and what they are told by their government. Simply put, there is a clear credibility gap today that has undermined the legitimacy of our government. A democracy cannot sustain a long war without honesty from its leaders. If this administration cannot be candid about what is happening in Southeast Asia, then it has no business asking the American people to continue supporting this conflict.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26317, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16230, \"description\": \"I’ve heard this concern many times, and it usually comes from people who mistake years in office for true judgment. I served a full term as governor, not to build a resume, but to support our schools, our hospitals, our communities that too often get ignored. Leadership is not about counting years in office. It’s about making the hard decisions and standing by them. I’ve done that in North Carolina, and I will do it for the nation.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26318, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16230, \"description\": \"President Nixon can have his parades and pageantry. What I bring is a record of working for people who never get a ribbon or a headline when their towns struggle or their families lose someone to a war they didn’t start. This country doesn’t need more political showmanship. It needs someone who understands what real families face day to day. What I may lack in experience, I make up for in integrity. But what Mr. Nixon lacks in integrity, he could damn never make up for in experience.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26319, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16230, \"description\": \"Every era needs leaders who are ready to take responsibility for the challenges at hand. That doesn’t mean disrespecting those who came before, and it doesn’t mean ignoring the value of experience. It means seeing clearly the problems that have built up over decades and being willing to do what is necessary to address them. I am ready to lead because I will listen, learn, and challenge the policies and habits that no longer serve our country.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26320, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16230, \"description\": \"It’s a little rich to hear concerns about my experience from a man who, when he rose to national office, had barely six years in Congress, half of which he spent looking for communists in the janitor’s closet. And after being rejected by the country and even his own state, he returned with a whole machine built around him. I’m no career politician. I’ve spent my career working for the people, facing the real problems of our communities, and standing by those fights. That is the kind of leadership this country can count on now.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26321, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16231, \"description\": \"I know voters in the South have seen plenty of campaigns come and go. I’m going to earn their trust the way I’ve always tried to in North Carolina: by listening and working alongside people, not lecturing them. That means more town halls, more visits to ordinary communities, and real conversations with teachers, farmers, and local leaders. Trust isn’t given willy-nilly, it takes time to build, and I’m ready to put in the work to prove we can govern responsibly, with everyone’s interests at heart.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26322, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16231, \"description\": \"This campaign is about steady, responsible leadership. I’m not running to settle every internal debate in the party, and I won’t let the loudest, most extreme voices define our message. Farmers, small business owners, and suburban families need to know they can rely on me to govern with common sense. I’ll always stand by my principles, but I also understand the need to unite a divided country and earn confidence from all sides.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26323, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16231, \"description\": \"People in North Carolina and across the South know me, and they know I respect the traditions that hold communities together. The family, the church, the sense of order that keeps these towns safe and secure, I get it. I’m not abandoning progress, but I will make sure that change comes thoughtfully and respects the lives and values people have built here. You can trust me to govern in a way that listens to both common sense and principle, and not let any outside pressures from Washington dictate what happens in our communities.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26324, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16231, \"description\": \"I don’t expect to solve every worry myself. But this campaign can show the good people of the South that their voices still matter. I plan to involve respected Southern leaders like Governors Jimmy Carter, and Reuben Askew, and so many others in listening sessions with communities across the region. That way, Southern Democrats see that our party values their input, respects their concerns, and includes them in shaping our vision for the future.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26325, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16232, \"description\": \"An unfortunate, but entirely expected development. All we can do is redouble our campaign efforts on the ground. I’ll visit every major city in the South, meet with local Democrats, and do as many rallies as I can. People need to see that we hear them and understand the challenges they face. Let’s see if we can’t light up some old loyalties the old-fashioned way.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26326, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16232, \"description\": \"John Connally is trying to use his connections to push Democrats into Nixon’s camp, but we have an ace up our sleeve here. It’s no secret that President Johnson and I have our disagreements, but there’s no way he’d ever endorse Nixon. I plan to go to President Johnson’s ranch, talk with him candidly, and ask him to publicly back our ticket. There’s no way Connally’s voice can compete with a heavyweight like Johnson.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26327, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16232, \"description\": \"Four years ago, Connally endorsed Hubert Humphrey against Dick Nixon. Today, he completely switches parties. You can’t possibly tell me this has anything to do with his convictions. I’ll make it clear that his endorsement isn’t a mark of wisdom or courage, it’s blatant opportunism, and it isn’t worth a damned nickel. Democrats deserve to know that the man who left his party to chase power in Washington doesn’t represent our values.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26328, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16232, \"description\": \"If John Connally wants to run this ‘Democrats for Nixon’ shtick, then fine. Two can play at that game. I’ll reach out to some former and even current Republicans who share our concerns. John Lindsay can take the lead, and see if we can’t get in touch with McCloskey and Reid. I hear Don Riegle might be switching over soon too. Nixon has spent the past four years pandering to the far right. Let’s show the voters just how many Republicans he’s chased away.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26329, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16233, \"description\": \"I understand why so many people are angry, and many of the concerns being raised in these protests are completely legitimate. The war, civil rights, and economic inequality have left a lot of Americans feeling shut out. But violence and destruction of property only weaken those causes and drag innocent people into the crossfire, and that’s not something I can support.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26330, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16233, \"description\": \"I’m not going to throw anyone under the bus, let alone so many patriotic Americans speaking out for what they feel is right. Peaceful protest is a constitutional right, and dissent is not the same thing as disorder. It is dangerous to treat protest itself as a threat to the country. President Nixon should be ashamed of himself for his brazen attempts to politicize our freedoms.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26331, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16233, \"description\": \"Look, a lot of these folks supported Mr. McGovern this year. They’re young, they’re idealistic. And they’re angry too, they want change. But ultimately, so many of these protests have gone too far, and they have done real damage to the credibility of the very causes these well-meaning young people claim to champion. If people want to change this country, they need to grow up and learn to work within the system instead of disrupting public life and turning voters against them.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26332, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16233, \"description\": \"I’m a proud Democrat, and I am no radical. Anyone who thinks I condone any kind of reckless law-breaking or unprovoked violence is mistaken. If a protest or march turns violent or disrupts the peace, then I support the protesters being punished to the extent the law calls for. To claim I think anything else is just another rumor started by Dick Nixon’s flying monkeys.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26333, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16234, \"description\": \"I’m not running against Spiro Agnew. I’m running against Richard Nixon and his record. The vice president seems to spend his days picking fights and throwing insults, and I don’t intend to join him. I’m going to keep talking about the war, the economy, and the direction of the country, and let voters decide who sounds like a president.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26334, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16234, \"description\": \"Agnew isn’t some independent actor. He says what Nixon wants to say, just louder and cruder. When he attacks protesters, the press, or whole groups of Americans, he’s doing it to distract from the failures of this administration. I’m going to remind people that his tone comes straight from the top.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26335, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16234, \"description\": \"The vice president is deliberately trying to pit Americans against each other. He mocks legitimate dissent, ridicules any disagreement, and treats whole parts of this country like mortal enemies. That might win applause at his campaign rallies, but it’s no way to govern a democracy, and it’s certainly no way to heal a country that’s already deeply divided.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26336, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16234, \"description\": \"I think most Americans are tired of being yelled at by their own government. They want a leadership that lowers the temperature, not raises it. I still believe that character counts in our country. I’ve been an honest public servant for 30 years. I’ve committed my life to serving the people of Indiana. Spiro Agnew? He’s just a lying attack dog who spends his free time golfing.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26337, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16235, \"description\": \"My time at Duke is just one chapter of a long career in public service. I served for four years as governor of North Carolina, managing complex state budgets, reviving our schools, and giving everyday families a leg up. I also helped run Hubert Humphrey’s campaign for president four years ago, building coalitions and making tough decisions under pressure. I bring a wealth of experience and a diversity in perspectives; I’d hardly say that’s a bad thing.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26338, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16235, \"description\": \"I’m proud of my record at Duke. We’ve grown our endowment, kept the peace during difficult campus protests, and strengthened the university’s programs so students could succeed. That’s real leadership: listening when needed, acting when necessary, and building lasting results. Contrast that with Nixon and Agnew yelling at students, insulting professors, and turning politics into nothing but theater. I understand people, and I deliver results; they know that, and so do the American people.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26339, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16235, \"description\": \"All this fuss about my time at Duke is just a distraction. President Nixon is in charge of our country; people should be asking him why he hasn’t fixed our welfare system, why healthcare is still a mess, why the war keeps dragging on. He’s made promises he hasn’t kept, and he’s lied more than once to cover it up. Meanwhile, I’ve been building relationships, defusing complex situations, and keeping a campus together during periods of real tension. That’s the kind of leadership this country actually needs, not more name-calling and finger-pointing.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26340, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16235, \"description\": \"Here’s the bigger picture: Nixon and Agnew spend all their time yelling. Yelling at college students, yelling at the press, yelling at Hollywood, yelling at any Tom, Dick, and Harry who disagrees with them. That may excite their base of pearl-clutchin’ suburbanites, but it doesn’t lead to good governance. My time at Duke shows I can lead without theatrics, without picking fights for the sake of headlines, and without turning every difference of opinion into a dang crisis. Real leadership is about getting results, and that’s what the country needs.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26341, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16236, \"description\": \"What happened in Munich was a tragedy, plain and simple. Innocent athletes went to represent their countries in peace, and they were murdered instead. Tonight, my thoughts are with the families of the Israeli team and with the German people who tried to protect their guests. Acts like this offend the conscience of all nations, and they deserve our shared grief and resolve to see that such crimes are never repeated.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26342, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16236, \"description\": \"The murder of Israeli athletes in Munich was an act of terror against innocent people and against the very idea of peaceful competition. Israel has the right to exist, the right to security, and the right to see its citizens protected from violence wherever they go. The United States must stand firmly with Israel in condemning this attack and in opposing terrorism in all its forms. There is no political cause that justifies the killing of civilians, ever.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26343, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16236, \"description\": \"What happened in Munich is a grim reminder that violence isn’t confined by borders, or uniforms, or battlefields. It threatens civilians, the international order, and the basic sense of safety people expect in the modern world. The United States must work with its allies to strengthen international cooperation against terrorism, improve intelligence sharing, and ensure that people are safe wherever they go.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26345, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16237, \"description\": \"If we reach out to Wallace, we have to keep the focus on the economic concerns that drove much of his support. Talk about wages, inflation, and the sense that working people have been squeezed while Washington argues over everything else. Make the case that this campaign is serious about those issues and isn’t just speaking to one part of the country. If he sees that we’re addressing the same frustrations his voters feel, he may not endorse us outright, but he could soften his tone and speak more favorably about the campaign.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26346, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16237, \"description\": \"If we reach out to Wallace, we need to ensure he feels respected and included. His supporters feel dismissed by both parties, and that resentment runs deep. Make it clear that we’re not writing anyone off and that there’s room in this campaign for voters who don’t feel at home anywhere else right now. This isn’t about agreeing on everything, it’s about acknowledging that their concerns deserve to be heard. If Wallace believes we’re serious about that, he might be willing to say something positive publicly, even if he stops short of endorsing us.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26347, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16237, \"description\": \"If we reach out to Wallace, we ought to talk to him about the broader need to settle things down politically. The country’s been through years of tension, and there’s an argument to be made for lowering the temperature and trying to bring people back into a shared political space. We can frame our campaign as an attempt to move past the constant division and give voters a sense that things don’t have to stay this polarized. It’s a harder sell, and he may push back, but it at least opens the door to a serious conversation about where the country goes from here.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26348, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16237, \"description\": \"No, we’re not going to seek George Wallace’s endorsement. His record and the kind of politics he’s built his career on are fundamentally at odds with what this campaign is trying to do, especially on civil rights and the broader direction of the party. Going after his backing would raise real questions about where we stand and risk undercutting the coalition we’ve spent months trying to build. If Wallace chooses to stay out of the race, that’s his decision, but we’re not going to invite him in.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26349, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16238, \"description\": \"If Secretary Kissinger’s statement proves true, then that is welcome news for every American family and every Vietnamese family that has paid the price for this war. I hope the administration is finally prepared to bring this conflict to an honorable conclusion, and I will judge their actions by whether the fighting actually stops and our troops truly come home.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26350, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16238, \"description\": \"We have been told for four years now that peace is just around the corner. And in that time, the president has invaded two other countries, authorized civilian bombing campaigns, and has continued to prop up a corrupt dictatorship abroad. I want this war to end as much as anyone, but I’m not going to take these sudden assurances at face value. I will believe peace is at hand when the war is actually over.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26351, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16238, \"description\": \"What the administration is now describing sounds very much like the settlement President Johnson was prepared to accept years ago. The difference is that since then, thousands more Americans and countless Vietnamese have died. If this is where we were always headed, then the real question is why this war was prolonged for so long to begin with.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26352, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16238, \"description\": \"Peace is not something you can announce at a press conference while we still have planes dropping bombs and soldiers knee deep in the Vietnamese mud. Peace means ending the war, bringing our troops home, and stopping the killing now. Anything less is not peace. This war has gone on long enough.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26353, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16239, \"description\": \"These are serious developments, and they deserve to be handled with utmost care. The facts need to be gathered, verified, and examined outside of partisan politics - I fully support Mr. Cronkite’s investigative work on the matter. And I’m calling for a full, independent congressional investigation so the public can know exactly what happened and who was responsible. The rule of law has to speak for itself.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26354, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16239, \"description\": \"When Americans watch a story like this unfold, they don’t just wonder about the kinds of things being done behind closed doors. They ask what kind of country we’re becoming. A democracy can’t survive on this level of secrecy, of paranoia, of political sabotage. We need honesty and integrity back in our public life. We need leaders who believe that winning an election is not worth losing the nation’s conscience.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26355, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16239, \"description\": \"This is a lifeline for our campaign, and it’s all the more reason we have to be careful here. There’s a criminal investigation underway, and that’s where this belongs. If the President says he had no involvement with the break-in this summer, that statement should be taken seriously unless proven otherwise. We shouldn’t rush to judgment or turn every accusation into a political weapon. Let the authorities do their work.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26356, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16239, \"description\": \"This isn’t your everyday dirty campaign trick, Mr. Cronkite is making that increasingly clear. If the White House is involved in breaking into the opposition party’s headquarters, that’s an abuse of power, plain and simple. No president is above the law. If these reports are true, Richard Nixon has no business being a free man, let alone occupying the Oval Office.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26357, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16240, \"description\": \"This election doesn’t have to be about fear or resentment. It can be about steady leadership and the hard work of healing a country strained by war and mistrust. I’m asking Americans to step back from the anger of the last few years and choose a future grounded in decency, responsibility, and the belief that we can do better than the status quo.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26358, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16240, \"description\": \"Every day this war drags on, more young Americans are asked to risk their lives without a clear purpose or end in sight. No last-minute announcement changes that fact. If you believe, as I do, that it’s time to bring this war to a responsible close and bring our sons home, then this election gives you a chance to make that a reality.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26359, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16240, \"description\": \"I’ve spent this campaign talking about good jobs, fair wages, and economic opportunity because that’s what working families think about long after the campaign speeches are over. They want a fair economy, good schools, and a government that works for people who play by the rules, not just those with influence. That’s always been the Democratic tradition, and it’s the choice voters still have in this election.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26360, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16240, \"description\": \"The president would prefer this election pass quietly, without facing any scrutiny or hard questions. I don’t believe that’s healthy for a democracy. The American people deserve honesty, accountability, and a government that levels with them, even when the truth is uncomfortable. If you want a change in tone and direction, this election is your opportunity to choose decency, responsibility, and integrity in public life.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26905, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16827, \"description\": \"I have spent my life in public service because I believe this country faces real dangers and real responsibilities. The world is not simple, and pretending otherwise doesn’t make anyone safer. Americans deserve leadership that understands power, understands alliances, and understands what it takes to keep the peace without surrendering our values. I am running because I believe experience matters, judgment matters, and this moment calls for both.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26906, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16827, \"description\": \"For generations, this party has carried the hopes and responsibilities of ordinary Americans, from the Depression years of Franklin Roosevelt, to the principled leadership of Harry Truman, through the unfinished promise of John Kennedy and the hard work of Lyndon Johnson. In times of trial and uncertainty, Democrats have always chosen resolve over retreat and responsibility over neglect. I am proud to stand in that tradition tonight, and I proudly accept the mantle to help lead our country out of the confusion and turmoil of these past few years, toward a future built on strength, opportunity, and confidence in American leadership.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26907, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16827, \"description\": \"The American people have been asked to accept too many half-truths and too many closed doors. They have been told one thing while being shown another, time and time again. That credibility gap has eroded confidence in our government at home and abroad. This administration believes that secrecy is a virtue and that questioning authority is a vice. I do not. I believe honesty is the first obligation of leadership, and accountability is not an option. This campaign will offer a clear alternative to a politics that demands the American people to stop asking questions.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26908, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16827, \"description\": \"I know many Democrats came to this convention wanting something new, something different, something bold. I respect that, I do. But change doesn’t only come from style. It comes from strength, from competence, and from the ability to govern in a dangerous world. I am asking this party to come together not around the newest, shiniest object, but around purpose, around drive. We can offer the country steady leadership, serious judgment, and a government that does its job. That is what I intend to provide.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26909, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16828, \"description\": \"The country is tired of politics that feels cynical and theatrical. I want someone who speaks about government in simpler terms, someone who approaches public service as a matter of honesty and competence rather than ideology. A younger Southern leader who has actually run things day to day and understands what people expect from government when it comes to good leadership. Someone who believes government should simply work the way people expect it to, and who carries himself like that still matters.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26910, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16828, \"description\": \"The party needs someone who can speak to people in parts of the country where Democrats have been losing ground. Not by lecturing them or pretending the last few years never happened, but by meeting them where they are and talking about progress in a language they trust. A Southern governor who has governed responsibly and who understands the pace of change in that region could help us reopen conversations we have not had in a long time.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26911, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16828, \"description\": \"What the country needs right now is a reminder that public service can still mean something honorable. I want someone on this ticket who has built a reputation for honest government and who has proven it in a place where politics is not always gentle. A Southern leader who talks about reform calmly, without bitterness, and who has shown voters that integrity in office is not just a campaign promise. That kind of voice might help restore a little faith in what government is supposed to be.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26913, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16829, \"description\": \"The images coming out of Southeast Asia are deeply troubling, and any loss of innocent life weighs heavily on the conscience of this country. War is never clean, and tragic errors can occur even when our forces are acting in good faith. If military authorities say this incident was unintended, that claim deserves serious consideration. Our responsibility is to reduce civilian harm, improve oversight, and continue the difficult work of bringing this conflict to a responsible conclusion.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26914, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16829, \"description\": \"No American can look at photographs of burned villages and wounded children without feeling shaken. These are human beings caught in the middle of forces far beyond their control. That moral reality can’t be brushed aside with technical explanations alone. If we are to justify this war to ourselves and to the world, we must show that civilian lives matter, and that our conduct reflects the values we claim to defend.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26915, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16829, \"description\": \"Incidents like this raise serious questions about whether this war is being conducted in a way that actually serves American interests. When civilian casualties mount, they undermine our credibility abroad, harden opposition at home, and make a political settlement more difficult to achieve. That isn’t just a moral problem, but a strategic one as well. It is time to reassess our tactics and press harder for a negotiated end to the conflict.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26916, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16829, \"description\": \"What troubles Americans most is the growing distance between what they see with their own eyes and what they are told by their government. Simply put, there is a clear credibility gap today that has undermined the legitimacy of our government. A democracy cannot sustain a long war without honesty from its leaders. If this administration cannot be candid about what is happening in Southeast Asia, then it has no business asking the American people to continue supporting this conflict.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26917, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16830, \"description\": \"I come out of the New Deal Democratic tradition, where politics has always been about improving people’s lives first and foremost. I learned that firsthand working alongside Jack Kennedy and Lyndon Johnson and Hubert Humphrey, fighting for civil rights, fair employment, and a government that actually listens to working families. That kind of work isn’t always loud, but it shapes the country in lasting ways. If voters want someone grounded in that tradition of steady progress and practical reform, I’m confident they’ll see exactly where I stand. My record speaks for itself.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26918, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16830, \"description\": \"There’s an assumption floating around that leadership has to sound angry and vulgar to be real. I don’t buy that. I’ve worked across parties, across regions, and across generations because I believe government reform only matters if it survives. What I offer voters is not a performance, but a seriousness of purpose. In a year this volatile, I think Americans are looking for someone who won’t gamble with the country just to prove a point.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26919, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16830, \"description\": \"If being cautious means thinking before acting, then I’ll take that label. We’ve lived through years of secrecy, impulse, and arrogance at the highest levels of government. The result has been a mounting credibility gap that’s created distrust at home and instability abroad. I believe strength comes from restraint, from knowing when power should be used and when it should not. That kind of leadership may not always be loud, but it lasts.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26920, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16830, \"description\": \"I don’t accept the idea that being a loyal Democrat means that you don’t have your own identity, or understand the issues of the day. This country is facing real challenges, and I intend to campaign with the urgency those challenges demand. I’ll be traveling across the country, laying out clear choices on the war, our economy, and on restoring trust in government. If some people think I’m too careful, they’re going to see a campaign that’s anything but passive. I’m going to make the case directly to the American people that steady leadership and decisive action are not mutually exclusive.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26921, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16831, \"description\": \"Senator McCarthy has every right to run whatever campaign he wants to run. That is part of the freedom our politics allows. But I am running a serious campaign to represent our whole nation, not just a tiny slice. The country is dealing with inflation, a war that still needs to be brought to a responsible conclusion, and a lot of uncertainty about where we go from here. My intention is to keep talking about those problems instead of chasing every side argument that appears during an election.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26922, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16831, \"description\": \"I know many of the people drawn to Senator McCarthy’s campaign care deeply about the same things I do. Ending this war responsibly, protecting civil rights, strengthening our schools, and building an economy where working families actually get ahead. Those are not fringe ideas in the Democratic Party. They are part of its core mission, and they are goals I am committed to pursuing as president. But none of them will move forward if we Democrats divide ourselves in this election. You can’t govern if you can’t win.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26923, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16831, \"description\": \"Senator McCarthy has built a career out of standing apart and declaring everyone else morally inadequate. That may be satisfying on a college campus, but it is not a serious way to govern a country. Being president means making tough, complex decisions, taking responsibility for what happens next, and working through the imperfect choices that got us there in the first place. Americans deserve a leadership that faces those realities instead of standing safely on the sidelines pointing fingers.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26924, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16831, \"description\": \"I find this whole exercise a little curious. Senator McCarthy insists he cannot support the Democratic nominee, yet the only practical effect of his campaign is to help Richard Nixon stay in office. That raises some obvious questions about what this effort is really about. The American people deserve better than a protest campaign that spins up headlines and collects donations while quietly paving the way for the very policies its supporters claim to oppose.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26925, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16832, \"description\": \"I have said from the beginning that I do not believe federally mandated busing is the right way to solve this problem. It has proven deeply unpopular in communities across the country, and in many cases it has created more bitterness than progress. I have spoken with plenty of parents, including Black parents, who want what every family wants: good schools close to home and real opportunity for their children. Integration must remain a national goal, but I do not believe Washington should force a solution that divides communities and undermines public confidence in our schools.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26926, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16832, \"description\": \"I will be honest about this. I have had serious reservations about busing for a long time. But over the past months I have met with civil rights leaders and families who have told me plainly that without stronger measures, equal education will always be just out of reach. I still have concerns about the policy and the strains it can place on communities, but the principle of equal opportunity matters more. If busing is necessary in some places to fulfill the promise of integration, then we cannot shrink from that responsibility.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26927, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16832, \"description\": \"The truth is that the debate over busing has started to crowd out other policies that could do far more to expand equal opportunity. We should be talking about supporting Black and minority-owned businesses, strengthening affirmative action in hiring and education, and investing in schools that have been neglected for decades. Those kinds of efforts strike at the root of inequality. If we are serious about civil rights, we need to broaden the conversation beyond a single policy.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26928, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16832, \"description\": \"This is an issue where emotions are running high across the country, and it isn’t something I can simply settle with a speech. The courts are already deeply involved in these decisions, and in many cases they are the proper place for them to be resolved. My view is that the states, local communities, and the judicial system will ultimately have to work out the practical details. The federal government’s role should remain focused on enforcing civil rights and making sure the law is applied fairly to everyone.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26929, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16833, \"description\": \"I understand why so many people are angry, and many of the concerns being raised in these protests are completely legitimate. The war, civil rights, and economic inequality have left a lot of Americans feeling shut out. But violence and destruction of property only weaken those causes and drag innocent people into the crossfire, and that’s not something I can support.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26930, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16833, \"description\": \"I’m not going to throw anyone under the bus, let alone so many patriotic Americans speaking out for what they feel is right. Peaceful protest is a constitutional right, and dissent is not the same thing as disorder. It is dangerous to treat protest itself as a threat to the country. President Nixon should be ashamed of himself for his brazen attempts to politicize our freedoms.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26931, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16833, \"description\": \"Look, a lot of these folks supported Mr. McGovern this year. They’re young, they’re idealistic. And they’re angry too, they want change. But ultimately, so many of these protests have gone too far, and they have done real damage to the credibility of the very causes these well-meaning young people claim to champion. If people want to change this country, they need to grow up and learn to work within the system instead of disrupting public life and turning voters against them.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26932, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16833, \"description\": \"I’m a proud Democrat, and I am no radical. Anyone who thinks I condone any kind of reckless law-breaking or unprovoked violence is mistaken. If a protest or march turns violent or disrupts the peace, then I support the protesters being punished to the extent the law calls for. To claim I think anything else is just another rumor started by Dick Nixon’s flying monkeys.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26933, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16834, \"description\": \"I’m not running against Spiro Agnew. I’m running against Richard Nixon and his record. The vice president seems to spend his days picking fights and throwing insults, and I don’t intend to join him. I’m going to keep talking about the war, the economy, and the direction of the country, and let voters decide who sounds like a president.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26934, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16834, \"description\": \"Agnew isn’t some independent actor. He says what Nixon wants to say, just louder and cruder. When he attacks protesters, the press, or whole groups of Americans, he’s doing it to distract from the failures of this administration. I’m going to remind people that his tone comes straight from the top.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26935, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16834, \"description\": \"The vice president is deliberately trying to pit Americans against each other. He mocks legitimate dissent, ridicules any disagreement, and treats whole parts of this country like mortal enemies. That might win applause at his campaign rallies, but it’s no way to govern a democracy, and it’s certainly no way to heal a country that’s already deeply divided.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26936, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16834, \"description\": \"I think most Americans are tired of being yelled at by their own government. They want a leadership that lowers the temperature, not raises it. I still believe that character counts in our country. I’ve been an honest public servant for 30 years. I’ve committed my life to serving the people of Indiana. Spiro Agnew? He’s just a lying attack dog who spends his free time golfing.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26937, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16835, \"description\": \"Let me be clear about something. My views on national defense were not written by Boeing or any other company. They were shaped by the simple belief that a free country has a responsibility to protect itself and to stand by its allies. The world we live in is not a gentle one. The Soviet Union is a real power with real ambitions, and pretending otherwise will not keep America safe. My position has always been that a strong defense is the foundation of both peace and credibility.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26938, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16835, \"description\": \"The aerospace industry in my home state employs thousands of skilled American workers. These are machinists, engineers, and technicians who take pride in building the aircraft and technology that help protect this country. I will never apologize for standing up for American jobs or for the men and women who do that work. Supporting a strong defense and supporting working families are not conflicting goals. In many parts of this country, they are the very same thing.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26939, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16835, \"description\": \"Some people in our party are uneasy with my views on defense, I get that. But this election is not a seminar debate, it is a choice about who will actually be president. If someone believes Richard Nixon is going to be more restrained, more transparent, or more honest than I am, then I have a bridge to sell you in Brooklyn. I have been upfront about my beliefs and my record. The real question is whether people who care about peace and reform want to spend the next four years arguing amongst themselves while Richard Nixon stays in the White House.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26940, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16835, \"description\": \"I support a strong defense and I support our international alliances, and I don’t apologize for that. But that doesn’t make me a warmonger. I want a responsible conclusion to the war in Vietnam just as much as any Democrat does. The difference is not whether we want peace, but how we achieve it without undermining our credibility or abandoning our commitments. You can be tough on defense without bombing countries back to the Stone Age. My record reflects a belief in strength, yes, but also in responsibility, restraint, and ultimately peace.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26941, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16836, \"description\": \"What happened in Munich was a tragedy, plain and simple. Innocent athletes went to represent their countries in peace, and they were murdered instead. Tonight, my thoughts are with the families of the Israeli team and with the German people who tried to protect their guests. Acts like this offend the conscience of all nations, and they deserve our shared grief and resolve to see that such crimes are never repeated.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26942, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16836, \"description\": \"The murder of Israeli athletes in Munich was an act of terror against innocent people and against the very idea of peaceful competition. Israel has the right to exist, the right to security, and the right to see its citizens protected from violence wherever they go. The United States must stand firmly with Israel in condemning this attack and in opposing terrorism in all its forms. There is no political cause that justifies the killing of civilians, ever.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26943, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16836, \"description\": \"What happened in Munich is a grim reminder that violence isn’t confined by borders, or uniforms, or battlefields. It threatens civilians, the international order, and the basic sense of safety people expect in the modern world. The United States must work with its allies to strengthen international cooperation against terrorism, improve intelligence sharing, and ensure that people are safe wherever they go.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26945, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16837, \"description\": \"If we reach out to Wallace, we have to keep the focus on the economic concerns that drove much of his support. Talk about wages, inflation, and the sense that working people have been squeezed while Washington argues over everything else. Make the case that this campaign is serious about those issues and isn’t just speaking to one part of the country. If he sees that we’re addressing the same frustrations his voters feel, he may not endorse us outright, but he could soften his tone and speak more favorably about the campaign.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26946, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16837, \"description\": \"If we reach out to Wallace, we need to ensure he feels respected and included. His supporters feel dismissed by both parties, and that resentment runs deep. Make it clear that we’re not writing anyone off and that there’s room in this campaign for voters who don’t feel at home anywhere else right now. This isn’t about agreeing on everything, it’s about acknowledging that their concerns deserve to be heard. If Wallace believes we’re serious about that, he might be willing to say something positive publicly, even if he stops short of endorsing us.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26947, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16837, \"description\": \"If we reach out to Wallace, we ought to talk to him about the broader need to settle things down politically. The country’s been through years of tension, and there’s an argument to be made for lowering the temperature and trying to bring people back into a shared political space. We can frame our campaign as an attempt to move past the constant division and give voters a sense that things don’t have to stay this polarized. It’s a harder sell, and he may push back, but it at least opens the door to a serious conversation about where the country goes from here.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26948, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16837, \"description\": \"No, we’re not going to seek George Wallace’s endorsement. His record and the kind of politics he’s built his career on are fundamentally at odds with what this campaign is trying to do, especially on civil rights and the broader direction of the party. Going after his backing would raise real questions about where we stand and risk undercutting the coalition we’ve spent months trying to build. If Wallace chooses to stay out of the race, that’s his decision, but we’re not going to invite him in.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26949, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16838, \"description\": \"If Secretary Kissinger’s statement proves true, then that is welcome news for every American family and every Vietnamese family that has paid the price for this war. I hope the administration is finally prepared to bring this conflict to an honorable conclusion, and I will judge their actions by whether the fighting actually stops and our troops truly come home.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26950, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16838, \"description\": \"We have been told for four years now that peace is just around the corner. And in that time, the president has invaded two other countries, authorized civilian bombing campaigns, and has continued to prop up a corrupt dictatorship abroad. I want this war to end as much as anyone, but I’m not going to take these sudden assurances at face value. I will believe peace is at hand when the war is actually over.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26951, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16838, \"description\": \"What the administration is now describing sounds very much like the settlement President Johnson was prepared to accept years ago. The difference is that since then, thousands more Americans and countless Vietnamese have died. If this is where we were always headed, then the real question is why this war was prolonged for so long to begin with.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26952, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16838, \"description\": \"Peace is not something you can announce at a press conference while we still have planes dropping bombs and soldiers knee deep in the Vietnamese mud. Peace means ending the war, bringing our troops home, and stopping the killing now. Anything less is not peace. This war has gone on long enough.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26953, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16839, \"description\": \"These are serious developments, and they deserve to be handled with utmost care. The facts need to be gathered, verified, and examined outside of partisan politics - I fully support Mr. Cronkite’s investigative work on the matter. And I’m calling for a full, independent congressional investigation so the public can know exactly what happened and who was responsible. The rule of law has to speak for itself.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26954, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16839, \"description\": \"When Americans watch a story like this unfold, they don’t just wonder about the kinds of things being done behind closed doors. They ask what kind of country we’re becoming. A democracy can’t survive on this level of secrecy, of paranoia, of political sabotage. We need honesty and integrity back in our public life. We need leaders who believe that winning an election is not worth losing the nation’s conscience.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26955, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16839, \"description\": \"This is a lifeline for our campaign, and it’s all the more reason we have to be careful here. There’s a criminal investigation underway, and that’s where this belongs. If the President says he had no involvement with the break-in this summer, that statement should be taken seriously unless proven otherwise. We shouldn’t rush to judgment or turn every accusation into a political weapon. Let the authorities do their work.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26956, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16839, \"description\": \"This isn’t your everyday dirty campaign trick, Mr. Cronkite is making that increasingly clear. If the White House is involved in breaking into the opposition party’s headquarters, that’s an abuse of power, plain and simple. No president is above the law. If these reports are true, Richard Nixon has no business being a free man, let alone occupying the Oval Office.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26957, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16840, \"description\": \"This election doesn’t have to be another chapter in the anger and division of the last few years. It can be about restoring steady leadership and doing the hard work of bringing the country back together after a long and difficult period. I’m asking Americans to step back from the shouting and look carefully at the future we want to build. One grounded in responsibility, seriousness of purpose, and the belief that our country is strongest when its leaders govern with discipline and respect for the public trust.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26958, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16840, \"description\": \"Every day this war continues, more young Americans are asked to risk their lives while the country waits for a clear and responsible end. No last-minute announcement can erase that reality. If you believe, as I do, that it is time to bring this war to a responsible conclusion and bring our sons home with honor, then this election offers a chance to move the country in that direction.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26959, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16840, \"description\": \"Throughout this campaign I have talked about jobs, wages, and economic opportunity because those are the concerns that stay with families long after the campaign rallies end. Americans want a fair economy, strong schools, and a government that works for people who work hard and play by the rules. That has always been at the heart of the Democratic tradition, and it remains the choice facing voters in this election.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 26960, \"fields\": {\"question\": 16840, \"description\": \"The president would prefer this election pass quietly, without much scrutiny or serious debate about the direction of the country. I don’t believe that serves the American people. Democracy depends on accountability and leaders who are willing to answer difficult questions honestly. The voters deserve a government that levels with them, that respects their judgment, and that approaches public service with integrity and responsibility. If Americans want a change in tone and direction, this election gives them that opportunity.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 27505, \"fields\": {\"question\": 17427, \"description\": \"I don’t stand here today to make grand promises or offer easy answers. I am here because this country is facing serious problems that require serious work. Inflation, taxes, jobs, the stability of our economy. These aren’t empty buzzwords. They’re responsibilities that affect us all. I have spent my life in the unglamorous business of governing, of reading the fine print, of making sure the numbers add up. If this party is asking me to carry its banner, then I intend to do it the same way I have always done my job: carefully, honestly, and with an eye toward keeping this country on solid footing.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 27506, \"fields\": {\"question\": 17427, \"description\": \"For generations, this party has carried the hopes and responsibilities of ordinary Americans, from the Depression years of Franklin Roosevelt, to the principled leadership of Harry Truman, through the unfinished promise of John Kennedy and the hard work of Lyndon Johnson. In difficult times, Democrats have always chosen resolve over retreat and responsibility over neglect. I am proud to stand in that tradition tonight, and I accept the responsibility to help guide this country through uncertainty with steadiness, experience, and respect for the institutions that hold us together.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 27507, \"fields\": {\"question\": 17427, \"description\": \"The American people have been asked to accept too many half-truths and too many explanations. They have been told one thing while being shown another, time and time again. That credibility gap has eroded confidence in our government at home and abroad. This administration believes that secrecy is a virtue and that questioning authority is a vice. I do not. I believe honesty is the first obligation of leadership, and accountability is not an option. This campaign will offer a clear alternative to a politics that demands the American people to stop asking questions.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 27508, \"fields\": {\"question\": 17427, \"description\": \"I know many people here are tired. This has been a long, long year, and not everyone got the outcome they hoped for. I respect that, I do. But I also know that the work ahead of us does not require excitement so much as endurance, and patience, and cooperation, and a willingness to put serious governance ahead of the latest fad. If we want to earn the country’s trust again, we have to show that we can manage our power responsibly. That is the task I am willing to take on, and I ask this party to take it on with me.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 27509, \"fields\": {\"question\": 17428, \"description\": \"The way I see it, this ticket needs steadiness. The convention showed how many different voices exist in this party, and the only way we win in November is by keeping those voices talking to each other instead of drifting apart again. I want a partner who understands labor, civil rights, and the legislative process well enough to keep a coalition together when the pressure builds. Someone serious, disciplined, and trusted inside the party. Not a showman. Someone who knows how the work actually gets done.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 27510, \"fields\": {\"question\": 17428, \"description\": \"This moment calls for seriousness. The country has watched too many politicians treat government like a stage for clever speeches. I want a running mate who approaches public office like a responsibility that has to be studied and respected. Someone who has spent years thinking carefully about the Constitution, how government is structured, and the way laws actually shape people’s lives. That kind of temperament would show voters that this ticket intends to govern thoughtfully and with purpose.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 27511, \"fields\": {\"question\": 17428, \"description\": \"I think what voters want right now more than anything is someone with good judgment. Someone who takes the time to understand a problem before speaking on it, who weighs the consequences instead of rushing to a headline, and who carries himself with a certain discipline in public life. I want a running mate known for that kind of steadiness, who has spent years working through difficult issues in detail and has earned respect for it. There’s a difference between sounding thoughtful and actually doing the work, and I think people can tell which is which.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 27513, \"fields\": {\"question\": 17429, \"description\": \"The images coming out of Southeast Asia are deeply troubling, and any loss of innocent life weighs heavily on the conscience of this country. War is never clean, and tragic errors can occur even when our forces are acting in good faith. If military authorities say this incident was unintended, that claim deserves serious consideration. Our responsibility is to reduce civilian harm, improve oversight, and continue the difficult work of bringing this conflict to a responsible conclusion.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 27514, \"fields\": {\"question\": 17429, \"description\": \"No American can look at photographs of burned villages and wounded children without feeling shaken. These are human beings caught in the middle of forces far beyond their control. That moral reality can’t be brushed aside with technical explanations alone. If we are to justify this war to ourselves and to the world, we must show that civilian lives matter, and that our conduct reflects the values we claim to defend.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 27515, \"fields\": {\"question\": 17429, \"description\": \"Incidents like this raise serious questions about whether this war is being conducted in a way that actually serves American interests. When civilian casualties mount, they undermine our credibility abroad, harden opposition at home, and make a political settlement more difficult to achieve. That isn’t just a moral problem, but a strategic one as well. It is time to reassess our tactics and press harder for a negotiated end to the conflict.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 27516, \"fields\": {\"question\": 17429, \"description\": \"What troubles Americans most is the growing distance between what they see with their own eyes and what they are told by their government. Simply put, there is a clear credibility gap today that has undermined the legitimacy of our government. A democracy cannot sustain a long war without honesty from its leaders. If this administration cannot be candid about what is happening in Southeast Asia, then it has no business asking the American people to continue supporting this conflict.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 27517, \"fields\": {\"question\": 17430, \"description\": \"I understand that some people in the party have doubts about me, and I’m not going to pretend those concerns don’t exist. A lot of younger Democrats came into politics through the civil rights movement and the protests against the war, and they want to know where this campaign stands. The best way to answer that is not through speeches but through conversations. We need to be on campuses and meeting directly with student leaders, civil rights groups, and activists who have been part of these movements. I’ve spent my career sitting down with people who disagree with me and working through problems step by step, and I’m prepared to do the same thing here.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 27518, \"fields\": {\"question\": 17430, \"description\": \"This campaign cannot become a conversation only within one faction of the party. Most Americans right now are worried about inflation, taxes, jobs, and whether the country feels stable again. Those concerns are shared by union households in the North, farmers in the Midwest, and working families across the South and West. My record has always been about practical economic policy and responsible government. If we keep our focus on those bread and butter concerns and hold the Democratic coalition together, we give ourselves a real chance to win and govern effectively.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 27519, \"fields\": {\"question\": 17430, \"description\": \"I’ve spent many years in public life, and anyone who has followed that record knows exactly where I stand. Some people in the party would prefer a different nominee and a different direction, and I respect their right to say so. But campaigns cannot be run by constantly shifting positions depending on who is speaking the loudest that week. What I offer voters is experience, seriousness about governing, and a long record of working through the details of policy instead of chasing political fashion.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 27520, \"fields\": {\"question\": 17430, \"description\": \"I don’t believe it helps anyone if this turns into a shouting match between different wings of the party. There are leaders within the Democratic Party who have strong credibility with the reform movement and the student activists, and their voices carry weight in those conversations. People like George McGovern, Frank Church, and others can help keep those lines of communication open. The goal isn’t to pressure anyone. It’s to remind people that the Democratic Party has always been a broad coalition, and that coalition only works if we keep talking to each other even when we disagree.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 27521, \"fields\": {\"question\": 17431, \"description\": \"Senator McCarthy has every right to run whatever campaign he wants to run. That is part of the freedom our politics allows. But I am running a serious campaign to represent our whole nation, not just a tiny slice. The country is dealing with inflation, a war that still needs to be brought to a responsible conclusion, and a lot of uncertainty about where we go from here. My intention is to keep talking about those problems instead of chasing every side argument that appears during an election.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 27522, \"fields\": {\"question\": 17431, \"description\": \"I know many of the people drawn to Senator McCarthy’s campaign care deeply about the same things I do. Ending this war responsibly, protecting the rule of law, strengthening our schools, and building an economy that truly works. Those are not fringe ideas in the Democratic Party. They are part of its core mission, and they are goals I am committed to pursuing as president. But none of them will move forward if we Democrats divide ourselves in this election. You can’t govern if you can’t win.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 27523, \"fields\": {\"question\": 17431, \"description\": \"Senator McCarthy has built a career out of standing apart and declaring everyone else morally inadequate. That may be satisfying on a college campus, but it is not a serious way to govern a country. Being president means making tough, complex decisions, taking responsibility for what happens next, and working through the imperfect choices that got us there in the first place. Americans deserve a leadership that faces those realities instead of standing safely on the sidelines pointing fingers.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 27524, \"fields\": {\"question\": 17431, \"description\": \"I find this whole exercise a little curious. Senator McCarthy insists he cannot support the Democratic nominee, yet the only practical effect of his campaign is to help Richard Nixon stay in office. That raises some obvious questions about what this effort is really about. The American people deserve better than a protest campaign that spins up headlines and collects donations while quietly paving the way for the very policies its supporters claim to oppose.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 27525, \"fields\": {\"question\": 17432, \"description\": \"I understand why some people look at my record and have questions about it. The South I grew up in was changing, and those changes didn’t come easily or all at once. But over the years I have supported the civil rights laws that now govern this country, and I accept that they must be enforced fairly and fully. What I have always tried to do is keep communities moving forward without tearing them apart in the process. Equal opportunity must remain the national goal, but the way we pursue it has to strengthen the country rather than deepen its divisions.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 27526, \"fields\": {\"question\": 17432, \"description\": \"I’m not going to stand here and pretend my record exists in a vacuum. I represented the people of Arkansas, and my responsibility in Congress was to speak for them and reflect the views of the communities that sent me there. Anyone who has served in public office understands that balance. But representing your constituents and respecting the law are not the same thing as resisting change. The civil rights laws passed in this country are now the law of the land, and like any responsible public official, I recognize that they must be respected and enforced, impartially and without prejudice.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 27527, \"fields\": {\"question\": 17432, \"description\": \"I understand why people want to revisit the past, but I also think the country ought to spend more time talking about the opportunities we can create today. That means investing in education, strengthening historically Black colleges, supporting minority and ethnic-owned businesses, and making sure economic growth actually reaches communities that have been left out for generations. The Democratic Party has always believed that economic security is the key to our nation’s prosperity. The kind of progress we built through the New Deal and the Great Society is the kind of progress I want to build on.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 27528, \"fields\": {\"question\": 17432, \"description\": \"Whatever personal views a person may have had earlier in life, the responsibility of the presidency is to uphold the Constitution and enforce the laws of the United States. The courts have already made clear where the country stands on civil rights, and those decisions are not optional. My duty as president would be to enforce the law fairly and impartially, without allowing personal feelings or regional loyalties to interfere with that responsibility. That is the standard every president ought to meet.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 27529, \"fields\": {\"question\": 17433, \"description\": \"I understand why so many people are angry, and many of the concerns being raised in these protests are completely legitimate. The war, civil rights, and economic inequality have left a lot of Americans feeling shut out. But violence and destruction of property only weaken those causes and drag innocent people into the crossfire, and that’s not something I can support.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 27530, \"fields\": {\"question\": 17433, \"description\": \"I’m not going to throw anyone under the bus, let alone so many patriotic Americans speaking out for what they feel is right. Peaceful protest is a constitutional right, and dissent is not the same thing as disorder. It is dangerous to treat protest itself as a threat to the country. President Nixon should be ashamed of himself for his brazen attempts to politicize our freedoms.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 27531, \"fields\": {\"question\": 17433, \"description\": \"Look, a lot of these folks supported Mr. McGovern this year. They’re young, they’re idealistic. And they’re angry too, they want change. But ultimately, so many of these protests have gone too far, and they have done real damage to the credibility of the very causes these well-meaning young people claim to champion. If people want to change this country, they need to grow up and learn to work within the system instead of disrupting public life and turning voters against them.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 27532, \"fields\": {\"question\": 17433, \"description\": \"I’m a proud Democrat, and I am no radical. Anyone who thinks I condone any kind of reckless law-breaking or unprovoked violence is mistaken. If a protest or march turns violent or disrupts the peace, then I support the protesters being punished to the extent the law calls for. To claim I think anything else is just another rumor started by Dick Nixon’s flying monkeys.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 27533, \"fields\": {\"question\": 17434, \"description\": \"I’m not running against Spiro Agnew. I’m running against Richard Nixon and his record. The vice president seems to spend his days picking fights and throwing insults, and I don’t intend to join him. I’m going to keep talking about the war, the economy, and the direction of the country, and let voters decide who sounds like a president.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 27534, \"fields\": {\"question\": 17434, \"description\": \"Agnew isn’t some independent actor. He says what Nixon wants to say, just louder and cruder. When he attacks protesters, the press, or whole groups of Americans, he’s doing it to distract from the failures of this administration. I’m going to remind people that his tone comes straight from the top.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 27535, \"fields\": {\"question\": 17434, \"description\": \"The vice president is deliberately trying to pit Americans against each other. He mocks legitimate dissent, ridicules any disagreement, and treats whole parts of this country like mortal enemies. That might win applause at his campaign rallies, but it’s no way to govern a democracy, and it’s certainly no way to heal a country that’s already deeply divided.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 27536, \"fields\": {\"question\": 17434, \"description\": \"I think most Americans are tired of being yelled at by their own government. They want a leadership that lowers the temperature, not raises it. I still believe that character counts in our country. I’ve been an honest public servant for 30 years. I’ve committed my life to serving the people of Indiana. Spiro Agnew? He’s just a lying attack dog who spends his free time golfing.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 27537, \"fields\": {\"question\": 17435, \"description\": \"I’ve heard that criticism before, and I understand why some people feel impatient with Washington. But the truth is that laws don’t pass themselves and reforms don’t magically appear because someone gives a good speech about them. They come from long hours in committees, negotiations between people who don’t agree, and the careful work of building majorities. That may not be glamorous politics, but it is how Social Security was strengthened, how Medicare was created, and how real progress has been made in this country.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 27538, \"fields\": {\"question\": 17435, \"description\": \"I understand why some younger Democrats are frustrated with the way Congress operates. Institutions that have been around a long time always need to be examined and, when necessary, improved. I’m not afraid of that conversation. If there are ways to make committees more open or to give newer members a stronger voice, we should be willing to look at them seriously. But reform has to be about making Congress work better, not simply tearing down the experience and relationships that allow legislation to move at all.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 27539, \"fields\": {\"question\": 17435, \"description\": \"Honestly, I think a lot of this anger over is misdirected. People are frustrated with the system, and the seniority rules just happen to be an easy target. This isn’t new. We’ve seen it in campaigns of times past - Henry Wallace, with Adlai Stevenson, with Gene McCarthy four years ago. Every generation of “new politics” rises up, yells about the insiders, and then eventually learns how the bread is buttered. Laws still have to pass, compromises have to be made, and over time, most people grow up and move on.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 27540, \"fields\": {\"question\": 17435, \"description\": \"I find it a little strange to be lectured on my supposed elitism coming from this administration. The Nixon White House has centralized more power than any presidency in recent memory, with decisions made behind closed doors and handed down to the rest of the government after the fact. Congress, for all its flaws, debates its decisions in the open and answers directly to the voters who send us there. If the country wants less concentration of power and more accountability, strengthening Congress is part of the answer, not the problem.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 27541, \"fields\": {\"question\": 17436, \"description\": \"What happened in Munich was a tragedy, plain and simple. Innocent athletes went to represent their countries in peace, and they were murdered instead. Tonight, my thoughts are with the families of the Israeli team and with the German people who tried to protect their guests. Acts like this offend the conscience of all nations, and they deserve our shared grief and resolve to see that such crimes are never repeated.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 27542, \"fields\": {\"question\": 17436, \"description\": \"The murder of Israeli athletes in Munich was an act of terror against innocent people and against the very idea of peaceful competition. Israel has the right to exist, the right to security, and the right to see its citizens protected from violence wherever they go. The United States must stand firmly with Israel in condemning this attack and in opposing terrorism in all its forms. There is no political cause that justifies the killing of civilians, ever.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 27543, \"fields\": {\"question\": 17436, \"description\": \"What happened in Munich is a grim reminder that violence isn’t confined by borders, or uniforms, or battlefields. It threatens civilians, the international order, and the basic sense of safety people expect in the modern world. The United States must work with its allies to strengthen international cooperation against terrorism, improve intelligence sharing, and ensure that people are safe wherever they go.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 27545, \"fields\": {\"question\": 17437, \"description\": \"If we reach out to Wallace, we have to keep the focus on the economic concerns that drove much of his support. Talk about wages, inflation, and the sense that working people have been squeezed while Washington argues over everything else. Make the case that this campaign is serious about those issues and isn’t just speaking to one part of the country. If he sees that we’re addressing the same frustrations his voters feel, he may not endorse us outright, but he could soften his tone and speak more favorably about the campaign.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 27546, \"fields\": {\"question\": 17437, \"description\": \"If we reach out to Wallace, we need to ensure he feels respected and included. His supporters feel dismissed by both parties, and that resentment runs deep. Make it clear that we’re not writing anyone off and that there’s room in this campaign for voters who don’t feel at home anywhere else right now. This isn’t about agreeing on everything, it’s about acknowledging that their concerns deserve to be heard. If Wallace believes we’re serious about that, he might be willing to say something positive publicly, even if he stops short of endorsing us.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 27547, \"fields\": {\"question\": 17437, \"description\": \"If we reach out to Wallace, we ought to talk to him about the broader need to settle things down politically. The country’s been through years of tension, and there’s an argument to be made for lowering the temperature and trying to bring people back into a shared political space. We can frame our campaign as an attempt to move past the constant division and give voters a sense that things don’t have to stay this polarized. It’s a harder sell, and he may push back, but it at least opens the door to a serious conversation about where the country goes from here.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 27548, \"fields\": {\"question\": 17437, \"description\": \"No, we’re not going to seek George Wallace’s endorsement. His record and the kind of politics he’s built his career on are fundamentally at odds with what this campaign is trying to do, especially on civil rights and the broader direction of the party. Going after his backing would raise real questions about where we stand and risk undercutting the coalition we’ve spent months trying to build. If Wallace chooses to stay out of the race, that’s his decision, but we’re not going to invite him in.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 27549, \"fields\": {\"question\": 17438, \"description\": \"If Secretary Kissinger’s statement proves true, then that is welcome news for every American family and every Vietnamese family that has paid the price for this war. I hope the administration is finally prepared to bring this conflict to an honorable conclusion, and I will judge their actions by whether the fighting actually stops and our troops truly come home.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 27550, \"fields\": {\"question\": 17438, \"description\": \"We have been told for four years now that peace is just around the corner. And in that time, the president has invaded two other countries, authorized civilian bombing campaigns, and has continued to prop up a corrupt dictatorship abroad. I want this war to end as much as anyone, but I’m not going to take these sudden assurances at face value. I will believe peace is at hand when the war is actually over.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 27551, \"fields\": {\"question\": 17438, \"description\": \"What the administration is now describing sounds very much like the settlement President Johnson was prepared to accept years ago. The difference is that since then, thousands more Americans and countless Vietnamese have died. If this is where we were always headed, then the real question is why this war was prolonged for so long to begin with.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 27552, \"fields\": {\"question\": 17438, \"description\": \"Peace is not something you can announce at a press conference while we still have planes dropping bombs and soldiers knee deep in the Vietnamese mud. Peace means ending the war, bringing our troops home, and stopping the killing now. Anything less is not peace. This war has gone on long enough.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 27553, \"fields\": {\"question\": 17439, \"description\": \"These are serious developments, and they deserve to be handled with utmost care. The facts need to be gathered, verified, and examined outside of partisan politics - I fully support Mr. Cronkite’s investigative work on the matter. And I’m calling for a full, independent congressional investigation so the public can know exactly what happened and who was responsible. The rule of law has to speak for itself.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 27554, \"fields\": {\"question\": 17439, \"description\": \"When Americans watch a story like this unfold, they don’t just wonder about the kinds of things being done behind closed doors. They ask what kind of country we’re becoming. A democracy can’t survive on this level of secrecy, of paranoia, of political sabotage. We need honesty and integrity back in our public life. We need leaders who believe that winning an election is not worth losing the nation’s conscience.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 27555, \"fields\": {\"question\": 17439, \"description\": \"This is a lifeline for our campaign, and it’s all the more reason we have to be careful here. There’s a criminal investigation underway, and that’s where this belongs. If the President says he had no involvement with the break-in this summer, that statement should be taken seriously unless proven otherwise. We shouldn’t rush to judgment or turn every accusation into a political weapon. Let the authorities do their work.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 27556, \"fields\": {\"question\": 17439, \"description\": \"This isn’t your everyday dirty campaign trick, Mr. Cronkite is making that increasingly clear. If the White House is involved in breaking into the opposition party’s headquarters, that’s an abuse of power, plain and simple. No president is above the law. If these reports are true, Richard Nixon has no business being a free man, let alone occupying the Oval Office.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 27557, \"fields\": {\"question\": 17440, \"description\": \"This election doesn’t have to be another chapter in the anger and division of the last few years. It can be about restoring steady leadership and doing the hard work of bringing the country back together after a long and difficult period. I’m asking Americans to step back from the shouting and look carefully at the future we want to build. One grounded in responsibility, seriousness of purpose, and the belief that our country is strongest when its leaders govern with discipline and respect for the public trust.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 27558, \"fields\": {\"question\": 17440, \"description\": \"Every day this war continues, more young Americans are asked to risk their lives while the country waits for a clear and responsible end. No last-minute announcement can erase that reality. If you believe, as I do, that it is time to bring this war to a responsible conclusion and bring our sons home with honor, then this election offers a chance to move the country in that direction.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 27559, \"fields\": {\"question\": 17440, \"description\": \"Throughout this campaign I have talked about jobs, wages, and economic opportunity because those are the concerns that stay with families long after the campaign rallies end. Americans want a fair economy, strong schools, and a government that works for people who work hard and play by the rules. That has always been at the heart of the Democratic tradition, and it remains the choice facing voters in this election.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 27560, \"fields\": {\"question\": 17440, \"description\": \"The president would prefer this election pass quietly, without much scrutiny or serious debate about the direction of the country. I don’t believe that serves the American people. Democracy depends on accountability and leaders who are willing to answer difficult questions honestly. The voters deserve a government that levels with them, that respects their judgment, and that approaches public service with integrity and responsibility. If Americans want a change in tone and direction, this election gives them that opportunity.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28105, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18027, \"description\": \"I know I am not the easy choice, and I am certainly not the comfortable one. I came into politics because I believed that the country was drifting away from its own people, and that government had grown distant from the lives it was meant to serve. I have never believed that reform comes from waiting your turn or keeping your head down. It comes from challenging habits that no longer work and from listening to voices that have been ignored for far too long. If this party is serious about change, then it has to be willing to take risks alongside me.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28106, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18027, \"description\": \"For generations, this party has carried the hopes and dreams of ordinary Americans, from the depression years of Franklin Roosevelt, to the courage and conviction of Harry Truman, through the promise of John Kennedy and the hard work of Lyndon Johnson. In times of trial and uncertainty, Democrats have always chosen action over despair and compassion over indifference. I am proud to stand in that tradition tonight, and I accept the responsibility to help lead our country out of the confusion and turmoil of these past few years, toward a future rooted in fairness, opportunity, and faith in one another.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28107, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18027, \"description\": \"The American people have been asked to accept too many half-truths and too many closed doors. They have been told one thing while being shown another, time and time again. That credibility gap has eroded confidence in our government at home and abroad. This administration believes that secrecy is a virtue and that questioning authority is a vice. I do not. I believe honesty is the first obligation of leadership, and accountability is not an option. This campaign will offer a clear alternative to a politics that demands the American people to stop asking questions.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28108, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18027, \"description\": \"I understand the doubts that many people hold in this hall. This has been a hard year, and not everyone feels heard or represented tonight. But I believe this party is strongest when it refuses to retreat into old divisions. Urban and rural, young and old, reformers and veterans, we are not enemies unless we decide to be. I am asking you to believe that this party can still surprise the country, not by looking backward for safety, but by stepping forward together with confidence and purpose.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28109, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18028, \"description\": \"A lot of voters out there respect when someone is truly independent, even when they don’t agree with every position that comes with it. They can tell when someone is speaking honestly instead of just following the party line, and there’s a real appetite for that right now. I want a running mate who has shown he’s willing to stand apart when it matters, someone who doesn’t shape every word around what party leaders want to hear. That kind of independence can frustrate people inside the system, but it’s exactly what earns trust from people outside of it.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28110, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18028, \"description\": \"The simple truth is this: we spend too much time in Washington talking to each other instead of listening to the people we claim to represent. I want someone who comes out of small town America, someone who talks the way families talk when they’re sitting around a kitchen table worrying about the bills or the future of their farm. A voice like that keeps politics grounded. It reminds the country that the Democratic Party is supposed to stand with people who feel like the system stopped noticing them.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28111, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18028, \"description\": \"I want this campaign to sound like it is thinking about the country ten or twenty years from now, not just about the next election. Americans are starting to understand that the air they breathe and the water they drink are not abstract concerns. They are part of everyday life. And there is someone who has been speaking about conservation and environmental responsibility long before it became fashionable. He would show voters that we are willing to look beyond the latest polls and talk about the future with some honesty for a change.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28113, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18029, \"description\": \"What I’m seeing in this photograph is horrifying, and no one should pretend otherwise. But war is chaotic, and mistakes do happen, especially in a conflict that has dragged on this long and sprawled across borders. If the Pentagon says this was an unintended strike, then that claim deserves to be examined seriously. The real obligation now is to make sure incidents like this don’t happen again, and to press harder for an end to a war that keeps producing them.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28114, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18029, \"description\": \"Is there anything braver about burning up children who live north of the seventeenth parallel or who live in Cambodia or Laos? They all feel pain. They all shed the same blood. They're all children of the same God. I don’t know how anyone can look at that photograph and see anything but a terrified child in unbearable pain. These bombings are indefensible, and these are the kinds of conditions we have to recover if we’re going to save the soul of this nation.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28115, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18029, \"description\": \"This is not an isolated tragedy, and it is not an accident in any meaningful sense. It is the product of a war that should never have been fought and a policy that treats civilian lives as collateral damage. Richard Nixon and Henry Kissinger have expanded this war, hidden its costs, and lied about its consequences. Richard Nixon is a war criminal. This war must end immediately, and the men responsible for carrying it on must be held accountable to the fullest extent possible.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28116, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18029, \"description\": \"What troubles Americans most is the growing distance between what they see with their own eyes and what they are told by their government. Simply put, there is a clear credibility gap today that has undermined the legitimacy of our government. A democracy cannot sustain a long war without honesty from its leaders. If this administration cannot be candid about what is happening in Southeast Asia, then it has no business asking the American people to continue supporting this conflict.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28117, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18030, \"description\": \"I’ve heard that line before, and I understand where it comes from. But the truth is that governing New York City means working every single day with the people who keep the city running. Sanitation workers, transit workers, hospital staff, teachers, police officers. We didn’t always agree on every issue, but we worked things out because the city depends on it. I’ve spent years dealing with the real problems working people face, from wages to housing to the condition of the neighborhoods they live in. Anyone who has watched the job closely knows that.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28118, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18030, \"description\": \"Look, I know I come from a different background than a lot of people in the party’s labor movement. That’s simply the truth. But what matters in politics is not where you started, it’s what you fight for. I’ve stood up for civil rights, for decent housing, for better schools, and for the idea that government should be on the side of people who don’t have lobbyists looking out for them. Those are the same principles that built the Democratic Party coalition in the first place.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28119, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18030, \"description\": \"I didn’t come up through the party machines, and I don’t apologize for that. When I first ran for Mayor, it was because people were tired of the backroom deals, the patronage, the sense that City Hall belonged to corrupt union bosses instead of the public. I’ve stood up for honest government, fair hiring, and public money going where it’s supposed to go instead of disappearing into someone’s pocket. Working people have the most to lose when government is crooked, and they know it. Cleaning that up is not a threat to the Democratic tradition. It’s part of restoring faith that government can actually serve the people who depend on it.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28120, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18030, \"description\": \"I’m not interested in arguing with any loyal Democrat. The way you build trust in politics is by sitting down with people and listening to them. That’s exactly what I intend to do, with the labor leaders, ward organizers, and the folks who carry the Democratic Party on their shoulders every election year. Their interests are my interests; it’s just a matter of showing them. I’m sure they’ll be interested in my new affordable housing plan. Once they know there’s plenty of cream to skim off the top, they’ll be 100 percent committed to our campaign.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28121, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18031, \"description\": \"Anyone who has governed New York City knows it is not an easy job. Eight million people, hundreds of neighborhoods, enormous social and economic pressures, and a government that touches almost every part of daily life. Of course there were conflicts and hard decisions. But leadership is not about avoiding those challenges. It is about facing them honestly and keeping the city moving forward through some of the most turbulent years in modern American life.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28122, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18031, \"description\": \"When I became mayor, New York was entering one of the most difficult periods in its history. Racial tensions were high, the economy was shifting, and cities across the country were experiencing unrest. My responsibility was to keep the city together and prevent those tensions from tearing it apart. We faced those moments without the kind of violence and breakdown that many feared. That required patience, restraint, and a willingness to listen to communities that often felt ignored.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28123, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18031, \"description\": \"If you want to understand the complexity of modern America, spend time governing a city like New York. Every issue the country faces shows up there first. Housing, education, labor relations, policing, immigration, economic change. Those experiences force you to deal with competing interests, difficult tradeoffs, and real consequences for millions of people. That kind of experience prepares a person well for the responsibilities of national leadership.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28124, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18031, \"description\": \"I find it a little strange to hear these kind of lectures from Dick Nixon’s people. From his time in Congress to the Vice Presidency, he’s hardly stepped foot outside Washington. He was never responsible for running a city, never responsible for balancing a municipal budget, never responsible for keeping schools open, transit moving, and neighborhoods functioning day after day. He couldn’t even win the governorship in his own state. Leading a metropolis like New York has forced me to deal with the realities of modern America in all their complexity. That’s something you learn on the ground, not from reading policy papers at Georgetown.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28125, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18032, \"description\": \"An unfortunate, but entirely expected development. All we can do is redouble our campaign efforts on the ground. I’ll visit every major city in the South, meet with local Democrats, and do as many rallies as I can. People need to see that we hear them and understand the challenges they face. Let’s see if we can’t light up some old loyalties the old-fashioned way.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28126, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18032, \"description\": \"John Connally is trying to use his connections to push Democrats into Nixon’s camp, but we have an ace up our sleeve here. It’s no secret that President Johnson and I have our disagreements, but there’s no way he’d ever endorse Nixon. I plan to go to President Johnson’s ranch, talk with him candidly, and ask him to publicly back our ticket. There’s no way Connally’s voice can compete with a heavyweight like Johnson.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28127, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18032, \"description\": \"Four years ago, Connally endorsed Hubert Humphrey in his campaign against Nixon. Today, he completely switches parties. You can’t possibly tell me this has anything to do with his convictions. I’ll make it clear that his endorsement isn’t a mark of wisdom or courage, it’s blatant opportunism, and it isn’t worth a damned nickel. Democrats deserve to know that the man who left his party to chase power in Washington doesn’t represent our values.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28128, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18032, \"description\": \"If John Connally wants to run this “Democrats for Nixon” shtick, then fine. Two can play at that game. I left my old party because of the direction they’ve gone in recent years, and I know plenty of Republicans who are just as uneasy. I’ll start reaching out to some of them myself. Pete McCloskey and Ogden Reid have both shown a willingness to speak their minds, and I understand Don Riegle may soon be making his own decision about where he belongs. Nixon has spent the past four years pandering to the far right. Let’s show the country how many Republicans he’s pushed away in the process.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28129, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18033, \"description\": \"I understand why so many people are angry, and many of the concerns being raised in these protests are completely legitimate. The war, civil rights, and economic inequality have left a lot of Americans feeling shut out. But violence and destruction of property only weaken those causes and drag innocent people into the crossfire, and that’s not something I can support.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28130, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18033, \"description\": \"I’m not going to throw anyone under the bus, let alone so many patriotic Americans speaking out for what they feel is right. Peaceful protest is a constitutional right, and dissent is not the same thing as disorder. It is dangerous to treat protest itself as a threat to the country. President Nixon should be ashamed of himself for his brazen attempts to politicize our freedoms.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28131, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18033, \"description\": \"Look, a lot of these kids are my supporters. And I get it, they’re angry, and they want change. But ultimately, so many of these protests have gone too far, and they have done real damage to the credibility of the very causes these well-meaning young people claim to champion. If people want to change this country, they need to grow up and learn to work within the system instead of disrupting public life and turning voters against them.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28132, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18033, \"description\": \"I’m a proud Democrat, and I am no radical. Anyone who thinks I condone any kind of reckless law-breaking or unprovoked violence is mistaken. If a protest or march turns violent or disrupts the peace, then I support the protesters being punished to the extent the law calls for. To claim I think anything else is just another rumor started by Dick Nixon’s flying monkeys.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28133, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18034, \"description\": \"I’m not running against Spiro Agnew. I’m running against Richard Nixon and his record. The vice president seems to spend his days picking fights and throwing insults, and I don’t intend to join him. I’m going to keep talking about the war, the economy, and the direction of the country, and let voters decide who sounds like a president.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28134, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18034, \"description\": \"Agnew isn’t some independent actor. He says what Nixon wants to say, just louder and cruder. When he attacks protesters, the press, or whole groups of Americans, he’s doing it to distract from the failures of this administration. I’m going to remind people that his tone comes straight from the top.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28135, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18034, \"description\": \"The vice president is deliberately trying to pit Americans against each other. He mocks legitimate dissent, ridicules any disagreement, and treats whole parts of this country like mortal enemies. That might win applause at his campaign rallies, but it’s no way to govern a democracy, and it’s certainly no way to heal a country that’s already deeply divided.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28136, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18034, \"description\": \"I think most Americans are tired of being yelled at by their own government. They want a leadership that lowers the temperature, not raises it. I still believe that character counts in our country. I’m an honest, hard-working man of the prairie, not a lying attack dog who spends his free time golfing.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28137, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18035, \"description\": \"The Knapp Commission exists because my administration refused to sweep corruption under the rug. When serious allegations surfaced, I didn’t look the other way. I created an independent commission, supported whistleblowers, and pushed for reforms to restore integrity to the NYPD. That isn’t a sign of weak leadership. It’s a sign of a mayor willing to confront uncomfortable truths and actually fix problems instead of pretending they don’t exist. Can President Nixon really say the same?\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28138, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18035, \"description\": \"The problems uncovered by the Knapp Commission didn’t begin the day I took office. These practices developed over decades, across multiple administrations, and became deeply embedded in the system. When I became mayor, I inherited those challenges, and instead of ignoring them, I chose to confront them head on. It would have been easier politically to look the other way, but I believed the people of New York deserved to be told the truth. I imagine that’s a hard concept for the president and his cronies to grasp.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28139, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18035, \"description\": \"I find it troubling that Mr. Nixon and Mr. Agnew think exposing corruption in law enforcement is somehow a weakness. Their answer to every urban problem is more force, more crackdowns, and less accountability. But Americans deserve both safety and integrity. Turning a blind eye to corruption doesn’t make communities safer. It undermines public trust and weakens law enforcement. My administration believes you can support the police while also insisting they uphold the law themselves.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28140, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18035, \"description\": \"The Knapp Commission revealed serious problems, and I won’t minimize that. But before you can strengthen a department, you have to clean house. My administration took the difficult first step by exposing corruption and implementing much-needed reforms to the NYPD. Now we must build on that foundation with better training, stronger oversight, and the resources - and funding - police officers need to do their jobs effectively. To say I’m soft on crime couldn’t be farther from the truth.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28141, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18036, \"description\": \"What happened in Munich was a tragedy, plain and simple. Innocent athletes went to represent their countries in peace, and they were murdered instead. Tonight, my thoughts are with the families of the Israeli team and with the German people who tried to protect their guests. Acts like this offend the conscience of all nations, and they deserve our shared grief and resolve to see that such crimes are never repeated.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28142, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18036, \"description\": \"The murder of Israeli athletes in Munich was an act of terror against innocent people and against the very idea of peaceful competition. Israel has the right to exist, the right to security, and the right to see its citizens protected from violence wherever they go. The United States must stand firmly with Israel in condemning this attack and in opposing terrorism in all its forms. There is no political cause that justifies the killing of civilians, ever.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28143, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18036, \"description\": \"What happened in Munich is a grim reminder that violence isn’t confined by borders, or uniforms, or battlefields. It threatens civilians, the international order, and the basic sense of safety people expect in the modern world. The United States must work with its allies to strengthen international cooperation against terrorism, improve intelligence sharing, and ensure that people are safe wherever they go.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28145, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18037, \"description\": \"If we reach out to Wallace, we have to keep the focus on the economic concerns that drove much of his support. Talk about wages, inflation, and the sense that working people have been squeezed while Washington argues over everything else. Make the case that this campaign is serious about those issues and isn’t just speaking to one part of the country. If he sees that we’re addressing the same frustrations his voters feel, he may not endorse us outright, but he could soften his tone and speak more favorably about the campaign.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28146, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18037, \"description\": \"If we reach out to Wallace, we need to ensure he feels respected and included. His supporters feel dismissed by both parties, and that resentment runs deep. Make it clear that we’re not writing anyone off and that there’s room in this campaign for voters who don’t feel at home anywhere else right now. This isn’t about agreeing on everything, it’s about acknowledging that their concerns deserve to be heard. If Wallace believes we’re serious about that, he might be willing to say something positive publicly, even if he stops short of endorsing us.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28147, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18037, \"description\": \"If we reach out to Wallace, we ought to talk to him about the broader need to settle things down politically. The country’s been through years of tension, and there’s an argument to be made for lowering the temperature and trying to bring people back into a shared political space. We can frame our campaign as an attempt to move past the constant division and give voters a sense that things don’t have to stay this polarized. It’s a harder sell, and he may push back, but it at least opens the door to a serious conversation about where the country goes from here.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28148, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18037, \"description\": \"No, we’re not going to seek George Wallace’s endorsement. His record and the kind of politics he’s built his career on are fundamentally at odds with what this campaign is trying to do, especially on civil rights and the broader direction of the party. Going after his backing would raise real questions about where we stand and risk undercutting the coalition we’ve spent months trying to build. If Wallace chooses to stay out of the race, that’s his decision, but we’re not going to invite him in.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28149, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18038, \"description\": \"If Secretary Kissinger’s statement proves true, then that is welcome news for every American family and every Vietnamese family that has paid the price for this war. I hope the administration is finally prepared to bring this conflict to an honorable conclusion, and I will judge their actions by whether the fighting actually stops and our troops truly come home.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28150, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18038, \"description\": \"We have been told for four years now that peace is just around the corner. And in that time, the president has invaded two other countries, authorized civilian bombing campaigns, and has continued to prop up a corrupt dictatorship abroad. I want this war to end as much as anyone, but I’m not going to take these sudden assurances at face value. I will believe peace is at hand when the war is actually over.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28151, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18038, \"description\": \"What the administration is now describing sounds very much like the settlement President Johnson was prepared to accept years ago. The difference is that since then, thousands more Americans and countless Vietnamese have died. If this is where we were always headed, then the real question is why this war was prolonged for so long to begin with.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28152, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18038, \"description\": \"Peace is not something you can announce at a press conference while we still have planes dropping bombs and soldiers knee deep in the Vietnamese mud. Peace means ending the war, bringing our troops home, and stopping the killing now. Anything less is not peace. This war has gone on long enough.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28153, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18039, \"description\": \"These are serious developments, and they deserve to be handled with utmost care. The facts need to be gathered, verified, and examined outside of partisan politics - I fully support Mr. Cronkite’s investigative work on the matter. And I’m calling for a full, independent congressional investigation so the public can know exactly what happened and who was responsible. The rule of law has to speak for itself.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28154, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18039, \"description\": \"When Americans watch a story like this unfold, they don’t just wonder about the kinds of things being done behind closed doors. They ask what kind of country we’re becoming. A democracy can’t survive on this level of secrecy, of paranoia, of political sabotage. We need honesty and integrity back in our public life. We need leaders who believe that winning an election is not worth losing the nation’s conscience.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28155, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18039, \"description\": \"This is a lifeline for our campaign, and it’s all the more reason we have to be careful here. There’s a criminal investigation underway, and that’s where this belongs. If the President says he had no involvement with the break-in this summer, that statement should be taken seriously unless proven otherwise. We shouldn’t rush to judgment or turn every accusation into a political weapon. Let the authorities do their work.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28156, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18039, \"description\": \"This isn’t your everyday dirty campaign trick, Mr. Cronkite is making that increasingly clear. If the White House is involved in breaking into the opposition party’s headquarters, that’s an abuse of power, plain and simple. No president is above the law. If these reports are true, Richard Nixon has no business being a free man, let alone occupying the Oval Office.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28157, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18040, \"description\": \"This election doesn’t have to be about anger or fear or division. It can be about healing a country that’s been pulled apart by war and distrust. I’m asking Americans to look past the noise of the last few years and decide whether we want more chaos, or whether we want to start bringing people back together again, honestly and without illusion.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28158, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18040, \"description\": \"Every day this war continues, more young Americans die for reasons no one can clearly explain anymore. No late promises and no last-minute announcements change that fact. If you believe, as I do, that this war has gone on long enough and that it’s time to bring our sons home, then you don’t have to sacrifice your principles anymore.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28159, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18040, \"description\": \"I’ve spent this campaign talking about the dignity of work, about fair wages, and about economic justice because that’s what most families worry about when the cameras are gone. A fair economy, decent schools, and a government that works for ordinary people instead of the well-connected. That’s what Democrats have fought for since the very beginning, and that’s the choice voters still have in front of them today.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28160, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18040, \"description\": \"The president wants this election to be over quietly, without questions and without accountability. Well I don’t think the country should be run that way. I think the American people deserve straight answers, an end to secret wars and operations, and a government that tells the truth, even when it’s inconvenient. If you want change - real change - then take a leap of faith this Fall and give honesty a chance.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28705, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18627, \"description\": \"To the marginalized, the downtrodden, the forgotten, the left behind, I want to speak to you plainly tonight. I have seen your pain, and I answer the call that brought you here. I answer your call with the golden torch of promise that my brother John set alight twelve years ago. I answer your call with the silver flame of justice that my brother Bobby held near and dear to his heart. These causes were never meant for one family or one moment. They belong to a nation still struggling to build a world of fairness and dignity for all. And if there’s one thing I want each and every one of you to remember tonight, it’s this: no matter how things may seem to change…\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28706, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18627, \"description\": \"For generations, this party has carried the hopes of working people. From the Depression years of Franklin Roosevelt, through the steely resolve of Harry Truman, to the golden promise of my brother John, and the grueling work of Lyndon Johnson. In moments of uncertainty, Democrats have always chosen responsibility over retreat and action over fear. I am proud to stand in that tradition tonight, and I am proud to be your nominee for president this year. But if there’s one thing I want each and every one of you to remember tonight, it’s this: no matter how things may seem to change…\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28707, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18627, \"description\": \"The American people have been asked to accept too many half-truths and too many explanations. They have been told one thing while being shown another, time and time again. That credibility gap has eroded confidence in our government at home and abroad. This administration believes that secrecy is a virtue and that questioning authority is a vice. I do not. I believe honesty is the first obligation of leadership, and accountability is not an option. But if there’s one thing I want each and every one of you to remember tonight, it’s this: no matter how things may seem to change…\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28708, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18627, \"description\": \"I do not seek this moment lightly, and I do not take it for granted. I have known triumph and loss, public duty and private pain, and I have learned the hard way that progress is never guaranteed. It survives only when people are willing to stand up for it and carry it forward. Tonight is not about one man or one election. It is about whether we still believe this country can grow more just with each generation. Because if there’s one thing I want each and every one of you to remember tonight, it’s this: no matter how things may seem to change…\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28709, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18628, \"description\": \"This moment calls for seriousness. The country has watched too many politicians treat government like a stage for clever speeches. I want a running mate who approaches public office like a responsibility that has to be studied and respected. Someone who has spent years thinking carefully about the Constitution, how government is structured, and the way laws actually shape people’s lives. That kind of temperament would show voters that this ticket intends to govern thoughtfully and with purpose.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28710, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18628, \"description\": \"This war has shaken people’s faith in the judgment of their leaders, and we cannot pretend otherwise. I want a running mate who has been willing to ask hard questions about foreign policy and who speaks about America’s power with a sense of restraint and responsibility. Someone who has taken the time to study these decisions and has not been afraid to question them when they no longer made sense. That kind of thoughtfulness matters right now. Strength in leadership is not just about resolve, it’s about knowing the limits of force and being willing to say so.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28711, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18628, \"description\": \"Politics can feel cold and distant to those who are struggling through hardship. I want a running mate whose life story carries a kind of moral authority, someone who has faced his own personal darkness and come back from it with humility, with candor, with grace. That kind of experience shapes how a person sees the role of government and what responsibility really means in public life. When he talks about personal responsibility and redemption, voters recognize he’s speaking from something real, that he’s speaking from the heart.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28713, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18629, \"description\": \"What I’m seeing in this photograph is horrifying, and no one should pretend otherwise. But war is chaotic, and mistakes do happen, especially in a conflict that has dragged on this long and sprawled across borders. If the Pentagon says this was an unintended strike, then that claim deserves to be examined seriously. The real obligation now is to make sure incidents like this don’t happen again, and to press harder for an end to a war that keeps producing them.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28714, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18629, \"description\": \"Is there anything braver about burning up children who live north of the seventeenth parallel or who live in Cambodia or Laos? They all feel pain. They all shed the same blood. They're all children of the same God. I don’t know how anyone can look at that photograph and see anything but a terrified child in unbearable pain. These bombings are indefensible, and these are the kinds of conditions we have to recover if we’re going to save the soul of this nation.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28715, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18629, \"description\": \"This is not an isolated tragedy, and it is not an accident in any meaningful sense. It is the product of a war that should never have been fought and a policy that treats civilian lives as collateral damage. Richard Nixon and Henry Kissinger have expanded this war, hidden its costs, and lied about its consequences. Richard Nixon is a war criminal. This war must end immediately, and the men responsible for carrying it on must be held accountable to the fullest extent possible.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28716, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18629, \"description\": \"What troubles Americans most is the growing distance between what they see with their own eyes and what they are told by their government. Simply put, there is a clear credibility gap today that has undermined the legitimacy of our government. A democracy cannot sustain a long war without honesty from its leaders. If this administration cannot be candid about what is happening in Southeast Asia, then it has no business asking the American people to continue supporting this conflict.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28717, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18630, \"description\": \"The Democratic Party had its disagreements this year. Nobody’s pretending otherwise. But what matters now is that Democrats across the country have come together behind a common purpose. Leaders from every wing of the party, labor, civil rights, students, and local organizations have rallied to this campaign because they know the stakes in November. That unity isn’t a weakness, it’s our legitimacy. It shows that this party has settled its differences and is ready to offer the country a serious alternative to the chaos of the past few years.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28718, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18630, \"description\": \"I didn’t wake up one morning and decide this race needed another candidate. For months people across the party had been asking me to step forward. There was a draft movement growing in the states, the polls showed Democrats looking for new leadership, and I heard the same message from union halls, campuses, and local party leaders. When your party calls on you like that, you don’t sit on the sidelines. You step up and do the job.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28719, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18630, \"description\": \"I understand why the Nixon campaign would rather spread ridiculous charges against me than talk about the issues. The president knows the country is frustrated, and he knows this election isn’t going to be as easy as he hoped. When a campaign starts throwing around desperate attacks that belong in the rubber chicken circuit, it usually means they’re worried about how it’s going to end. I’m happy to let the voters decide whose arguments hold up best.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28720, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18630, \"description\": \"It’s a little rich to hear Richard Nixon questioning anyone’s legitimacy. This is the same man who left Washington in 1960 after losing the presidency, went back to California, and couldn’t even win his own state’s governorship in 1962. In 1968 he came back and won the nomination without even receiving the most votes in the Republican primaries. So I’m not sure he’s in much of a position to lecture anyone about how a candidate earns the support of their party.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28721, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18631, \"description\": \"What happened at Chappaquiddick was a terrible tragedy, and it’s something I’ve carried with me every day since. A young woman lost her life, and no words can ever change that. I’ve accepted responsibility for my actions and answered the questions put to me by the courts and by the public. I understand why people still ask about it, and I don’t shy away from that. All I can do is continue the work I’ve tried to dedicate my life to and prove, through service and leadership, that I’ve learned from that terrible night.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28722, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18631, \"description\": \"The events at Chappaquiddick have been investigated and discussed at great length, and I’ve spoken about them openly before the people of Massachusetts and the country. I understand why it remains part of the public conversation. But this election is about more than one man’s past. It’s about the future of the country, about ending a war, strengthening our economy, and restoring confidence in government. I intend to keep this campaign focused on those responsibilities.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28723, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18631, \"description\": \"I’m not surprised the Nixon campaign wants to drag this campaign into the gutter. That’s the kind of politics they’ve practiced for years. Instead of talking about their record, they rely on whispers, leaks, and insinuations meant to tear people down. I believe the American people are better than that. They want a campaign about the country’s future, not one built on rumors and political dirty tricks.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28724, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18631, \"description\": \"There’s no way to satisfy everyone on this horrible tragedy. When the subject comes up in the press, I’ll answer any questions briefly and respectfully, because a young woman lost her life in this incident. But we can’t let this define our campaign. If the president wants to make character an issue, then the American people deserve the full story. They deserve to hear about the money and favors he’s given to his friends in South Florida, about the six figures in loan money they’ve gotten from Howard Hughes, about the long history of financial questions that have followed Tricky Dick all the way back to the Checkers Scandal in 1952. If they want a debate about integrity in public life, we’re ready to have it.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28725, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18632, \"description\": \"An unfortunate, but entirely expected development. All we can do is redouble our campaign efforts on the ground. I’ll visit every major city in the South, meet with local Democrats, and do as many rallies as I can. People need to see that we hear them and understand the challenges they face. Let’s see if we can’t light up some old loyalties the old-fashioned way.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28726, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18632, \"description\": \"John Connally is trying to use his connections to push Democrats into Nixon’s camp, but we have an ace up our sleeve here. It’s no secret that President Johnson and I have our disagreements, but there’s no way he’d ever endorse Nixon. I plan to go to President Johnson’s ranch, talk with him candidly, and ask him to publicly back our ticket. There’s no way Connally’s voice can compete with a heavyweight like Johnson.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28727, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18632, \"description\": \"Four years ago, Connally endorsed Hubert Humphrey in his campaign against Nixon. Today, he completely switches parties. You can’t possibly tell me this has anything to do with his convictions. I’ll make it clear that his endorsement isn’t a mark of wisdom or courage, it’s blatant opportunism, and it isn’t worth a damned nickel. Democrats deserve to know that the man who left his party to chase power in Washington doesn’t represent our values.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28728, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18632, \"description\": \"If John Connally wants to run this ‘Democrats for Nixon’ shtick, then fine. Two can play at that game. I’ll reach out to some former and even current Republicans who share our concerns. John Lindsay can take the lead, and see if we can’t get in touch with McCloskey and Reid. I hear Don Riegle might be switching over soon too. Nixon has spent the past four years pandering to the far right. Let’s show the voters just how many Republicans he’s chased away.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28729, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18633, \"description\": \"I understand why so many people are angry, and many of the concerns being raised in these protests are completely legitimate. The war, civil rights, and economic inequality have left a lot of Americans feeling shut out. But violence and destruction of property only weaken those causes and drag innocent people into the crossfire, and that’s not something I can support.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28730, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18633, \"description\": \"I’m not going to throw anyone under the bus, let alone so many patriotic Americans speaking out for what they feel is right. Peaceful protest is a constitutional right, and dissent is not the same thing as disorder. It is dangerous to treat protest itself as a threat to the country. President Nixon should be ashamed of himself for his brazen attempts to politicize our freedoms.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28731, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18633, \"description\": \"Look, a lot of these kids are my supporters. And I get it, they’re angry, and they want change. But ultimately, so many of these protests have gone too far, and they have done real damage to the credibility of the very causes these well-meaning young people claim to champion. If people want to change this country, they need to grow up and learn to work within the system instead of disrupting public life and turning voters against them.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28732, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18633, \"description\": \"I’m a proud Democrat, and I am no radical. Anyone who thinks I condone any kind of reckless law-breaking or unprovoked violence is mistaken. If a protest or march turns violent or disrupts the peace, then I support the protesters being punished to the extent the law calls for. To claim I think anything else is just another rumor started by Dick Nixon’s flying monkeys.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28733, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18634, \"description\": \"I’m not running against Spiro Agnew. I’m running against Richard Nixon and his record. The vice president seems to spend his days picking fights and throwing insults, and I don’t intend to join him. I’m going to keep talking about the war, the economy, and the direction of the country, and let voters decide who sounds like a president.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28734, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18634, \"description\": \"Agnew isn’t some independent actor. He says what Nixon wants to say, just louder and cruder. When he attacks protesters, the press, or whole groups of Americans, he’s doing it to distract from the failures of this administration. I’m going to remind people that his tone comes straight from the top.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28735, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18634, \"description\": \"The vice president is deliberately trying to pit Americans against each other. He mocks legitimate dissent, ridicules any disagreement, and treats whole parts of this country like mortal enemies. That might win applause at his campaign rallies, but it’s no way to govern a democracy, and it’s certainly no way to heal a country that’s already deeply divided.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28736, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18634, \"description\": \"I think most Americans are tired of being yelled at by their own government. They want a leadership that lowers the temperature, not raises it. I still believe that character counts in our country. I’m an honest, hard-working man of the prairie, not a lying attack dog who spends his free time golfing.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28737, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18635, \"description\": \"Political campaigns have a way of producing a lot of rumors, especially when a candidate is gaining momentum. I’ve spent my adult life in public service, and people can look at that record for themselves. They can see the work we’ve done on civil rights, education, health care, and the needs of working families. That’s the real measure of a candidate, not the gossip that gets passed around during an election year.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28738, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18635, \"description\": \"I’m not going to pretend I’ve lived my life under a microscope without ever making mistakes. No one who serves in public life can say that honestly. What matters is whether you take your responsibilities seriously and whether you’re prepared to do the hard work the job demands. I believe my years in the Senate and the causes I’ve fought for show that commitment clearly enough.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28739, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18635, \"description\": \"I suppose I should plead guilty to enjoying a drink now and then. I didn’t realize the president had become a prohibitionist - I sure hope his newfound party doesn’t hear about it. The truth is, most campaigns have always had a way of turning ordinary human habits into scandalous headlines. The American people understand that public servants are still people. What matters is whether you show up every day ready to do the work and carry the responsibilities of the office. That’s what I’ve tried to do in the Senate, and that’s the standard I’ll bring to this campaign.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28740, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18635, \"description\": \"This is yet another case of hypocrisy coming from the Nixon White House. Anyone who has spent time around Washington knows the president himself has never exactly been a stranger to a late night or a stiff drink. I’m not interested in turning this election into a contest over who can spread the most rumors about the other fellow. The country deserves a more serious debate than that.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28741, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18636, \"description\": \"What happened in Munich was a tragedy, plain and simple. Innocent athletes went to represent their countries in peace, and they were murdered instead. Tonight, my thoughts are with the families of the Israeli team and with the German people who tried to protect their guests. Acts like this offend the conscience of all nations, and they deserve our shared grief and resolve to see that such crimes are never repeated.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28742, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18636, \"description\": \"The murder of Israeli athletes in Munich was an act of terror against innocent people and against the very idea of peaceful competition. Israel has the right to exist, the right to security, and the right to see its citizens protected from violence wherever they go. The United States must stand firmly with Israel in condemning this attack and in opposing terrorism in all its forms. There is no political cause that justifies the killing of civilians, ever.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28743, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18636, \"description\": \"What happened in Munich is a grim reminder that violence isn’t confined by borders, or uniforms, or battlefields. It threatens civilians, the international order, and the basic sense of safety people expect in the modern world. The United States must work with its allies to strengthen international cooperation against terrorism, improve intelligence sharing, and ensure that people are safe wherever they go.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28745, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18637, \"description\": \"If we reach out to Wallace, we have to keep the focus on the economic concerns that drove much of his support. Talk about wages, inflation, and the sense that working people have been squeezed while Washington argues over everything else. Make the case that this campaign is serious about those issues and isn’t just speaking to one part of the country. If he sees that we’re addressing the same frustrations his voters feel, he may not endorse us outright, but he could soften his tone and speak more favorably about the campaign.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28746, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18637, \"description\": \"If we reach out to Wallace, we need to ensure he feels respected and included. His supporters feel dismissed by both parties, and that resentment runs deep. Make it clear that we’re not writing anyone off and that there’s room in this campaign for voters who don’t feel at home anywhere else right now. This isn’t about agreeing on everything, it’s about acknowledging that their concerns deserve to be heard. If Wallace believes we’re serious about that, he might be willing to say something positive publicly, even if he stops short of endorsing us.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28747, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18637, \"description\": \"If we reach out to Wallace, we ought to talk to him about the broader need to settle things down politically. The country’s been through years of tension, and there’s an argument to be made for lowering the temperature and trying to bring people back into a shared political space. We can frame our campaign as an attempt to move past the constant division and give voters a sense that things don’t have to stay this polarized. It’s a harder sell, and he may push back, but it at least opens the door to a serious conversation about where the country goes from here.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28748, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18637, \"description\": \"No, we’re not going to seek George Wallace’s endorsement. His record and the kind of politics he’s built his career on are fundamentally at odds with what this campaign is trying to do, especially on civil rights and the broader direction of the party. Going after his backing would raise real questions about where we stand and risk undercutting the coalition we’ve spent months trying to build. If Wallace chooses to stay out of the race, that’s his decision, but we’re not going to invite him in.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28749, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18638, \"description\": \"If Secretary Kissinger’s statement proves true, then that is welcome news for every American family and every Vietnamese family that has paid the price for this war. I hope the administration is finally prepared to bring this conflict to an honorable conclusion, and I will judge their actions by whether the fighting actually stops and our troops truly come home.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28750, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18638, \"description\": \"We have been told for four years now that peace is just around the corner. And in that time, the president has invaded two other countries, authorized civilian bombing campaigns, and has continued to prop up a corrupt dictatorship abroad. I want this war to end as much as anyone, but I’m not going to take these sudden assurances at face value. I will believe peace is at hand when the war is actually over.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28751, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18638, \"description\": \"What the administration is now describing sounds very much like the settlement President Johnson was prepared to accept years ago. The difference is that since then, thousands more Americans and countless Vietnamese have died. If this is where we were always headed, then the real question is why this war was prolonged for so long to begin with.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28752, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18638, \"description\": \"Peace is not something you can announce at a press conference while we still have planes dropping bombs and soldiers knee deep in the Vietnamese mud. Peace means ending the war, bringing our troops home, and stopping the killing now. Anything less is not peace. This war has gone on long enough.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28753, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18639, \"description\": \"These are serious developments, and they deserve to be handled with utmost care. The facts need to be gathered, verified, and examined outside of partisan politics - I fully support Mr. Cronkite’s investigative work on the matter. And I’m calling for a full, independent congressional investigation so the public can know exactly what happened and who was responsible. The rule of law has to speak for itself.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28754, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18639, \"description\": \"When Americans watch a story like this unfold, they don’t just wonder about the kinds of things being done behind closed doors. They ask what kind of country we’re becoming. A democracy can’t survive on this level of secrecy, of paranoia, of political sabotage. We need honesty and integrity back in our public life. We need leaders who believe that winning an election is not worth losing the nation’s conscience.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28755, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18639, \"description\": \"This is a lifeline for our campaign, and it’s all the more reason we have to be careful here. There’s a criminal investigation underway, and that’s where this belongs. If the President says he had no involvement with the break-in this summer, that statement should be taken seriously unless proven otherwise. We shouldn’t rush to judgment or turn every accusation into a political weapon. Let the authorities do their work.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28756, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18639, \"description\": \"This isn’t your everyday dirty campaign trick, Mr. Cronkite is making that increasingly clear. If the White House is involved in breaking into the opposition party’s headquarters, that’s an abuse of power, plain and simple. No president is above the law. If these reports are true, Richard Nixon has no business being a free man, let alone occupying the Oval Office.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28757, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18640, \"description\": \"This election doesn’t have to be about anger or fear or division. It can be about healing a country that’s been pulled apart by war and distrust. I’m asking Americans to look past the noise of the last few years and decide whether we want more chaos, or whether we want to start bringing people back together again, honestly and without illusion.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28758, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18640, \"description\": \"Every day this war continues, more young Americans die for reasons no one can clearly explain anymore. No late promises and no last-minute announcements change that fact. If you believe, as I do, that this war has gone on long enough and that it’s time to bring our sons home, then you don’t have to sacrifice your principles anymore.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28759, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18640, \"description\": \"I’ve spent this campaign talking about the dignity of work, about fair wages, and about economic justice because that’s what most families worry about when the cameras are gone. A fair economy, decent schools, and a government that works for ordinary people instead of the well-connected. That’s what Democrats have fought for since the very beginning, and that’s the choice voters still have in front of them today.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer\", \"pk\": 28760, \"fields\": {\"question\": 18640, \"description\": \"The president wants this election to be over quietly, without questions and without accountability. Well I don’t think the country should be run that way. I think the American people deserve straight answers, an end to secret wars and operations, and a government that tells the truth, even when it’s inconvenient. If you want change - real change - then take a leap of faith this Fall and give liberalism a chance.\"}}]");

