campaignTrail_temp.answer_feedback_json = JSON.parse("[{\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5001, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2001, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"You can feel the relief in the room. The old-money donors nod almost unanimously - they like that you’re respecting the game they know. At the same time, some of your staff can’t help but grimace, thinking about how distant these meetings feel from the grassroots. You’re securing a lot of cash, but you’re also reminding the younger activists that their energy alone won’t pay the bills.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5002, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2002, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Your staff perk up as you talk about the importance of small dollar donations. There’s excitement in the room, people start brainstorming small fundraising events and community drives. But the senior leadership looks skeptical. They know the big checks aren’t coming from these groups, and some fear you’re spreading the party too thin. You’re generating some energy at least.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5003, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2003, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"The party bosses laugh and shake heads a little as you rattle off a list of the latest musicians and actors, but they’re intrigued too. Everyone agrees the publicity potential is huge, and quite a few major donations could come from the studio crowd. The risk is obvious though: if you rely too much on glitz and glamor, you might alienate the traditional donors who think the party is chasing style over substance.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5004, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2004, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Labor leaders and state chairs like what they’re hearing. That you remember them after the mess last year. Everyone can see this could solidify the backbone of your coalition before the next election. The flip side is that minorities feel left out, and the activist networks might grumble about being ignored. You’re playing the long game here, keeping the party grounded, durable.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5201, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2201, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Some of your staff nod appreciatively, especially the younger organizers who’ve been pushing for more inclusion. There’s a sense of energy in the room, as if something new could finally take hold. At the same time, a few senior figures look uneasy - they worry that focusing on these new demographics could alienate older donors and that the small contributions might not add up to much. Still, everyone recognizes how important the new black bloc is, and if you play your cards right, it could pay off big in key urban areas.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5202, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2202, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"The older Southern congresspeople seem relieved that you’re acknowledging their concerns and history with the party. They talk about the long nights of negotiating in the cloakrooms, helping keep the party afloat in difficult times. Some of your team wince at your subtle nods to the region’s sensitivities, but they can see how this could bring real money back to the party. It’s a careful balancing act, and one wrong step could split the party in two yet again.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5005, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2005, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This definitely calms some nerves, even if you’re not giving Meany everything he wants. Labor appreciates the emphasis on bread-and-butter issues, even if they’re frustrated you won’t move on other things. Reformers and minority leaders certainly feel less threatened. You’re trying to hold labor inside the coalition without letting it dictate the party’s tone, a balancing act that could easily tip either way.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5006, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2006, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Labor is firmly with you after this. Meany feels like he’s really shaping the party’s strategy again. Southern Democrats and old-line state chairs are reassured that the Democrats aren’t abandoning their voters. At the same time, civil rights groups and antiwar organizers are deeply uneasy. You’ve made a conscious choice to shift the party’s public culture rightward, and there will be a backlash.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5007, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2007, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"That’s a slippery move, and everyone in the room knows it. Labor hears enough flexibility to keep pushing their line at the state level. Reformers hear enough distance to keep organizing. Southern parties quietly appreciate the latitude. The cost is clarity. You’ve avoided an immediate rupture for now, but you’re also kicking the can down the road on an unavoidable party split.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5008, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2008, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Your words land like a slap in the face to ol’ George Meany, who stares at you in shock. Reformers and civil rights leaders are relieved, even grateful. Younger staffers feel like they finally know where you stand. But labor leadership is furious, and some union officials are already talking about going around the DNC. Party morale drops sharply in the Midwest. You’ve drawn a bright line in the sand; there’s no turning back now, Fred.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5009, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2009, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Civil rights leaders hear a voice of conviction, and that matters. You’ve made it clear the party isn’t going to flinch just because the backlash is loud. But boy, the backlash is loud. Southern Democrats are pissed, and labor leaders in Northern cities are already warning about angry parents and school walkouts. Party morale takes a major hit, and you’re tying the national party directly to one of the most explosive issues in local politics.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5010, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2010, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Southern Democrats are relieved, and a number of labor leaders quietly agree with you, especially in places where fights over busing have already been brewing. You’ve also eased fears among suburban parents. But civil rights groups are disappointed, and the emergent left worries you’re retreating at the first sign of resistance. Still, it’s not like busing is particularly popular anyway.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5011, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2011, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This feels like a measured position to take, and some in the room are grateful for it. Party leaders recognize a governing mindset. Civil Rights groups appreciate that you didn’t dismiss the idea of busing outright, even if they worry you’re slowing things down. Southern Democrats don’t feel directly attacked. The tradeoff is momentum. You’ve blunted the immediate backlash toward the party, but you’ve also left yourself open to criticism from all sides for not taking a firmer stand.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5012, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2012, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This will buy you some breathing room, at least on the surface. State parties appreciate the latitude, and party leaders see a way to keep the coalition from snapping in half. At least you’ve avoided forcing anyone into a corner. Civil rights groups are uneasy, though, and some quietly accuse you of passing the buck. Regardless, it’s not like busing is particularly popular anyway.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5013, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2013, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"The impoverished underclass is relieved that you’re willing to stand behind what the Democratic Party actually did this past decade. Some party leaders are uneasy, though, considering the emerging backlash to the welfare state. Southern Democrats are also increasingly skeptical, especially as they face pressure on their state budgets. Regardless, the Great Society is still pretty popular with average voters.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5014, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2014, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This’ll reassure a lot of people in the room. Labor leaders like that Medicare and Medicaid aren’t being questioned. Policy staff and governors alike appreciate the ideological flexibility. The left isn’t thrilled, obviously, but they don’t feel threatened either. You’re buying credibility as someone who wants to make the system work, and that’ll pay dividends down the road.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5015, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2015, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Southern Democrats are enormously relieved, especially those facing intense resistance to federal welfare programs at home. Governors and state party chairs feel like you are finally acknowledging the political reality they live with. Labor and civil rights groups are definitely worried about what might happen to the Great Society, though. Not to mention, you’ve pissed off Johnson’s allies to boot. There’s a real anxiety underneath the hood that you can’t ignore.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5016, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2016, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This definitely catches some people off guard. You’re not just defending the Great Society or reacting to Nixon, you’re actually trying to box him in. A few advisors immediately start thinking about whether the White House can really defend the Family Assistance Plan from their own right flank if you push it this way. Others worry you’re giving Nixon’s idea more oxygen than it deserves. Still, it’s clear you’re trying to force a choice and see who flinches first. For the first time this year, it feels like the party is on the offensive again.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5017, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2017, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"You can feel the shift in the room when you finish. Younger staffers and the people who have been talking to antiwar groups look relieved, like someone finally said out loud what they have been thinking for months. Labor advisers are quieter. One of them reminds you that a lot of their members still have kids over there. At the end of the day, the Moratorium protests may be popular with the yuppies, but they’re not the ones leaving flowers in Arlington.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5018, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2018, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Most of the table nods along. This feels like a safe stance to take, and people recognize that. Union advisors appreciate that you’re not forcing them to defend an official endorsement back home. Activists are going to be frustrated, and everyone knows it, but you’ve kept the door open with almost every faction of the party. It’s not inspiring, but it is stabilizing.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5019, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2019, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Some senior figures look visibly relieved. This is the answer they were hoping to hear. Others look uneasy, especially the people tracking youth registration and campus organizing. You’ve drawn a firm line in the sand, and it reassures a lot of traditional Democrats, but it also creates enormous distance with an already militant left. A few advisors speculate on how the party plans to motivate its younger base if this is the message it sends.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5020, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2020, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"A true politician’s answer, which isn’t a bad thing. People see the logic immediately. It gives activists something to point to and gives southern and midwestern Democrats cover when they’re asked where the party stands. Nobody thinks this will satisfy everyone, but it keeps the party in the center of the debate instead of reacting to it. For now, your advisors trust that you’re trying to hold things together rather than duck the issue.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5021, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2021, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Your advisors know this is the clearest possible signal. You are tying the party’s credibility directly to the cause for structural change. Reformers feel validated, and participation surges across the board. At the same time, long-time power brokers are terrified of losing their leverage at the conventions, and some quietly question whether the national party still represents them.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5022, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2022, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This will certainly calm the nerves of party chairs and union leaders. They hear that their authority is safe, at least for now. The problem is that activists and younger voters hear something else entirely. To them, it sounds like the party is offering process tweaks instead of real influence. The anger, the rot, the stench of a party that lost its way, you can’t cover it up forever.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5023, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2023, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Your advisors see this as an attempt to manage the transition rather than resist it. You create space for new voices while keeping experienced leaders involved in the final outcome. It’s a delicate balancing act, but it’s probably the best choice if you want to keep the party from tearing itself apart.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5024, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2024, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"By staying out of the fight, you avoid becoming the focal point of every grievance. That restraint earns you some respect, but it also leaves a vacuum. Reformers organize aggressively inside the commission, while party leaders realize too late that no one is guarding their procedural strengths. When the recommendations arrive, they feel less like a consensus and more like a fait accompli.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5025, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2025, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"It doesn’t cause much of a scene. The phones slow down. A few state chairs sound relieved. In Oklahoma, things cool off, though they don’t snap back to how they were. Some activists are irritated, but most of them saw this coming. Inside the party, people read it the same way you did - it was about whether you could still do the job effectively. For now, stepping aside answers that question.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5029, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2029, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Amidst a major press conference held in D.C.’s warm April sun, you announce your support for the Earth Day protests and the new wave of environmental legislation. There’s no telling how organized labor might feel about this, but if it’s any solace, Senator Nelson has made it clear just how interconnected environmental issues are with worker issues. With your blessing of support, the pressure is on Congress to act.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5030, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2030, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Definitely a more restrained tone coming from the DNC Chair, but if you want to keep Meany in your good graces, that can’t be a bad thing. In any event, the latest push for environmental reform seems to command some level of bipartisan support, so the pressure is on Congress to act.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5031, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2031, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Trying to turn the environment into a states’ rights issue will help in the south, but it doesn’t make much sense elsewhere, especially considering Nixon’s own assertions that the EPA helped clean up a decades-long bureaucratic mess. Some congressional Democrats are unsure as to exactly what the party’s national messaging on the issue really is. The liberals are also a bit put off by your reticence here.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5032, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2032, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Turning the focus of environmental issues onto job creation is a shrewd strategy, though it might just work. In any event, your roundtable discussion convinces Democratic leadership to draft a new <i>Community Cleanup and Industrial Standards Act</i>; known by allies in Congress as the <i>Superfund</i> bill. With generous state-level funding allotted for industrial cleanup programs and equipment production, Nixon’s veto is easily overriden by Congress.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5033, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2033, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Are you insane??? Shock crackles amongst Democrats nationwide as they struggle to explain to their voters why the National DNC Chair is going on record supporting unconditional amnesty for draft dodgers. Your own office lines are blowing up with calls from state party chairs and Congressional leadership, and many southern Democrats have already released statements steadfastly opposing your stance. Even Humphrey and Muskie have a hard time understanding your logic here. Whatever gains the party makes with the emergent left will all but be drowned out by losses elsewhere.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5034, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2034, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"You’re ahead of your time here, Chairman. You can couch your language all you want, but most swing voters aren’t ready to support amnesty for draft dodgers. Still, your words play kindly in the northeast and west coast, and it’s not like the Vietnam War itself is popular. Just keep in mind that much of your party itself is not happy with your stance.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5035, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2035, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This is probably for the best. You can’t possibly commit the whole Democratic Party to supporting such a controversial stance, but it’s not like the Vietnam War itself is popular. The courts are, of course, always the best scapegoat for an unpopular issue. Sometimes not taking a position is the best choice.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5036, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2036, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Interesting pivot. And another shrewd political maneuver. The draft lottery system is enormously controversial. Even if people don’t support the draft dodgers, they certainly don’t like the idea of American boys being selected at random to go fight in Vietnam. Your stance is echoed by Congressional Democratic leadership as well as many 1972 Democratic hopefuls, though you do get a lengthy call from Byrd who proceeds to <i>drone on about the fall of the Roman Empire</i>, for whatever reason.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5037, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2037, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"In a televised press conference, you stand side by side with McGovern, Church, Hughes, and Yarborough as you viciously tear into the president for his incursion into Cambodia. Despite your harsh rhetoric, Nixon’s actions in Indochina have generated enormous opposition from most voters, and with your blessing of support, protesters take to the streets over such flagrantly illegal and dangerous escalation of the war.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5038, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2038, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Why are you letting the president off the hook? The incursion into Cambodia is enormously unpopular, and your statements come across as out of touch with your own party. With support from the Senate’s liberals, protesters are taking to the streets over such a flagrantly illegal and dangerous escalation of the war. Your advisors think this is a major opportunity you’re missing out on.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5039, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2039, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"In a joint press conference with Senate and House leadership, you issue a fierce condemnation of the president’s incursion into Cambodia. Some on the activist left feel you could’ve reached out to the Senate liberals, but Fulbright’s announcement of an investigation from his committee has put the Nixon White House in a tough spot. Not to mention, you’ve received support from across the aisle - John Sherman Cooper and Frank Church have announced a bipartisan congressional effort to limit the president’s authority in Cambodia, much to the GOP’s dismay.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5040, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2040, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"In a joint press conference with Senate and House leadership, you issue a fierce condemnation of the president’s leadership on the Vietnam War. Shortly thereafter, Fulbright’s announcement of an investigation from his committee has put the Nixon White House in a tough spot. Not to mention, you’ve received support from across the aisle - John Sherman Cooper and Frank Church have announced a bipartisan congressional effort to limit the president’s authority in Cambodia, much to the GOP’s dismay.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5041, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2041, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Your heart is in the right place, but polls are showing that most Americans blame the protesters for the violence at Kent State. No matter how right you are on the merits, many moderates and conservatives in your party are loath to agree with you, and you’re facing some intense criticism from Nixon and the GOP. Despite this, some leading Democrats, including Humphrey and Muskie, have expressed tempered opposition to the soldiers’ actions in Ohio. You’ve at least earned some goodwill with the activist left, for what it’s worth.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5042, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2042, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Student protesters wince at your harsh language, but it’s hard to say you have much wiggle room here. A new Gallup poll shows 58% of Americans blame the protesters, while only 11% blame the guardsmen. If you’re trying to keep your party competitive with swing voters in this year’s midterms, this is, unfortunately, the way to go. Despite this, some leading Democrats, including Humphrey and Muskie, have expressed tempered opposition to the soldiers’ actions in Ohio.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5043, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2043, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"You’re more or less reflecting the party mainstream here. Unfortunately for the DNC Chair, the party mainstream isn’t exactly in tune with the voters. A new Gallup poll shows 58% of Americans blame the protesters, while only 11% blame the guardsmen. By trying to walk the tightrope on Kent State, you’ve only incurred mild dissatisfaction from most voters. Despite this, some leading Democrats, including Humphrey and Muskie, have expressed tempered opposition to the soldiers’ actions in Ohio.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5044, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2044, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Your pivot to the so-called Hard Hat Riots in New York has earned you praise from Mayor Lindsay, and your attacks on the Nixon White House certainly sting a little. Regardless, whatever gains your party might make with middle class college educated voters will almost certainly be balanced out by losses with union voters. It doesn’t matter how you word it; attacking Nixon’s response to the riots feels like an attack on the construction workers who protested the antiwar movement. Don’t expect Meany to take your calls any time soon.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5045, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2045, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"You’re echoing the same talking points that helped you return to your old job leading the DNC, much to the chagrin of the activist left. It’s certainly not ideal, but the massive diversity in the Democratic coalition, especially compared to the GOP, means that a Democrat in Mississippi will be very, very different from one in New York.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5046, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2046, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"You’ve pressed this point before, and it’s hard to find someone who disagrees with you here. If you want to keep organized labor happy without alienating the New Left, then taking a bread-and-butter approach to the midterms is probably your best bet. Of course, what that means will certainly be different depending on where you are.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5047, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2047, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Going all in on the party’s liberal flank is a risky strategy, and it’s certainly going to take some explaining to do with organized labor as well as the south. Your party will certainly have a surplus of campus organizers to knock on doors and host candidate rallies, but at the cost of crucial fundraising numbers from the unions and other traditional financial backers.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5047, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2048, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Trying to repair the Democratic Party’s image will certainly take more than one election cycle, but you’ve got to start somewhere. The echoes of 1968 still ring in many voters’ minds. As of now, the Nixon White House is doing its best to portray your party as one of chaos and dysfunction. If you want your party to have a chance come 1972, you’re going to have to find a way around that.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5049, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2049, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"With the tailwinds of public support, you stand side-by-side with a majority of Democrats in defending the Great Society at all costs. It’s not pretty, especially compared to Agnew’s vicious attacks against “lazy freeloaders,” as well as the rising concerns about inflation. But organized labor has your back here, and for now, that’s more than enough.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5050, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2050, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Some liberals are certainly deflated by your flexibility on the Great Society, but if you want to mute any criticism from the right without pissing off the party mainstream, this is the way to go. In any event, buoyed by your statements, President Nixon begins negotiating with Wilbur Mills on a bipartisan welfare reform package, much to the chagrin of the Senate’s liberals.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5051, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2051, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"You’ve made the Senate liberals happy, but the public has little appetite for even further expansions of the Great Society, even if the existing programs are relatively popular overall. The Nixon White House wastes no time in painting you as an out of touch liberal elite. Even leading Democrats are heard hedging on the matter during the nightly news segments.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5052, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2052, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"The Family Assistance Plan earned bipartisan praise last year, though it’s since stalled in Congress due to a lack of consensus on the proposed income floor. You’re certainly taking a big swing by trying to force the president’s hand on the matter, but with your blessing of support, the House has agreed to lower the proposed income floor in exchange for stricter work requirements, teeing up the bill for passage by next year. Just be forewarned that getting it by the Senate is a different beast altogether.\"}},  {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5053, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2053, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Your principles are admirable, and outside the deep south, your statement resonates with a broad majority of Democrats. Civil Rights groups in particular are relieved to see you stand with them against Wallace’s comeback. Despite this, there’s still some concern from organized labor about Wallace’s appeal in the Midwest. You’ve also incurred yourself a nasty public fight with the governor, much to the chagrin of your party.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5054, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2054, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Your stance on Wallace seems far too conciliatory for a politician who ran a third party presidential campaign only two years ago. You’ve caused some consternation amongst your party’s liberals as well as several Civil Rights groups. Behind the scenes, however, Wallace has a certain appeal with working class white ethnics in the north and Midwest. If you’re trying to box him in as a regional candidate, you’re on the right track.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5055, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2055, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Trying to deny oxygen to a demagogue like Wallace is a hard undertaking when he is so strongly adept at commanding public attention. Whether you like it or not, many working class families are concerned about cultural issues as much as they’re concerned about their pocketbooks. Trying to downplay those concerns will only get you so far.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5056, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2056, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Staying quiet on Wallace’s comeback will do you no favors with your party’s liberal wing, nor will it help you stem his appeal. Civil Rights groups in particular are disappointed in your refusal to speak out, especially since many of the party’s leading liberals are issuing their own condemnations of the man. You’re going to have to do something if you want to avoid a repeat of 1968 - or worse, a Wallace nomination outright.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5057, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2057, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"It was worth a shot, I guess. The amendment fails 55-42, with little chance of another vote being brought up any time soon. As for now, the conservatives in your party can’t help but recoil at the DNC Chair hitching the party’s brand to such a radically pacifistic piece of legislation. Agnew wastes no time attacking you as an anti-American communist sympathizer. Behind the scenes, however, you’ll be due a massive dividend if the war continues on its current path. In due time, you tell yourself. In due time.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5058, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2058, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"It’s not like anyone seriously expected you to come out in support of this amendment, considering how unlikely it is to pass. Of course, your silence on the proposal has done you no favors with the party’s left flank. In any event, the amendment fails on a 55-39 vote, though McGovern and his allies have made it clear they intend on trying to pass it again. For now, Nixon’s incursions into Cambodia and Laos will continue unabated.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5059, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2059, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"A good compromise, noble even. Of course, there’s still not enough support to push it through Congress; even with your support and the new revisions to the amendment, it fails on a 55-42 vote. Still, McGovern and his allies are thankful to have your support, even if it’s somewhat conditional. If the war continues on its current path, you’ll no doubt be due a massive dividend.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5060, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2060, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"It’s not like anyone seriously expected you to come out in support of this amendment, considering how unlikely it is to pass. By turning the attention around on Nixon’s ever-shifting explanations, you’re at least able to keep the ball in your court and keep your party united against a common enemy. In any event, the amendment fails on a 55-39 vote, though McGovern and his allies have made it clear they intend on trying to pass it again. For now, Nixon’s incursions into Cambodia and Laos will continue unabated.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5061, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2061, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"The activist class certainly appreciates your kind words, but in spite of the bleeding fucking obvious, most Americans support the Nixon Administration’s crackdown. It’s hard to break the support of the Silent Majority, no matter how brutal the White House has treated the protesters. Regardless, most leading Democrats have issued statements cautiously criticising the president’s response to the protests, so you’re not entirely alone. For now, you’re going to have to do something about the militant New Left, or 1972 could turn into a disaster.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5062, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2062, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Harsh words from the DNC Chair of all people, but you do have a point. Most Americans broadly support the president’s handling of the protests, and still more support the police response to the chaos. You’re bound to get some backlash from McGovern and the other Senate liberals, but it still gets drowned amidst the sea of press coverage anyway. For now, you’re going to have to do something about the militant New Left, or 1972 could turn into a disaster.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5063, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2063, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This is probably the closest you can get to a middle ground here. Of course, what you lose in outspoken opposition from the right, you gain in mild dissatisfaction from both sides. Still, it’s not like you have much room to work with. In any event, most leading Democrats have issued statements cautiously criticising the president’s response to the chaos. For now, you’re going to have to do something about the militant New Left, or 1972 could turn into a disaster.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5064, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2064, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Trying to sideline the protests altogether is leaving a glaring elephant in the room for the rubber chicken circuit. Without so much as a peep from the DNC Chair, it seems more and more that the Democratic Party is one of chaos and disorder, even if the protests weren’t specifically organized by the party. Still, your persistent focus on Congress might pay off; Fulbright has announced a new effort to pass the McGovern-Hatfield Amendment this year. For now, you’re going to have to do something about the militant New Left, or 1972 could turn into a disaster.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5065, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2065, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Ralph Nader’s sensational hearings a few years ago still ring in the ears of twitchy suburban housewives. There is, of course, some skepticism of his proposals from organized labor, as well as parts of the south. But by and large, the emerging consumer movement is enormously popular with the American people, and by hitching onto it, Democrats have a chance to score gains with middle class voters not seen in decades. With your statements of support, the Senate’s liberal bloc has stepped into action, putting the pressure on the Nixon White House to get something passed.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5066, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2066, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"It’s one thing to stand with organized labor; it’s another to leave a golden opportunity on the table. The burgeoning consumer movement is broadly popular, especially with the middle class; why would you give Nixon an opportunity to outflank your party on the issue? Not only that, but you’re leaving the Senate’s liberal bloc to negotiate with the GOP alone, so any consumer rights bill is sure to get watered down by big business. Day by day, the chasm between party leadership and the New Left continues to grow.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5067, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2067, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Ralph Nader’s proposals may be a tough sell with some of organized labor’s more conservative elements, but by and large, the emerging consumer movement is broadly popular with the American people. Trying to bring everyone to the table is your best bet of getting something past Congress. In any event, a new Consumer Product Safety Act begins to make its way through committee - including, among other regulations, a federal agency to regulate consumer goods. A win-win for everyone it seems, as it tees up for bipartisan passage next year.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5068, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2068, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Some advisors think you’re leaving an opportunity on the table here. Despite some tepid opposition from organized labor, Ralph Nader’s proposals are broadly popular with the American people, and particularly with the middle class. Either way, it’s not like it’s a particularly contentious issue. In any event, a bipartisan Consumer Product Safety Act begins to make its way through committee. A win-win for everyone it seems, as it tees up for relatively easy passage next year.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5069, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2069, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Your passion is admirable, and there’s no denying that the revelations concerning the Nixon Administration’s conduct in South Asia is certainly alarming to many voters. Some of your party’s hawks chafe at your strong language, but with polls showing most Americans believe that the Times’ decision to publish the Pentagon Papers was justified, public opinion is at your side. In any event, Ed Muskie is leading some of the early opinion polls, and public pressure continues to build on the Nixon White House. Can our party really make Dick Nixon a one-term president?\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5070, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2070, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Do you? Or do you not? It’s hard to tell, considering you just contradicted yourself three times. Do you not know that Senator Gravel read the Pentagon Papers in a session of Congress? Playing to the center is never really a bad choice, per se, but polls show that most Americans believe the Times’ decision to publish the Pentagon Papers was justified. Either way, your tepid statement is unlikely to break through the media noise one way or another. And in any event, Ed Muskie is leading some of the early opinion polls, and public pressure continues to build on the Nixon White House.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5071, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2071, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Way to read the room, Larry. Public support for the war has reached a new low, and most Americans believe that the Times was justified in publishing the Pentagon Papers. Why would you give Nixon an opening here? Agnew, of course, is wasting no time in taking advantage of your statements to use as a bludgeon for poor Mike. Not to mention, you’re facing quite a bit of criticism from your party’s liberals. Hopefully this doesn’t dampen your party’s enthusiasm in next year’s primaries.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5072, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2072, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"A good statement, a responsible statement. If you want to endorse Senator Gravel’s actions without specifically doing so, this is the way to go. The party mainstream has picked up the “credibility gap” as a solid argument, with Senator Bayh in particular taking the lead. And with polls showing most Americans believe that the Times’ decision to publish the Pentagon Papers was justified, public opinion is at your side. In any event, Ed Muskie is leading some of the early opinion polls, and public pressure continues to build on the Nixon White House. Can our party really make Dick Nixon a one-term president?\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5073, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2073, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This is the safest thing you can say here, as irritating as it is. Nixon has just blown up Bretton Woods, and the public is reacting much better than even the president thought. There’s a sense out there that something had to give, and this makes Democrats look like they understand that. You’re not fighting the economic reset, you’re helping to explain it and give it the bipartisan legitimacy needed to truly work. That matters in a moment where people just want some economic stability again.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5074, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2074, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This is a tough sell in the current climate, both to the average voter and to your party. The country is clearly responding well to Nixon’s actions, and even a lot of Democrats aren’t eager to pick a fight with him. Talking up high interest rates and market discipline reads like you’re trying to unwind what people just experienced as a decisive action. It puts you on the wrong side of both public sentiment and where your own party has stood on fiscal policy for decades.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5075, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2075, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This comes out of left field relative to what voters are actually focused on right now. The Nixon Shock is dominating the conversation because people feel like the system just changed overnight, and they’re broadly in support. Shifting immediately to Vietnam reads like you’re trying to make an ideological statement straight out of the SDS manifesto. Guns and butter may be popular in theory, but what happens when your opponent has more guns than you? You’re not just fighting inflation, you’re fighting the entire Cold War.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5076, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2076, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"An unusual response from you, and one far ahead of its time, but you’re treating the breakdown of Bretton Woods as something that’s already happened and already broadly accepted, and that gets people into asking what comes next. How do we make the economy actually work better after this? No one’s really sure what your proposals would entail, but a growing cadre of new economists are in support.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5077, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2077, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"You sure you’re not on some of that stuff yourself, sir? Your statements are practically lifted straight out of the SDS manifesto; not even George McGovern is going as far as you did. You receive a torrent of criticism from most of your party, organized labor, and much of the press. The Nixon White House pounces at the opportunity as well, with Agnew in particular referring to you as a “hippie bum.” There might’ve been a better way to try to appeal to the New Left.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5078, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2078, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Some on the New Left are no doubt disappointed with your hardline rhetoric, but it’s not like Students for a Democratic Society has any serious base of support outside of Berkeley. Nixon’s War on Drugs is overwhelmingly popular with the American people, and most leading Democrats - including Humphrey and Muskie - have professed support for the new offensive. If you want to restore your party’s reputation with terrified suburbanites, this is a good place to start.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5079, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2079, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"You’ve got good intentions here, but no amount of understanding will be acceptable to the Nixon White House and his ravenous Silent Majority. Agnew wastes no time attacking your position as a trojan horse for drug legalization. Behind the scenes, though, the Senate liberals have quietly affirmed to you that you’ve said the right thing, even if they can’t support you publicly. For now, Nixon’s War on Drugs will continue to maintain bipartisan support.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5080, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2080, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"It takes some effort on the part of Congress, but Nixon agrees to go along with the creation of a commission to study drug policy in America, with former Pennsylvania Governor Raymond Shafer slated to be its chair. Of course, in the meantime, most Democrats have come out in favor of Nixon’s War on Drugs, which continues to maintain bipartisan support.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5081, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2081, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"You’re planting a clear flag here, and most of the party’s liberal wing is in full support. Women’s groups, reform Democrats, and a surprising number of Northeastern Republicans take note of your courage. At the same time, Catholic Democrats and some Southern leaders quietly stiffen. You haven’t blown the coalition apart, but you’ve made it clear which direction the party is moving, and that clarity comes with a real cost in a few states the Democrats are already struggling to hold.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5082, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2082, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This reassures a lot of nervous Democrats who worry the party is sprinting faster than the country. Catholic leaders and Southern officeholders appreciate the restraint, and nobody can accuse you of forcing a litmus test on yet another hot-button issues. Luckily for you, support for abortion rights cuts across party lines, and the opposition is fairly muted, so the only thing your statements are really doing is reinforcing the status quo.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5083, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2083, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"You’re trying to hold the center together, and for the most part, it works. Many voters appreciate the nuance on a still-evolving but divisive issue, and it keeps all factions of your party content for now. Most voters don’t want to see a free-for-all on abortion, but they don’t support unnecessary restrictions either. For now, the issue remains relatively low-salience, so you’ve got that going for you at least.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5084, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2084, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Considering how divisive this emerging issue, this will keep your party out of another internal war. But letting the courts carry the weight has its own risks. When the decision arrives, Democrats will still be expected to answer for it, whether they shaped the outcome or not. Some advisers worry that by staying silent now, you’re surrendering ground to conservative Republicans and women’s rights activists alike.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5085, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2085, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"You’re drawing a line in the sand, and the party regulars appreciate it. Muskie’s allies are relieved to see the national party step in, and many Democrats quietly suspect Republican dirty tricks anyway. Still, some reporters bristle at being told what to investigate, and a few rivals mutter that you’re protecting the frontrunner too aggressively. Either way, Muskie’s initial response seems to have damaged his candidacy, as seen in a weaker-than-expected win in the primary. It’s unclear whether he can truly hold his front-runner status.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5086, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2086, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This strikes most party strategists as the safest course. You’re signaling that the party won’t tolerate any sort of sabotage, while avoiding a greater public spectacle that could overshadow the primary itself. Muskie gets some breathing room without looking propped up by party bosses, and other candidates don’t feel shut out. Either way, Muskie’s initial response seems to have damaged his candidacy, as seen in a weaker-than-expected win in the primary. It’s unclear whether he can truly hold his front-runner status.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5087, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2087, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"You’re betting that voters are smarter than this smear, and many are indeed. Some Democrats admire the party’s refusal to get dragged into the mud, and Muskie’s message stays focused on bread-and-butter issues. But party operatives worry this leaves a vacuum. If these dirty tricks have already worked once, they’ll work again, and organizers on the ground are concerned that the party looks passive when one of its own is being targeted in plain sight. Either way, Muskie’s initial response seems to have damaged his candidacy, as seen in a weaker-than-expected win in the primary. It’s unclear whether he can truly hold his front-runner status.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5088, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2088, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This will appeal to the activist wing of the party, many of whom remember 1968 well. But it lands cold with Muskie’s supporters - including many congressional heavyweights - who feel he’s being left to fend for himself against something bigger than ordinary hardball. Some are worried that Democrats will be stuck with some third-rate kook come July. Either way, Muskie’s initial response seems to have damaged his candidacy, as seen in a weaker-than-expected win in the primary. It’s unclear whether he can truly hold his front-runner status.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5089, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2089, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Once again, the president has the Democrats on the backfoot - a whirlwind tour of Beijing and a landmark meeting with Mao Zedong has stunned pretty much everyone. Even many leading Democrats are at a loss for how to respond. At the very least, foreign policy hands appreciate that you’re not reflexively opposing such a watershed moment of diplomacy just because it has Nixon’s fingerprints on it. But grumbling from the right aside, Dick Nixon will no doubt go down in history on this one.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5090, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2090, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Once again, the president has the Democrats on the backfoot - a whirlwind tour of Beijing and a landmark meeting with Mao Zedong has stunned pretty much everyone. You’ve given a measured response, at least, which plays well with most voters and gives your party some much-needed credibility. Foreign policy hands also appreciate that you’re not reflexively opposing such a watershed moment of diplomacy just because it has Nixon’s fingerprints on it. But grumbling from the right aside, Dick Nixon will no doubt go down in history on this one.\"}},  {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5091, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2091, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"An ambitious stance to take, but it just comes off as desperate pandering. How would any Democrat outdo what Nixon’s accomplished? Once again, the president has the Democrats on the backfoot - a whirlwind tour of Beijing and a landmark meeting with Mao Zedong has stunned pretty much everyone. Grumbling from the right aside, Dick Nixon will no doubt go down in history on this one.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5092, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2092, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Are you trying to make a play for Ashbrook here? You’ve pretty much alienated everyone with this stance. Most leading Democrats chafe at your hawkish turn on China. And it’s not like the conservative right will start voting for Democrats because of the latest statements from the DNC Chair. Once again, the president has the Democrats on the backfoot - a whirlwind tour of Beijing and a landmark meeting with Mao Zedong has stunned pretty much everyone. Grumbling from the right aside, Dick Nixon will no doubt go down in history on this one.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5093, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2093, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This is a safe, standard way to respond in the immediate aftermath of political violence. It keeps the party out of speculation, emphasizes law enforcement’s role, and avoids escalating tensions while details are still unclear. It also helps keep the convention planning insulated from a fast-moving news story, which is especially important with emotions already running high and the press moving in like sharks.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5094, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2094, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Ordinarily, even hinting that a political figure’s rhetoric played a role in something like this would trigger a wave of criticism and condemnation. Wallace is treated differently, though. He’s a fringe figure, with few friends in Washington. Outside the South, the reaction is mostly muted or uncomfortable criticism rather than an angry backlash. Southern and conservative Democrats and editorial writers clutch their pearls, but it doesn’t really gain traction. Wallace himself leans into it, though, and he makes it clear he intends to stay in the race and directly calling you out for your statements.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5095, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2095, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"You’re not wrong about the political dynamics here, but the problem is what comes after Wallace. His support isn’t transferable in any clean way, and people underestimate how personally anchored a lot of it is. Even if he drops out, the anger and hatred surrounding him doesn’t disappear, and there’s no obvious figure who can fully absorb it without losing something essential in the process. At best it fragments into smaller, less predictable blocs, at worst it hardens without him as a release valve and becomes harder to contain. That makes any attempt to redirect his base a lot more unstable than it looks on paper, especially heading into a convention where everyone is already on edge.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5096, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2096, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This is drawing predictable backlash for even implying indirect responsibility from the administration, but it’s not landing as a unified condemnation. There’s a quieter current among some Democrats and commentators who think you’re saying out loud what others only hint at about Nixon’s willingness to push the bounds of political conflict when it suits him. The GOP is attacking your statements hard, though, with Spiro Agnew taking the charge. The White House is demanding a retraction, too, though that obviously doesn’t mean much.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5097, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2097, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"You’ve seen enough conventions to know where this is going. Reformers and antiwar delegates take your statement of support for the prohibitive frontrunner as a green light, and McGovern’s strategists begin planning his acceptance speech. Labor leaders and party regulars, already in a state of panic, put up every effort they can think of to stop him, but you know it’s a futile one.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5098, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2098, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"The eve of the convention is loud and raucous, with congressional leaders and party bosses still testing whether there’s any way to block McGovern’s nomination. Floor speeches drag on and backroom meetings multiply. In the end, the numbers do hold for the insurgent South Dakotan, but the process is sure to leave some scars. Many in the party’s old guard walk away feeling that they lost control of something they once assumed was theirs.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5099, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2099, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"From the opening sessions, you spend your time moving between hotel rooms and committee offices, pulling McGovern delegates into the same conversations as labor leaders and congressional figures. No one trusts anyone else, and no major compromises are reached, but at least they’re talking. On the convention hall, the chaos quiets down into tension as the delegates resign themselves to the inevitable.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5100, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2100, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"It becomes clear almost immediately that you are encouraging the efforts to block McGovern’s nomination. Labor leaders and party bosses respond with urgency, working the phones and probing for a candidate who might meet the moment. McGovern’s delegates react just as fast, openly discussing a walkout and daring the leadership to try them. McGovern himself steps in to cool things down, ultimately agreeing to talk rather than letting the situation spiral. The convention holds its breath.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5101, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2101, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Midnight approaches, and the convention hall settles into an uneasy calm, the kind that comes when everyone realizes that something is truly inevitable and all that’s left is to live with it.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5105, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2105, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This is the speech people will remember, even if almost nobody hears it live. In the convention hall, it cuts through the fatigue and the bitterness without pretending the last few years never happened. It’s inspiring, and unmistakably yours. Once it airs in full the next day, it plays better than expected with voters who are tired of the chaos and cynicism that has taken hold of Washington. It doesn’t solve the party’s problems overnight, but it gives the campaign a moral center to build around.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5106, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2106, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This was the safest way to calm the room and extend an olive branch to the Democratic center. You’re speaking their language and placing yourself squarely in the party’s governing tradition, which helps with the party’s donors, elected officials, and skeptical committee chairs. As a general-election opener, it’s competent and reassuring, though not especially distinctive. At three in the morning, that tradeoff makes sense. It steadies the party, even if it doesn’t yet define the race on your terms.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5107, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2107, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Even late at night, the contrast with Nixon is unmistakable, and it gives the convention some badly needed catharsis. The danger is that it pulls the campaign into an attack contest before most voters have even tuned in. Recordings will travel farther than the context, and some Democrats worry you’re picking a fight before you’ve even fully introduced yourself. It sharpens the stakes, but it also hardens the battle lines very early on.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5108, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2108, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"No one can really blame you for going this route. The hall is half-empty, the country is half-asleep, and burning your acceptance speech now would be a mistake. The following night, you deliver a soaring address to a full national audience, and it lands better than many expected. Voters hear of hope instead of cynicism, of compassion instead of distrust, and <i>Come Home, America!</i> becomes your party’s rallying cry.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5109, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2109, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Why are you letting the president off the hook here? There’s no reason to give Dick Nixon of all people the benefit of the doubt when  a lot of people are asking why he keeps escaping responsibility. At a moment when public opinion is turning, it feels like a missed chance to hold the administration to account, and yet another confirmation to the base that you’re nothing but an establishment stooge.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5110, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2110, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"These are inspiring words that land with real force. Most Americans look at the dike bombings and the napalm photograph with outright disgust, and you’re clearly speaking to that reaction instead of trying to manage it. Your moral clarity plays better than the campaign might have expected, and the president himself simply doesn’t have an answer.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5111, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2111, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This goes too far for the moment. The public is already skeptical of the war and increasingly critical of how it’s being run, and that gives you real leverage. But accusing the president and his chief foreign policy advisor of being criminals? Hard to take seriously when one of them is on the verge of winning the Nobel Prize. Instead of pressing Nixon where he’s weakest, you’re only giving him an opening to change the subject and rally his base. Way to squander an opportunity.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5112, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2112, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This is familiar ground, the standard Democratic plank that has found its way onto the rubber chicken circuit, and that’s both its strength and its limitation. You’re echoing a line most Democrats already agree with, and polling suggests Nixon is increasingly isolated on this question. It doesn’t inspire people the way you had hoped, but it does have broad appeal and keeps the focus where the administration is vulnerable. In a general election, that steadiness has value, even if it doesn’t generate headlines on its own.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5113, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2113, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"The compassion you’re showing is certainly admirable, but this opens a door you can’t close. Admitting you didn’t vet your running mate thoroughly immediately becomes the new headline. While many voters respect the sentiment, party leaders and donors hear a different story. You’ve humanized Senator Eagleton, but you’ve also raised doubts about your own judgment as well, and that’s a problem you didn’t need on top of everything else.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5114, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2114, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This sounds like an attempt to wish this story away, and it isn’t working. The press isn’t going to drop this just because you refuse to engage, and voters don’t appreciate being told what they should care about when a real question is hanging in the air. Instead of protecting the campaign’s focus, this is only going to magnify the issue. Every day you don’t address it, it grows harder to control.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5115, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2115, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This feels like the responsible move in the moment, and that’s why your advisors breathe a sigh of relief. Transparency gives you a way to confront the story head-on and signals that you’re not hiding anything. But it also keeps the issue alive and ties the campaign’s fate to something most voters don’t fully understand. What starts as an effort to clarify this story quickly becomes an endless test of reassurance, and the clock keeps ticking.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5116, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2116, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"It takes several difficult conversations with advisors and party leaders, but you decide that a quick response is the only way to contain the damage. The announcement shocks many supporters and fuels a fresh round of headlines, but there’s also a quiet sense of relief among nervous Democrats who feared the controversy would define your campaign. Still, the whole episode raises major questions about your judgment and leadership abilities.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5117, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2117, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This actually takes the sting out of the attack. By calmly and plainly stating what you do and don’t support, you give middle-of-the-road voters something solid to hold onto when they hear this damaging attack. It reassures people who were starting to wonder if the caricature had any truth to it. You’ll of course annoy some students and activists who were hoping you’d actually take these positions, but electorally that’s a manageable trade. The attack line loses some of its power once it sounds exaggerated.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5118, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2118, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"What on Earth are you doing here? You’ve just leaned into every part of this damaging slogan and treated it like a badge of honor. These positions are well outside where most Americans are in 1972, and saying you’re proud of them only confirms the worst fears Republicans are trying to plant. This is nothing short of political malpractice. Instead of blunting the attack, you’ve turned it into a self-inflicted wound that will follow you everywhere. Are you taking some of that acid yourself?\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5119, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2119, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This sounds like you’re trying to pretend this attack line doesn’t exist. Voters are hearing it whether you like it or not, and refusing to engage leaves the GOP to fill in the blanks themselves. That doesn’t make the attack go away, it lets it fester and define you in the background. Staying on message is important, but so is combatting the Nixon attack machine. Right now, you’re doing the former at the expense of the latter.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5120, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2120, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This is a respectable response, even if it’s not as aggressive as some advisors had hoped. By grounding yourself in your record and personal history, you’re reminding voters of who you really are. It reassures a lot of people who were starting to feel uneasy, even if it doesn’t erase every doubt they had. The slogan itself doesn’t disappear, but it loses some of its bite when it’s stacked against a life story that doesn’t match the smear.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5121, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2121, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"On paper, this looks like the decisive move. In practice, it lands badly. Most voters actually think you should keep Eagleton on your ticket, and replacing him now makes you look both cruel and incompetent. The press spends less time on his past and more time replaying how you selected him, how you defended him, and how quickly you reversed yourself. With your already dismal polling situation worsening by the day, whatever chances you had at winning this election are all but gone now.\"}},  {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5122, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2122, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"You’re a brave man George, but the voters can be unpredictable too. By sticking with Eagleton, the story gradually shifts away from his medical history and toward your willingness to stand by someone under fire. A World War II fighter pilot from the Plains showing compassion to the marginalized? That doesn’t make the controversy disappear, but it sure makes you look good. Many Americans see your strong character here, even if they’re still unsure about your candidacy.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5123, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2123, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"If you’re trying to starve the story of oxygen, that’s not what’s happening. By refusing to address the story and drop Eagleton at the same time, Nixon and the press keep pushing the issue, and your silence gives them room to do it. Most voters think you should keep him, but they also want to hear you explain why. By declining to engage, you let the president frame the narrative, and the campaign stays stuck on defense yet again.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5124, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2124, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This turns the situation around as much as it can be. Tom Eagleton’s well-received interview with Walter Cronkite portrays him as calm, thoughtful, and disarming. He comes across as well-articulated and humble, and for many voters that reassurance matters more than the original revelations about his health. Most Americans already thought you should keep him, and this gives them permission to move on. The story doesn’t vanish, sure, but it does lose its edge outside of some Republican circles, and the campaign finally regains its footing.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5125, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2125, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"An earnest effort, but it undersells the scale of the problem. Retail politics and handshakes can shore up existing local relationships (not that you have many to begin with) and some longtime Democrats appreciate seeing you show up rather than write the South off. Still, Connally isn’t winning over the region because people haven’t met you. He’s giving already-nervous Democrats permission to leave. You’re going to need something bigger to directly blunt the signal he’s sending.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5126, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2126, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This is the strongest move available, even if it doesn’t move the needle much. Johnson may not care for you personally, but he loathes Nixon, and party loyalty still means something to him. A public endorsement from LBJ reminds wavering Democrats that Connally’s defection isn’t out of principle. And the fact that he was once Johnson’s protégé only sharpens the contrast. It doesn’t reverse the damage overnight, but it blunts Connally’s authority in the region.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5127, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2127, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This will feel good to say, and it certainly stings a little for the former governor, but it mostly stays inside the press bubble. Liberal columnists are enjoying the new quip, and Connally’s image takes a small hit among party loyalists who already disliked him. Beyond that, it doesn’t move many votes. Voters who are drifting toward Nixon aren’t especially concerned with petty party politics, and those staying with you didn’t need any convincing.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5128, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2128, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This is an interesting pivot, even if it doesn’t pay dividends right now. Reaching out to disaffected Republicans reframes the election as a referendum against Nixon rather than a Democratic collapse, and figures like Lindsay and McCloskey help make the case. In 1972, it’s a tough sell, and it won’t counter the immediate bleeding in the South. Still, you’re laying the groundwork for a different map, a different future, one where moderate and liberal Republicans have somewhere to go when their own party leaves them behind.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5129, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2129, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This response lands solidly with most voters. You’re drawing a clear line against vigilante violence, which reassures more moderate Democrats who are uneasy with the unrest of late. Some activists grumble that you’re scolding their movement, but the broad electorate largely agrees that any protests should remain peaceful, and they appreciate you saying it plainly.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5130, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2130, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Your base certainly loves this, and many student activists cheer loud and clear. But many swing voters are offended by your defiant defense of what they consider nothing but violent agitators. Most Americans have come to associate any protests with the chaos of the past few years, and this response leaves some with the impression that you’re minimizing those fears. Even worse, the Nixon camp seizes on it as proof that Democrats won’t draw lines.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5131, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2131, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Harsh words from George McGovern of all people, but this plays well with frustrated moderates and conservatives who are exhausted by the constant unrest. Still, it comes at a real cost with young people and movement liberals. Many voters do think the protests have gone too far, but they’re uneasy with such a dismissive tone, which risks making you sound condescending to your own voters rather than presidential.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5132, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2132, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This is a big swing you’re taking, but you successfully blunt Nixon’s law-and-order attacks and reassure anxious swing voters that you won’t tolerate any violence. Still, your language is far more harsh than most Democrats are comfortable with, and some liberals worry you’re echoing the Nixon camp’s own attack lines. It may calm fears in Middle America, but at the expense of much-needed enthusiasm among students and civil liberties-minded Democrats.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5133, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2133, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Most voters don’t like Agnew and they don’t really want to hear more from him, so this lands better than it sounds on paper. You come off as steady and serious, and a lot of people appreciate that you’re not getting dragged into his food fights. Still, some Democrats wish you’d take a swing at him while you have the chance. Nixon certainly doesn’t mind Agnew taking the heat if it means you never quite pin it back on him.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5134, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2134, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This is a surprisingly smart way to handle Spiro Agnew. You don’t treat him like a clown, and you don’t pretend he’s acting on his own either. Voters who already find him obnoxious are reminded that he’s saying exactly what the old Nixon once said before he became the “New Nixon.” It doesn’t flip any conservative voters, but it does make some swing voters a little more uncomfortable with the administration’s tone as a whole.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5135, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2135, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This strikes the right note with a lot of people who feel like the country is being pulled apart. You sound calm, reasonable, and above the fray, which plays well against Agnew’s style. Even voters who agree with some of his law and order rhetoric don’t love the constant sneering. It doesn’t stop him from firing up his base, but it does limit how far it travels.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5136, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2136, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This line gets a chuckle at your rallies and your people enjoy hearing you punch back, but it’s a mixed bag. Some voters like seeing you show a little edge, others think it’s beneath you. And the divide isn’t really on ideology either. Agnew thrives on this kind of exchange, and Nixon would much rather have the race turn into a personality contest than a referendum on the Vietnam War or the economy.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5137, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2137, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This is the most authentic version of your case, even if your proposal is viewed as being too far left. You sound like someone who’s actually met the families caught in the welfare system instead of just reading about them in your briefing papers. Some middle of the road Democrats still get nervous about how big the idea sounds, but at least you don’t come across as naïve or careless.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5138, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2138, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This is a decent way to smooth over the concerns about your proposal, but you’re kidding yourself if you think anyone in the GOP short of Pete McCloskey would touch it with a ten foot pole. Still, everyone can agree the current welfare system is broken, and your pragmatic tone will go a long way with reassuring some skeptical liberals and labor voters.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5139, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2139, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"It might be best to walk back such a radical proposal. It’ll certainly take the edge off the issue, at least. Voters who were uneasy with the proposal feel better with your flexibility, and it’s not like your base is particularly honed in on welfare reform anyway.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5140, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2140, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Your attacks definitely sting a bit, and you are right on the merits. But does the truth really matter when you’re going up against Dick Nixon? Besides, the Family Assistance Plan has been dead for a while now. And no matter how you much you try to couch it, Nixon’s reputation as a conservative will always insulate him from criticisms on the right in a way you could never hope to get away with.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5141, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2141, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This is a safe and humane answer to such a terrible tragedy.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5142, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2142, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"It’s a little cynical, sure, but using the massacre at Munich to shore up your credibility on Israel is more shrewd than some Democrats thought you capable of. You’re certainly leaving no room for Nixon to peel off any more of the Jewish vote.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5143, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2143, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"A statesman-like answer, which is surprising coming from you. You sound serious and informed, which helps boost your lagging credibility on foreign policy.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5145, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2145, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"The conversation stays focused on bread-and-butter issues, and Wallace seems to appreciate the direction you take. As expected, he declines to give you an outright endorsement, but in the days that follow he noticeably softens his tone. When asked about your campaign, he points to concerns you both share over the interests of working people and avoids his usual hemming and hawing. Ultimately, it’s enough to shift some press coverage and make a small dent with his voters.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5146, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2146, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"The conversation is polite, but it ultimately goes nowhere. Wallace listens, nods at parts of what you’re saying, but gives very little back in return. When he speaks publicly afterward, his tone is almost identical to what it was before, keeping his distance and avoiding any clear signal to his supporters. There’s no backlash, but no benefit either. The campaign walks away having spent time and political capital for a result that hardly moves the needle.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5147, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2147, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Unfortunately, the meeting quickly turns tense. Wallace takes offense at the way you frame the conversation around party unity alone, brushing it off as a vague slogan that avoids the real disagreements at hand. He makes it clear he doesn’t see much substance behind it, and the discussion never really recovers. Not long after, he begins taking sharper shots at your campaign in the press, criticizing it as empty and disconnected from the concerns that drove his own support. The attacks don’t reshape the race entirely, but they reinforce doubts among some working-class voters and make them harder to win over this late in the game.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5148, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2148, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Your advisors think you could’ve given it another go, but it’s not likely it would’ve worked anyway. Choosing not to engage with Wallace keeps things exactly where they are - he continues to stay out of the race, offering no endorsement and little commentary beyond his usual remarks. There’s a certain safety in that outcome. You avoid the risks that come with reaching out, but you also give up any chance of influencing how his supporters view your campaign.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5149, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2149, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Even many moderate Democrats recognize Nixon’s statement for the cheap political ploy it is. Why let him off the hook like this?\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5150, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2150, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This is a biting attack on the president, although it probably won’t have too much of an effect. Still, a few reporters manage to ask him what he thinks of your comments before being whisked away by the Secret Service, and when you see it on the news, you can’t help but notice his eyes shift uncomfortably in the aftermath.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5151, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2151, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Your words play well with many moderate Democrats, some of whom are beginning to question whether Hubert Humphrey really was screwed out of the presidency back in ‘68. Still, most voters just want the war to be over.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5152, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2152, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Your moral tone rings hollow against the president’s announcement, no matter how much you try to stay on the offensive. Most voters just want the war to be over.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5153, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2153, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This is the safest and most responsible way to handle this shocking new development, and voters can feel that. You sound presidential and steady - a long way from your image as a hippie radical. Most voters, of course, are still sticking with the president, so you’re right to let the process play out.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5154, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2154, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This response will mean something when it lands. You aren’t making any specific accusations, but you are framing the story as something bigger than a campaign scandal alone. A lot of voters respond well to your tone, and even though most are sticking by the president, it’s become apparent that the American people are getting tired of Nixon’s cynical politics.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5155, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2155, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"You’re too kind for your own good, George. Why would you slow down now, when for months all the signs surrounding the break-in at the Watergate Hotel have pointed to White House involvement? You’re leaving too much space for Nixon to control the narrative. In the end, the press moves on without you shaping the story, and that is a missed opportunity.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5156, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2156, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Way to put your foot in your mouth yet again. Are you really calling for the president to be imprisoned? The Cronkite Report quickly gets subsumed into a larger story of partisan bickering, as Agnew comes to his boss’s defense, accusing you of collaborating with the “pussyfooted liberal media” to slander him. If you were hoping for Cronkite’s segment to land, you’re doing a piss poor job of helping him do it.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5157, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2157, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"A gentle, statesman-like message to close on. Maybe your party was right. Maybe you really are a wild-eyed radical, too far left for the American people. As you walk down the tarmac in Mitchell, you can’t help but wonder where American politics went so wrong.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5158, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2158, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"You know in your gut that Nixon is lying through his teeth. He always does. Do the American people really buy his bullshit? Of course they do, you say to yourself. As you glance out the window of your plane on the way to South Dakota, you can’t help but wonder where American politics went so wrong.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5159, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2159, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"You’ve spent so much of your campaign trying to reach ordinary people and convince them you’re on their side. How Jack and Bobby managed to do it, you’ll never truly know. Your mind begins to race as you think of the possibilities, of the <i>things that never were.</i>. As you glance out the window on the way to your campaign headquarters, you can’t help but wonder where American politics went so wrong.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5160, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2160, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"You try to say it with conviction, not that it matters much in today’s America. How did things change so much from when you were a kid? People used to look out for each other. They used to have each others’ backs. As you board the plane to South Dakota, you can’t help but wonder where American politics went so wrong.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5305, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2305, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"“Come Home, America!” as your acclaimed speech comes to be known. In the convention hall and on millions of TVs nationwide, it cuts through the fatigue and the bitterness without pretending the last few years never happened. It’s inspiring, and unmistakably yours. Voters are tired of the chaos and cynicism that has taken hold of Washington, and you’ve tapped into that feeling with conviction.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5306, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2306, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This was the safest way to calm the room and extend an olive branch to the Democratic center. You’re speaking their language and placing yourself squarely in the party’s governing tradition, which helps with the party’s donors, elected officials, and skeptical committee chairs. As a general-election opener, it’s competent and reassuring, though not especially distinctive. Still, it steadies the party, even if it doesn’t yet define the race on your terms.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5307, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2307, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"The contrast with Nixon is unmistakable, and it gives the convention some badly needed catharsis. The danger is that it pulls the campaign into an attack contest before the general election season has begun. Some Democrats worry you’re picking a fight before you’ve even fully introduced yourself. It sharpens the stakes, but it also hardens the battle lines very early on.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5309, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2309, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Why are you letting the president off the hook here? There’s no reason to give Dick Nixon of all people the benefit of the doubt when  a lot of people are asking why he keeps escaping responsibility. At a moment when public opinion is turning, it feels like a missed chance to hold the administration to account, and yet another confirmation to the base that you’re nothing but an establishment stooge.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5310, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2310, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"These are inspiring words that land with real force. Most Americans look at the dike bombings and the napalm photograph with outright disgust, and you’re clearly speaking to that reaction instead of trying to manage it. Your moral clarity plays better than the campaign might have expected, and the president himself simply doesn’t have an answer.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5311, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2311, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This goes too far for the moment. The public is already skeptical of the war and increasingly critical of how it’s being run, and that gives you real leverage. But accusing the president and his chief foreign policy advisor of being criminals? Hard to take seriously when one of them is on the verge of winning the Nobel Prize. Instead of pressing Nixon where he’s weakest, you’re only giving him an opening to change the subject and rally his base. Way to squander an opportunity.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5312, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2312, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This is familiar ground, the standard Democratic plank that has found its way onto the rubber chicken circuit, and that’s both its strength and its limitation. You’re echoing a line most Democrats already agree with, and polling suggests Nixon is increasingly isolated on this question. It doesn’t inspire people the way you had hoped, but it does have broad appeal and keeps the focus where the administration is vulnerable. In a general election, that steadiness has value, even if it doesn’t generate headlines on its own.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5313, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2313, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"The conversation is tense at first, but your reassurance that White's role will be limited to his campaign efforts helps cool some of their opposition. They're still wary, though, and Kennedy in particular doesn't sound enthusiastic, but by the end of the meeting the threat of open revolt begins to fade. Just be aware that it's unlikely Kennedy will do much campaigning for you.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5314, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2314, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Your meeting with Kennedy and the Massachusetts delegates turns more constructive as you emphasize White's urban leadership and executive experience. Some of the delegates nod along, clearly receptive to the argument that voters care about rising prices and decaying city conditions. Kennedy remains guarded, but he's less openly frustrated by the end. Just be aware that it's unlikely he'll do much campaigning for you.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5315, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2315, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"The emotional history of Dr. King's assassination resonates with several delegates, and even Kennedy softens somewhat as the discussion moves toward White's leadership amidst the nationwide riots four years ago. Their frustration doesn't disappear, but the meeting ends on calmer footing than it began. Just be aware that it's unlikely Kennedy will do much campaigning for you.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5316, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2316, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"After a series of difficult conversations with Kennedy and other party leaders, it becomes clear the divisions are too much to overcome. White's removal from the ticket creates a media circus, and while some delegates worry about the chaos, many feel a sense of relief. It's an abrupt and messy resolution, but the immediate threat of a larger fracture begins to subside.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5513, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2513, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"You sit down with Humphrey and several liberal leaders for a rather frank conversation. Humphrey makes it clear he isn't happy with your selection, but your promise that Mills is only there as a sop to the south helps cool some of the tension. They're still uneasy, though, and some activists remain openly skeptical. You can't just ignore the elephant in the room for the whole campaign.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5514, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2514, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Your meeting with Humphrey turns into a long discussion about the legislative realities of modern America. Your comparison to Lyndon Johnson's selection for Vice President in 1960 doesn't resonate much, but he acknowledges the practical logic. Still, you can't just ignore the elephant in the room for the whole campaign.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5515, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2515, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"It's a cold pill for Humphrey to swallow, but there's no denying Mills' expertise on taxation and fiscal policy. Humphrey still looks uneasy, but he concedes the campaign needs credibility on economic issues. Still, you can't just ignore the elephant in the room for the whole campaign.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5516, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2516, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"After meeting with Humphrey and other party leaders, it becomes clear the divisions are too much to overcome. Mills' removal from the ticket creates a media circus, and while some delegates worry about the chaos, many feel a sense of relief. It's an abrupt and messy resolution, but the immediate threat of a larger fracture begins to subside.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5709, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2709, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This is the kind of choice that reassures pretty much everyone. Walter Mondale is well liked across the party, and with your vote of confidence behind him, the delegates fall in line without much resistance. He helps bridge the gap between organized labor, the party regulars, and your own insurgent base, and the nomination goes through on the first ballot. It may not excite every corner of the convention, but it calms things down in a way your campaign desperately needs.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5710, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2710, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Gaylord Nelson gives the ticket a clear sense of purpose, especially on an emerging issue that younger voters and activists care deeply about. With your vote of confidence behind him, there is enough goodwill in the hall to carry him through on the first ballot. Some of the old guard remain skeptical, but Nelson’s long record of public service helps reassure their concerns, and the broader feeling is that he’s a forward-looking choice who can bridge the growing generational gap in your party.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5711, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2711, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"It takes a bit of convincing on your part, but Reubin Askew brings a different kind of strength to the ticket, one rooted in credibility and reform-minded leadership in the changing South. With your vote of confidence, delegates rally behind him on the first ballot without much trouble. Askew is seen as a thoughtful attempt to broaden the party’s reach without abandoning its direction, and it gives the campaign a much-needed sense of balance heading into the general election.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5712, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2712, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"It takes a while to even get him into a proper conversation about it. When you do, he listens closely and takes it seriously, but it’s clear he’s already thinking about the strain it would put on his own work and responsibilities. After some consideration, he steps away from the offer. There’s no tension in it, just a straightforward refusal that feels more practical than personal. With Kennedy out, attention quickly moves to Sargent Shriver, one of the few remaining figures still willing to step in at this point in the race.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5909, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2909, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This is the kind of choice that reassures pretty much everyone. Walter Mondale is well liked across the party, and with your vote of confidence behind him, the delegates fall in line without much resistance. He helps bridge the gap between organized labor, the party regulars, and your own insurgent base, and the nomination goes through on the first ballot. It may not excite every corner of the convention, but it calms things down in a way your campaign desperately needs.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5910, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2910, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Gaylord Nelson gives the ticket a clear sense of purpose, especially on an emerging issue that younger voters and activists care deeply about. With your vote of confidence behind him, there is enough goodwill in the hall to carry him through on the first ballot. Some of the old guard remain skeptical, but Nelson’s long record of public service helps reassure their concerns, and the broader feeling is that he’s a forward-looking choice who can bridge the growing generational gap in your party.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5911, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2911, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"It takes a bit of convincing on your part, but Reubin Askew brings a different kind of strength to the ticket, one rooted in credibility and reform-minded leadership in the changing South. With your vote of confidence, delegates rally behind him on the first ballot without much trouble. Askew is seen as a thoughtful attempt to broaden the party’s reach without abandoning its direction, and it gives the campaign a much-needed sense of balance heading into the general election.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5912, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2912, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"It takes hours of private meetings and immense personal persuasion, and for a time it seems like your efforts might collapse. But you eventually bring Kennedy on board. Once he agrees, the mood in the hall shifts almost instantly. Delegates who had been divided rally behind the ticket, and labor leaders, liberals, and party regulars alike close ranks. The nomination goes through with overwhelming support, finally giving your campaign some much-needed momentum.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5713, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2713, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Why are you letting the president off the hook here? There’s no reason to give Dick Nixon of all people the benefit of the doubt when  a lot of people are asking why he keeps escaping responsibility. At a moment when public opinion is turning, it feels like a missed chance to hold the administration to account, and yet another confirmation to the base that you’re nothing but an establishment stooge.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5714, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2714, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"These are inspiring words that land with real force. Most Americans look at the dike bombings and the napalm photograph with outright disgust, and you’re clearly speaking to that reaction instead of trying to manage it. Your moral clarity plays better than the campaign might have expected, and the president himself simply doesn’t have an answer.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5715, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2715, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This goes too far for the moment. The public is already skeptical of the war and increasingly critical of how it’s being run, and that gives you real leverage. But accusing the president and his chief foreign policy advisor of being criminals? Hard to take seriously when one of them is on the verge of winning the Nobel Prize. Instead of pressing Nixon where he’s weakest, you’re only giving him an opening to change the subject and rally his base. Way to squander an opportunity.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5716, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2716, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This is familiar ground, the standard Democratic plank that has found its way onto the rubber chicken circuit, and that’s both its strength and its limitation. You’re echoing a line most Democrats already agree with, and polling suggests Nixon is increasingly isolated on this question. It doesn’t inspire people the way you had hoped, but it does have broad appeal and keeps the focus where the administration is vulnerable. In a general election, that steadiness has value, even if it doesn’t generate headlines on its own.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5321, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2321, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"There’s some disappointment among those who think Eleanor could be a real asset, but most people understand where you’re coming from. She seems relieved to stay out of the spotlight, and the campaign avoids any unnecessary strain. It doesn’t change much politically though.\"}},  {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5322, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2322, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This approach lands well with some more conservative elements of the electorate, especially older women and suburban families who respond kindly to your message. At the same time, some younger activists and feminists feel it sells her short. Still, Eleanor proves to be an effective voice on pocketbook concerns, and it helps round out your campaign’s appeal.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5323, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2323, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This is a risky move, but it ultimately pays off. Eleanor’s message cuts through in a way few campaign surrogates can manage, and her appearances resonate deeply with families affected by the war. It’s emotional, but it feels real, and it gives the campaign a powerful connection to voters who have been hard to reach.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5324, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2324, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"You may come to regret how much you’ve thrown her into the spotlight. Eleanor certainly holds her own, but this campaign is not especially gentle, and the extra attention brings its own kind of strain. For all the authenticity it adds, it also opens the door to moments you cannot fully control.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5233, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2233, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Nixon’s pivot on Soviet relations has certainly turned heads, but the Democrats have been the less hawkish party for almost two decades now. On one hand, the party base generally supports a more cooperative position abroad, including nuclear disarmament. On the other hand, they hate Dick Nixon. It’s hard to know what to make of the president’s actions here.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5234, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2234, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Perhaps outflanking the president is the best way to criticise him without abandoning the Democratic Party’s commitments to peace abroad. Still, most voters don’t really care who supports what when the country is still mired in Vietnam, and even some Republicans are looking to reduce tensions with the Soviet bloc. You’ve won points with the emergent left, at least.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5235, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2235, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"You’re definitely siding more with the hawkish wing of your party. Most Democrats, as well as some Republicans, support a cooling of tensions with the Soviets. This is going to put another gap between Democratic leadership and the emerging New Left, and that could cost you serious credibility with the activist base come ‘72.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5236, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2236, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"You’re banging your head against a wall trying to outflank the nation’s most committed Cold Warrior on … well … the Cold War. No matter what Nixon does in the White House, his reputation is far too solid to break. Not only that, but your stridently hawkish statements don’t even play well with most mainstream Democrats, let alone the activist left, who have taken to launching protests outside your office. Your phone lines are blowing up with confusion from Congressional leadership, wondering what they’re supposed to say.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5433, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2433, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Shock crackles through the party as your full-throttled support of the Chicago 7 is played on repeat on national television. The phone lines are blowing up with Congressional leadership, as Albert demands to know how they explain this to his caucus. Several southern Democrats go on record condemning your statements. Meany and the other labor leaders are ignoring your calls, too. Whatever gains your party might make with the emergent left, you’ll lose tenfold elsewhere. What were you thinking?!?\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5434, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2434, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This is the closest you can probably get to a middle ground on such a deeply polarizing issue. You’re certainly giving plenty of room for the moderate and conservative Democrats in Congress to maneuver as they need. Regardless, support for the Chicago 7 of any kind still only polls well with a minority of Americans. Your words do play kindly with the activist left, however.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5435, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2435, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Sometimes it’s best to just bow to public opinion. Your statements gain some flaccid criticism from George McGovern and a few other liberals, but aside from SDS wailing into the void, your position has drawn little controversy. Congressional leadership is certainly relieved, especially with the upcoming Midterm elections.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5436, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2436, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Smart pivot. You can’t possibly come out in support of the Chicago 7, but the Nixon DOJ’s prosecution of antiwar protesters has generated enormous opposition from the activist left. Nixon was, of course, hoping to turn this into another culture war spectacle. But by sidestepping the issue entirely and turning back to policy, you’re giving your party something concrete to latch onto. Congressional leadership, especially, is more than happy to establish a police conduct commission.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5633, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2633, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Pledging your party’s support for forced busing is a risky move. You’ve earned the loyalty of many liberals and black voters, as well as several calls of support from the party’s liberal caucus. But most other Democrats are put off by your strident statements, if not outright opposed. Several southern senators and governors denounce your leadership in a press conference. It might not be a smart idea tying the party’s brand to such a controversial issue.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5634, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2634, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This is the closest you can probably get to a middle ground on such a deeply polarizing issue. You’re certainly giving plenty of room for the moderate and conservative Democrats in Congress to maneuver as they need. Regardless, you’ve traded a base of support from the liberals with tempered dissatisfaction from everyone. At the very least, you’ve given the Nixon Administration little room to work with in attacking your party.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5635, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2635, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Sometimes it’s best to just bow to public opinion. Your statements certainly gain some caustic criticism from the Senate liberals, as well as many Civil Rights leaders. But even amongst some black families, busing is unpopular. And with the president hoping to speed up enforcement of desegregation before the Midterms, you’ve put the Nixon White House on the backfoot in much of middle America.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5636, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2636, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"There’s no shame in trying to sidestep such a polarizing issue. But leaving your party’s congressional and gubernatorial candidates to fend for themselves on the issue without an official party line is risky in its own way. Still, forced busing is enormously controversial, and any statement you make runs the risk of alienating someone in your party.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5253, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2253, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Ouch. Your attacks lead to noticeable damage on Wallace’s reputation in the Midwest, and the media rushes in on the Alabama governor with sensational stories about the conditions of his state’s industrial backwater. Undercutting Wallace’s appeal with union voters is a surefire way to box him in as a regional candidate. Congressional leadership is relieved, and Meany gives you props for how you baited the media. Muskie should certainly have little trouble securing the nomination in ‘72.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5254, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2254, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Your stance on Wallace seems far too conciliatory for a politician who ran a third party presidential campaign only two years ago. You’ve caused some consternation amongst your party’s liberals as well as several Civil Rights groups. Behind the scenes, however, Wallace has a certain appeal with working class white ethnics in the north and Midwest. If you’re trying to box him in as a regional candidate, you’re on the right track.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5255, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2255, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Trying to deny oxygen to a demagogue like Wallace is a hard undertaking when he is so strongly adept at commanding public attention. Whether you like it or not, many working class families are concerned about cultural issues as much as they’re concerned about their pocketbooks. Trying to downplay those concerns will only get you so far.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5256, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2256, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Trying to have it both ways on the Wallace Question isn’t gonna win you any mentions in the next <i>Profiles in Courage</i>, and weighing a racist demagogue against a friend of the Kennedys is certainly off-putting to the party’s liberals. Behind the scenes, some liberals and Civil Rights leaders are worried that your leadership in the party is too hands-off and tolerant of too many ideologies in the tent.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5453, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2453, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Wallace’s comeback bid was enormously controversial, even in Alabama, so it’s unclear just how much of a shock it is that he lost, especially considering the narrow margins of the race. Yarborough’s win, on the other hand, has certainly turned heads; the November elections ultimately hand him a narrow win against Congressman Bush. Either way, you’ve earned yourself two southern liberals as strong allies going forward.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5454, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2454, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Albert Brewer’s winning coalition in Alabama has rocketed the political world and turned Governor Brewer into a Democratic icon, with many in the media declaring George Wallace’s career all but over. Yarborough’s win, though less unusual, has also strengthened the senator’s standing; the November elections ultimately hand him a narrow win against Congressman Bush. Either way, you’ve earned yourself two southern liberals as strong allies going forward.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5455, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2455, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"The “Politics of Substance,” as it comes to be called, is an emerging rallying cry in your party as Democrats seek to undercut the GOP’s growing appeal with working class voters. Wallace was the off-ramp into Nixonland; without the governorship, he has no platform to stand on. Yarborough, of course, is the ultimate foil, the working class Democrat who defies hatred and bigotry; the November elections end up rewarding him with a narrow win against Congressman Bush. Either way, you’ve earned yourself two southern liberals as strong allies going forward.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5456, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2456, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"You’re staying cautious, and that’s not a bad thing. God only knows where the south pins its fury and rage now that Wallace is out of the picture. Regardless, your party’s liberals can’t help but celebrate as its conservative wing takes a beating. Yarborough too, sees his standing gain in strength; the November elections ultimately hand him a narrow win against Congressman Bush. Looks like you’ve earned yourself two southern liberals as strong allies going forward.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5653, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2653, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Many liberals in your party can’t hide their disappointment in Yarborough’s primary defeat. But a new crop of southern Democrats seem to be emerging, displacing the old era of populist firebrands and personality politics. The party bosses certainly breathe a sigh of relief. Wallace’s loss in Alabama is a surefire sign that the times, they are-a changin’.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5654, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2654, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Albert Brewer’s winning coalition in Alabama has rocketed the political world and turned Governor Brewer into a Democratic icon, with many in the media declaring George Wallace’s career all but over. Although many liberals can’t hide their disappointment in Yarborough’s loss, emphasizing the importance of coalition building could help keep the party in their good graces; after all, Yarborough was a rather poor fit for his state anyway. The party bosses certainly breathe a sigh of relief. Wallace’s loss in Alabama is a surefire sign that the times, they are-a changin’.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5655, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2655, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"The “Politics of Substance,” as it comes to be called, is an emerging strategy in your party as Democrats seek to undercut the GOP’s growing appeal with working class voters. Wallace was the off-ramp into Nixonland; without the governorship, he has no platform to stand on. Yarborough’s loss was no doubt disappointing to the liberals, but that only strengthens the contrast; after all, Yarborough was a rather poor fit for his state anyway. The party bosses certainly breathe a sigh of relief. Wallace’s loss in Alabama is a surefire sign that the times, they are-a changin’.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5656, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2656, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"You’re staying cautious, and that’s not a bad thing. God only knows where the south pins its fury and rage now that Wallace is out of the picture. Regardless, your party’s liberals can’t help but celebrate as its conservative wing takes a beating. Yarborough’s loss was no doubt disappointing, but that only strengthens the contrast; after all, Yarborough was a rather poor fit for his state anyway. The party bosses certainly breathe a sigh of relief. Wallace’s loss in Alabama is a surefire sign that the times, they are-a changin’.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5277, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2277, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"You’re asking the Senate to move mountains here, Mr. O’Brien. Under your advice, the Senate’s liberal bloc paints a bullseye on Rehnquist’s reactionary record. The hearings are deeply contentious, but Rehnquist’s forceful defense of his record and impressive legal acumen help sway many of his opponents. In the end, he is confirmed to the Supreme Court with a narrow majority, and Democrats take a major blow to their political capital. For the first time since Nixon’s election, the liberals have lost a court battle.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5278, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2278, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"“The judiciary is a sacred institution, Mr. O’Brien.” Byrd lectures you on the sanctity of the constitution and the separation of powers. “Not every justice can be a bleedin’ heart activist like-uh Mistah Harlan,” he drones on in his southern drawl. Despite Rehnquist’s deeply conservative record, he handles the Senate’s questioning with a forceful defense, and is ultimately confirmed with ease.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5279, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2279, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"It takes some convincing on your part, but by reminding Byrd of the Haynsworth and Carswell nominations, you convince him that the liberal bloc still has some juice left. Byrd ultimately convinces Nixon to withdraw his nomination of Rehnquist in favor of Caspar Weinberger, from OMB, who is confirmed along with Lewis Powell with near unanimous consent. The best you could’ve hoped for, in all likelihood.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5280, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2280, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Some of the senate’s liberals were hoping for support from party leadership, but the simple truth is that the liberal bloc has lost much of its juice in the past few years. Not only that, but Rehnquist’s forceful defense of his record and impressive legal acumen have swayed much of the opposition. He’s confirmed with ease not long after, a disappointing loss for your party’s left flank.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5477, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2477, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Most mainstream politicians support the ERA - which has gained considerable momentum in the past year - but your official statement of support is received warmly with the liberals. In any event, with your party’s support, Griffith’s bill passes the House with flying colors, and tees up for passage in the Senate next year.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5478, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2478, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Your statement of opposition to the ERA lands with an absolute thud, and invokes a torrent of criticism from much of your party. Even Nixon finds himself outflanking you to your left. Griffiths, in particular, takes to the House Floor, to call out the DNC Chair by name for his “reactionary, undemocratic views.” Your advisors are flummoxed as to why you’d come out against such a common-sense bill; most mainstream politicians support the ERA (unless you count a guy like Reagan).\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5479, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2479, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Most mainstream politicians support the ERA - which has gained considerable momentum in the past year - though some liberals would’ve appreciated a formal statement of support. In any event, with your party’s support, Griffith’s bill passes the House with flying colors, and tees up for passage in the Senate next year.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5480, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2480, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"It’s good to support the union vote, but the ERA commands bipartisan support, including from many union workers. Your statements come under quite a bit of criticism from many liberals, with Griffiths in particular calling you out by name for your “outdated, out of touch views.” Interestingly, Nixon himself has remained quiet, likely because his administration has been trying to win over the union vote for over two years now.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5677, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2677, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"You’re making it clear the party isn’t going to blink on voting rights, and Civil Rights leaders are noticing. That kind of clarity reassures activists and minority voters who worry the latest Voting Rights Act will get watered down quietly like so many beforehand. But you’re also asking Southern Democrats to swallow this whole, and some of them are already bristling. You’ve drawn a clean moral line, which strengthens the party’s credibility, even if it costs you goodwill in parts of the South.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5678, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2678, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This strikes a careful balance. You’re not retreating from the law, but you are giving Southern officials a way to save face and stay inside the tent. It certainly won’t satisfy everyone, but it lowers the temperature and gives party leaders something practical to point to besides mandates from the courts. It also helps you blunt any attacks from the Nixon White House. You’re trading some moral clarity for cohesion, and for now, that keeps the coalition from fraying any further.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5679, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2679, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Southern Democrats are certainly happy with what they’re hearing, and that buys you some breathing room with state parties that are already under pressure from their constituents. At the same time, Civil Rights groups are deeply uneasy, worried that this “good faith” will become an excuse to delay any enforcement of black voting rights. Advisors also question whether the party will truly be able to win back the southern vote at the national level. You’ve chosen to manage the region’s resentment rather than confront it, which steadies things in the short term but does you no favor with the party’s liberals.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5680, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2680, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"You’ve avoided a public fight at least, but you’re also ceding the ground. By stepping back, you’ve left enforcement to the courts and the bureaucracy, and neither will really take the political heat off the party. Some Southern leaders appreciate the flexibility, but Civil Rights groups were hoping for more clarity from the DNC Chair. Nixon, of course, certainly won’t miss the opening. He’s perfectly happy to define this debate for you while your party stays on the sidelines.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5281, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2281, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"You’re fully aligning yourself with Kennedy here, and the reaction is exactly what you’d expect. Organized labor and liberal activists are energized, and the party’s base feels reassured that you’re not trimming your sails. The concern, of course, is that you’re tying the party to a proposal that Nixon will attack as reckless at a moment when voters are already uneasy about rising costs. Still, universal healthcare remains popular, and with your party united behind the Kennedy bill, the pressure is on Nixon to get something passed.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5282, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2282, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Your newfound flexibility on healthcare raises eyebrows among labor leaders and some liberals, especially since Nixon has been floating a similar framework himself. Still, enough Democrats stay on board to keep the coalition from cracking, and a handful of moderate Republicans quietly welcome the overlap. In the end, Chairman Mills gives his signature of approval on the so-called Affordable Healthcare Act, which passes the House comfortably and tees up for a Senate vote next year. Just don’t expect Kennedy to let up on the issue.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5283, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2283, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"You’re clearly making a gesture for states rights here, and Southern Democrats take notice. Byrd and Mills both see it as a sign of good faith and help move the American Healthcare Act out of committee, even if everyone understands it’s likely to stall later. Some liberals grumble that you’re bending too far to accommodate the South, but the move buys you breathing room in a region where the party is already under pressure. It’s a not a transformative play, but it is a tactical one.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5284, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2284, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This may calm some nerves among suburban moderates and Southern conservatives who are wary of additional spending commitments, but it also feels like a step back from where the party actually is. Universal healthcare polls well with most Democrats and even some Republicans, and many liberals read this as an unnecessary caution. Advisers worry you’re leaving energy on the table at a moment when the party wants to press forward rather than consolidate what already exists. Don’t expect Kennedy to let up on the issue anytime soon.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5481, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2481, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Civil Rights leaders and younger activists respond enthusiastically to this stance, seeing it as a long-overdue acknowledgment by the party that formal legal equality for blacks hasn’t delivered real results. Inside the party, however, some strategists worry this gives Republicans an easy attack line and could alienate white working-class voters in the North and border states if we’re too careless.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5482, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2482, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Old-line party hands see this as the safest answer, especially in industrial states where racial resentment amongst normally Democratic white ethnics already runs high. It reassures labor leaders and moderate Democrats who fear the politics of backlash. On the other hand, Civil Rights groups are frustrated, with many arguing that this sounds like the party is declaring victory too soon and backing away from Civil Rights enforcement just as the fight shifts to genuine economic inclusion.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5483, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2483, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Many of your party’s strategists view this as the most politically nimble approach. You’re showing black voters and northern liberals that the Democratic Party is serious about ensuring fair hiring practices, while avoiding the language of quotas and coercion. The risk, some warn, is that your proposed incentives may be too weak to satisfy activists who want clear, enforceable standards, and Nixon may still attack the approach as “affirmative action by another name.” But if you’re trying to blunt as much criticism as possible, this is the way to go.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5484, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2484, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Your statements earn praise for reframing the issue away from bureaucratic enforcement and toward economic empowerment. It plays well with more established black leaders focused on long-term institution-building and reassures white ethnics and other moderate Democrats uneasy with the idea of a racial quota system. Still, some advisors caution that you’re dodging the immediate question of discrimination in hiring, and some of the more radical activists are already claiming that your proposals are insufficient in the face of ongoing discrimination. You win some, you lose some.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5681, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2681, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Some of your advisors applaud the clarity and moral force of this approach. Calling out Dick Nixon directly will go a long with energizing Civil Rights groups and northern liberals who are tired of equivocation on the issue. But some of the more electability-minded Democrats quietly worry that this line only hardens southern resistance and risks accelerating Republican gains down-ballot, where the region has remained overwhelmingly Democratic. Nixon, of course, denies accusations of a southern strategy, instead insisting his administration has been “race-blind.” Sleezy bastard, always has been.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5682, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2682, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Party strategists see this as a pragmatic attempt to limit the damage and mute the effects of Nixon’s efforts. By keeping southern races focused on local issues and avoiding national flashpoints, you’re giving the party some much-needed breathing room and denying the GOP an easy target for Congressional gains. Still, liberals and black voters up north grumble that this feels like a retreat on Civil Rights, and there’s concern that the party looks hesitant to defend its own record.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5683, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2683, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This strategy will appease most inside the DNC for its flexibility. It preserves the party’s national commitments while recognizing political realities on the ground in the south. On the other hand, some advisors warn that too much decentralization risks muddying your party’s messaging, and Nixon’s team will exploit any daylight between national Democrats and their southern counterparts.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5684, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2684, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Several senior aides see this as the cold-eyed, long-term play. Shifting your party’s focus to Northern and Western swing states could expand the Democratic coalition and capitalize on fractures in the Republican coalition. But Nixon is no Goldwater, and most Rockefeller Republicans remain content enough with his leadership in the party (unless you’re talking about someone like Javits or McCloskey). Other advisors caution that openly conceding the South, even tactically, could demoralize southern Democrats and make Nixon’s realignment a self-fulfilling prophecy.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5297, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2297, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"With the full backing of organized labor and a solid majority of delegates, party leadership is ready to act. Word moves fast through the delegations. Labor feels reassured. Wavering committee members fall in line. McGovern’s people are furious, of course, but they can see what’s happening. By the time the doors open, the convention is all but ready for the general election.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5298, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2298, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Your advisors question the logic in stretching this out any further, but all you keep thinking about is 1968. McGovern’s delegates believe they still have a shot, and organized labor knows it has to defend its ground in public. The stage is set for a loud, unpredictable convention, with credentials fights and late-night talks looming. Humphrey is still the favorite, of course, but the outcome feels less controlled, and the next few days could get messy.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5299, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2299, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"You spend the night before the convention moving between hotel rooms and union halls, trying to lower the temperature. No one walks away satisfied, but some lines of communication open up. Humphrey’s allies signal a willingness to give ground on the platform, and McGovern’s spokesmen agree to keep their people inside the hall. There’s no real unity yet, but there’s at least a shared interest in not letting things blow up.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5300, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2300, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"A bigger idea. An unusual one too. One that immediately raises eyebrows across the party. The party bosses are listening, though. Humphrey’s camp doesn’t commit to anything, not at first, but the conversation alone changes the dynamic. As the delegates arrive, there’s a sense that the nomination may come with strings attached, and that the final shape of the ticket is still very much in play.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5497, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2497, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"With the full backing of the committee chairs and a solid majority of delegates, party leadership is ready to act. Word moves fast through the delegations. Organized labor feels reassured. Wavering committee members fall in line. McGovern’s people are furious, of course, but they can see what’s happening. By the time the doors open, the convention is all but ready for the general election.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5498, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2498, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Your advisors question the logic in stretching this out any further, but all you keep thinking about is 1968. McGovern’s delegates believe they still have a shot, and the party leadership knows it has to defend its ground in public. The stage is set for a loud, unpredictable convention, with credentials fights and late-night talks looming. Muskie is still the favorite, of course, but the outcome feels less controlled, and the next few days could get messy.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5499, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2499, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"You spend the night before the convention moving between hotel rooms and congressional offices, trying to lower the temperature. No one walks away satisfied, but some lines of communication open up. Muskie’s allies signal a willingness to give ground on the platform, and McGovern’s spokesmen agree to keep their people inside the hall. There’s no real unity yet, but there’s at least a shared interest in not letting things blow up.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5500, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2500, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"A bigger idea. An unusual one too. One that immediately raises eyebrows across the party. The party bosses are listening, though. Muskie’s camp doesn’t commit to anything, not at first, but the conversation alone changes the dynamic. As the delegates arrive, there’s a sense that the nomination may come with strings attached, and that the final shape of the ticket is still very much in play.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5697, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2697, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"You make it clear, quietly and then not so quietly, that a Wallace nomination is a line you will not cross. Phones start ringing immediately. Labor leadership springs into action, governors and congressmen coordinate, and liberal delegations feel a rare moment of relief. The Wallace delegates are furious, of course, but they can also tell the walls are closing in. By the time the convention opens, Wallace still leads on paper, but the anti-Wallace coalition is moving in.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5698, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2698, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Your refusal to intervene shocks much of your party. Wallace’s people take it as a sign of respect and arrive emboldened. Liberals and minority delegates feel exposed and angry, convinced the party is about to repeat its worst mistakes. As the convention’s opening night begins and the momentum slips away, the simple truth becomes unavoidable: George Wallace may be unstoppable after all. You bury your face in your hands.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5699, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2699, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"The delegates regroup after hours of quiet arm twisting and late night backroom meetings, but your effort to find a compromise candidate stalls. Southern and conservative delegates hesitate - they’re simply unwilling to openly block George Wallace and risk the backlash from his supporters. As the convention’s opening night begins and momentum slips away, the simple truth becomes unavoidable: George Wallace may be unstoppable after all. Your bury your face in your hands.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5700, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2700, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"One last olive branch, the only one you can think of. Wallace rejects it outright. He laughs in your face when you bring it up - why would he have any interest in playing second fiddle? He has a plurality of delegates and he knows it. As the convention’s opening night begins and the momentum slips away, the simple truth becomes unavoidable: George Wallace may be unstoppable after all. You bury your face in your hands.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5897, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2897, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"You move quickly and efficiently. Panicked party leaders breathe a sigh of relief, and the phones light up across union offices, governors’ mansions, and Capitol Hill. At the same time, Black delegates, women’s caucuses, and reform groups feel blindsided and furious. What had been excitement hardens into defiance. By the time the convention opens, the nomination is entirely in the air, and the hall is divided into camps that no longer trust one another.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5898, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2898, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"You stay out of the way and let the process run. Chisholm’s supporters take this as a sign of legitimacy and arrive energized and organized. Party regulars are in a state of panic, but they can’t seriously claim the outcome is being engineered against them. The mood is tense. Everyone understands that whatever happens on the floor will carry real consequences, and no one can hide behind party procedure once the gavel comes down.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5899, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2899, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"The delegates regroup as the late night meetings finally wind down, but the fragile opening you tried to create slips away. Reformers grow wary of any move that sidelines Shirley Chisholm, while party leaders can’t agree on a single alternative. A short list of compromise candidates circulates quietly, but none gain enough support to hold the party together. As the convention’s opening night begins and control of the floor slips away, the simple truth becomes unavoidable: Shirley Chisholm may be unstoppable after all. You bury your face in your hands.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5900, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2900, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"You approach Chisholm privately with the only proposal you can think of. She listens carefully. Patiently. After a long silence, you hear a firm, resounding “no.” She makes it clear she did not run to be a symbol or a concession. As the convention’s opening night begins and control of the floor slips away, the simple truth becomes unavoidable: Shirley Chisholm may be unstoppable after all. You bury your face in your hands.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5301, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2301, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Not in a million years did you think you’d see this day. George McGovern has done what Henry Wallace, Estes Kefauver, and Gene McCarthy never could. As the dust settles in Miami, you wonder what’s to become of your beloved Democratic Party.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5501, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2501, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"The room settles on someone whose record speaks for itself without needing to shout it to the rooftops. The McGovern delegates don’t get everything they want, but they recognize the choice as a serious and defensible one. Around the hall, the mood shifts from panic to cautious excitement. The party breathes.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5502, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2502, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"You’re betting that moral weight alone matters more right now than ideology or geography. McGovern’s people soften, some of them visibly, because this feels like you’re showing them real respect rather than just trying to sideline them. At the same time, the party regulars exchange uneasy looks, wondering if reverence alone is enough to carry what comes next.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5503, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2503, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"You’re anchoring the deal in plain language and shrewd political instinct, and that steadies the room for everyone. McGovern’s people don’t erupt with joy, not initially, but they see someone who they know has committed himself to the liberal cause.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5504, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2504, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"An unusual agreement, but you’re just trying to accept where the party is going. The insurgents feel validated immediately, like the convention has finally caught up with them. Across the aisle, veterans of a dozen conventions brace themselves, knowing exactly what this choice will say about where the party is heading.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5701, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2701, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"You say what most people in the hall are already thinking, even if they don’t love hearing it out loud. Humphrey didn’t glide to this moment, and no one mistakes the applause for enthusiasm, but there’s a sense of closure in the room. Organized labor feels vindicated, the congressional leadership feels relieved, and even some of the skeptics on the left quietly accept that the party has made its choice.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 5901, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 2901, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"You say what most people in the hall are already thinking, even if they don’t love hearing it out loud. Humphrey didn’t glide to this moment, and no one mistakes the applause for enthusiasm, but there’s a sense of closure in the room. Organized labor feels vindicated, the congressional leadership feels relieved, and even some of the skeptics on the left quietly accept that the party has made its choice.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 51101, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 21101, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"You strike a tone that reassures the people who were most nervous about where this convention might land. The committee chairs and party donors hear what they wanted to hear, and for the first time all week, the back rooms quiet down. Whatever doubts linger about Muskie’s spark, there is a broad sense that the party has chosen a solid candidate, and for now, that might be good enough.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 51301, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 21301, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"You strike a tone that reassures the people who were most nervous about where this convention might land. The committee chairs and party donors hear what they wanted to hear, and for the first time all week, the back rooms quiet down. Whatever doubts linger about Muskie’s spark, there is a broad sense that the party has chosen a solid candidate, and for now, that might be good enough.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 51501, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 21501, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"You don’t sugarcoat what just happened, and that honesty lands with a lot of delegates who are still rattled. Labor leaders nod along, relieved to hear someone speak plainly about the situation without panicking. Wallace’s exit dominates the headlines, but inside the hall, the focus starts to narrow around Humphrey as the last figure with real institutional gravity. The coalition is different now, and angrier in a lot of places, but it feels real again, and for the first time since the convention opened, the Democrats have a path forward.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 51502, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 21502, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Your tone matches the mood in the room: tired, sober, and wary of any talk of victory. The Wallace walkout dominates the headlines, but Muskie’s name steadies the conversation among members of Congress and party professionals who just want the bleeding to stop. There’s no surge of enthusiasm, but there is acceptance, and in this moment that’s enough. The delegates increasingly begin to frame the choice less as an ideological statement and more as damage control, and Muskie fits that frame almost too well.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 51701, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 21701, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"You watched it happen in real time, and there was nothing left to say once the gavel came down. George Wallace has won the nomination, and with it, redefined the Democratic Party. The protests and walkouts dominate the headlines, the recriminations already spilling into the press before the convention hall even empties. Whatever comes next, you know this much for certain: the party which you led into this convention no longer exists in any recognizable form.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 51901, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 21901, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"You watched it happen in real time, and there was nothing left to say once the gavel came down. George Wallace has won the nomination, and with it, redefined the Democratic Party. The protests and walkouts dominate the headlines, the recriminations already spilling into the press before the convention hall even empties. Whatever comes next, you know this much for certain: the party which you led into this convention no longer exists in any recognizable form.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 52101, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 22101, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"You watched it happen in real time, and there was nothing left to say once the gavel came down. George Wallace has won the nomination, and with it, redefined the Democratic Party. The protests and walkouts dominate the headlines, the recriminations already spilling into the press before the convention hall even empties. Whatever comes next, you know this much for certain: the party which you led into this convention no longer exists in any recognizable form.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 51909, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 21909, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This is a forward-looking defense that helps ease tensions inside the party. By focusing on McGovern’s ability to energize younger voters and communities that feel overlooked, you make the case that his presence strengthens the ticket rather than weakens it. Some older and more moderate Democrats remain uneasy about his reputation, but many recognize the need to expand the party’s coalition.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 51910, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 21910, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This is a deeply personal and issue-driven argument that resonates strongly with antiwar voters and much of the party’s progressive wing. By tying your own evolution on Vietnam to your running mate’s leadership on the issue, you present the ticket as morally grounded and forward-moving. Some of your advisors worry you’re leaning too heavily into the antiwar message at the expense of broader bread-and-butter concerns, but if you want to energize the emerging New Left, this is the way to do it.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 51911, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 21911, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This is solid, effective defense of McGovern’s record. Emphasizing his electoral success in South Dakota will help immensely in pushing back on the idea that he makes your ticket “unelectable.” Voters also respond well to his personal story. While your response may not fully win over every critic, it does a solid job of softening your running mate’s national perception.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 52301, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 22301, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"You don’t sugarcoat what just happened, and that honesty lands with a lot of delegates who are still rattled. Labor leaders nod along, relieved to hear someone speak plainly about the situation without panicking. Chisholm’s walkout dominates the headlines, but inside the hall, the focus starts to narrow around Humphrey as the last figure with real institutional gravity. The coalition is different now, and angrier in a lot of places, but it feels real again, and for the first time since the convention opened, the Democrats have a path forward.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 52302, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 22302, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Your tone matches the mood in the room: tired, sober, and wary of any talk of victory. The Chisholm rebellion dominates the headlines, but Muskie’s name steadies the conversation among members of Congress and party professionals who just want the bleeding to stop. There’s no surge of enthusiasm, but there is acceptance, and in this moment that’s enough. The delegates increasingly begin to frame the choice less as an ideological statement and more as damage control, and Muskie fits that frame almost too well.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 52501, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 22501, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"You never thought you’d see this day, and neither did anyone else. Shirley Chisholm has pulled it off, and she did it on her own terms. The convention hall is electric, and her supporters are ecstatic. Some party leaders are already bracing for November, but there’s no denying she earned this. You did what you could to keep the process orderly, and now the question is whether the rest of the party is ready to follow.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 52701, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 22701, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"You never thought you’d see this day, and neither did anyone else. Shirley Chisholm has pulled it off, and she did it on her own terms. The convention hall is electric, and her supporters are ecstatic. Some party leaders are already bracing for November, but there’s no denying she earned this. You did what you could to keep the process orderly, and now the question is whether the rest of the party is ready to follow.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 52901, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 22901, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"You never thought you’d see this day, and neither did anyone else. Shirley Chisholm has pulled it off, and she did it on her own terms. The convention hall is electric, and her supporters are ecstatic. Some party leaders are already bracing for November, but there’s no denying she earned this. You did what you could to keep the process orderly, and now the question is whether the rest of the party is ready to follow.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 53101, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 23101, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"The room settles on someone whose record speaks for itself without needing to shout it to the rooftops. The McGovern delegates don’t get everything they want, but they recognize the choice as a serious and defensible one. Around the hall, the mood shifts from panic to cautious excitement. The party breathes.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 53102, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 23102, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"You’re betting that moral weight alone matters more right now than ideology or geography. McGovern’s people soften, some of them visibly, because this feels like you’re showing them real respect rather than just trying to sideline them. At the same time, the party regulars exchange uneasy looks, wondering if reverence alone is enough to carry what comes next.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 53103, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 23103, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"You’re anchoring the deal in plain language and shrewd political instinct, and that steadies the room for everyone. McGovern’s people don’t erupt with joy, not initially, but they see someone who they know has committed himself to the liberal cause.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 53104, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 23104, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"An unusual agreement, but you’re just trying to accept where the party is going. The insurgents feel validated immediately, like the convention has finally caught up with them. Across the aisle, veterans of a dozen conventions brace themselves, knowing exactly what this choice will say about where the party is heading.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 53301, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 23301, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"The union leadership relaxes almost immediately. This is a name they know, someone they’ve dealt with across years of negotiations and quiet agreements, and that familiarity matters tonight. It doesn’t electrify the hall, but it restores a sense of order. For the first time in days, labor looks less like a pressure point and more like an anchor holding the convention in place.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 53302, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 23302, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This lands as a bet on credibility first and foremost. Labor leaders listen closely, weighing his record and his relationships, and you can feel them testing whether this is someone they can actually work with when the cameras are gone. There’s no rush of enthusiasm, but there is acceptance. You’ve bought the party some breathing room, even if no one is quite ready to exhale.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 53303, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 23303, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"The reaction in the room is complicated, to say the least. Some labor delegates are drawn to the idea of strength and clarity after days of uncertainty, while others quietly wonder how much leverage they’d really have. Still, the room doesn’t push back. Stability, even imperfect stability, starts to sound appealing. It’s not a unifying moment, but it keeps the unions from drifting away when the convention can least afford it.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 53501, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 23501, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"The response is immediate and predictable in the best possible way. Heads nod, notebooks come out, conversations quiet down. This is someone they’ve worked with, argued with, trusted before. No one mistakes it for inspiration, but that isn’t the point. The congressional leadership and committee chairs feel seen, and for the moment, that’s enough to keep them from hardening their positions.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 53502, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 23502, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This lands as a reasonable compromise, though not an especially comforting one. The governors and congressional leaders take him seriously, even if they’re not entirely convinced he can keep every Democrat in line. Still, the idea feels workable. You can sense people recalculating their expectations, which in a room like this, counts for something.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 53503, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 23503, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"The tension drops noticeably. This is a name that signals order, familiarity, and control of the process itself. No one pretends it answers the deeper questions facing the party, but that’s not why he’s appealing to the men sitting before you. After days of chaos, simply knowing how the party functions feels like relief. The convention doesn’t move forward exactly, not at first, but it stops spiraling.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 53701, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 23701, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"You watch the room shift as you talk. Not much acceptance, not exactly relief either. A bit of enthusiasm, almost? This is a gamble, and everyone knows it, but it’s a gamble rooted in class and geography rather than ideology alone. George Wallace doesn’t own every angry white voter in the South, you assert to the rest of the table, and for the first time all week, people start talking about competing for their votes instead of writing off the whole region.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 53702, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 23702, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This lands quietly, but it lands. No one pretends it’s a silver bullet, and no one mistakes his thoughtful moderation for anything inspiring, but the idea itself feels survivable, livable. You’re offering the party a way to say it hasn’t abandoned the South without surrendering to grievance politics. It doesn’t stop the bleeding entirely, but it slows it enough for people to believe the coalition might hold.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 53703, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 23703, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"The labor leaders don’t smile, but they nod along. This is familiar ground to them, a fight they understand. You’re conceding the Deep South without saying it out loud, and in exchange you’re drawing a hard line around the industrial heartland. It’s defensive, maybe even grim, but after the last few days, maybe that’s for the best?\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 53704, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 23704, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"The reaction is muted, but certainly resigned. This choice doesn’t excite anyone, and that’s exactly why it calms the room. After nights of shouting and brinkmanship, stability starts to sound like a virtue. The insurgents might still bolt, but even if they do, it’s unlikely some third party kook could mean much in a general election. For the first time since the convention spun out of control, the delegates start acting like they might actually leave this hall with a nominee.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 53901, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 23901, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"You’re anchoring the deal in plain language and shrewd political instinct, and that steadies the room for everyone. The Civil Rights leaders and community organizers don’t erupt with joy, not initially, but they see someone who they know has committed himself to the liberal cause.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 53902, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 23902, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"There’s a sense of steadiness that settles over the hall. Some of the insurgent voices shift, not with applause but with cautious nods. They sense that someone is at least trying to meet them halfway. The party elders exchange subtle glances, weighing whether his kind of credibility can hold the coalition together. For now, it seems possible.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 53903, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 23903, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"An unusual agreement, but you’re just trying to accept where the party is going. The insurgents feel validated immediately, like the convention has finally caught up with them. Across the aisle, veterans of a dozen conventions brace themselves, knowing exactly what this choice will say about where the party is heading.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 54101, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 24101, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Only a few seconds actually go by, but it feels like an eternity. The convention hall has quieted throughout the night, the delegates slumping into chairs or wandering the corridors, murmuring amongst themselves. Finally, you hear a sigh on the other end of the line, heavy and deliberate. Then the words you’ve been hoping for: he agrees. Not happily, but he agrees to step into the fray. Relief washes over you, but there’s no time to celebrate yet. There are still alliances to manage, promises to keep, and a convention to guide to the finish. The thread is holding by a single strand, and it’s up to you to make sure it doesn’t snap.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 52709, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 22709, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This is a forward-looking defense that helps ease tensions inside the party. By focusing on McGovern’s ability to energize younger voters and communities that feel overlooked, you make the case that his presence strengthens the ticket rather than weakens it. Some older and more moderate Democrats remain uneasy about his reputation, but many recognize the need to expand the party’s coalition.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 52710, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 22710, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This is a deeply personal and issue-driven argument that resonates strongly with antiwar voters and much of the party’s progressive wing. By tying your own evolution on Vietnam to your running mate’s leadership on the issue, you present the ticket as morally grounded and forward-moving. Some of your advisors worry you’re leaning too heavily into the antiwar message at the expense of broader bread-and-butter concerns, but if you want to energize the emerging New Left, this is the way to do it.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 52711, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 22711, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"This is solid, effective defense of McGovern’s record. Emphasizing his electoral success in South Dakota will help immensely in pushing back on the idea that he makes your ticket “unelectable.” Voters also respond well to his personal story. While your response may not fully win over every critic, it does a solid job of softening your running mate’s national perception.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 51097, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 21097, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Your outreach to Humphrey brings a quiet sense of organization to the floor as union-aligned delegates begin coordinating and floating names. The steadiness draws interest from some party regulars, but insurgents and many minority leaders grow wary, concerned about another 1968 all over again. Southern delegates watch cautiously, waiting to see how much momentum Humphrey can actually build. When you meet with him, labor feels more unified, even as the rest of the convention grows more tense.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 51098, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 21098, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"As word spreads that you’re reaching out to Muskie, governors and party regulars begin quietly aligning around a handful of experienced names. The shift toward stability calms some nerves, but many of the insurgents and black delegates grow suspicious about the decisions forming behind closed doors. Southern leaders remain cautious, unsure whether this effort will account for their priorities. By the time you sit down with Muskie, the room feels more serious, though not necessarily more unified.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 51297, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 21297, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Your outreach to Humphrey brings a quiet sense of organization to the floor as union-aligned delegates begin coordinating and floating names. The steadiness draws interest from some party regulars, but insurgents and many minority leaders grow wary, concerned about another 1968 all over again. Southern delegates watch cautiously, waiting to see how much momentum Humphrey can actually build. When you meet with him, labor feels more unified, even as the rest of the convention grows more tense.\"}}, {\"model\": \"campaign_trail.answer_feedback\", \"pk\": 51298, \"fields\": {\"answer\": 21298, \"candidate\": 201, \"answer_feedback\": \"Word spreads quickly of your outreach to McGovern, and the insurgents begin clustering together, comparing numbers and testing possibilities. Some Black and Hispanic delegates drift toward those conversations, intrigued by where this might lead. Across the floor, labor leaders and party regulars grow uneasy, while Southern delegates react more sha