<h3><b>Address to the Nation from the Oval Office</b></h3>
<p><b>August 8, 1974</b></p>
<p><b>Richard M. Nixon</b></p>
<p><b>Office of the President of the United States</b></p>

<p>The television lights were unusually soft that evening. Washington had the strange stillness that comes after a long crisis, when everyone knows something has already ended but no one is sure what replaces it.</p>

<p>President Richard Nixon sat at the Resolute Desk with a folded speech he had rewritten twice that afternoon. Outside the White House gates, reporters stood in tight clusters waiting for a moment that had been building for two years.</p>

<p>Inside the East Room, staff members watched on a muted monitor. No one spoke.</p>

<p><i>“Good evening,”</i> the President began.</p>

<p><i>“This is the thirty-seventh time I have spoken to you from this office on matters of national importance.”</i></p>

<p>He paused, glancing down at the page.</p>

<p><i>“All of you know the circumstances that have brought us to this moment.”</i></p>

<p>For a moment, he stopped again, as if deciding whether to continue in the same tone.</p>

<p><i>“I have always believed it is the duty of the President to place the interests of the country above all personal considerations.”</i></p>

<p>In the briefing room, aides avoided eye contact with one another.</p>

<p><i>“Over the past year,”</i> Nixon continued, <i>“I have tried to do what I believed was necessary to protect the integrity of the Presidency and to preserve the confidence of the American people.”</i></p>

<p>He swallowed, briefly tightening his grip on the lectern.</p>

<p><i>“But I now recognize that in doing so, I may have placed an additional burden on the nation itself.”</i></p>

<p>A reporter in the press pool leaned forward instinctively.</p>

<p><i>“It is clear to me that I no longer have a strong enough political base in Congress to justify continuing this struggle.”</i></p>

<p>Inside the White House, someone quietly exhaled.</p>

<p><i>“Therefore, effective at noon tomorrow, I will resign the office of the Presidency of the United States.”</i></p>

<p>There was no dramatic pause after the sentence. Only silence.</p>

<p>He continued reading.</p>

<p><i>“In leaving this office, I do so with a profound sense of regret that I was not able to complete the term to which I was elected in 1972.”</i></p>

<p>His voice steadied slightly.</p>

<p><i>“But I also leave with gratitude for the opportunity to serve this nation, which I have loved deeply throughout my life.”</i></p>

<p>Outside, cameras flashed in uneven bursts as the words were relayed across the wire services.</p>

<p><i>“I ask all Americans,”</i> he said, <i>“to support the new President as he assumes office at a difficult moment in our history.”</i></p>

<p>He looked up from the page for the first time in several minutes.</p>

<p><i>“May God bless America, and may God bless each and every one of you.”</i></p>

<p>He stepped away from the podium.</p>

<p>For several seconds, no one in the room moved.</p>

<p>In the East Room, a young staffer quietly asked no one in particular:</p>

<p><i>“What happens now?”</i></p>

<p>No one answered immediately.</p>

