<h3><b>A Future to Believe In</b></h3>
<p><b>The New York Times</b><br><b>July 19, 2020</b><br><b>By Alexander Burns and Maggie Haberman</b></p>

<p>Nearly half a century after George McGovern reshaped the Democratic Party and reoriented American liberalism around grassroots activism, antiwar politics, and small-dollar fundraising, another insurgent senator from the party’s left flank has captured its presidential nomination amid one of the most turbulent moments in modern American history.</p>

<p>Senator Bernie Sanders of Vermont enters the general election campaign against President Donald Trump with a Democratic coalition that, to many political historians, bears striking similarities to the movement McGovern assembled in 1972. Younger voters have surged into the political process once again. College-educated suburban voters continue drifting toward the Democratic Party. Large numbers of independents and first-time participants have rallied behind a campaign built less around party machinery than ideological commitment and grassroots enthusiasm.</p>

<p>Yet the parallels extend beyond coalition politics. Like McGovern before him, Mr. Sanders secured the nomination during a period of deep national unease. The coronavirus pandemic has disrupted daily life across the country, killing more than 130,000 Americans and pushing unemployment to levels not seen since the Great Depression. Abroad, long-standing American alliances have frayed, while tensions with China, Russia, and Iran continue to escalate. Weeks of nationwide protests following the killing of George Floyd have further intensified political divisions and renewed debates about policing, inequality, and the legitimacy of public institutions.</p>

<p>Against that backdrop, Mr. Sanders has framed his candidacy not as a restoration of stability but as a call for structural political and economic change. His campaign slogan, “A Future to Believe In,” echoes themes that have defined reform-oriented Democratic politics since the McGovern era: skepticism toward concentrated power, distrust of political insiders, and the belief that ordinary citizens can fundamentally reshape public life.</p>

<p>“You cannot understand Sanders without understanding McGovern,” said Julian Zelizer, a presidential historian at Princeton University. “The idea that campaigns should rely on small donors, volunteer networks, younger voters, and issue-driven activism rather than party bosses really begins in 1972. Sanders is operating inside a Democratic Party that McGovern, in many ways, helped create.”</p>

<p>The similarities have not gone unnoticed among Republicans. President Trump and his allies have repeatedly sought to portray Mr. Sanders as an ideological extremist disconnected from middle-class voters, reviving lines of attack once directed at McGovern during the height of the Cold War. Conservative commentators have openly compared the Vermont senator’s candidacy to the Democratic Party’s leftward shift in the early 1970s, warning that the country is once again drifting toward political radicalism during a moment of domestic unrest.</p>

<p>But many Democrats argue that the comparison overlooks how dramatically the country has changed since McGovern’s time. Policies once considered politically marginal, including universal health care expansion, tuition-free public college, and aggressive climate legislation, now command broad support among Democratic voters. Within the party itself, younger activists increasingly view the cautious centrism that dominated Democratic politics in the 1990s and early 2000s as inadequate for the scale of the nation’s current crises.</p>

<p>Mr. Sanders’s nomination also reflects the culmination of changes that have steadily transformed Democratic politics over decades. The small-donor fundraising systems pioneered during McGovern’s campaign have evolved into massive online networks capable of competing with traditional party structures. Activist organizations now exert greater influence over the party’s direction than many elected officials. And social movements, rather than political machines, increasingly drive the energy of Democratic presidential campaigns.</p>

<p>Even some Democrats who remain skeptical of Mr. Sanders privately acknowledge the historical resonance of his candidacy. The Democratic Party that once treated McGovern as an electoral cautionary tale has, over time, absorbed many of the reforms and assumptions his movement introduced. Today’s Democratic coalition is more secular, more suburban, younger, and more ideologically polarized than the party McGovern inherited in 1972.</p>

<p>Whether Mr. Sanders can succeed where McGovern struggled remains uncertain. National polls show a deeply divided electorate, and President Trump retains intense loyalty among Republican voters despite mounting crises. Yet as the country enters one of the most volatile general elections in generations, the echoes of 1972 have become increasingly difficult to ignore.</p>

<p>“Every era of political upheaval revives old arguments about what the Democratic Party is supposed to be,” said Leah Wright Rigueur, a political historian at Harvard University. “In moments like this, the McGovern legacy tends to reappear, because it speaks to a recurring belief that American politics can be rebuilt from the ground up.”</p>

<p>For many Democrats, that belief now stands at the center of the party once again.</p>

