<h3><b>THE NEW YORK TIMES<br>JANUARY 15, 2021</b></h3>

<h2><b>As Trump Faces Impeachment Again, Historians Revisit the Shadow of George Wallace</b></h2>

<p><i>By Michael Shear and Maggie Haberman</i></p>

<p>WASHINGTON — As President Trump faces an unprecedented second impeachment and mounting criminal investigations after the assault on the Capitol last week, historians and political analysts have increasingly turned to another deeply divisive presidency from modern American history: George Wallace.</p>

<p>For decades, Wallace’s brief administration occupied an uneasy place in American political memory. His presidency, which lasted from 1973 until his resignation in 1974 following corruption charges and impeachment proceedings, was often treated as an aberration born out of the political chaos of the early 1970s.</p>

<p>But in recent years, scholars have begun revisiting Wallace’s presidency less as a historical detour and more as an early expression of the populist realignment that would later reshape both political parties.</p>

<p>Now, in the final days of the Trump presidency, those comparisons have moved from academic journals into mainstream political discussion.</p>

<p>"There are obvious differences between the two men, both stylistically and ideologically," said Beverly Gage, a historian at Yale University who studies modern conservatism and political institutions. "But what connects Wallace and Trump is the way both men positioned themselves against established political systems while simultaneously using the machinery of government for intensely personal and political ends."</p>

<p>Wallace entered office after the chaotic 1972 election, defeating President Nixon in one of the most turbulent campaigns in modern history. Though initially dismissed by many national Democrats as a regional populist candidate with limited appeal outside the South, Wallace managed to assemble an unstable coalition of white working-class voters, disaffected Democrats, and anti-establishment independents frustrated by inflation, unrest, and distrust of federal institutions.</p>

<p>Once in office, Wallace governed through confrontation as much as negotiation. He attacked the press relentlessly, publicly feuded with members of his own party, and cultivated a political style that relied heavily on direct appeals to voters over the heads of traditional party leadership.</p>

<p>His administration was also plagued by constant allegations of corruption and abuse of power. Congressional investigations into patronage systems, campaign finance violations, and political retaliation consumed much of Wallace’s presidency before ultimately contributing to his resignation in late 1974.</p>

<p>"The Wallace administration normalized a level of political hostility that many Americans at the time found shocking," said Julian Zelizer, a Princeton historian. "What feels striking now is how familiar parts of it sound."</p>

<p>Trump’s allies have rejected the comparisons outright, arguing that Democrats and the media are attempting to retroactively attach historical labels to a presidency they opposed from the beginning.</p>

<p>"This is just another attempt to criminalize political disagreement," Representative Matt Gaetz of Florida said Thursday. "President Trump fought for voters who felt ignored by Washington elites, just like millions of Americans still are today."</p>

<p>Still, the parallels have become increasingly difficult to ignore for many historians.</p>

<p>Both Wallace and Trump built intensely loyal political movements rooted in distrust of national institutions and cultural resentment toward perceived elites. Both men struggled to maintain stable relationships with their own parties once in office. And both presidencies ultimately became consumed by investigations into abuses of power and efforts to obstruct scrutiny from Congress and federal investigators.</p>

<p>There are major differences as well. Wallace governed as a Democrat during the collapse of the New Deal coalition, while Trump reshaped the Republican Party around his own brand of populist nationalism. Wallace’s political base was concentrated heavily in the South and among segregationist Democrats, whereas Trump’s coalition extended across much of the modern Republican electorate.</p>

<p>But historians increasingly argue that Wallace’s presidency foreshadowed broader changes in American politics that neither party fully understood at the time.</p>

<p>"Wallace represented an early form of populist politics centered around cultural grievance, distrust of institutions, and personal loyalty to a political figure over party structures," said Nicole Hemmer, a historian at Columbia University. "What we are seeing now is not identical, but it exists within the same historical tradition."</p>

<p>Outside the Capitol on Thursday, National Guard troops continued patrolling fenced-off streets as Congress prepared for a Senate trial that could begin after President-elect Joseph R. Biden Jr. takes office next week.</p>

<p>For some historians, the imagery itself carried echoes of another unsettled political moment nearly half a century earlier.</p>

<p>"The Wallace years left Americans with a lasting fear that political institutions were becoming weaker than the forces pulling against them," Ms. Gage said. "A lot of people thought that period was unique. What the last few years have shown is that those tensions never really disappeared."</p>


