<h3><b>The Washington Post</b></h3>
<p><b>Politics and Policy | October 18, 2013</b></p>
<p><b>Obama to Nominate Kamala Harris to Supreme Court After Ginsburg Retirement</b></p>
<p><b>By Dan Balz and Anne E. Kornblut</b></p>

<p>WASHINGTON - President Obama is expected to nominate California Attorney General Kamala Harris to the Supreme Court following Justice Ruth Bader Ginsburg’s retirement announcement earlier this week, according to senior Democratic officials familiar with the decision.</p>

<p>Harris, 48, would become the first Black woman appointed to the Court and one of the youngest justices confirmed in the modern era if approved by the Senate.</p>

<p>The expected nomination immediately set off another round of reflection inside Democratic circles about the political trajectory of the party since the election of former President Shirley Chisholm more than forty years ago. Several senior Democrats privately described the moment less as a sudden breakthrough than as the continuation of institutional changes that had been developing gradually since the 1970s.</p>

<p>Obama is expected to formally announce the nomination this weekend.</p>

<p>Ginsburg informed the White House earlier this year that she intended to retire following Obama’s reelection, according to administration officials. Though Harris had circulated for years as a potential future nominee, aides said Obama became increasingly focused on her prosecutorial background and political profile during the administration’s second term.</p>

<p>Harris, a former San Francisco district attorney elected statewide in California in 2010, has developed a reputation among Democrats as a disciplined and politically careful prosecutor with strong ties to the party’s younger leadership wing. Within legal circles, she is viewed as pragmatic more than ideological, though Republicans are already signaling they intend to challenge her record aggressively during confirmation hearings.</p>

<p>The broader symbolism of the appointment, however, was difficult to separate from the announcement itself.</p>

<p>Chisholm’s election in 1972 fundamentally altered the composition of the Democratic coalition and accelerated the expansion of women and minority representation across government institutions. Historians still debate the extent to which her presidency directly transformed the electorate, but there is far less disagreement about its long-term effects on political recruitment inside the Democratic Party.</p>

<p>By the late 1970s and early 1980s, women and minority candidates were running for state legislatures, mayoralties, and congressional seats in significantly larger numbers than they had before Chisholm’s presidency. Many of the party officials now occupying senior leadership positions entered politics during that period.</p>

<p><i>“The Chisholm years changed the assumptions people made about who was supposed to be in the room,”</i> said one former Democratic National Committee official who worked during Ann Richards' administration in the 1990s. <i>“That doesn’t mean everything changed overnight. Most of it happened slowly. But it changed the pipeline.”</i></p>

<p>Obama’s own political rise is often discussed internally within the party through that framework. Senior Democrats close to the president have long argued that Obama’s coalition politics, particularly his emphasis on urban turnout, younger voters, and professional-class suburbs, reflects electoral shifts that began during the Chisholm era and matured over several decades.</p>

<p>Several Obama advisers privately pushed back Friday against attempts to overstate the historical parallels surrounding Harris’s nomination, arguing that the White House wanted to keep attention focused on her qualifications and legal experience rather than treating the process primarily as a symbolic milestone.</p>

<p>Still, even some Republicans acknowledged the larger political context surrounding the selection.</p>

<p><i>“There’s a reason Democrats are framing this historically,”</i> one Republican Senate aide said Friday. <i>“They see these appointments as part of a longer project.”</i></p>

<p>Ginsburg’s retirement also carries institutional significance beyond the nomination itself. Appointed during the final years of the Chisholm administration, Ginsburg became one of the Court’s central liberal voices on gender discrimination, civil liberties, and voting rights. Her departure removes one of the last remaining judicial figures whose career was closely tied to the political realignment of the 1970s.</p>

<p>That generational transition was evident around Washington on Friday as Democratic officials, civil rights organizations, and legal advocacy groups began preparing for what is expected to become one of the most closely watched confirmation fights in years.</p>

<p>Outside the White House, the reaction was notably less dramatic than similar moments in earlier decades might have produced. Several Democratic strategists noted that the prospect of a Black woman nominated to the Supreme Court, while historically significant, no longer carried the same sense of political improbability it once would have.</p>

<p>That normalization, many Democrats argued Friday, was itself part of the story.</p>

<p>In 1972, Shirley Chisholm’s presidential campaign was still widely treated in Washington as an experiment unlikely to survive the primary calendar. Four decades later, a Black president selecting a Black woman for the Supreme Court was poised to become one of the defining personnel decisions of his second term.</p>

<p>For many Democrats, the distance between those two moments no longer felt quite as large as it once did.</p>

