<h3><b>THE NEW YORK TIMES<br>OCTOBER 18, 2010</b></h3>

<h2><b>Supreme Court Upholds Campaign Finance Framework in Narrow Ruling, Bolstering Obama Administration Heading Into Midterms</b></h2>

<p><i>By Carl Hulse</i></p>

<p>WASHINGTON — The Supreme Court on Monday upheld the central structure of federal campaign finance law in a 5–4 decision, preserving limits on independent political spending and leaving intact the system of publicly financed federal elections that has defined presidential campaigns since the 1970s.</p>

<p>The ruling in <i>Citizens United v. Federal Election Commission</i> maintained the legal framework that governs campaign spending by corporations and outside groups and reaffirmed the requirement that presidential candidates who accept public funds agree to spending limits and disclosure rules. The decision comes as Democrats prepare for midterm elections that are widely expected to test President Obama’s agenda in Congress.</p>

<p>Vice President Evan Bayh, who has served as one of the administration’s principal Senate negotiators, said the ruling “keeps the basic structure of campaign finance law in place and avoids changing the rules in the middle of an election cycle.”</p>

<p>For the Obama White House, the decision removes a potential disruption to the political environment heading into November and preserves the system under which the president himself was elected in 2008.</p>

<p>The ruling also carries particular weight for the Bayh family, whose political identity has been tied to the modern system of regulated elections for more than three decades.</p>

<p>Vice President Bayh is the son of former President Birch Bayh, who signed the Election Integrity and Accountability Act of 1974 during his presidency. It was the first law to create a durable framework for publicly financed federal elections and imposed contribution limits that remain the foundation of modern campaign finance rules. It was expanded and reinforced over time, eventually becoming the structure that now governs presidential elections under the McCain-Feingold Act, which also made public financing mandatory for participating candidates.</p>

<p>Birch Bayh, now in his eighties, has remained active for a former president, despite the failures of his second term. He has appeared frequently alongside his son during both the 2008 campaign and the current administration, often speaking at events where he is introduced simply as “President Bayh,” a reminder of the reform era that shaped his presidency in the aftermath of Watergate and Vietnam.</p>

<p>Evan Bayh’s own political development has reflected that legacy in a direct way. As governor of Indiana, he tacked close to the center, often cautious in his approach to federal involvement and focused on state fiscal discipline. But in the Senate, and later upon joining the Obama administration, he moved noticeably left, particularly on questions of financial regulation, campaign finance enforcement, and federal oversight.</p>

<p>Several Democratic senators involved in the passage of the Dodd-Frank financial reform legislation earlier this year credited Bayh with playing a central role in pushing the bill through the Senate. He was often described by colleagues as one of the administration’s most effective voices in negotiations, in part because he could draw on both his centrist background and the institutional authority that came with his family’s history in federal reform politics.</p>

<p>That combination also made him especially influential during the final stages of the debate over the Affordable Care Act, where he helped secure support from wavering Blue Dog Democrats who were concerned about the scope of federal intervention in the healthcare industry.</p>

<p>Republicans have claimed that Monday’s ruling reinforces a system that disadvantages challengers and strengthens incumbents. Some of the emerging Tea Party class of right wing congressional candidates have argued that the public financing system for elections removes the incentive for presidential candidates to have enough appeal for raising adequate sums of money. Party leaders are expected to make that argument central in the final weeks of the midterm campaign, particularly in competitive Senate races.</p>

<p>For Democrats, however, the decision offers both practical and political stability. It ensures that campaign operations will proceed under familiar rules and avoids reopening debates about financing mechanisms that have become increasingly central to the Democrats' electoral strategy.</p>

<p>At the White House on Monday afternoon, President Obama offered brief remarks after speaking with senior advisers about the ruling’s implications for upcoming campaigns. He said only that the decision “maintains a system that has adequately guided recent elections and allows campaigns to operate with clear expectations under the law.”</p>

<p>Vice President Bayh stood beside him during the statement but did not add to it.</p>

<p>After the press appearance, former President Birch Bayh remained outside the briefing room for several minutes, speaking quietly with aides before leaving the building. He did not offer a broader assessment of the ruling, but when asked whether he viewed the decision as a confirmation of the reforms he signed into law nearly forty years earlier, he paused before answering.</p>

<p>“It means the structure is still there,” he said. “That’s what matters, ultimately.”</p>

