<h3><b>1998 PBS Roundtable</b></h3>
<p><b><i>"The Managed Economy? Reconsidering the Mondale Years"</i></b></p>
<p><b>PBS NewsHour Special Broadcast - October 1998</b></p>

<p><i>JIM LEHRER:</i> Good evening. Tonight marks twenty-five years since the passage of the Fair Employment and Income Security Act, the centerpiece of President Walter Mondale’s domestic agenda. Historians still argue over a broader question: did the Mondale administration merely slow America’s economic transition away from industrial liberalism, or did it leave something more lasting behind?</p>

<p><i>ALAN BRINKLEY:</i> Honestly? They remember stability. Which sounds less impressive now than it did in the early seventies.</p>

<p>Vietnam was still hanging over public life. Inflation was worsening. Manufacturing jobs were disappearing. Public trust in institutions was collapsing.</p>

<p>Mondale’s achievement was that the Democratic coalition held together longer than many people expected it would.</p>

<p><i>LEHRER:</i> Even though the economy struggled for much of his presidency?</p>

<p><i>BRINKLEY:</i> Yes, because Mondale never presented himself as someone who was going to reinvent the economy overnight. He campaigned as a negotiator. Somebody who understood how institutions worked and how to keep them functioning under pressure.</p>

<p><i>ROBERT REICH:</i> The White House understood fairly early that deindustrialization wasn’t temporary. Factories were automating. Foreign competition was accelerating. Entire Midwestern cities were losing population and tax revenue.</p>

<p>The administration’s basic view was that government had a responsibility to soften the landing.</p>

<p><i>WILLIAM KRISTOL:</i> And conservatives at the time believed many of those policies were making the broader economic problems worse.</p>

<p>There’s a tendency to look back at the seventies as this humane transitional period, but people also remember inflation, stagnation, and declining business confidence. There’s a reason conservatism gained so much ground by the end of the decade.</p>

<p><i>JOHN SWEENEY:</i> Working people didn’t experience Mondale as ideological.</p>

<p>My father was a transit worker in Queens. He wasn’t reading economic theory. What mattered to him was that somebody in government still seemed to care whether wages kept up with prices.</p>

<p><i>BRINKLEY:</i> Probably among the more important transitional presidents.</p>

<p>He wasn’t charismatic in the way Americans usually romanticize. He projected competence more than inspiration.</p>

<p>But the modern Democratic Party’s governing style owes a great deal to the Mondale years.</p>

<p>He kept an older form of liberalism functioning long enough for the party to adapt to what came after.</p>


