<h2>Supreme Court Questions Government Use of Commercial Data Analysis in Case Testing Limits of Post-Watergate Surveillance Law</h2>

<p><b>By Charlie Savage</b></p>

<p>WASHINGTON — The Supreme Court on Monday heard arguments in a case challenging a federal practice that relies on purchasing analytical reports derived from commercially collected consumer data, a system critics say allows the government to obtain detailed information about individuals without using warrants or meeting traditional Fourth Amendment standards.</p>

<p>The case was brought by the Electronic Privacy Information Center (EPIC), which argues that federal agencies are effectively recreating the same surveillance capability the Court rejected in a 1980s decision involving the Reagan administration, only now through a more indirect structure. In 1983, the government had attempted to purchase raw consumer data directly from collection firms in order to build behavioral profiles of Americans without judicial authorization. The Court struck that effort down under the Church-era surveillance framework established after Watergate.</p>

<p>The current dispute turns on a further step removed from that arrangement. Federal agencies are not buying raw data. Instead, they purchase finished analytical products from private firms that aggregate and interpret commercial data into predictive behavioral profiles.</p>

<p>EPIC argues that this distinction is formal. The group maintains that the government is still obtaining individualized information without a warrant, regardless of intermediaries.</p>

<p><i>EPIC counsel:</i> “What the government receives is still a reconstruction of private life based on data it could not lawfully compel directly.”</p>

<p><i>EPIC counsel:</i> “The fact that it is packaged as analysis does not change the constitutional issue.”</p>

