<p>Senator Amy Klobuchar adjusted her microphone.</p>

<p><i>“Today’s hearing concerns the Federal Trade Commission’s antitrust actions against Meta and Alphabet under authorities expanded during the Harris Administration.”</i></p>

<p>She glanced toward the photograph.</p>

<p><i>“Fifty years later, we are still arguing about the same thing Fred Harris argued about in the seventies: what happens when economic power becomes too concentrated for democratic institutions to keep up with it.”</i></p>

<p>Senator Josh Hawley leaned forward.</p>

<p><i>“And Harris was not anti-business the way people sometimes describe him now. He believed concentrated private power eventually spills over into politics.”</i></p>

<p>Khan nodded.</p>

<p><i>“Yes. That was central to how he thought about antitrust.”</i></p>

<p>Hawley continued.</p>

<p><i>“Most people today hear the word antitrust and think about prices. Harris talked about communities losing influence over their own economies.”</i></p>

<p><i>“He did,”</i> Khan replied. <i>“He worried about local banks disappearing. Local newspapers disappearing. Independent agriculture disappearing. He thought people were slowly losing control over decisions affecting their daily lives.”</i></p>

<p>Senator Cory Booker smiled slightly.</p>

<p><i>“You sound like you have spent a lot of time reading him.”</i></p>

<p>A few people laughed quietly.</p>

<p><i>“I have,”</i> Khan said.</p>

<p>Senator Ted Cruz leaned toward his microphone.</p>

<p><i>“But Harris was talking about industrial monopolies. Steel. Energy. Telecommunications. You are applying these laws to speech platforms.”</i></p>

<p>Khan answered calmly.</p>

<p><i>“We are applying them to market concentration.”</i></p>

<p>Cruz frowned.</p>

<p><i>“These companies are not Standard Oil.”</i></p>

<p>Khan folded her hands.</p>

<p><i>“Standard Oil did not control how billions of people communicate with each other.”</i></p>

<p>The room became noticeably quieter.</p>

<p>Near the end of the hearing, Senator Jon Tester looked toward Khan.</p>

<p><i>“Do you think Harris would recognize this economy?”</i></p>

<p>Khan thought for a moment.</p>

<p><i>“More than people would probably like.”</i></p>

<p>Tester smiled.</p>

<p><i>“And what do you think he would say about it?”</i></p>

<p>Khan looked down briefly before answering.</p>

<p><i>“Probably something uncomfortable.”</i></p>

<p>The room laughed softly.</p>

<p><i>“I think Harris believed people can live with inequality for a long time. What they struggle to live with is the feeling that nobody listens to them anymore. Once people stop believing they have any influence over the systems around them, trust in institutions starts disappearing very quickly.”</i></p>

