<h3>
  <b>
    UNITED STATES DISTRICT COURT<br>
    FOR THE DISTRICT OF COLUMBIA<br>
    October 28, 1974<br>
    United States of America v. Richard Milhous Nixon<br>
    Criminal No. 74-291
  </b>
</h3>
</center>
<p>
  The courtroom is already filled when the jury files in. A low murmur fades as they take their seats. Reporters lean forward slightly, notebooks open.
</p>

<p><b>THE COURT:</b> The jury is present. Counsel, you may proceed.</p>

<p>
  The prosecutor stands, buttoning his jacket and briefly glancing toward the jury box before stepping forward.
</p>

<p><b>PROSECUTOR:</b> Thank you, Your Honor. The United States calls John Wesley Dean the Third.</p>

<p>
  John Dean rises from the front row of the gallery and walks to the witness stand at a measured pace. He adjusts his glasses slightly before sitting down.
</p>

<p><b>THE CLERK:</b> Please raise your right hand.</p>

<p>
  Do you solemnly swear that the testimony you are about to give shall be the truth, the whole truth, and nothing but the truth?
</p>

<p><b>DEAN:</b> I do.</p>

<p><b>THE CLERK:</b> Please be seated. State your name and occupation for the record.</p>

<p>
  <b>DEAN:</b> My name is John Wesley Dean III. I am an attorney in private practice. From July 9, 1970 until January 20, 1973, I served as Counsel to the President of the United States. In that role, I worked closely with senior White House staff and frequently advised the President directly on legal and administrative matters.
</p>

<p>
  The prosecutor steps closer to the witness stand, resting one hand lightly on the lectern.
</p>

<p><b>PROSECUTOR:</b> Mr. Dean, could you briefly describe the nature of your responsibilities in that role?</p>

<p>
  <b>DEAN:</b> My responsibilities included advising the President and senior White House staff on legal matters, coordinating responses to investigations, and reviewing issues that might create legal exposure for the administration. I also served as a liaison between the White House and outside counsel when necessary. After the June 1972 break-in at the Democratic National Committee headquarters, my duties increasingly involved assessing potential connections between individuals involved in that incident and people associated with the reelection effort, as well as evaluating what legal risks those connections might present.
</p>

<p><b>PROSECUTOR:</b> When did you first become aware that the break-in might present a legal or political problem for the White House?</p>

<p>
  <b>DEAN:</b> Within a few days of the arrests. There were reports that individuals taken into custody had prior associations with the Committee to Re-Elect the President. As additional information began to surface, it became clear that investigators would likely look into whether there were broader connections. That created concern inside the White House that the matter might not remain confined to those initially arrested.
</p>

<p><b>PROSECUTOR:</b> Did those concerns lead to discussions inside the White House?</p>

<p>
  <b>DEAN:</b> Yes. There were a number of meetings with senior aides. We discussed what information existed, who might be exposed, and what the administration’s public posture should be. There was also discussion about how to respond to press inquiries, and whether additional internal review was necessary to determine what individuals connected to the campaign might have known.
</p>

<p>
  Dean pauses briefly, glancing toward the jury.
</p>

<p><b>PROSECUTOR:</b> Did those discussions include the defendant?</p>

<p>
  <b>DEAN:</b> Yes, they did. As the situation developed, I briefed the President directly on the potential legal implications and on the information that was becoming available through internal channels and press reporting.
</p>

<p>
  Dean briefly glances toward the defense table, then back to the prosecutor.
</p>

<p><b>PROSECUTOR:</b> During those conversations, did the subject of financial assistance to the defendants arise?</p>

<p>
  <b>DEAN:</b> Yes. There was concern that the individuals arrested might cooperate with investigators if they felt abandoned. Discussions took place about whether financial assistance would continue to be provided to them or to their families, and whether such support would help maintain their willingness to refrain from cooperating.
</p>

<p><b>PROSECUTOR:</b> What was the purpose of that assistance, as it was discussed?</p>

<p>
  <b>DEAN:</b> The purpose, as I understood it, was to maintain their cooperation and discourage them from providing information to investigators. It was discussed in terms of support, but the underlying concern was whether those individuals would remain silent as the investigation progressed.
</p>

<p>
  The prosecutor takes a sip of water before continuing.
</p>

<p><b>PROSECUTOR:</b> Mr. Dean, I’d like to direct your attention to December 1973, shortly before the defendant left office. Did you have a conversation with President Nixon regarding these matters?</p>

<p><b>DEAN:</b> Yes, I did. I requested the meeting because I believed the situation was becoming increasingly difficult to manage, and I felt it was important to communicate that directly.</p>

<p><b>PROSECUTOR:</b> Where did that conversation occur?</p>

<p><b>DEAN:</b> In the Oval Office, during a scheduled meeting.</p>

<p><b>PROSECUTOR:</b> Was that conversation recorded?</p>

<p><b>DEAN:</b> Yes, it was, as many conversations in the Oval Office were at that time.</p>

<p><b>PROSECUTOR:</b> Your Honor, the government moves to admit Government Exhibit 14, a tape recording dated December 21, 1973.</p>

<p>
  Defense counsel leans forward slightly.
</p>

<p><b>DEFENSE COUNSEL:</b> No objection, Your Honor, subject to foundation.</p>

<p><b>THE COURT:</b> The exhibit will be admitted.</p>

<p>
  A court clerk wheels a small cart forward with a reel-to-reel tape recorder. The faint mechanical hum becomes audible as it is positioned.
</p>

<p>
  The prosecutor walks over and adjusts the reel.
</p>

<p><b>PROSECUTOR:</b> Mr. Dean, before we play this recording, could you describe the context of that conversation?</p>

<p>
  <b>DEAN:</b> I had requested the meeting because I believed the situation was becoming increasingly serious. There were mounting legal risks, and I felt the President needed to understand that the investigation might eventually move beyond the individuals already charged. I also believed it was important to discuss the issue of continued financial support and what that might imply.
</p>

<p><b>PROSECUTOR:</b> What did you tell him?</p>

<p>
  <b>DEAN:</b> I told him that the situation was becoming serious, that continued payments to defendants were being discussed, and that investigators might eventually reach into the White House. I also explained that the longer the matter continued, the more difficult it would become to contain.
</p>

<p><b>PROSECUTOR:</b> Your Honor, at this time the government requests permission to play a portion of Exhibit 14.</p>

<p><b>THE COURT:</b> You may proceed.</p>

<p>
  The prosecutor presses the button.
</p>

<p>
  The tape begins.
</p>

<p><b>NIXON (TAPE):</b> How much money do you need?</p>

<p><b>DEAN (TAPE):</b> I would say these people are going to cost a million dollars over the next couple of years.</p>

<p><i>[A pause.]</i></p>

<p><b>NIXON (TAPE):</b> We could get that. You could get a million dollars. And you could get it in cash.</p>

<p>
  The tape stops.
</p>

<p>
  One juror shifts slightly. Another writes something down.
</p>

<p><b>PROSECUTOR:</b> Mr. Dean, what was your reaction when the President made that statement?</p>

<p>
  <b>DEAN:</b> I understood it as an acknowledgment that funds would be made available if necessary. Given the context of the conversation, I interpreted it as an indication that continued financial support would be considered acceptable.
</p>

<p><b>PROSECUTOR:</b> Was there discussion about how such funds would be raised?</p>

<p>
  <b>DEAN:</b> Yes. There was discussion about using political contributors and intermediaries so that the payments would not be directly traceable to official campaign structures.
