<h3><b>1998 Guest Lecture at Terry Sanford High School, Fayetteville, North Carolina</b></h3>
<p><b>Civics Week Lecture Series</b></p>
<p><b>Recorded April 14, 1998</b></p>

<p>Sanford stood at the front of the auditorium with a yellow legal pad he barely used. He spoke casually, like he was talking to a small group rather than a full room of students.</p>

<p><i>“I should probably start by saying presidents don’t really know what they’re doing as often as people think they do.”</i></p>

<p>A few students laughed.</p>

<p><i>“Most of the job is trying to make decisions without enough information and keeping everyone else calm while you do it.”</i></p>

<p>He paused and leaned on the podium.</p>

<p><i>“In the early seventies, the country felt worn down. Vietnam, inflation, all of it. People were tired of each other in a way that is just real hard to describe now.”</i></p>

<p>A student raised her hand.</p>

<p><i>“Did you think you were going to win?”</i></p>

<p>Sanford smiled.</p>

<p><i>“Course not.”</i></p>

<p>Laughter in the room.</p>

<p><i>“We thought we might shape the conversation a bit. The party was already split in a lot of directions after 1968.”</i></p>

<p>Another student asked:</p>

<p><i>“Why you?”</i></p>

<p><i>“Because I didn’t scare off too many people.”</i></p>

<p>More chuckles.</p>

<p>A teacher asked from the side wall:</p>

<p><i>“Was the New South real?”</i></p>

<p>Sanford nodded.</p>

<p><i>“Yes, but not the way people talk about it now.”</i></p>

<p><i>“It was about whether the South could change - economically and socially - without falling apart politically.”</i></p>

<p>He looked out at the students.</p>

<p><i>“Nobody really knew if that would work.”</i></p>

<p>A student asked quietly:</p>

<p><i>“Did it?”</i></p>

<p>Sanford hesitated.</p>

<p><i>“Partly. For a while.”</i></p>

<p>He straightened.</p>

<p><i>“What I learned is that change doesn’t wait for politics to catch up. It keeps going until somebody uses it.”</i></p>

<p>He tapped the podium once.</p>

<p><i>“That is why I still think education mattered most. Community colleges. Regional universities. Training programs. The things nobody paid attention to at the time.”</i></p>

<p>A student asked:</p>

<p><i>“Do you regret being president?”</i></p>

<p>Sanford shook his head.</p>

<p><i>“No.”</i></p>

<p>Then, after a pause:</p>

<p><i>“We all play a role in the world, that’s why you can’t sell yourself short. And we all have to live with the decisions we make.”</i></p>


