<h3><b>1996 Strategic Command Training Simulation</b></h3>
<p><b>National Defense University - Advanced Cold War Decision Exercise</b></p>
<p><b>Scenario Briefing Room C</b></p>

<p>The projector hummed softly while officers settled into their seats carrying binders stamped CONFIDENTIAL SIMULATION MATERIAL. Across the whiteboard someone had written:
<br><b>JACKSON DOCTRINE EXERCISE</b>
<br><b>SPRING 1978</b></p>

<p>Rear Admiral Stansfield Turner stood beside the map table.</p>

<p><i>“All right, let’s begin. This exercise examines the decision-making process within the Jackson Administration after the Soviet Naval Crisis of 1978.”</i></p>

<p>An Air Force major leaned back in his chair.</p>

<p><i>“Every year they run this scenario.”</i></p>

<p>Someone beside him shrugged.</p>

<p><i>“Every year somebody thinks they can outsmart Jackson.”</i></p>

<p>Turner looked up.</p>

<p><i>“You aren’t here to debate whether Jackson was right politically. You’re here to understand how he thought.”</i></p>

<p>He clicked the projector remote. A slide appeared showing oil imports and manufacturing decline during the early seventies.</p>

<p><i>“Jackson believed that military strength and industrial capacity and energy policy and alliance management were inseparable. He thought the country entered the seventies exhausted, and he worried Americans were starting to confuse military restraint with weakness.”</i></p>

<p>A younger analyst raised her hand.</p>

<p><i>“So he really wasn’t Reagan before Reagan?”</i></p>

<p>Turner shook his head.</p>

<p><i>“No. Jackson was still a New Deal liberal at heart. He supported infrastructure spending, labor unions, industrial planning, scientific research, arms control. The New Left viewed American power through the lens of ‘Nam. Jackson never stopped viewing Soviet power through the lens of postwar Europe.”</i></p>

<p>The screen changed again. Nuclear plants, shipyards, aerospace facilities.</p>

<p><i>“The energy program is usually taught as a function of Keynesian economic policy,”</i> Turner said. <i>“Jackson saw it as a strategic military investment.”</i></p>

<p>A civilian historian near the wall nodded.</p>

<p><i>“A lot of the federal research partnerships from the eighties trace back to those programs.”</i></p>

<p>Turner stepped back toward the table.</p>

<p><i>“All right. Scenario setup. It is March 1978. Soviet naval activity in the North Atlantic has intensified after NATO missile deployments in West Germany. Intelligence suggests Moscow believes the administration is politically constrained by anti-defense sentiment inside Congress.”</i></p>

<p>Someone near the back laughed quietly.</p>

<p><i>“Jackson probably took that personally.”</i></p>

<p><i>“Extremely personally,”</i> Turner replied dryly.</p>

<p>An analyst raised his hand.</p>

<p><i>“Did Jackson win the argument historically?”</i></p>

<p>The room grew quieter.</p>

<p>Turner thought for a moment.</p>

<p><i>“I think he understood earlier than most people that industrial decline and geopolitical decline were interconnected.”</i></p>

<p>The historian near the back added quietly:</p>

<p><i>“Jackson belonged to a generation that still believed that the government could be powerful without becoming corrupt.”</i></p>

<p>For a moment the room stayed silent.</p>

<p>Then Turner looked around the room and sighed.</p>

<p><i>“All right. Let’s see how many of you can keep NATO together without starting World War Three.”</i></p>


