<h3><b>1988 Congressional Member Orientation Handbook</b></h3>
<p><b>United States House of Representatives</b></p>
<p><b>Office of Institutional Procedures and Legislative Administration</b></p>
<p><b>Chapter 4</b></p>
<p><b>Budget Coordination, Committee Authority, and the Modern Legislative State</b></p>

<p>New members entering Congress after the 1970s often find it difficult to understand how much federal budgeting changed during the Mills Administration. Many procedures now treated as routine did not exist in stable form before his reforms.</p>

<p>At the start of the 1970s, budgeting was fragmented across committees and agencies. Long-term forecasting was inconsistent. Tax and spending decisions were often built through overlapping jurisdictions with weak coordination, and agencies frequently submitted competing baseline numbers that did not align.</p>

<p>The economic instability of the early 1970s exposed these problems.</p>

<p>President Mills entered office in 1973 with deep familiarity with congressional procedure. As former chair of the House Ways and Means Committee, he treated Congress less as an obstacle and more as the center of governing.</p>

<p>Former staff often called his White House “a committee government scaled upward.”</p>

<p>Cabinet meetings were highly procedural. Cabinet Secretaries arrived with detailed budget impact reports, jurisdiction notes, and multi-year projections. Internal memos regularly included updated revenue estimates and inflation-adjusted scoring tables.</p>

<p>Treasury Secretary Russell Long later said of the president:</p>

<p><i>“Mills could remember tax provisions the way other men remembered baseball scores.”</i></p>

<p>The Tax Simplification and Fiscal Coordination Act of 1974 took eighteen months of negotiation between committees and agencies. It required repeated reconciliation between House and Senate scoring models and constant updates to revenue assumptions.</p>

<p>The law introduced changes that still shape budgeting today:</p>

<p>• Five- and ten-year professional budget projections<br>
• Coordinated payroll tax systems for Social Security and Medicare<br>
• Standardized federal reporting rules across all agencies<br>
• Indexed tax deduction formulas<br>
• Permanent budget scoring offices<br>
• Formal reconciliation between revenue and spending plans</p>

<p>At the time, the reforms drew limited attention, but historians now view them as highly consequential to future budgeting policies.</p>

<p>Mills governed through the weight of institutional stability rather than any sort of charismatic public messaging. He relied on committee structures, seniority, and negotiation rather than executive-style media politics.</p>

<p>His administration formalized practices like early bipartisan consultation before markup and standardized scorekeeping across committees, especially after the 1973 oil crisis.</p>

<p>The Mills presidency is often described as the final full expression of New Deal-era congressional government, where the laws passed depended on negotiated relationships between committees, agencies, and party leadership.</p>

<p>Critics called it technocratic and detached from the people, especially in the wake of the emerging Watergate Scandal (of which the president himself oversaw the prosecution). But Mills rarely focused on public messaging, and aides often noted he preferred discussing fiscal projections over political rhetoric.</p>

<p>One aide recalled:</p>

<p><i>“He thought intellectual competence alone could carry the argument. Television politics just didn’t work that way anymore.”</i></p>

<p>Still, Congress supported him because he respected the institutional process.</p>

<p>In healthcare, Mills favored incremental reforms: Medicare reimbursement coordination, hospital billing standards, and federal-state regulatory alignment rather than large structural changes.</p>

<p>Many modern healthcare budgeting systems still trace back to this period.</p>

<p>As media politics grew stronger in the late 1970s and 1980s, Mills’ style became less electorally viable.</p>

<p>Even so, many lawmakers later described him as one of the most effective procedural presidents in modern history.</p>

<p>Speaker of the House Thomas P. O’Neill Jr. once said:</p>

<p><i>“Wilbur Mills understood Congress better than any president of the twentieth century save for Lyndon Johnson. It didn’t always make him popular, but it made him effective.”</i></p>


