<h3><b>July 29, 2004</b></h3>
<p><b>The New York Times</b></p>
<p><b>National Politics Desk</b></p>
<p><b>After Obama Speech, Democrats Hear Echoes of an Earlier Urban Liberalism</b></p>
<p><b>By Adam Nagourney</b></p>

<p>BOSTON - By the time Illinois Senate candidate Barack Obama finished speaking Tuesday night, delegates on the convention floor were already making comparisons many Democrats have not heard seriously in years.</p>

<p><b>John Lindsay.</b></p>

<p>The resemblance was not ideological in any exact sense. Obama is more restrained than the former president ever was, less aristocratic in style, less openly identified with the technocratic reform politics that shaped much of the Lindsay administration during the 1970s. But for many older Democrats watching Obama deliver the keynote address at the Democratic National Convention, the similarities still felt unmistakable.</p>

<p>A young, charismatic urban politician speaking about metropolitan America not as a national burden, but as the country’s political and economic center of gravity.</p>

<p><i>“It reminded me of the way liberals used to talk about cities before everybody got so defensive about them,”</i> said former Lindsay administration housing official Karen Phillips. <i>“There was optimism in the way we used to talk about urban America.”</i></p>

<p>Obama, a state senator from Chicago heavily favored in this fall’s Illinois Senate race, has spent much of his political career navigating a Democratic Party that increasingly depends on metropolitan regions but still remains somewhat uneasy discussing them in explicitly political terms.</p>

<p>His speech Tuesday touched repeatedly on themes that have steadily become more central to Democratic electoral strategy since the decline of the old New Deal coalition: suburban diversification, public infrastructure, immigration, education investment, regional economic planning, and the growing interdependence between cities and suburbs.</p>

<p><i>“The old divisions between city and suburb don’t hold the way they used to,”</i> Obama said during the address. <i>“Our economies are connected now. Our futures are connected. We are one country, bound together by the threads of our shared history and the promise of a shared future.”</i></p>

<p>Several delegates afterward described the line as sounding “very Lindsay.”</p>

<p>President John Lindsay remains a complicated figure inside Democratic politics more than twenty years after leaving office. Admired by many liberals for his transit, environmental, and urban redevelopment policies, Lindsay also became an early symbol of the metropolitan liberalism conservatives spent decades attacking afterward as elitist, overplanned, and culturally detached from smaller-town America.</p>

<p>Still, much of the Democratic coalition today increasingly resembles the one Lindsay attempted to build in the 1970s: minority voters, professionals, public-sector workers, younger suburbanites, university communities, and college-educated metropolitan counties.</p>

<p><i>“Back then people thought Lindsay was describing a future that didn’t really exist yet,”</i> said Democratic strategist Paul Begala. <i>“Now most national elections are fought on terrain he recognized earlier than almost anybody.”</i></p>

<p>Obama himself has rarely referenced Lindsay publicly, though several advisers say he has studied parts of the administration’s urban policy closely, particularly transit investment and regional development planning. One former Lindsay aide watching Tuesday night said Obama appeared noticeably more comfortable speaking about urban growth than most national Democrats of the last two decades.</p>

<p><i>“For a long time Democrats talked about cities almost apologetically,”</i> the aide said. <i>“They’d almost disparage the very idea of living in a city in every other breath they’d take. Obama doesn’t sound apologetic about the urban landscape at all. It’s exhilarating.”</i></p>

<p>After the speech ended, television commentators immediately began speculating about Obama’s political future beyond the Senate race itself. Several delegates leaving the convention hall compared the atmosphere to earlier breakout convention speeches that launched national careers almost overnight.</p>

<p>One New York delegate laughed when asked whether the keynote could eventually make Obama a presidential contender.</p>

<p><i>“In this party?”</i> he said. <i>“That’s usually how it starts.”</i></p>


