  <b>DEAN:</b> The concern was that documents could later be subpoenaed or otherwise become part of an investigation, potentially creating additional exposure for individuals involved.
</p>

<p><b>PROSECUTOR:</b> Did you take any actions consistent with those discussions?</p>

<p>
  <b>DEAN:</b> Yes. I limited written communications and advised others to avoid unnecessary documentation that might later become subject to review.
</p>

<p><b>PROSECUTOR:</b> Your Honor, the government moves to admit Exhibit 22, a memorandum prepared by Mr. Dean summarizing concerns about the investigation.</p>

<p><b>DEFENSE COUNSEL:</b> No objection.</p>

<p><b>THE COURT:</b> Exhibit 22 is admitted.</p>

<p>
  A document is handed to the jurors. Several lean forward, scanning the page.
</p>

<p><b>PROSECUTOR:</b> Mr. Dean, do you recognize this document?</p>

<p><b>DEAN:</b> Yes. It’s a memorandum I prepared in late 1972 outlining legal risks and possible investigative developments.</p>

<p><b>PROSECUTOR:</b> Does it reflect your concerns at the time?</p>

<p><b>DEAN:</b> Yes, it does. It reflects my understanding of how the investigation might develop and what legal exposure might result.</p>

<p><b>PROSECUTOR:</b> Mr. Dean, were you aware of any discussions about tape recordings within the White House?</p>

<p><b>DEAN:</b> Yes.</p>

<p><b>PROSECUTOR:</b> What was discussed?</p>

<p>
  <b>DEAN:</b> There was concern about whether recordings of conversations might be subject to subpoena, and whether investigators might eventually seek access to them.
</p>

<p><b>PROSECUTOR:</b> Did you discuss this with the defendant?</p>

<p><b>DEAN:</b> Yes.</p>

<p><b>PROSECUTOR:</b> What was his response?</p>

<p>
  <b>DEAN:</b> He expressed concern about how those recordings might be interpreted and whether they would accurately reflect the context of conversations.
</p>

<p><b>PROSECUTOR:</b> After January 20, 1973, when the defendant left office, did your formal role end?</p>

<p><b>DEAN:</b> Yes.</p>

<p><b>PROSECUTOR:</b> And your testimony today relates to conversations that occurred before that date?</p>

<p><b>DEAN:</b> That’s correct.</p>

<p>
  Defense counsel rises slowly, buttoning his jacket.
</p>

<p><b>DEFENSE COUNSEL:</b> Mr. Dean, you’ve testified that you were deeply involved in these matters from the beginning, correct?</p>

<p><b>DEAN:</b> Yes.</p>

<p><b>DEFENSE COUNSEL:</b> You attended meetings, advised staff, and communicated directly with the President?</p>

<p><b>DEAN:</b> That’s correct.</p>

<p><b>DEFENSE COUNSEL:</b> And in some instances, you personally handled communications relating to defendants and legal strategy?</p>

<p><b>DEAN:</b> Yes.</p>

<p><b>DEFENSE COUNSEL:</b> So when you describe discussions about payments or documentation, you were not simply observing. You were participating directly in those conversations and decisions.</p>

<p><b>DEAN:</b> Yes, I was.</p>

<p><b>DEFENSE COUNSEL:</b> You also understood at the time that these matters carried legal risk?</p>

<p><b>DEAN:</b> Yes.</p>

<p><b>DEFENSE COUNSEL:</b> And yet you continued in your role?</p>

<p><b>DEAN:</b> I did.</p>

<p><b>DEFENSE COUNSEL:</b> You did not resign immediately?</p>

<p><b>DEAN:</b> No.</p>

<p><b>DEFENSE COUNSEL:</b> Nor did you report your concerns to investigators at that time?</p>

<p><b>DEAN:</b> Not immediately, no.</p>

<p>
  Dean glances briefly toward the jury.
</p>

<p><b>DEFENSE COUNSEL:</b> Mr. Dean, you’ve testified about the conversation on December 21. Prior to that meeting, you had already been involved in similar discussions concerning the necessity of monetary payments, correct?</p>

<p><b>DEAN:</b> Yes.</p>

<p><b>DEFENSE COUNSEL:</b> So this was not a new subject.</p>

<p><b>DEAN:</b> No, it was not.</p>

<p><b>DEFENSE COUNSEL:</b> And the phrase "million dollars" came up in the context of your estimate?</p>

<p><b>DEAN:</b> Yes.</p>

<p><b>DEFENSE COUNSEL:</b> That was your figure?</p>

<p><b>DEAN:</b> Yes.</p>

<p><b>DEFENSE COUNSEL:</b> Not the defendant’s?</p>

<p><b>DEAN:</b> That’s correct.</p>

<p>
  Defense counsel pauses briefly.
</p>

<p><b>DEFENSE COUNSEL:</b> No further questions.</p>

<p>
  The prosecutor rises again.
</p>

<p><b>PROSECUTOR:</b> Briefly, Your Honor.</p>

<p>
  Mr. Dean, regardless of whose estimate the figure was, did the defendant respond by indicating that such funds could be obtained?
</p>

<p><b>DEAN:</b> Yes.</p>

<p><b>PROSECUTOR:</b> And that conversation occurred while he was still President?</p>

<p><b>DEAN:</b> Yes.</p>

<p><b>PROSECUTOR:</b> No further questions.</p>

<p><b>THE COURT:</b> The witness may step down.</p>

<p>
  Dean rises slowly, stepping down from the stand. He walks past the defense table without looking over.
</p>

<p>
  Former President Nixon sits quietly beside his counsel, hands folded, eyes fixed forward.
</p>

