<h3><b>The New York Times</b></h3>
<p><b>National Politics Desk</b></p>
<p><b>After Years of Reluctance, the Next Kennedy Weighs a Presidential Run</b></p>
<p><b>By Adam Nagourney and Gail Collins</b></p>

<p>WASHINGTON - For most of his public life, New York Senator John F. Kennedy Jr. insisted he had little interest in becoming what one family friend once called “the latest caretaker of the family mythology.”</p>

<p>He preferred committee work to rallies. Former aides say he visibly disliked being discussed as the continuation of a dynasty rather than as a politician in his own right.</p>

<p>For years, many Democrats believed him.</p>

<p>But after cruising to a second term this past November, advisers close to Mr. Kennedy now acknowledge he is moving steadily toward entering the 2008 Democratic presidential race, setting up what could become one of the most emotionally charged Democratic primary contests in decades. Governor Hillary Rodham of Illinois - fresh off her own landslide reelection - is also expected to announce her candidacy soon, likely with significant support from the party establishment. Former Vice President Al Gore continues weighing another campaign. Senators Russ Feingold and Joe Biden, along with Governor Bill Richardson, are also considering runs.</p>

<p>Yet much of the conversation inside Democratic circles increasingly revolves around a different question entirely: whether the party is preparing to revisit the political legacy of the Kennedy presidency itself.</p>

<p>Not John Kennedy’s presidency. Ted Kennedy’s.</p>

<p>For Democrats over fifty, the possibility carries an almost surreal quality. The administration of President Ted Kennedy remains one of the central reference points of modern liberalism. Universal healthcare. Universal childcare. Housing investments. Labor protections. Much of the modern Democratic welfare state traces back to legislation negotiated during the Kennedy years.</p>

<p>So does much of the modern conservative movement.</p>

<p><i>“The country has basically been arguing about Ted Kennedy’s presidency for thirty years,”</i> said presidential historian Doris Kearns Goodwin. <i>“Government, federal authority, income inequality, healthcare - all of those debates hardened during the 1980s.”</i></p>

<p>By the time Mr. Kennedy entered politics formally with his successful 2000 Senate campaign in New York, he had already spent years avoiding the rapid ascent many Democrats once assumed was inevitable. Inside the Senate, he developed a reputation as a detail-oriented institutionalist focused heavily on healthcare administration, voting access policy, and infrastructure funding.</p>

<p><i>“He’s much closer temperamentally to Ted than to Jack,”</i> said former Senate Majority Leader Tom Daschle. <i>“Less romantic. More legislative. He’s always been a humble, down-to-earth guy.”</i></p>

<p>That comparison surfaces constantly among Democrats who worked underneath President Ted Kennedy. Whereas John Kennedy projected glamour and generational optimism, Ted Kennedy’s presidency became associated more with coalition management and legislative construction.</p>

<p><i>“He’s not mystical in person,”</i> one former Cuomo administration official remarked of the younger Kennedy. <i>“People expecting Camelot are going to misunderstand him completely. The man still takes public transit to the Senate floor.”</i></p>

<p>Still, the symbolism matters.</p>

<p>No Democratic family remains more closely tied to the emotional identity of postwar liberalism than the Kennedys. Labor unions remain deeply loyal to the family. So do many older Black political organizations and women’s advocacy groups whose national influence expanded significantly during the administration.</p>

<p><i>“There’s still an entire generation of Democrats who talk about Ted Kennedy the way Republicans talk about Reagan,”</i> said former White House adviser Elaine Kamarck.</p>

<p>Republicans, meanwhile, appear almost eager for the possibility of a Kennedy candidacy. Several conservative strategists privately acknowledged this week that a Kennedy campaign would reopen ideological fights over the size and role of government that have shaped national politics since the late 1970s.</p>

<p>Yet Democrats argue the country looks very different than it did during the Reagan years. Healthcare costs continue rising sharply. Wage stagnation has intensified across much of the Midwest. Public frustration with the Iraq stabilization mission has weakened confidence in Republican governance.</p>

<p>Last week, Senator Kennedy briefly stopped outside his Capitol Hill office after a meeting with veterans’ healthcare advocates. Asked whether he intended to run for president, he smiled awkwardly.</p>

<p><i>“I’ve spent most of my career trying not to answer that question,”</i> he replied.</p>

<p>When another reporter asked whether the decision had become harder recently, Kennedy paused.</p>

<p><i>“The country’s in a very different place than it was ten years ago,”</i> he said quietly. <i>“People are finally asking the bigger questions again.”</i></p>


