<p><b>Nixon:</b> Which ones are we looking at?</p>

<p><b>Haldeman:</b> Washington is complicated. Illinois is possible. California depends on suburban members who are nervous about being fully absorbed into Muskie’s reform agenda once it becomes nationalized.</p>

<p><b>Ehrlichman:</b> Muskie’s advantage is his cohesion among the northern liberals and coastal moderates. The counterweight is the South. Connally is already working the region, and if they hold, it prevents Muskie from turning this into a clean institutional sweep in the House.</p>

<p><b>Nixon:</b> So we keep the South solid and force him to stretch out everything else.</p>

<p><b>[11:53 PM]</b></p>

<p><b>[A television anchor briefly mentions “constitutional uncertainty” before the volume is lowered.]</b></p>

<p><b>Haldeman:</b> We also have to think about how this looks outside. If this stretches into December, people are going to start wondering if the country actually has a government.</p>

<p><b>Ehrlichman:</b> Muskie will try to frame the House vote as the system correcting itself. They still think the Nixon Administration was an aberration.</p>

<p><b>Nixon:</b> The will of the people, huh.</b></p>

<p><b>[Nixon shakes his head slightly.]</b></p>

<p><b>Nixon:</b> Four years of hearing the system is broken, and now everybody’s suddenly a constitutional expert.</p>

<p><b>[12:01 AM]</b></p>

<p><b>Haldeman:</b> We should get congressional leadership on the phone tonight. No waiting until morning.</p>

<p><b>Ehrlichman:</b> Agreed. We need commitments before the press sets the narrative.</p>

<p><b>Nixon:</b> Call Ford first. Then Rhodes. I want every delegation counted before sunrise.</p>

<p><b>[A long pause follows as projections flicker across the screens.]</b></p>

<p><b>Nixon:</b> You know what the worst part of this is?</p>

<p><b>Ehrlichman:</b> No, sir.</p>

<p><b>Nixon:</b> We were supposed to be done with this tonight. Instead, we’re sitting here counting congressmen one at a time like it’s 1876.</p>

<p><b>[12:09 AM]</b></p>

<p><b>Haldeman:</b> We can still win this.</p>

<p><b>Nixon:</b> I know we can. The question is whether the party can stay together long enough to make it happen.</p>

<p><b>Ehrlichman:</b> If the delegations hold, we get there.</p>

<p><b>Nixon:</b> Then make sure they hold.</p>

<p><b>[A pause.]</b></p>

<p><b>Nixon:</b> And somebody get Connally on the phone. If the South’s going to decide this thing, I want to know that he’s working every line he’s got.</p>

<p><b>[12:18 AM]</b></p>

