<center><h3><b>THE NEW YORK TIMES<br>OPINION<br>OCTOBER 14, 2008</b></h3>

<p><b>How the Election of 1972 Still Shapes a Country on Edge</b></p></center>

<p><b>By Adam Nagourney</b></p>

<p><b>October 18, 2008</b></p>

<p><b>How the Election of 1972 Still Shapes a Country on Edge</b></p>

<p><b>By Adam Nagourney</b></p>

<p><b>WASHINGTON —</b> The 1972 presidential election is often remembered in shorthand as a tie that no one was prepared for, followed by a constitutional scramble that left the country arguing less about who won than about how the winner should be certified at all.</p>

<p>What followed, in the years after Congress resolved the outcome, was the Bayh-Cellar Amendment in 1974. It preserved the Electoral College in name but effectively replaced it with a national popular vote system. At the time, its supporters described it as a safeguard against procedural collapse. Critics warned it would flatten regional politics. Neither description quite captures what it became in practice.</p>

<p>By 2008, campaigns are run on national totals. Polling is discussed in percentages rather than electoral pathways. Even the idea of a “battleground state” has shifted into something closer to shorthand than strategy.</p>

<p>The first full test of that system came in 1992.</p>

<p>President George H. W. Bush won reelection with roughly 38 percent of the vote. Governor Bill Clinton finished near 35 percent. Ross Perot took about 24 percent.</p>

<p>Perot’s support cut across several political lines, but not evenly. He drew heavily from protectionist Democrats in industrial states, especially voters who had long identified with Democratic economics but were uneasy with the party’s direction on trade. He also drew a smaller but noticeable share from Republican voters in rural areas and in more moderate urban and inner-suburban regions, particularly in the Midwest and Northeast, where economic frustration outweighed party loyalty.</p>

<p>One Democratic Senate aide from the period later described the pattern as “people leaving different rooms in the same house, just through different doors.”</p>

<p>Because there was no incentive for consolidation, Perot remained in the race through Election Day, and both campaigns were forced to treat him as a permanent third presence rather than a late disruption.</p>

<p>Bush’s second term carried a clearer governing majority than his vote share suggested, but it was defined by a set of early policy decisions that shaped both foreign and domestic alignment. On foreign policy, the administration expanded NATO coordination in Eastern Europe and maintained a sustained diplomatic framework in the Balkans that relied heavily on joint European enforcement rather than unilateral American action. It also deepened intelligence cooperation in the Middle East following the 1993 World Trade Center attack, which became an early organizing point for counterterrorism policy across both parties.</p>

<p>Domestically, Bush signed the 1993 deficit stabilization package, which combined spending caps with targeted tax adjustments, and a 1995 trade adjustment framework that expanded worker retraining programs in exchange for broader tariff reductions. The trade bill drew quiet support from Democrats but created visible tension within Republican ranks, particularly among members aligned with emerging protectionist sentiment in the party’s base.</p>

<p>The 1994 midterms produced minor Democratic gains in both the House and Senate. Republicans had entered the cycle expecting a standard midterm environment and instead faced an electorate that had already normalized close national outcomes. The result deepened frustration among the emerging conservative right, which increasingly viewed the party’s governing approach as insufficiently responsive.</p>

<p>In the Senate, Jim Sasser became Democratic leader in 1995 following George Mitchell’s retirement. Sasser’s leadership was defined by discipline and low tolerance for internal fragmentation, shaped by the party’s extended period of presidential losses and thin national margins. Tom Daschle served as whip and later deputy leader, building influence through procedural control and caucus management.</p>

<p>By 1996, Republicans nominated Jack Kemp after Dan Quayle collapsed in a crowded primary field. Kemp chose Carroll Campbell of South Carolina as his running mate, aiming to hold together a coalition increasingly pulled in different directions.</p>

<p>Kemp’s presidency was defined by an unusually high volume of legislative negotiation with Democratic leadership. He signed the 1997 Balanced Growth and Budget Reform Act, which paired entitlement restructuring with middle-income tax relief and discretionary spending caps. In 1998, he signed the National Infrastructure Modernization Act, expanding federal transportation investment alongside state matching requirements. In 1999, he signed a financial deregulation package that loosened restrictions on banking consolidation while increasing capital reserve requirements, a compromise that satisfied neither Wall Street critics nor populist conservatives.</p>

<p>On foreign policy, Kemp continued Bush-era NATO coordination but expanded American involvement in peace enforcement operations in the Balkans and authorized limited strikes in response to embassy attacks in East Africa in 1998. His administration also began early planning frameworks for counterterrorism cooperation that would later be expanded under Clark.</p>

<p>Within his own party, however, Kemp faced growing resistance. Newt Gingrich, who became House Republican leader in 1997, consolidated the conference around a more aggressive legislative posture. That approach strengthened Republican performance in parts of the South, where the party continued to gain ground in congressional races, but it also accelerated losses in moderate Republican territory in the Northeast, the Midwest, and the West Coast, particularly in inner suburban and urban-adjacent districts that had once formed the party’s governing backbone.</p>

<p>By 2000, the Republican Party entered the presidential cycle with internal exhaustion rather than momentum.</p>

<p>Kemp initially ran for a second term, but his campaign collapsed under sustained criticism from the party’s right flank, which argued that his presidency had relied too heavily on Democratic votes in Congress and failed to advance a unified conservative agenda. He withdrew during the primary process, leaving the party fragmented and unable to consolidate around a single successor.</p>

<p>The result was a disorganized general election effort led by Carroll Campbell, who ultimately lost in a decisive landslide to Wesley Clark.</p>

<p>Clark’s presidency, beginning in 2001, was shaped immediately by the September 11 attacks. He authorized the invasion of Afghanistan later that year, building a broad international coalition that included NATO partners and regional allies. The war remained central to his first term, including a 2003 troop surge that expanded counterinsurgency operations and increased long-term American military presence in the region.</p>

<p>Domestically, Clark signed the No Child Left Behind Act in 2002, which significantly increased federal education spending tied to standardized accountability measures, expanding funding for Title I schools and literacy programs. In 2003, he signed Medicare Part D, establishing a prescription drug benefit for seniors that reshaped federal healthcare spending over the following decade.</p>

<p>In 2005, his administration enacted CHIP expansion for universal coverage of all children under 18, extending federal-state cost sharing for pediatric care and broadening eligibility thresholds in most states. That same period also saw passage of a paid family and medical leave program, alongside a bipartisan immigration reform package that combined border enforcement funding with a pathway to legal status for long-term undocumented residents meeting employment and residency requirements.</p>

<p>Clark also signed a Social Security and Medicaid expansion package in 2006 that modestly raised payroll contributions while increasing long-term solvency projections, paired with a broader budget reduction agreement that trimmed discretionary domestic spending growth without altering core entitlement structures.</p>

<p>His second term was marked by continued stabilization efforts in Afghanistan and gradual drawdown planning, as well as expanded NATO coordination in counterterrorism operations following renewed threats in the mid-2000s.</p>

<p>Politically, Democrats held the Senate through the 2004 elections despite the unexpected defeat of Senator Tom Daschle, which removed the party’s whip and forced a leadership transition. Harry Reid, who had been third in line in the leadership structure before Daschle’s loss and second afterward, became minority leader in 2007 following Jim Sasser’s retirement. That transition came after Republicans captured both houses of Congress in the 2006 midterms, ending more than a decade of divided control and reshaping the legislative environment entering the 2008 cycle.</p>

<p>Now, in October 2008, the presidential race has tightened into a narrow national margin.</p>

<p>National polling averages show Senator John McCain leading Vice President John Edwards by roughly one to three percentage points, with most surveys falling within the margin of error. The race has remained unusually stable given the severity of the financial crisis that has defined the final year of the Clark administration.</p>

<p>Edwards benefits from a unified Democratic Party and continued approval of Clark’s handling of both foreign policy and domestic stabilization efforts during the financial downturn. McCain, however, continues to outperform among independents and moderate Republicans, particularly in inner suburban districts across the Midwest, Northeast, and West Coast where party loyalty has weakened over the past decade.</p>

<p>One Republican strategist described the environment simply. “He’s ahead, but it’s thin everywhere you look.”</p>

<p>With both campaigns closely matched and the electorate still sorting through the economic collapse, neither side is assuming any early resolution.</p>

<p>In a system that has been defined for decades by national accumulation rather than regional advantage, that uncertainty has become the constant feature of presidential politics.</p>
